    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: How does TCP sender perceive congestion? ( )\n\nOptions: \nA. Through a timeout event\nB. Through a receiving duplicate ACK-s event\nC. Both A and B\nD. Either A or B\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The correct answer is \\boxed{C}.\n\nTCP senders perceive congestion through a few mechanisms. A timeout event (A) is a strong indicator of congestion, as it means the sender hasn't received an acknowledgment for a transmitted segment within a certain period. Receiving duplicate ACKs (B) also signals congestion because it indicates that segments are arriving out of order at the receiver, implying that some segments might have been dropped or delayed.",
