    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\nfrom collections import deque\n\ndef solve():\n    n1, n2, m = map(int, input().split())\n    n = n1 + n2\n    adj = [[] for _ in range(n + 1)]\n    for _ in range(m):\n        u, v = map(int, input().split())\n        adj[u].append(v)\n        adj[v].append(u)\n\n    def bfs(start_node, num_nodes):\n        dist = [-1] * (num_nodes + 1)\n        dist[start_node] = 0\n        q = deque([start_node])\n        max_dist = 0\n\n        while q:\n            u = q.popleft()\n            max_dist = max(max_dist, dist[u])\n            for v in adj[u]:\n                if dist[v] == -1:\n                    dist[v] = dist[u] + 1\n                    q.append(v)\n        return dist, max_dist\n\n    # Calculate distances from vertex 1 within the first component\n    dist_from_1, _ = bfs(1, n1)\n\n    # Calculate distances from vertex n within the second component\n    dist_from_n, _ = bfs(n, n)\n\n    max_d = 0\n\n    # Iterate through all possible edges to add\n    # u is in the first component (1 to n1)\n    # v is in the second component (n1+1 to n)\n    for u in range(1, n1 + 1):\n        for v in range(n1 + 1, n + 1):\n            # The distance between vertex 1 and vertex n after adding edge (u, v) is:\n            # min(dist(1, u) + 1 + dist(v, n))\n            # The distance dist(1, u) is the distance within the first component.\n            # The distance dist(v, n) is the distance within the second component.\n            # Since the first component is fully connected and the second is fully connected,\n            # bfs(1, n1) will give us correct distances from 1 to any node in the first component,\n            # and bfs(n, n) will give us correct distances from n to any node in the second component.\n\n            # We need to consider the path: 1 -> ... -> u -> v -> ... -> n\n            # dist(1, u) is dist_from_1[u]\n            # dist(v, n) is dist_from_n[v]\n            # The path length is dist_from_1[u] + 1 + dist_from_n[v]\n\n            if dist_from_1[u] != -1 and dist_from_n[v] != -1:\n                current_d = dist_from_1[u] + 1 + dist_from_n[v]\n                max_d = max(max_d, current_d)\n\n    # A more optimized approach using the maximum distances within components\n    # The maximum distance from vertex 1 to any node in the first component.\n    max_dist_1_to_n1_component = 0\n    for i in range(1, n1 + 1):\n        if dist_from_1[i] != -1:\n            max_dist_1_to_n1_component = max(max_dist_1_to_n1_component, dist_from_1[i])\n\n    # The maximum distance from vertex n to any node in the second component.\n    max_dist_n_to_n2_component = 0\n    for i in range(n1 + 1, n + 1):\n        if dist_from_n[i] != -1:\n            max_dist_n_to_n2_component = max(max_dist_n_to_n2_component, dist_from_n[i])\n\n    # The maximum possible d is achieved when we connect a vertex in the first component\n    # that is furthest from vertex 1, to a vertex in the second component that is furthest\n    # from vertex n.\n    # The path would be 1 --(max_dist_1_to_n1_component edges)-- u --(1 edge)-- v --(max_dist_n_to_n2_component edges)-- n\n    # Total length = max_dist_1_to_n1_component + 1 + max_dist_n_to_n2_component\n    print(max_dist_1_to_n1_component + 1 + max_dist_n_to_n2_component)\n\n\nsolve()\n```",
