    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_263",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. The function should take stdin as input and print the output. Simply call the function after the definition. Question:\nN players, player 1, player 2, ..., player N, participate in a game tournament. Just before the tournament starts, each player forms a one-person team, so there are N teams in total.\nThe tournament has a total of N-1 matches. In each match, two different teams are chosen. One team goes first, and the other goes second. Each match will result in exactly one team winning. Specifically, for each i = 1, 2, \\ldots, N-1, the i-th match proceeds as follows.\n\n- The team with player p_i goes first, and the team with player q_i goes second.\n- Let a and b be the numbers of players in the first and second teams, respectively. The first team wins with probability \\frac{{a}}{{a+b}}, and the second team wins with probability \\frac{{b}}{{a+b}}.\n- Then, the two teams are combined into a single team.\n\nThe result of each match is independent of those of the others.\nFor each of the N players, print the expected number of times the team with that player wins throughout the tournament, modulo 998244353.\n How to print an expected value modulo 998244353\nIt can be proved that the sought expected value is always rational. Also, the constraints of this problem guarantee that if the sought expected value is expressed as an irreducible fraction \\frac{{y}}{{x}}, then x is not divisible by 998244353. Now, there is a unique integer z between 0 and 998244352, inclusive, such that xz \\equiv y \\pmod{{998244353}}. Report this z.\n\nInput\n\nThe input is given from Standard Input in the following format:\nN\np_1 q_1\np_2 q_2\n\\vdots\np_{{N-1}} q_{{N-1}}\n\nOutput\n\nFor each i = 1, 2, \\ldots, N, print E_i, the expected number, modulo 998244353, of times the team with player i wins throughout the tournament, separated by spaces, in the following format:\nE_1 E_2 \\ldots E_N\n\nConstraints\n\n\n- 2 \\leq N \\leq 2 \\times 10^5\n- 1 \\leq p_i, q_i \\leq N\n- Just before the i-th match, player p_i and player q_i belong to different teams.\n- All input values are integers.\n\nSample Input 1\n\n5\n1 2\n4 3\n5 3\n1 4\n\nSample Output 1\n\n698771048 698771048 964969543 964969543 133099248\n\nWe call a team formed by player x_1, player x_2, \\ldots, player x_k as team \\lbrace x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_k \\rbrace.\n\n- The first match is played by team \\lbrace 1 \\rbrace, with player 1, and team \\lbrace 2 \\rbrace, with player 2. Team \\lbrace 1 \\rbrace wins with probability \\frac{{1}}{{2}}, and team \\lbrace 2 \\rbrace wins with probability \\frac{{1}}{{2}}. Then, the two teams are combined into a single team \\lbrace 1, 2 \\rbrace.\n- The second match is played by team \\lbrace 4 \\rbrace, with player 4, and team \\lbrace 3 \\rbrace, with player 3. Team \\lbrace 4 \\rbrace wins with probability \\frac{{1}}{{2}}, and team \\lbrace 3 \\rbrace wins with probability \\frac{{1}}{{2}}. Then, the two teams are combined into a single team \\lbrace 3, 4 \\rbrace.\n- The third match is played by team \\lbrace 5 \\rbrace, with player 5, and team \\lbrace 3, 4 \\rbrace, with player 3. Team \\lbrace 5 \\rbrace wins with probability \\frac{{1}}{{3}}, and team \\lbrace 3, 4 \\rbrace wins with probability \\frac{{2}}{{3}}. Then, the two teams are combined into a single team \\lbrace 3, 4, 5 \\rbrace.\n- The fourth match is played by team \\lbrace 1, 2 \\rbrace, with player 1, and team \\lbrace 3, 4, 5 \\rbrace, with player 4. Team \\lbrace 1, 2 \\rbrace wins with probability \\frac{{2}}{{5}}, and team \\lbrace 3, 4, 5 \\rbrace wins with probability \\frac{{3}}{{5}}. Then, the two teams are combined into a single team \\lbrace 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 \\rbrace.\n\nThe expected numbers of times the teams with players 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 win throughout the tournament, E_1, E_2, E_3, E_4, E_5, are \\frac{{9}}{{10}}, \\frac{{9}}{{10}}, \\frac{{53}}{{30}}, \\frac{{53}}{{30}}, \\frac{{14}}{{15}}, respectively.\n\nSample Input 2\n\n15\n9 2\n8 10\n13 6\n12 11\n7 10\n4 10\n14 2\n5 4\n1 15\n15 2\n6 9\n8 11\n6 3\n2 8\n\nSample Output 2\n\n43970290 310168785 806914186 501498951 950708909 272140427 335124893 168750835 310168785 168750835 280459129 280459129 272140427 476542843 43970290",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport sys\n\ndef solve():\n    N = int(sys.stdin.readline())\n    matches = []\n    for _ in range(N - 1):\n        p, q = map(int, sys.stdin.readline().split())\n        matches.append((p - 1, q - 1)) # Adjust to 0-indexed\n\n    MOD = 998244353\n\n    # Function to calculate modular inverse (a^(MOD-2) % MOD)\n    def mod_inverse(n):\n        return pow(n, MOD - 2, MOD)\n\n    # Adjacency list for the tree structure formed by matches\n    adj = [[] for _ in range(N)]\n    parent = [-1] * N\n    match_index_of_edge = {}\n\n    for i, (u, v) in enumerate(matches):\n        adj[u].append((v, i))\n        adj[v].append((u, i))\n\n    # Perform BFS to build the tree and determine parent/child relationships\n    # This also helps to map each player to their 'birth' match as a child\n    queue = [(0, -1, -1)] # (current_node, parent_node, match_idx_to_parent)\n    visited = [False] * N\n    visited[0] = True\n    dfs_order = []\n    \n    q_idx = 0\n    bfs_q = [0]\n    bfs_parent_info = [(-1, -1)] * N # (parent_node, match_idx_to_parent)\n    \n    while q_idx < len(bfs_q):\n        u = bfs_q[q_idx]\n        q_idx += 1\n        dfs_order.append(u) # For post-order traversal later if needed\n\n        for v, match_idx in adj[u]:\n            if not visited[v]:\n                visited[v] = True\n                bfs_parent_info[v] = (u, match_idx)\n                bfs_q.append(v)\n    \n    # Each player initially forms a team of size 1.\n    # The expected number of wins for player 'i' when team 't' wins is 1/size(t).\n    # Since expected value is linear, E[sum X_i] = sum E[X_i]\n    # For a specific match, player `x` in team `A` (size `a`) vs team `B` (size `b`):\n    # If team `A` wins, player `x` contributes `1/a` to their total expected wins.\n    # The probability of `A` winning is `a/(a+b)`.\n    # So, for player `x` in team `A`: contribution = (a/(a+b)) * (1/a) = 1/(a+b)\n    # This holds if `x` is in the winning team, regardless of whether it's the first or second team.\n    #\n    # We need to find for each player `i`, and for each match `k` where player `i`'s team participates:\n    # 1. The size of player `i`'s team in that match.\n    # 2. The size of the opposing team in that match.\n\n    # Let size[u] be the number of players in the subtree rooted at u\n    # after all matches within the subtree have occurred.\n    subtree_size = [1] * N # Start with each player as a team of size 1\n    \n    # We need to process players in post-order (leaves to root for subtree sizes)\n    # The BFS order in reverse gives a valid post-order for this tree structure\n    \n    # Calculate subtree sizes (number of original players in the combined team)\n    for u in reversed(dfs_order):\n        if bfs_parent_info[u][0] != -1:\n            p_u = bfs_parent_info[u][0]\n            subtree_size[p_u] += subtree_size[u]\n            \n    # dp_e[u] will store the sum of 1/(total_size_of_match) for all matches\n    # where player u's team participated and was on the 'child' side of the final combine.\n    # More specifically, it stores the sum of conditional expected wins for player u\n    # contributed by matches where u's team was the \"child\" team in the (u, parent_u) edge.\n    # This is essentially the expected wins from matches that occur \"above\" u in the tree,\n    # where u is part of the team rooted by its parent.\n    \n    expected_wins = [0] * N\n    \n    # Initialize expected_wins for each player if they were to win their 'first' match (subtree match).\n    # This considers all players within their initial team of size 1.\n    # For each match (p_i, q_i), one of them is the 'parent' and the other is the 'child'.\n    # A player u is in the team formed by its subtree. When this team merges with its parent's team,\n    # the player u is considered part of the \"child\" team from the perspective of that specific merge.\n    # The probability of winning: child_size / (child_size + parent_size)\n    # Expected wins for *any* player in the child team: (child_size / (child_size + parent_size)) * (1 / child_size)\n    # = 1 / (child_size + parent_size)\n    \n    # Let total_match_sum_inv[u] be the sum of 1/total_size for all matches where player u is part of the winning team,\n    # specifically when u's team is a 'child team' that wins and its size gets absorbed by its parent.\n    # This effectively sums 1/(size_team_A + size_team_B) for each match where player u is involved\n    # and considered part of the team that gets 'absorbed' into a larger structure.\n\n    # We iterate through the tree using BFS_ORDER (top-down) for the DP.\n    # expected_wins[u] will store the total expected wins for player u.\n    # Initialize: Each player's team starts with size 1.\n    # For each match `(p_idx, q_idx)`:\n    # Say `p_idx` is the parent of `q_idx`.\n    # Player `q_idx` (and players in its subtree) get `1 / (size[q_idx] + (total_N - size[q_idx]))`\n    #   if `q_idx` is the 'child' in the match.\n    # The `total_N - size[q_idx]` is the size of the team that `q_idx` merges into.\n    # This is equivalent to `1 / N` for each match edge.\n    \n    # Re-evaluate the contribution:\n    # A player `i` contributes to the expected number of wins for their team\n    # whenever their team (containing `i`) wins a match.\n    # If player `i` is in team `T_A` (size `a`) and `T_A` beats `T_B` (size `b`),\n    # `T_A` wins with probability `a / (a+b)`.\n    # Player `i`'s expected individual wins contribution from this match is `a / (a+b) * (1/a) = 1 / (a+b)`.\n    # If `T_B` beats `T_A`, `T_B` wins with probability `b / (a+b)`.\n    # Player `i`'s expected individual wins contribution from this match is `0`.\n    #\n    # So, for player `i`, we need to sum `1 / (a+b)` for all matches where `i`'s team wins.\n    # This is actually simpler: it's just `1 / (size_of_first_team + size_of_second_team)` if player `i` is in the first team,\n    # and the same if `i` is in the second team. Every player in the *winning* team contributes `1/winning_team_size` to their own total.\n    # The probability of winning for any player in that team is `1 / (size_first + size_second)`.\n    # There are `N-1` matches.\n    # For each match `k`, let the two teams be `T_1` and `T_2` with sizes `s_1` and `s_2`.\n    # The probability `T_1` wins is `s_1 / (s_1 + s_2)`. The probability `T_2` wins is `s_2 / (s_1 + s_2)`.\n    # The expected contribution to player `i` from this match `k` is:\n    # if `i` is in `T_1`: (s_1 / (s_1 + s_2)) * (1/s_1) = `1 / (s_1 + s_2)`\n    # if `i` is in `T_2`: (s_2 / (s_1 + s_2)) * (1/s_2) = `1 / (s_1 + s_2)`\n    #\n    # THIS IS THE KEY INSIGHT: for any match a player is involved in, their expected contribution\n    # to their personal win count is `1 / (sum of team sizes)`.\n    # Example Walkthrough:\n    # N=5\n    # Matches: (1,2), (4,3), (5,3'), (1',4')\n    #\n    # Match 1: {1} vs {2}. Sizes 1, 1. Total size 2.\n    # E_1 contributes 1/2. E_2 contributes 1/2.\n    # Combined team: {1,2} (size 2)\n    #\n    # Match 2: {4} vs {3}. Sizes 1, 1. Total size 2.\n    # E_4 contributes 1/2. E_3 contributes 1/2.\n    # Combined team: {3,4} (size 2)\n    #\n    # Match 3: {5} vs {3,4}. Sizes 1, 2. Total size 3.\n    # E_5 contributes 1/3. E_3 contributes 1/3. E_4 contributes 1/3.\n    # Combined team: {3,4,5} (size 3)\n    #\n    # Match 4: {1,2} vs {3,4,5}. Sizes 2, 3. Total size 5.\n    # E_1 contributes 1/5. E_2 contributes 1/5.\n    # E_3 contributes 1/5. E_4 contributes 1/5. E_5 contributes 1/5.\n    # Combined team: {1,2,3,4,5} (size 5)\n    #\n    # Total E_1 = 1/2 + 1/5 = 7/10.  Wait, sample output is 9/10. What's wrong?\n    #\n    # The sample output interpretation for player 1: E_1 = 9/10\n    # \"Expected number of times the team with that player wins\"\n    # Match 1: player 1's team is {1}. Wins prob 1/2. Contribution 1 * 1/2.\n    # After Match 1, team {1,2}.\n    #\n    # Match 4: player 1's team is {1,2}. Sizes 2 vs 3. Wins prob 2/5. Contribution 1 * 2/5.\n    # Total E_1 = 1/2 + 2/5 = 9/10. This matches the sample!\n    #\n    # So the expected value for player `i` is the sum of probabilities that their *current team* wins,\n    # for all matches their team is involved in.\n    #\n    # This means for each player `i`, we need to track:\n    # 1. The sequence of teams player `i` is a part of.\n    # 2. For each such team, its size and the opposing team's size.\n    # This requires simulating the tournament or using a more complex DP on the tree.\n    #\n    # Let's consider the matches (p_k, q_k) in the input order.\n    # We need to maintain the current set of players in each team.\n    # A DSU (Disjoint Set Union) structure can help here.\n    \n    parent_dsu = list(range(N))\n    team_members = [set([i]) for i in range(N)] # Store actual player members for each root\n    team_size = [1] * N # Size of team for each root\n\n    def find(i):\n        if parent_dsu[i] == i:\n            return i\n        parent_dsu[i] = find(parent_dsu[i])\n        return parent_dsu[i]\n\n    # Expected wins for each player\n    E = [0] * N\n\n    for match_idx, (p_player, q_player) in enumerate(matches):\n        root_p = find(p_player)\n        root_q = find(q_player)\n\n        # Before combining, get team sizes\n        size_p_team = team_size[root_p]\n        size_q_team = team_size[root_q]\n\n        total_size = size_p_team + size_q_team\n        inv_total_size = mod_inverse(total_size)\n\n        # Calculate contributions\n        # Player p_player's team (root_p) goes first\n        # It wins with probability size_p_team / total_size\n        prob_p_wins = (size_p_team * inv_total_size) % MOD\n        # Player q_player's team (root_q) goes second\n        # It wins with probability size_q_team / total_size\n        prob_q_wins = (size_q_team * inv_total_size) % MOD\n\n        # Add contributions to all players in these teams\n        for player_in_p_team in team_members[root_p]:\n            E[player_in_p_team] = (E[player_in_p_team] + prob_p_wins) % MOD\n        for player_in_q_team in team_members[root_q]:\n            E[player_in_q_team] = (E[player_in_q_team] + prob_q_wins) % MOD\n            \n        # Combine the teams (union operation)\n        # We can merge root_q into root_p, for example.\n        parent_dsu[root_q] = root_p\n        team_members[root_p].update(team_members[root_q])\n        team_size[root_p] = total_size\n        # Clear out root_q's set to reduce memory if needed, though not strictly necessary\n        # team_members[root_q] = set() # This can be problematic if root_q is later used as a representative for its original players\n\n    sys.stdout.write(\" \".join(map(str, E)) + \"\\n\")\n\nsolve()\n\n```",
