      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport sys\n\ndef solve():\n    N, M = map(int, sys.stdin.readline().split())\n\n    edges = []\n    node_to_group = [[] for _ in range(N + 1)]\n    group_edges = []\n\n    for i in range(M):\n        line1 = list(map(int, sys.stdin.readline().split()))\n        K_i = line1[0]\n        C_i = line1[1]\n        S_i = list(map(int, sys.stdin.readline().split()))\n\n        # Add edges between 'special' node M + i + 1 and nodes in S_i\n        # All these edges have weight C_i\n        # This transforms a clique into a star, preserving connectivity properties\n        # when we consider the weights in a specific way for MST.\n        # However, for Kruskal's, we need actual edges. The trick is to sort edges by weight.\n\n        # Store edges in a format suitable for Kruskal's\n        # Pair (weight, u, v)\n        # For a clique, we don't explicitly add all K_i * (K_i - 1) / 2 edges.\n        # Instead, we use a different graph construction where each S_i forms a star\n        # with a 'supernode' representing the clique.\n        # The 'supernode' for S_i will be N + i.\n        # The edges will be (C_i, u, N + i) for all u in S_i.\n        # The number of vertices becomes N + M.\n\n        if K_i >= 2:\n            group_edges.append((C_i, S_i)) # Store (weight, nodes_in_group)\n            \n    # Kruskal's algorithm\n    # Edges are sorted by weight.\n    # The trick for dense cliques is specific. For a group S_i with weight C_i,\n    # the edges (u,v) for u, v in S_i all have weight C_i.\n    # When Kruskal's considers edges of weight C_i, if it can connect a node\n    # in S_i to another node in S_i (or a component containing another node in S_i)\n    # using weight C_i, it means we can use an edge from this group.\n    # We can model this by adding at most K_i - 1 edges of weight C_i.\n    # A cleaner approach for Kruskal's on \"clique operations\":\n    # Create a new graph with N + M nodes. The first N nodes are the original vertices.\n    # The next M nodes (N+1 to N+M) are \"helper\" nodes, one for each operation.\n    # For each operation i (with weight C_i and set S_i):\n    # Add an edge (u, N+i) with weight C_i for all u in S_i.\n    # The MST of this new graph gives the correct result.\n    \n    all_kruskal_edges = []\n    \n    for i in range(M):\n        line1 = list(map(int, sys.stdin.readline().split())) # Read again to maintain consistency if not actually seeking to avoid re-reading\n        K_i = line1[0]\n        C_i = line1[1]\n        S_i = list(map(int, sys.stdin.readline().split()))\n        \n        super_node = N + i + 1 # Nodes N+1 to N+M are helper nodes\n        for u in S_i:\n            all_kruskal_edges.append((C_i, u, super_node))\n\n    # Sort all edges by weight\n    all_kruskal_edges.sort()\n\n    parent = list(range(N + M + 1))\n    rank = [0] * (N + M + 1)\n    \n    def find(i):\n        if parent[i] == i:\n            return i\n        parent[i] = find(parent[i])\n        return parent[i]\n\n    def union(i, j):\n        root_i = find(i)\n        root_j = find(j)\n        if root_i != root_j:\n            if rank[root_i] < rank[root_j]:\n                parent[root_i] = root_j\n            elif rank[root_i] > rank[root_j]:\n                parent[root_j] = root_i\n            else:\n                parent[root_j] = root_i\n                rank[root_i] += 1\n            return True\n        return False\n\n    mst_weight = 0\n    connected_components = N # Initially, only real nodes are counted as components\n\n    active_components = 0\n    for i in range(1, N + 1):\n        if parent[i] == i:\n            active_components += 1\n            \n    # For Kruskal's, we iterate through sorted edges. The \"nodes\" are now N+M.\n    # However, we only care about connecting the original N nodes.\n    # A total of N-1 edges are needed to connect N nodes.\n    # Kruskal's with N+M nodes will add N+M-1 edges for a spanning tree of the supergraph.\n    # The critical insight is that if we form an MST on the N+M graph,\n    # the original N nodes will be connected if and only if the full N+M graph is connected\n    # (since the helper nodes act as intermediaries to connect cliques).\n    # And the MST weight for the original N nodes is correctly found.\n\n    # We need to explicitly count how many real nodes are in the same component.\n    # A simple way to track this is to count the number of nodes whose root is themselves,\n    # and only count roots that belong to the original N nodes.\n    \n    # Let's track connected components of the ORIGINAL N vertices.\n    # Use a separate DSU for the original N vertices to check connectivity.\n    parent_orig = list(range(N + 1))\n    rank_orig = [0] * (N + 1)\n    \n    def find_orig(i):\n        if parent_orig[i] == i:\n            return i\n        parent_orig[i] = find_orig(parent_orig[i])\n        return parent_orig[i]\n\n    def union_orig(i, j):\n        root_i = find_orig(i)\n        root_j = find_orig(j)\n        if root_i != root_j:\n            if rank_orig[root_i] < rank_orig[root_j]:\n                parent_orig[root_i] = root_j\n            elif rank_orig[root_i] > rank_orig[root_j]:\n                parent_orig[root_j] = root_i\n            else:\n                parent_orig[root_j] = root_i\n                rank_orig[root_i] += 1\n            return True\n        return False\n\n    num_connected_components_orig = N # Initially N separate components\n\n    for weight, u, v in all_kruskal_edges:\n        if union(u, v):\n            mst_weight += weight\n            # If both u and v are original nodes, or one is original and the other is a supernode\n            # and that supernode connects to another original node, it effectively bridges components.\n            # We need to track the components of the original nodes carefully.\n            # When an edge (u, v) is added in the N+M graph:\n            # If 'v' is a supernode (N+i), and 'u' is an original node (1..N),\n            # this means 'u' is now connected to the clique represented by N+i.\n            # We need to consider all other nodes in S_i.\n            # This is why the N+M node DSU is designed: it simplifies clique logic.\n            # However, for counting components of original N nodes, we just need to observe\n            # if an edge between two ORIGINAL nodes 'x' and 'y' (possibly through helper nodes)\n            # is effectively created.\n            # The standard DSU on N+M nodes correctly finds the MST weight.\n            # To check connectivity for the original N nodes:\n            \n            # The union operation (u, v) connects component_u and component_v.\n            # If both u and v are original nodes, then we just do union_orig(u,v)\n            # If one is a supernode (v_super) and the other is an original node (u_orig):\n            # Then all nodes in S_i for operation_i (which created v_super) are\n            # now effectively connected to u_orig's component.\n            # This is complex to track.\n\n            # Simple solution for connectivity check:\n            # After building the MST on N+M vertices, iterate through all original nodes (1 to N).\n            # Find the root for each of them. If all their roots are the same, they are connected.\n            # This requires running `find` on all N nodes at the end, and comparing their roots.\n            pass # union(u,v) already updates parent for the bigger graph\n            \n    # Check connectivity of original N nodes\n    if N == 0: # Handle edge case if N is allowed to be 0 or 1. Constraints say N >= 2\n        print(0)\n        return\n\n    first_root = find(1)\n    is_connected = True\n    for i in range(2, N + 1):\n        if find(i) != first_root:\n            is_connected = False\n            break\n\n    if not is_connected:\n        print(-1)\n    else:\n        print(mst_weight)\n\nsolve()\n```",
