    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. The function should take stdin as input and print the output. Simply call the function after the definition. Question:\nOn a coordinate plane, there are N points P_1, P_2, \\ldots, P_N, where point P_i has coordinates (X_i, Y_i).\nThe distance \\text{{dist}}(A, B) between two points A and B is defined as follows:\n\nA rabbit is initially at point A.\nA rabbit at position (x, y) can jump to (x+1, y+1), (x+1, y-1), (x-1, y+1), or (x-1, y-1) in one jump.\n\\text{{dist}}(A, B) is defined as the minimum number of jumps required to get from point A to point B.\nIf it is impossible to get from point A to point B after any number of jumps, let \\text{{dist}}(A, B) = 0.\n\nCalculate the sum \\displaystyle\\sum_{{i=1}}^{{N-1}}\\displaystyle\\sum_{{j=i+1}}^N \\text{{dist}}(P_i, P_j).\n\nInput\n\nThe input is given from Standard Input in the following format:\nN\nX_1 Y_1\nX_2 Y_2\n\\vdots\nX_N Y_N\n\nOutput\n\nPrint the value of \\displaystyle\\sum_{{i=1}}^{{N-1}}\\displaystyle\\sum_{{j=i+1}}^N \\text{{dist}}(P_i, P_j) as an integer.\n\nConstraints\n\n\n- 2 \\leq N \\leq 2 \\times 10^5\n- 0 \\leq X_i, Y_i \\leq 10^8\n- For i \\neq j, (X_i, Y_i) \\neq (X_j, Y_j)\n- All input values are integers.\n\nSample Input 1\n\n3\n0 0\n1 3\n5 6\n\nSample Output 1\n\n3\n\nP_1, P_2, and P_3 have coordinates (0,0), (1,3), and (5,6), respectively.\nThe rabbit can get from P_1 to P_2 in three jumps via (0,0) \\to (1,1) \\to (0,2) \\to (1,3), but not in two or fewer jumps,\nso \\text{{dist}}(P_1, P_2) = 3.\nThe rabbit cannot get from P_1 to P_3 or from P_2 to P_3, so \\text{{dist}}(P_1, P_3) = \\text{{dist}}(P_2, P_3) = 0.\nTherefore, the answer is \\displaystyle\\sum_{{i=1}}^{{2}}\\displaystyle\\sum_{{j=i+1}}^3\\text{{dist}}(P_i, P_j)=\\text{{dist}}(P_1, P_2)+\\text{{dist}}(P_1, P_3)+\\text{{dist}}(P_2, P_3)=3+0+0=3.\n\nSample Input 2\n\n5\n0 5\n1 7\n2 9\n3 8\n4 6\n\nSample Output 2\n\n11",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport sys\n\ndef solve():\n    N = int(sys.stdin.readline())\n    points = []\n    for _ in range(N):\n        x, y = map(int, sys.stdin.readline().split())\n        points.append((x, y))\n\n    total_distance = 0\n\n    for i in range(N):\n        for j in range(i + 1, N):\n            x1, y1 = points[i]\n            x2, y2 = points[j]\n\n            # Condition for reachability: (x1 + y1) and (x2 + y2) must have the same parity.\n            # This is because each jump changes both x and y by +-1.\n            # So, (dx + dy) and (dx - dy) are always even.\n            # x_new = x_old +- 1\n            # y_new = y_old +- 1\n            # x_new + y_new = (x_old +- 1) + (y_old +- 1)\n            # Possible changes to x+y: (+1)+(+1)=+2, (+1)+(-1)=0, (-1)+(+1)=0, (-1)+(-1)=-2\n            # So x_new + y_new has the same parity as x_old + y_old.\n            # Similarly for x-y.\n            if (x1 + y1) % 2 != (x2 + y2) % 2:\n                continue\n\n            # The minimum number of jumps from (x1, y1) to (x2, y2) is\n            # max(|x2 - x1|, |y2 - y1|).\n            # Let dx = |x2 - x1| and dy = |y2 - y1|.\n            # We need to make a total of dx steps in the x-direction and dy steps in the y-direction.\n            # Each jump contributes +-1 to x and +-1 to y.\n            # Consider the transformation u = x + y and v = x - y.\n            # A jump to (x+1, y+1) changes u to (x+1)+(y+1) = x+y+2, and v to (x+1)-(y+1) = x-y.\n            # A jump to (x+1, y-1) changes u to (x+1)+(y-1) = x+y, and v to (x+1)-(y-1) = x-y+2.\n            # A jump to (x-1, y+1) changes u to (x-1)+(y+1) = x+y, and v to (x-1)-(y+1) = x-y-2.\n            # A jump to (x-1, y-1) changes u to (x-1)+(y-1) = x+y-2, and v to (x-1)-(y-1) = x-y.\n            # In all cases, changes in u and v are by even numbers.\n            # Therefore, u_final and u_initial must have the same parity, and v_final and v_initial must have the same parity.\n            # This is equivalent to (x1 + y1)%2 == (x2 + y2)%2 AND (x1 - y1)%2 == (x2 - y2)%2.\n            # If (x1 + y1) and (x2 + y2) have the same parity, then (x1 - y1) and (x2 - y2) also have the same parity.\n            # Check: (x1 + y1) - (x2 + y2) = (x1 - x2) + (y1 - y2)\n            #        (x1 - y1) - (x2 - y2) = (x1 - x2) - (y1 - y2)\n            # If A + B is even, and A - B is even, then 2A and 2B are even, meaning A and B can be any parity.\n            # If A + B is even, then A and B are both even or both odd.\n            # If A and B are both even, A - B is even.\n            # If A and B are both odd, A - B is even.\n            # So, if (x1 + y1)%2 == (x2 + y2)%2, then (x1 - y1)%2 == (x2 - y2)%2 is also true.\n\n            # Let the number of jumps be k.\n            # Without loss of generality, assume x2 >= x1 and y2 >= y1.\n            # We can make jumps that increase x and y.\n            # Each jump increases x by 1 and y by 1, or decreases x by 1 and y by 1, or changes x but not y's parity relative to x, or changes y but not x's parity relative to y.\n            # The core idea is that the parity of x+y remains invariant.\n            # (x+1) + (y+1) = x+y+2\n            # (x+1) + (y-1) = x+y\n            # (x-1) + (y+1) = x+y\n            # (x-1) + (y-1) = x+y-2\n            # So, if (x1+y1) and (x2+y2) have different parities, it's impossible.\n\n            # If possible, the minimum number of jumps is related to the Manhattan distance, but not directly.\n            # It's actually determined by the maximum difference in either x or y.\n            # Let dx = |x2 - x1| and dy = |y2 - y1|.\n            # We need to cover a difference of dx in x and dy in y.\n            # Each jump changes x by +-1 and y by +-1.\n            # Let the number of jumps be k.\n            # Suppose we make 'a' jumps of type (x+1, y+1), 'b' jumps of type (x+1, y-1),\n            # 'c' jumps of type (x-1, y+1), and 'd' jumps of type (x-1, y-1).\n            # Total jumps k = a + b + c + d.\n            # Change in x: (a+b) - (c+d) = x2 - x1\n            # Change in y: (a+c) - (b+d) = y2 - y1\n            # Add these two equations: (a+b+a+c) - (c+d+b+d) = (x2-x1) + (y2-y1)\n            # 2a - 2d = (x2-x1) + (y2-y1)\n            # a - d = ((x2-x1) + (y2-y1)) / 2\n            # Subtract the second equation from the first: (a+b-c-d) - (a+c-b-d) = (x2-x1) - (y2-y1)\n            # 2b - 2c = (x2-x1) - (y2-y1)\n            # b - c = ((x2-x1) - (y2-y1)) / 2\n            # Since a, b, c, d are non-negative, and we need to minimize k = a+b+c+d.\n            # We need to satisfy the difference equations with minimal k.\n            # The minimum k is achieved when we maximize the use of \"diagonal\" jumps if available, or \"anti-diagonal\".\n            # Consider the differences |x2 - x1| and |y2 - y1|.\n            # If |x2 - x1| = |y2 - y1|, we can reach in |x2 - x1| jumps by always moving diagonally towards the target.\n            # For example, from (0,0) to (3,3), we can do (1,1), (2,2), (3,3) in 3 jumps.\n            # From (0,0) to (3,-3), we can do (1,-1), (2,-2), (3,-3) in 3 jumps.\n            # If |x2 - x1| > |y2 - y1|, let dx = |x2 - x1|, dy = |y2 - y1|.\n            # We can make dy diagonal jumps (e.g., if x2>x1, y2>y1, use (x+1, y+1)).\n            # This covers dy in both x and y. We are left with a difference of dx - dy in x, and 0 in y.\n            # We need to make dx - dy more steps in x. Since y is at the target, we must pair x steps with y steps that cancel out their effect on y's position.\n            # For example, from (0,0) to (5,3). dx = 5, dy = 3.\n            # 3 jumps of (x+1,y+1) takes us to (3,3). We still need to reach (5,3).\n            # From (3,3), we need to move 2 units in x, keeping y at 3.\n            # We can use (x+1, y-1) and (x-1, y+1) for this.\n            # (3,3) -> (3+1, 3-1) = (4,2)\n            # (4,2) -> (4+1, 2+1) = (5,3)\n            # This took 2 more jumps. Total 3 + 2 = 5 jumps.\n            # The number of extra jumps needed is (dx - dy).\n            # So, the total number of jumps is dy + (dx - dy) = dx.\n            # This implies that if reachable, the minimum number of jumps is max(|x2 - x1|, |y2 - y1|).\n\n            dist = max(abs(x1 - x2), abs(y1 - y2))\n            total_distance += dist\n\n    print(total_distance)\n\nsolve()\n```",
