      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef solve():\n    N, X = map(int, input().split())\n    T = list(map(int, input().split()))\n    MOD = 998244353\n\n    # Precompute modular inverse of N\n    inv_N = pow(N, MOD - 2, MOD)\n\n    # dp[i][j] is the probability that the total duration of songs\n    # played is exactly j, and the last song played is song i.\n    # To simplify, dp[j] will be the probability that the total duration is j.\n    # We need to distinguish the last song played.\n    # Let dp[j] be the sum of probabilities that the total duration of songs played is j.\n    # However, this won't work easily to determine if song 1 is the *current* song.\n\n    # Let's try a different DP state:\n    # dp[k][s] = probability that after playing k songs, the total duration is exactly s.\n    # This state is too complex because X can be large, k can be large.\n\n    # The maximum total time can be X + max(T).\n    # Since X is up to 10^4 and T_i is up to 10^4, this is 2 * 10^4.\n    max_time = X + max(T)\n\n    # dp[s] = probability that a random sequence of songs results in a total duration of exactly s,\n    # and the *last song played* in that sequence *just finished* its playback at time s.\n    # We are interested in the song playing at time X + 0.5.\n    # This means the song started playing at some time t_start <= X and finished at some time t_end > X.\n    # And the (X + 0.5) must be within [t_start, t_end).\n    # Also, we assume song 1 is the one playing.\n\n    # Define dp[t] as the probability that a song chosen (possibly after other songs)\n    # has just finished playing at cumulative time `t`.\n    # And we need to know what song it was.\n\n    # Let dp_sum[t] be the probability that the cumulative duration of songs *just completed* is `t`.\n    # And dp_prob_s1_ends_at[t] be the probability that the cumulative duration is `t` AND the song that just finished was song 1.\n\n    # This seems like a knapsack type problem.\n    # Let dp[s] be the probability that *some sequence of songs* results in a total duration of exactly `s`.\n    # The crucial part is that the last song ending at `s` must be able to start at `s - t_i`.\n    # And it has to be song 1.\n\n    # Define P[s]: the probability that the total duration of the songs played *exactly reaches* `s`\n    # for the first time *at `s`*, and the song that made it reach `s` was song 1.\n\n    # Let's consider the state `f[k]` as the probability that the total duration of songs played becomes `k`.\n    # `f` should be an array where `f[s]` stores the total probability that some sequence of plays sums to `s`.\n    # For each `s` from 0 to `max_time`, `f[s]` is initialized to 0.\n    # `f[0]` = 1 (representing the state where no songs have been played, total duration is 0).\n\n    # To calculate the required sum:\n    # We need to find the probability that at time X + 0.5, song 1 is playing.\n    # This means song 1 started playing at some time `t_start` such that `t_start <= X`,\n    # and ends at `t_end = t_start + T_1` where `t_end > X`.\n    # So, `t_start` can range from `max(0, X - T_1 + 1)` up to `X`.\n\n    # Let dp[s] be the probability that a sequence of songs ends at time `s`.\n    # If the last song was song `j`, and it had duration `T_j`, then it started at `s - T_j`.\n    # dp[s] = Sum_{j=1 to N} (dp[s - T_j] * inv_N)  (if s - T_j >= 0)\n    # The base case is dp[0] = 1 (representing the start).\n\n    # This is a standard dynamic programming approach for expected value.\n    # We want to find the total probability for time `s` that the last song chosen\n    # was song 1, and its duration `T_1` covers `X+0.5`.\n\n    # Define `dp[j]` as the probability that the sum of durations of songs *just played* is exactly `j`.\n    # Since `dp[0]` is 1 (the initial state, before any song is played, sum is 0).\n    # All `dp` values represent probabilities of *reaching* a certain cumulative duration.\n    # The maximum relevant cumulative duration is X + max(T).\n    # Let's consider `dp` array of size `X + max(T) + 1`.\n    dp = [0] * (X + max(T) + 1)\n    dp[0] = 1 # Probability of having accumulated 0 time is 1 at the beginning.\n\n    # Iterate through all possible accumulated durations `s`.\n    # `s` goes from 0 up to `X + max(T) - 1`. Because the new song adds its duration.\n    # We are calculating dp[s + T_k] based on dp[s].\n    for s in range(len(dp)): # Iterate up to X + max(T)\n        if dp[s] == 0:\n            continue\n        # From state `s`, we play a new song.\n        # Each song `k` is chosen with probability `inv_N`.\n        # If we choose song `k`, the new accumulated time is `s + T_k`.\n        # The probability of reaching `s + T_k` is `dp[s] * inv_N`.\n        for k in range(N):\n            T_k = T[k]\n            if s + T_k < len(dp):\n                dp[s + T_k] = (dp[s + T_k] + dp[s] * inv_N) % MOD\n    # After this loop, dp[s] contains the *total* probability that a sequence of songs\n    # result in *exactly* `s` accumulated time, and the song that made it `s` *just finished* at `s`.\n\n    # Now we need to sum up the probabilities for song 1.\n    # Song 1 is playing at `X + 0.5` if it started at `s_start` and ended at `s_end = s_start + T_1`,\n    # such that `s_start <= X` and `s_end > X`.\n    # This means `s_start` qualifies if `X < s_start + T_1`.\n    # Or, equivalently, `s_start` can be any value from `max(0, X - T_1 + 1)` up to `X`. (inclusive upper bound for s_start)\n    # The song which *just finished* at `s` must be song 1.\n    # The accumulated time `s` is the ending time of *some* song.\n    # The state `dp[s]` is the probability that *some song* ends at `s`.\n    # If the song playing at X+0.5 is Song 1, then it started at `s - T_1` and ended at `s`.\n    # So we need `dp[s - T_1] * inv_N` where `s-T_1` is the actual state.\n\n    # Let `ans` be the final probability.\n    ans = 0\n\n    # Iterate over all possible ending times `s_end` for song 1.\n    # `s_end` is the time when song 1 finishes playing.\n    # Song 1 must end *after* X, so s_end > X.\n    # Song 1 must have started *at or before* X, so s_end - T_1 <= X.\n    # Combining these: X < s_end <= X + T_1.\n\n    # We need the probability that some sequence of songs exactly accumulated to `s_start = s_end - T_1`.\n    # And then, the next song chosen was song 1.\n    # (The `dp[s]` calculated above already includes the `inv_N` factor implicit in the transitions for all songs.)\n\n    # The issue here is `dp[s]` is the probability that some sequence of songs leads to cumulative time `s` *and the last song ends at s*.\n    # When `X = 0`, the song being played at 0.5 seconds must be the very first song chosen.\n    # If the first song chosen is song 1, it starts at 0 and ends at T_1.\n    # So `0 <= 0` and `T_1 > 0`. This condition is satisfied for any `T_1 >= 1`.\n    # The probability is `1/N`.\n    # With the current `dp` state, `dp[0]=1`.\n    # Then `dp[T_k]` gets `1 * inv_N`.\n    # `s_end` for song 1 is `T_1`. We want to add `dp[0] * inv_N`.\n    # `s_start = 0`. Condition: `0 <= X` and `0 + T_1 > X`.\n    # So if `X < T_1`, we add `dp[0] * inv_N` (which is `1 * inv_N`).\n    if X == 0:\n        actual_ans = inv_N\n    else:\n        # Sum up probabilities where song 1 finishes at `s_end`, and `s_end - T_1 <= X < s_end`.\n        # This implies `s_end` must be in `(X, X + T_1]`.\n        # So, iterate s_end from X + 1 up to X + T_1.\n        # For each such s_end, we need the probability that a sequence\n        # of songs resulted in `s_start = s_end - T_1` as the total duration,\n        # and then song 1 was chosen.\n        # The probability that a sequence takes `s_start` time is implicitly stored in `dp[s_start]`.\n        # If `dp[s_start]` represents \"probability that *ANY* song ends at `s_start`\",\n        # then the probability that song 1 is chosen next is `dp[s_start] * inv_N`. This is the probability of\n        # song 1 starting at `s_start`.\n\n        # The initial state is `dp[0]=1`. If the first song is chosen, what adds to which.\n        # `dp[s]` represents the probability that the *current* song has just finished, and cumulative time is `s`.\n        # For `s` from `X+1` up to `X+T_1`, we need to sum `dp[s - T_1] * inv_N`.\n        # No, not `dp[s - T_1] * inv_N`.\n        # The dp calculation *already* incorporates the `inv_N` for all songs.\n        # So `dp[s]` already represents the probability that *some* song finishes at `s`.\n        # This `dp[s]` includes contributions from `dp[s - T_1]` via song 1,\n        # `dp[s - T_2]` via song 2, etc.\n\n        # Let `dp[k]` be defined as the probability that the cumulative duration of played songs is exactly `k`.\n        # `dp[0] = 1`.\n        # For `k` from 0 to `max_time` - 1:\n        #   If `dp[k]` is non-zero:\n        #     For each song `j` from 1 to `N`:\n        #       `dp[k + T_j] = (dp[k + T_j] + dp[k] * inv_N) % MOD`\n        # This is the `dp` we've calculated.\n\n        # Now, how to use this `dp` for `X + 0.5`?\n        # Song 1 is playing at `X + 0.5` if:\n        # It started at `s_start` and finished at `s_end = s_start + T_1`.\n        # Conditions: `s_start <= X` and `s_end > X`.\n        # Which is `s_start <= X` and `s_start + T_1 > X`.\n        # Or, `X - T_1 < s_start <= X`.\n\n        # So we iterate `s_start` from `max(0, X - T_1 + 1)` up to `X`.\n        # For each such `s_start`, we need the probability that some sequence of songs\n        # *ends exactly at `s_start`*, AND that the next song chosen is song 1.\n        # The probability of reaching `s_start` exactly is `dp[s_start]`.\n        # Then, the probability that song 1 is chosen next is `inv_N`.\n        # So we add `dp[s_start] * inv_N` to `ans`.\n\n        # Example: N=3, X=6, T=[3,5,6] (Sample 1)\n        # T_1 = 3.\n        # Range for s_start: max(0, 6 - 3 + 1) to 6 => max(0, 4) to 6 => 4, 5, 6.\n\n        # Correct calculation:\n        # `dp[0]=1`\n        # `dp[3] += dp[0] * inv_N`\n        # `dp[5] += dp[0] * inv_N`\n        # `dp[6] += dp[0] * inv_N`\n        # For s = 3: `dp[3]` is now `inv_N`.\n        #   `dp[3+3] += dp[3] * inv_N` => `dp[6] += inv_N * inv_N`\n        #   `dp[3+5] += dp[3] * inv_N` => `dp[8] += inv_N * inv_N`\n        #   `dp[3+6] += dp[3] * inv_N` => `dp[9] += inv_N * inv_N`\n        # ... and so on.\n\n        # The `dp[s]` value after the loop IS the probability that a song just finished at time `s`.\n        # We need the probability that:\n        #   1. A sequence of songs accumulated to `s_start`. (This is `dp[s_start]`)\n        #   2. The *next* song chosen was song 1. (Probability `inv_N`)\n        #   3. This song 1 starts at `s_start` and plays for `T_1` seconds.\n        #   4. `s_start <= X` and `s_start + T_1 > X`.\n\n        # So, the range for `s_start` is `max(0, X - T[0] + 1)` to `X`.\n        # For each `s_start` in this range, we add `(dp[s_start] * inv_N) % MOD` to `ans`.\n        # (This implies that song 1 was the one chosen *after* the cumulative time `s_start` was reached.)\n\n        for s_start in range(max(0, X - T[0] + 1), X + 1):\n            ans = (ans + dp[s_start] * inv_N) % MOD\n\n    print(ans)\n\nsolve()\n```",
