between these \"star center\" nodes are formed by sequences of connected arm-nodes.\n    # These sequences are paths where all intermediate nodes have degree 2.\n    # These paths terminate at star centers or at leaves.\n\n    # We need to find the \"active\" edges that were added.\n    # The number of such edges is M-1.\n    # Each existing star was level L_i. It had (L_i+1) vertices and L_i edges.\n    \n    # Key property to distinguish:\n    # A degree 2 vertex `v` connects to two neighbors `n1` and `n2`.\n    # If `v` is an original center of a level-2 star, then both `n1` and `n2` were originally arms of `v`.\n    # If `v` is an original arm vertex that got connected, then one neighbor was its original center,\n    # and the other neighbor was another arm vertex from a different star.\n\n    # The important observation in these problems is often about \"contracting\" paths.\n    # A \"path\" of degree-2 nodes linking two higher-degree nodes (or a higher-degree node and a leaf)\n    # represents a single effective connection.\n\n    # Let's count the number of leaves `num_leaves = len(leaves)`.\n    # Each level-L star has L arms. So sum(L_i) original arms.\n    # `num_leaves` arms remain as leaves.\n    # `sum(L_i) - num_leaves` arms are involved in connections.\n    # Since each connection involves two arms, `(sum(L_i) - num_leaves) / 2` is the number of connections.\n    # This must be equal to `M - 1`.\n    # So `M - 1 = (sum(L_i) - num_leaves) / 2`.\n\n    # Another approach:\n    # A single star with level k is a set of k+1 nodes. One center, k arms.\n    # If we have only one star (M=1), then N = k+1. T itself is a star.\n    # In this case, `degrees[center] = k`, `degrees[arms] = 1`.\n    # `star_levels = [k]`.\n    # `num_leaves = k`.\n    # `M-1 = 0`. `(sum(L_i) - num_leaves) / 2 = (k - k) / 2 = 0`. This holds.\n\n    # If we have multiple stars:\n    # The connections form paths composed of degree-1 vertices from the original stars.\n    # Each such path has its ends rooted in two different stars (or a star and a leaf).\n    # The intermediate vertices on such a path will all have degree 2 in T.\n    # The endpoints of such paths will either be true centers (already accounted for, degree > 2)\n    # or they will be degree 2 (if they are a level-2 center)\n    # or degree 1 (if they are an unconnected arm).\n\n    # Let's use a Breadth-First Search (BFS) or Depth-First Search (DFS)\n    # starting from leaves and \"trimming\" the tree.\n    # Trim all degree-1 nodes from the tree.\n    # What remains are the \"core\" of the connections.\n\n    # Let's do a BFS to find distances of all nodes from any leaf.\n    # This isn't quite right.\n\n    # Consider the sum of (degree - 2) for all vertices.\n    # For leaves: (1 - 2) = -1.\n    # For degree-2 internal nodes: (2 - 2) = 0.\n    # For internal nodes with degree `d > 2`: `d - 2`.\n\n    # Sum of (degrees[i] - 2) for all `i` from 1 to N:\n    # `SUM(degrees[i]) - 2*N = 2*(N-1) - 2*N = -2`.\n    # So, `sum_{v is leaf} (-1) + sum_{v is deg=2} (0) + sum_{v is deg>2} (degrees[v] - 2) = -2`.\n    # `-num_leaves + sum_{v is deg>2} (degrees[v] - 2) = -2`.\n    # `sum_{v is deg>2} (degrees[v] - 2) = num_leaves - 2`.\n    # `sum_{v is deg>2} degrees[v] - 2 * num_deg_greater_than_2 = num_leaves - 2`.\n\n    # The sum of levels: sum(L_i).\n    # We found that any node with degree `d > 2` is a center of a level-`d` star.\n    # So `sum_{v is deg>2} degrees[v]` is a partial sum of levels.\n    # Let `partial_L = sum_{v is deg>2} degrees[v]`.\n    # `partial_L - 2 * num_deg_greater_than_2 = num_leaves - 2`.\n\n    # What remains to be accounted for are the effective \"paths\" that create M-1 connections.\n    # These \"paths\" are composed of internal degree-2 nodes.\n    # Each path connects either two different high-degree centers, or a high-degree center and a leaf,\n    # or perhaps two level-2 centers.\n\n    # The simplest interpretation that fits samples:\n    # A graph `T` is given.\n    # We identify all nodes `u` such that `degrees[u] > 1`.\n    # For each such node `u`, count how many of its neighbors are leaves (degree 1).\n    # Let this be `leaf_neighbors[u]`.\n    # If `degrees[u] - leaf_neighbors[u] == 0`, then `u` is a center of a star whose all arms are still leaves.\n    # In this case, `degrees[u]` is clearly a star level.\n    # If `degrees[u] - leaf_neighbors[u] > 0`, it means `u` is connected to other internal nodes.\n    # These other internal nodes could be other centers or connected arms.\n\n    # Let's consider the example 1: N=6, path graph 1-2-3-4-5-6.\n    # Degrees: deg(1)=1, deg(2)=2, deg(3)=2, deg(4)=2, deg(5)=2, deg(6)=1.\n    # Leaves: 1, 6. `num_leaves = 2`.\n    # Nodes with degree > 2: None. `star_levels = []`.\n    # All internal nodes have degree 2.\n    # If we apply the rule blindly: any node with degree > 2 gives its degree as a level.\n    # What level-2 stars are there?\n    # The sample output is \"2 2\". This means two level-2 stars (centers have degree 2).\n    # If node 2 was a center of a level-2 star, and node 5 was a center of a level-2 star.\n    # Original: (1-2-3 isolated) and (4-5-6 isolated).\n    # To get path 1-2-3-4-5-6, we need to connect 3 to 4.\n    # This means 3 was an arm of 2. 4 was an arm of 5.\n    # 2 is a center. 1, 3 are arms.\n    # 5 is a center. 4, 6 are arms.\n    # When 3-4 connected, original degree-1 nodes 3 and 4 now have degree 2.\n    # Node 2 is still degree 2. Node 5 is still degree 2.\n    # This implies that the 'centers' are nodes 2 and 5 (degrees 2).\n    # And their levels are 2.\n\n    # So, the rule \"any node with degree > 2 must be an original star center, and its degree is its level\" is potentially wrong for degree 2.\n    # But it must be right for degree > 2.\n    # If a node `u` has `deg(u) = L > 2`, then `u` must be a center.\n    # Why? If `u` were an arm, its original degree was 1. When it forms an edge with another arm, its degree becomes 2. It cannot be `L > 2`.\n    # So, for degree `d > 2`, `d` is certainly a star level.\n\n    # For degree 2 vertices, they can be a center of a level-2 star, or a connected arm.\n    # How to distinguish?\n    # Consider what *remains* if we identify and remove all \"chain\" parts of the graph.\n    # A connected component of nodes with degree 2 (and possibly 1 at the ends)\n    # could be seen as one 'long edge'.\n\n    # Let's re-read the problem: \"Takahashi had a graph consisting of stars. He repeated the following operation until every pair of vertices in the graph was connected: choose two vertices in the graph. Here, the vertices must be disconnected, and their degrees must be both 1. Add an edge that connects the chosen two vertices.\"\n\n    # This means the *only* edges added are between original degree-1 vertices.\n    # This confirms that the degree of an original star center is unaffected by these operations.\n    # So, if `c` is an original star center of level `L`, `degrees[c]` in T must be `L`.\n    # This applies to ALL original star centers.\n\n    # So, the original star centers are exactly those nodes `c` for which `degrees[c]` equals the level of some star.\n    # If a node `v` is an arm, its original degree is 1.\n    # If `v` becomes connected to another arm, its degree becomes 2.\n    # If `v` is not connected, its degree remains 1.\n\n    # So, any vertex `v` in T with `degrees[v] > 1` is either an original star center\n    # OR an original arm that got connected to another arm.\n    # An original arm that got connected to another arm has degree exactly 2 in T.\n    # If `degrees[v] > 2`, then `v` *must* be an original star center. Its level is `degrees[v]`.\n\n    # What about vertices with `degrees[v] == 2`?\n    # These are the problematic ones. They can be either:\n    #   A) A center of a level-2 star.\n    #   B) An arm vertex that got connected to another arm vertex.\n\n    # Consider the endpoints of the graph resulting from removing all degree-2 nodes and connecting their neighbors.\n    # Or, equivalently, count leaves correctly.\n    # The sum of all degrees in T is 2(N-1).\n    # Let `L_count` be the number of leaves (degree 1).\n    # Let `T2_count` be the number of degree 2 nodes.\n    # Let `GT2_count` be the number of degree > 2 nodes.\n    # `N = L_count + T2_count + GT2_count`.\n    # `2(N-1) = L_count*1 + T2_count*2 + sum_{d>2} d`.\n\n    # Each star has a center. The total number of centers is M.\n    # `sum(L_i)` is the sum of levels of all stars.\n    # `sum(L_i)` is precisely `sum_{c is center} degrees[c]`.\n\n    # Example 1: N=6 path 1-2-3-4-5-6\n    # Degrees: {1:1, 2:2, 3:2, 4:2, 5:2, 6:1}\n    # Nodes with degree > 2: []\n    # `num_leaves = 2` (nodes 1, 6)\n    # `star_levels = []` initially.\n    # We need two level-2 stars. So suppose nodes `c1, c2` are centers and levels are `degrees[c1]=2, degrees[c2]=2`.\n    # The value that `sum(L_i)` has to be is 2+2=4.\n    # The total number of leaves (nodes 1, 6) is 2.\n    # The number of connected arms: (4 - 2) = 2.\n    # Number of connections: 2/2 = 1.\n    # Number of stars M: 1 + 1 = 2. (M-1 = 1)\n    # This all fits. The sample output means that nodes 2 and 5 are the centers.\n\n    # How do we determine that a degree-2 node is a center, vs. an arm?\n    # If `v` is a center of a level-2 star: its two neighbors are its arms.\n    #   If both neighbors are leaves of T: then it's clearly a level-2 star. e.g. 1-2-3\n    #   If one neighbor is a leaf, and the other is internal: e.g. (1-2)-(3-4-5). Here 2-3 is path\n    #     2 has neighbor 1 (leaf) and 3 (internal).\n    #     If 2 is a center for L=2, it means 1 and 3 were its arms.\n    #     This implies that 3 must have been an arm to 2, and then 3 was connected to an arm of some other star.\n    #   If both neighbors are internal: e.g., if 2 and 5 are centers for L=2, and paths connect them.\n    #     2: neighbors 1 (leaf), 3 (internal). Degree of 2 is 2.\n    #     5: neighbors 6 (leaf), 4 (internal). Degree of 5 is 2.\n    # This is tricky.\n\n    # Let's count \"effective leaves\".\n    # Each star L_i has L_i leaves.\n    # `num_leaves_in_T = sum(L_i) - 2*(M-1)`.\n    # `M-1` is the number of connecting edges added.\n    # Each vertex `v` has neighbors `adj[v]`.\n    # We can effectively \"contract\" paths of degree-2 nodes.\n    # These chains of degree 2 nodes represent a single \"added edge\" from the operation.\n    # E.g., if we have A -- B -- C -- D, where B, C are degree 2 and A, D are not:\n    # This path B-C acts like a single edge connecting A and D.\n    # So, we can reduce the graph by removing all degree-2 nodes and adding edges between their non-degree-2 neighbors.\n\n    # Algorithm Idea:\n    # 1. Identify all nodes that have degree 1 (leaves) in T.\n    # 2. Identify all nodes that have degree > 2 in T (`potential_centers_gt2`).\n    #    Add `degrees[u]` for each `u` in `potential_centers_gt2` to `star_levels`.\n    # 3. What about the nodes with degree 2?\n    #    These form paths. Some of these paths might represent connections between stars.\n    #    Others might represent level-2 stars where both arms were connected further (e.g., a path of length 2 where the center is deg=2).\n\n    # The sum of levels of all stars is `sum(L_i)`.\n    # The number of edges in the initial forest is `sum(L_i)`.\n    # The number of vertices in the initial forest is `sum(L_i + 1) = sum(L_i) + M`.\n    # We know `N = sum(L_i) + M`.\n    # We know `len(leaves) = sum(L_i) - 2(M-1)`.\n    # So, `sum(L_i) = len(leaves) + 2(M-1)`.\n    # Substitute sum(L_i) into N equation:\n    # `N = len(leaves) + 2(M-1) + M`\n    # `N = len(leaves) + 2M - 2 + M`\n    # `N = len(leaves) + 3M - 2`\n    # `N - len(leaves) + 2 = 3M`\n    # `M = (N - len(leaves) + 2) / 3`.\n    # This relates `N`, `len(leaves)`, and `M`.\n    # This must be an integer. `N - len(leaves) + 2` must be divisible by 3.\n\n    # Let's check sample 1: N=6, leaves={1,6}, len(leaves)=2.\n    # `M = (6 - 2 + 2) / 3 = 6 / 3 = 2`. So M=2 stars.\n    # `sum(L_i) = len(leaves) + 2(M-1) = 2 + 2(2-1) = 2 + 2 = 4`.\n    # So, we expect two stars whose levels sum to 4. For instance, L1=2, L2=2. This is the output.\n\n    # Sample 2: N=9, leaves = {1, 2, 8, original_3_arm, original_7_arm, 9} (Wait, no. The leaves of T are fixed)\n    # The input lines are: (3,9), (7,8), (8,6), (4,6), (4,1), (5,9), (7,3), (5,2)\n    # Degrees:\n    # 1: 1 (neighbor 4)\n    # 2: 1 (neighbor 5)\n    # 3: 2 (neighbors 9, 7)\n    # 4: 2 (neighbors 6, 1)\n    # 5: 2 (neighbors 9, 2)\n    # 6: 2 (neighbors 8, 4)\n    # 7: 2 (neighbors 8, 3)\n    # 8: 2 (neighbors 7, 6)\n    # 9: 2 (neighbors 3, 5)\n    # All internal nodes have degree 2.\n    # Leaves: {1, 2} --> `len(leaves) = 2`.\n    # `M = (9 - 2 + 2) / 3 = 9 / 3 = 3`. So M=3 stars.\n    # `sum(L_i) = len(leaves) + 2(M-1) = 2 + 2(3-1) = 2 + 4 = 6`.\n    # So we need three stars whose levels sum to 6. For instance, (2, 2, 2). This is the output.\n\n    # This is a solid approach!\n    # All original star centers sum their degrees to `sum(L_i)`.\n    # We found `M` and `sum(L_i)`.\n    # We also know that any `v` with `degrees[v] > 2` *must* be an original star center, and its level is `degrees[v]`.\n    # So, we add `degrees[v]` for all `v` s.t. `degrees[v] > 2` to `star_levels`.\n    # Let `current_sum_L = sum(star_levels)`.\n    # Let `current_M_count = len(star_levels)`.\n    # The remaining `M - current_M_count` stars must be level-2 stars.\n    # Why? If there were a level-1 star, that's just an edge (two nodes, one edge).\n    # But a star must have k >= 2 edges. So level-1 star is not allowed. `k >= 2`.\n    # So minimum level is 2.\n    # If the remaining stars are level-2 stars, their sum of levels must be `2 * (M - current_M_count)`.\n    # So this has to match `sum(L_i) - current_sum_L`.\n    # `sum(L_i) - current_sum_L = 2 * (M - current_M_count)`.\n    # This must hold, and it means we fill in the rest with 2s.\n\n    # Let's perform this algorithm:\n    # 1. Calculate degrees of all nodes.\n    # 2. Count `len(leaves)`.\n    # 3. Calculate `M = (N - len(leaves) + 2) / 3`. If this is not an integer or < 1, something is wrong with the problem statement or my understanding (but problem guarantees solution is unique).\n    # 4. Calculate `total_sum_L = len(leaves) + 2 * (M - 1)`.\n    # 5. Initialize `star_levels = []` and `current_sum_L = 0`.\n    # 6. For each node `i` from 1 to N:\n    #    If `degrees[i] > 2`, then:\n    #       `star_levels.append(degrees[i])`\n    #       `current_sum_L += degrees[i]`\n    # 7. The number of remaining stars needed is `M - len(star_levels)`.\n    #    The remaining sum of levels needed is `total_sum_L - current_sum_L`.\n    #    Each remaining star must be of level 2 (minimum level allowed).\n    #    Verify that `(total_sum_L - current_sum_L) // 2` is equal to `(M - len(star_levels))`.\n    #    If so, append `2` to `star_levels` for `M - len(star_levels)` times.\n    # 8. Sort `star_levels` and print.\n\n    # Check `N-len(leaves)+2` divisibility by 3:\n    # N = sum(L_i) + M\n    # len(leaves) = sum(L_i) - 2(M-1)\n    # Subtracting second from first:\n    # N - len(leaves) = M + 2(M-1) = M + 2M - 2 = 3M - 2\n    # N - len(leaves) + 2 = 3M\n    # So `(N - len(leaves) + 2)` is always divisible by 3. And `M` is positive.\n\n    # Check that `M - len(star_levels)` is non-negative.\n    # The number of stars M. Each star has one center.\n    # `len(star_levels)` is the count of nodes whose degree `d > 2`. These are centers.\n    # The remaining `M - len(star_levels)` centers must have been degree 2.\n    # So the count of centers with degree 2 is `M - len(star_levels)`.\n    # The sum of degrees for these `M - len(star_levels)` centers must be `2 * (M - len(star_levels))`.\n    # This must equal `total_sum_L - current_sum_L`.\n    # This is correct.\n\n    # Final Algorithm:\n    # 1. Compute `degrees` array.\n    # 2. Count `num_leaves = sum(1 for d in degrees if d == 1)`.\n    # 3. Calculate `M = (N - num_leaves + 2) // 3`.\n    # 4. Calculate `total_sum_L = num_leaves + 2 * (M - 1)`.\n    # 5. Initialize `star_levels = []`.\n    # 6. For `i` from 1 to `N`:\n    #    If `degrees[i] > 2`:\n    #       `star_levels.append(degrees[i])`\n    # 7. Add `(M - len(star_levels))` times the value `2` to `star_levels`.\n    # 8. Sort `star_levels` and print space-separated.\n\n    # Let's test with Sample 1: N=6, path graph.\n    # degrees = [0, 1, 2, 2, 2, 2, 1] (index 0 unused)\n    # `num_leaves = 2` (nodes 1, 6)\n    # `M = (6 - 2 + 2) // 3 = 6 // 3 = 2`.\n    # `total_sum_L = 2 + 2 * (2 - 1) = 2 + 2 = 4`.\n    # `star_levels = []`.\n    # Iterate nodes 1 to 6:\n    # degrees[1]=1 (skip)\n    # degrees[2]=2 (skip)\n    # degrees[3]=2 (skip)\n    # degrees[4]=2 (skip)\n    # degrees[5]=2 (skip)\n    # degrees[6]=1 (skip)\n    # `star_levels` is still `[]`.\n    # `M - len(star_levels) = 2 - 0 = 2`.\n    # Add `2` twice to `star_levels`. `star_levels = [2, 2]`.\n    # Sort: `[2, 2]`. Print: \"2 2\". Matches sample.\n\n    # Test with Sample 2: N=9.\n    # Degrees after calculation:\n    # 1: 1\n    # 2: 1\n    # 3: 2\n    # 4: 2\n    # 5: 2\n    # 6: 2\n    # 7: 2\n    # 8: 2\n    # 9: 2\n    # `num_leaves = 2` (nodes 1, 2)\n    # `M = (9 - 2 + 2) // 3 = 9 // 3 = 3`.\n    # `total_sum_L = 2 + 2 * (3 - 1) = 2 + 4 = 6`.\n    # `star_levels = []`.\n    # No nodes have degree > 2. So `star_levels` remains `[]`.\n    # `M - len(star_levels) = 3 - 0 = 3`.\n    # Add `2` three times to `star_levels`. `star_levels = [2, 2, 2]`.\n    # Sort: `[2, 2, 2]`. Print: \"2 2 2\". Matches sample.\n\n    # Test with Sample 3: N=20.\n    # Edges: (8,3), (8,18), (2,19), (8,20), (9,17), (19,7), (8,7), (14,12), (2,15), (14,10), (2,13), (2,16), (2,1), (9,5), (10,15), (14,6), (2,4), (2,11), (5,12)\n    # Degrees:\n    # 1: 1 (neighbor 2)\n    # 2: 7 (neighb 19,15,13,16,1,4,11)\n    # 3: 1 (neighbor 8)\n    # 4: 1 (neighbor 2)\n    # 5: 2 (neighb 9,12)\n    # 6: 1 (neighbor 14)\n    # 7: 2 (neighb 19,8)\n    # 8: 4 (neighb 3,18,20,7)\n    # 9: 2 (neighb 17,5)\n    # 10: 2 (neighb 14,15)\n    # 11: 1 (neighbor 2)\n    # 12: 2 (neighb 14,5)\n    # 13: 1 (neighbor 2)\n    # 14: 4 (neighb 12,10,6,15)\n    # 15: 3 (neighb 2,10,14)\n    # 16: 1 (neighbor 2)\n    # 17: 1 (neighbor 9)\n    # 18: 1 (neighbor 8)\n    # 19: 2 (neighb 2,7)\n    # 20: 1 (neighbor 8)\n\n    # `num_leaves`: nodes with degree 1.\n    # Leaves: 1, 3, 4, 6, 11, 13, 16, 17, 18, 20. Total `num_leaves = 10`.\n    # `M = (20 - 10 + 2) // 3 = 12 // 3 = 4`.\n    # `total_sum_L = 10 + 2 * (4 - 1) = 10 + 6 = 16`.\n    # `star_levels = []`.\n    # Nodes with degree > 2:\n    #   Node 2: degrees[2]=7. `star_levels.append(7)`.\n    #   Node 8: degrees[8]=4. `star_levels.append(4)`.\n    #   Node 14: degrees[14]=4. `star_levels.append(4)`.\n    #   Node 15: degrees[15]=3. `star_levels.append(3)`.\n    # `star_levels = [7, 4, 4, 3]`.\n    # `M - len(star_levels) = 4 - 4 = 0`.\n    # No more `2`s to add.\n    # Sort `star_levels`: `[3, 4, 4, 7]`. Print: \"3 4 4 7\".\n    # Wait, sample output is `2 3 4 7`. Why is one of my 4s a 2? What went wrong?\n    # Ah, my manual total_sum_L from star_levels: 7+4+4+3 = 18.\n    # Expected `total_sum_L` was 16.\n    # My sums don't match. `total_sum_L - current_sum_L`: `16 - 18 = -2`. This is nonsense.\n\n    # My interpretation that \"any node with degrees[v] > 2 must be an original star center\" is correct.\n    # \"and its level is degrees[v]\" is also correct.\n    # The problem must be in how I apply `M` and `total_sum_L`.\n\n    # Let's re-verify the equations for `num_leaves` and `N`.\n    # Let L_1, ..., L_M be the levels.\n    # Each star i has L_i+1 vertices and L_i edges.\n    # Total vertices in the initial state (before connections): SUM(L_i + 1) = SUM(L_i) + M.\n    # This must be N. So `N = SUM(L_i) + M`. (Equation 1)\n\n    # The connecting operation adds M-1 edges. These edges connect degree-1 vertices.\n    # Each connection consumes two degree-1 vertices, converting them to degree-2 vertices in the chain.\n    # Initial count of degree-1 vertices (arms) = SUM(L_i).\n    # Final count of degree-1 vertices (leaves in T) = `num_leaves`.\n    # So `num_leaves = SUM(L_i) - 2 * (M - 1)`. (Equation 2)\n\n    # From (2): `SUM(L_i) = num_leaves + 2M - 2`.\n    # Substitute into (1): `N = (num_leaves + 2M - 2) + M`\n    # `N = num_leaves + 3M - 2`\n    # `3M = N - num_leaves + 2`\n    # `M = (N - num_leaves + 2) / 3`. (Equation for M is correct)\n\n    # From (1) and (2), we have a system of linear equations for SUM(L_i) and M.\n    # SUM(L_i) = N - M\n    # SUM(L_i) = num_leaves + 2M - 2\n    # So `N - M = num_leaves + 2M - 2`\n    # `N - num_leaves + 2 = 3M`. This is consistent.\n\n    # The problem might be about what exactly constitutes the \"level of a star\" from the perspective of how they connect.\n    # The *initial* definition of a star is crucial: `k+1` vertices and `k` edges.\n    # One center, `k` arms.\n\n    # My understanding:\n    # - If `v` is a center of a level-`L` star, then `degrees[v]` in T is `L`.\n    # - If `v` is an arm, its original degree is 1. If it gets connected, its degree becomes 2. If it remains a leaf, its degree is 1.\n    # This means all `degrees[v]` for `v` that are original star centers must exactly be the `L_i`s.\n    # The remaining `M - len(star_levels_from_deg_gt_2)` stars must have centers that happen to have degree 2 in T. These must be level-2 stars.\n\n    # Let's count potential centers differently.\n    # The \"actual\" star centers are the nodes that are not arms.\n    # A node `v` is an arm if its degree in the final tree is 1, OR if its degree is 2 and both its neighbors have degrees such that `v` forms a bridge between them conceptually.\n    # What if a center of a degree-2 star got one arm connected to a leaf and another connected to another center via a path of 2-degree nodes?\n\n    # Consider the example 3 output: `2 3 4 7`. Sum = 16. M = 4.\n    # My algorithm gave `3 4 4 7`. Sum = 18. M = 4.\n    # The difference is one `2` vs one `4`.\n    # This means one node that *I* identified as a center of a level-4 star using `degrees[u]` was actually a center of a level-2 star, AND two 'arms' got connected in such a way that it made its degree 4.\n    # But how? The operations only occur between degree-1 nodes.\n    # A center's degree *cannot* change due to the operations.\n    # This implies that `degrees[c]` for a center `c` in T should always be `L_i`.\n\n    # The discrepancy in sample 3 (`2 3 4 7` vs my `3 4 4 7`) means my assumption `degrees[i]` is exactly `L_i` for `degrees[i]>2` is problematic for *some* `degrees[i]` values.\n    # Specifically, one of the '4's in my result should be a '2'.\n    # Looking at the degrees:\n    # Node 2: deg 7 (correctly L=7)\n    # Node 8: deg 4 (my algo L=4, sample L=4)\n    # Node 14: deg 4 (my algo L=4, sample L=?) - this is the likely candidate for change\n    # Node 15: deg 3 (my algo L=3, sample L=3)\n    # If Node 14 is the center with level 2 (instead of 4), then what are the other two degrees?\n    # Another 2 must be added somewhere.\n\n    # Aha! The problem states: \"The resulting graph is a tree; we call it T.\"\n    # Suppose T has an edge (u,v).\n    # If `degrees[u] == 1` and `degrees[v] == 1`, this edge must be one that was added during the \"connecting\" phase.\n    # No, that's wrong. If `u` and `v` are both leaves in T, that implies they are connected directly.\n    # E.g., 1-2-3-4. 1 is leaf, 4 is leaf. If (1,4) was an edge, then degree(1)=1, deg(4)=1.\n    # But this is never the case in a tree of more than 2 nodes. If deg(u)=1, its single neighbor deg(v) must be > 1.\n    # Except if N=2, (1,2), then both deg=1. This is a level-1 star (not allowed), or one level-2 star where the center and one arm were connected? No.\n    # N >= 3. So leaves always have an internal node neighbor.\n\n    # Re-examine \"Takahashi had a graph consisting of stars. He repeated ... operation ... vertices must be disconnected, and their degrees must be both 1.\"\n    # The key insight must lie in the fact that original arm vertices involved in connection will have degree 2.\n    # Star centers keep their degree.\n    # So `L_i` for any star `i` is exactly `degrees[center_i]` in T.\n    # Let `all_star_centers` be the set of vertices that were initially centers of stars.\n    # Then `sum_{c in all_star_centers} degrees[c]` gives `sum(L_i)`.\n    # And `len(all_star_centers)` gives `M`.\n\n    # We need to identify `all_star_centers`. Any node with degree > 2 *must* be a star center.\n    # Add these degrees to `star_levels`.\n    # The remaining nodes with degree 2 are a mix of level-2 centers and connected arms.\n    # How to distinguish?\n    # This is often solved by focusing on leaf nodes or by \"peeling\" the tree.\n    # Let's count how many leaves each internal node `u` is directly connected to.\n    # `leaf_connections[u] = count of v in adj[u] where degrees[v] == 1`.\n    # If `u` is a center of a level-`L` star, then `L` is `degrees[u]`.\n    # The `L` neighbors were its arms.\n    # `leaf_connections[u]` of these arms are still leaves.\n    # `degrees[u] - leaf_connections[u]` of these arms got connected to other arms (these are internal nodes themselves).\n    # So, `degrees[u] - leaf_connections[u]` is the number of 'paths' or 'chains' originating from `u`.\n\n    # Sum of levels: `SUM(L_i)`.\n    # Number of stars: `M`.\n    # `N = SUM(L_i) + M`\n    # `num_leaves = SUM(L_i) - 2(M-1)`\n\n    # What if we use `leaf_connections`?\n    # For every internal node `u` (degree `deg_u`):\n    # `deg_u - leaf_connections[u]` is the number of neighbors of `u` that are not leaves.\n    # These non-leaf neighbors are either other centers or \"chain\" nodes (arms that got connected).\n\n    # The sum of degrees must be 2(N-1).\n    # `sum_{u in T} degrees[u] = 2(N-1)`.\n    # Let `C` be the set of original star centers.\n    # `sum_{c in C} L_c = sum(L_i)`.\n    # For any `u` not in `C`, `degrees[u]` in T is either 1 (if it's an un-connected arm) or 2 (if it's a connected arm).\n    # Let `V_arms_unconnected` be the set of vertices that were original arms and ended up as leaves in T. $|V_arms_unconnected| = num_leaves$.\n    # Let `V_arms_connected` be the set of vertices that were original arms and ended up with degree 2 in T.\n\n    # Total degrees sum: `sum_{c in C} L_c + sum_{v in V_arms_unconnected} 1 + sum_{v in V_arms_connected} 2 = 2(N-1)`.\n    # `sum(L_i) + num_leaves + 2 * |V_arms_connected| = 2(N-1)`.\n\n    # From `N = SUM(L_i) + M`: `SUM(L_i) = N - M`.\n    # From `num_leaves = SUM(L_i) - 2(M-1)`: `num_leaves = N - M - 2M + 2 = N - 3M + 2`.\n    # So `3M = N - num_leaves + 2`. (My M calculation is definitely correct).\n\n    # Now, `sum(L_i) = N - M`.\n    # `N - M + num_leaves + 2 * |V_arms_connected| = 2(N-1)`.\n    # `num_leaves + 2 * |V_arms_connected| = N - 2 + M`.\n    # Use `N - num_leaves + 2 = 3M`. So `N - 2 + M = num_leaves + 4M - 4`.\n    # `num_leaves + 2 * |V_arms_connected| = num_leaves + 4M - 4`.\n    # `2 * |V_arms_connected| = 4M - 4`.\n    # `|V_arms_connected| = 2M - 2`.\n    # This means total number of *connected* arm nodes is `2M - 2`.\n    # Each connection involves 2 arm nodes. There are `M-1` connections. So `2(M-1)` arm nodes are involved. This is correct.\n\n    # So what is wrong with the sample 3 interpretation?\n    # Degrees: deg(2)=7, deg(8)=4, deg(14)=4, deg(15)=3. All > 2.\n    # My algorithm identified these as centers with levels 7, 4, 4, 3.\n    # The sample output has 2, 3, 4, 7.\n    # This means one node that I considered a level-4 center was actually a level-2 center.\n    # This implies that the degree of that node in T was 4, BUT its actual level is 2.\n    # This is a contradiction to `degrees[center_i] = L_i`.\n    # How could a center with two arms have its degree become 4?\n    # This can only happen if it was connected to its two arms, and EACH of those arms then connected to another distinct arm, AND those other arms came from ANOTHER star's arms, then the original two arms connected to the arms of the same original star. This is too complicated and does not fit the definition.\n\n    # \"A graph ... is called a level-k star if and only if:\n    # - it has a vertex that is connected to each of the other k vertices with an edge, and there are no other edges.\"\n    # The center has degree k. The other k vertices have degree 1 *in this isolated star*.\n    # When edges are added, they are between *degree-1* vertices.\n    # These operations do NOT change the degrees of star centers.\n    # So it MUST be that `degrees[c]` in T is `L_c` for any center `c`.\n\n    # What if the problem setter counts an *effective* star level?\n    # No, that's not how these competitive programming problems typically work.\n\n    # Let's verify sum of degrees in sample 3 output:\n    # Output: 2 3 4 7 -> `sum(L_i) = 2+3+4+7 = 16`.\n    # My calculation for sum(L_i) `total_sum_L = 16`. (Matches)\n    # Output: 2 3 4 7 implies 4 stars. `M=4`. (Matches)\n\n    # My interpretation correctly obtains `M` and `sum(L_i)`.\n    # The structure of the levels `L_i` is the problem.\n\n    # Back to Sample 3.\n    # My derived levels from `degrees > 2` : `[7, 4, 4, 3]`. Sort: `[3, 4, 4, 7]`.\n    # Sample output: `[2, 3, 4, 7]`.\n    # My `[3, 4, 4, 7]` has one '4' where the sample has '2'.\n    # This means one of the nodes with `degrees[u]=4` is actually a level-2 star center, AND two \"extra\" paths are attached to it.\n    # This contradicts `degrees[c] = L_c`.\n\n    # The ONLY way for `degrees[c] = L_c` to be violated is if the operation adds an edge to a center.\n    # \"choose two vertices .. must be disconnected, and their degrees must be both 1.\"\n    # This explicitly states that centers are not involved in operations.\n    # This is a strong constraint.\n\n    # Is it possible that the final set of vertices is not N, but N + some \"dummy\" nodes? No.\n    # \"T has (N-1) edges, the i-th of which connects u_i and v_i.\"\n\n    # Could there be a mistake in transcribing Sample 3 degrees?\n    # Adjacency list:\n    # 1: [2]\n    # 2: [19,15,13,16,1,4,11] (7 neighbors, deg 7) - OK\n    # 3: [8]\n    # 4: [2]\n    # 5: [9,12] (2 neighbors, deg 2)\n    # 6: [14]\n    # 7: [19,8] (2 neighbors, deg 2)\n    # 8: [3,18,20,7] (4 neighbors, deg 4) - OK\n    # 9: [17,5] (2 neighbors, deg 2)\n    # 10: [14,15] (2 neighbors, deg 2)\n    # 11: [2]\n    # 12: [14,5] (2 neighbors, deg 2)\n    # 13: [2]\n    # 14: [12,10,6,15] (4 neighbors, deg 4) - OK\n    # 15: [2,10,14] (3 neighbors, deg 3) - OK\n    # 16: [2]\n    # 17: [9]\n    # 18: [8]\n    # 19: [2,7] (2 neighbors, deg 2)\n    # 20: [8]\n\n    # Leaves: [1, 3, 4, 6, 11, 13, 16, 17, 18, 20] - 10 leaves. `num_leaves = 10`.\n    # Nodes with degree > 2: [2 (7), 8 (4), 14 (4), 15 (3)].\n    # This looks correct. My degrees are correct.\n\n    # What if the stars are \"virtual\" and are composed from the tree T based on some rules?\n    # Such that a central node `C` of degree `D_C` could actually originate from a level `L` star,\n    # where `D_C` != `L`? This means connections were made to the center itself, not just the arms.\n    # But the rule \"degrees must be both 1\" implies centers are not connected to.\n\n    # There could be a subtle ambiguity in \"level-k star\".\n    # \"it has a vertex that is connected to each of the other k vertices with an edge, and there are no other edges.\"\n    # This means (CENTER, ARM1), (CENTER, ARM2), ..., (CENTER, ARMk).\n    # Center has degree k. Arms have degree 1.\n    # This is a very standard definition.\n\n    # Consider the example from the problem statement: two level-2 stars.\n    # Star 1: C1 --- A1, C1 --- A2. Degrees: C1=2, A1=1, A2=1.\n    # Star 2: C2 --- A3, C2 --- A4. Degrees: C2=2, A3=1, A4=1.\n    # Initial graph: {C1, A1, A2}, {C2, A3, A4}. Vertices = 6. Edges = 4.\n    # Operation: connect two degree-1 vertices. Say A2 and A3.\n    # Resulting graph (T):\n    # C1 --- A1\n    # |\n    # A2 --- A3\n    # |\n    # C2 --- A4\n    # The vertex degrees in T:\n    # A1: 1 (leaf)\n    # A4: 1 (leaf)\n    # A2: 2\n    # A3: 2\n    # C1: 2\n    # C2: 2\n    # Here, centers C1, C2 both have degree 2. Arms A2, A3 have degree 2. Arms A1, A4 have degree 1.\n    # For sample 1, my algorithm worked based on this exact structure:\n    # `star_levels = []` because no degree > 2.\n    # `M = 2`. All `M` stars must be degree 2. `star_levels = [2, 2]`. This works.\n\n    # Why Sample 3 fails for '4' vs '2'?\n    # It must mean a center of a level-2 star can be made to look like degree 4 in T.\n    # This is not possible if operations strictly connect arms-to-arms.\n    # This is incredibly confusing given the strict definition for the operation.\n\n    # What if `k` in `level-k star` is not the number of arms, but the number of other original stars it connects to?\n    # No, that's not standard usage.\n\n    # Is it related to the number of *internal* nodes a node is connected to?\n    # No, that affects how the arms are counted, not the center itself.\n\n    # Let's consider the problem again. \"Find the number and levels of the stars that he initially had.\"\n    # The structure of the problem suggests that the final tree T encodes this information directly.\n    # The simple method I derived for M and sum(L_i) seems robust.\n    # `M = (N - num_leaves + 2) / 3`\n    # `sum(L_i) = N - M`\n\n    # From sample 3 output `2 3 4 7`:\n    # `M = 4`.\n    # `sum(L_i) = 2+3+4+7 = 16`.\n    # `N = 20`. `num_leaves = 10`.\n    # Checks: `M = (20 - 10 + 2) / 3 = 12 / 3 = 4`. Correct.\n    # `sum(L_i) = N - M = 20 - 4 = 16`. Correct.\n\n    # So the total count of stars (M) and the sum of their levels (sum(L_i)) are absolutely correctly derived.\n    # The issue is attributing `L_i` = `degrees[c]` for all centers `c`.\n\n    # Could it be that the degrees > 2 are not necessarily L_i, but `degrees[c] - (num_of_paths_from_c)`? NO.\n    # Every edge (c, arm_i) is part of a path from `c` to a leaf, or `c` to another `c'`.\n\n    # This is a classic \"contract paths of degree-2 nodes\" scenario.\n    # The graph formed by connecting two stars via an arm-to-arm edge creates a path.\n    # C1-A1-A2-A3-A4-C2. Where A1, A4 are leaves. A2, A3 are chain nodes.\n    # All nodes on the path A1-A2-A3-A4-C2 except A1 and C2 would conceptually be degree 2.\n    # In the tree T, the nodes that \"form the connections\" between star centers are sequences of degree-2 nodes.\n    # If we \"contract\" every maximal path consisting only of degree-2 nodes into a single edge,\n    # the resulting graph will have vertices which are the original star centers,\n    # and edges corresponding to the connections. This resulting graph must be a tree of `M` vertices.\n\n    # The vertices in this contracted graph are the original star centers.\n    # The degree of a vertex `c` in this contracted graph represents `L_c - (num_of_leaves_at_c)`.\n    # THIS IS THE KEY!\n    # In the contracted tree, the degree of a center `c` IS `degrees[c] - leaf_connections[c]`.\n    # Where `leaf_connections[c]` is count of neighbors `v` of `c` that are leaves in T.\n\n    # Let's verify this hypothesis for Sample 1: N=6 path 1-2-3-4-5-6\n    # Degrees in T: 1:1, 2:2, 3:2, 4:2, 5:2, 6:1.\n    # `num_leaves = 2`. `M=2`. `sum(L_i)=4`.\n    # Calculate `leaf_connections[u]` for all `u`.\n    # `leaf_connections[1]=0`, `leaf_connections[2]=1` (neighbor 1 is leaf),\n    # `leaf_connections[3]=0`, `leaf_connections[4]=0`, `leaf_connections[5]=1` (neighbor 6 is leaf),\n    # `leaf_connections[6]=0`.\n    # Actual centers are 2 and 5 (from sample output meaning).\n    # For center 2: `degrees[2]=2`. `leaf_connections[2]=1`.\n    # Level it effectively contributes to contracted graph = `degrees[2] - leaf_connections[2] = 2 - 1 = 1`.\n    # It also directly generates one leaf, so that's a level contribution.\n    # This is saying that node 2 has 1 arm that became a leaf, and 1 arm that became part of a chain (to node 3).\n    # Its level is 2. The number of arms that are still leaves is 1.\n    # The number of arms that got connected is `degrees[center] - leaf_connections[center]`.\n\n    # This is what's called a \"tree decomposition\" or \"block-cut tree\" style approach.\n    # Let's count how many arms each center effectively supplies to connection edges.\n    # Any node `u` with `degrees[u] > 1` is an \"active\" node.\n    # The total number of edges in the resulting M stars is `sum(L_i)`.\n    # This is `N - M`.\n\n    # Each degree-1 node in T represents one \"arm\" that did *not* get connected.\n    # Each degree-2 node `x` in T which is part of a longer path `...-u-x-v-...` where `u,v` are also internal degree 2 (or more)\n    # is an \"arm\" node that got connected.\n    # Each degree-2 node `x` in T whose neighbor `u` has `degrees[u] > 2` and `v` has `degrees[v]=1` is also an \"arm\" node.\n\n    # Re-evaluate the source of `M` and `sum(L_i)`.\n    # The logic that `degrees[c]` = `L_c` must be correct.\n    # If the output `2 3 4 7` sums to 16, and mine `3 4 4 7` sums to 18, and `total_sum_L = 16`,\n    # then one of my 4s should be 2. This implies my code is picking up nodes that it shouldn't.\n\n    # What if a center *of a single star* for some reason has degree `L_i` in T, but appears to be much higher degree?\n    # Consider only nodes `v` in T where `degrees[v] > 1`.\n    # These are potential centers or connected arms.\n    # Run a DFS/BFS from leaves up to the \"core\".\n    # Or, the problem is about how many 'effective' edges each central node has.\n    # The crucial point must be `degrees[v] == 1` for choices.\n    #\n    # The degrees given in T are fixed. It is a tree.\n    # The number of stars M and their levels L_i are such that:\n    # 1. N = sum(L_i) + M\n  