    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. Return the function body without invoking it at the final solution. Question:\nYou are given a string s that consists of lowercase English letters.\nA string is called special if it is made up of only a single character. For example, the string \"abc\" is not special, whereas the strings \"ddd\", \"zz\", and \"f\" are special.\nReturn the length of the longest special substring of s which occurs at least thrice, or -1 if no special substring occurs at least thrice.\nA substring is a contiguous non-empty sequence of characters within a string.\n \nExample 1:\n\nInput: s = \"aaaa\"\nOutput: 2\nExplanation: The longest special substring which occurs thrice is \"aa\": substrings \"aaaa\", \"aaaa\", and \"aaaa\".\nIt can be shown that the maximum length achievable is 2.\n\nExample 2:\n\nInput: s = \"abcdef\"\nOutput: -1\nExplanation: There exists no special substring which occurs at least thrice. Hence return -1.\n\nExample 3:\n\nInput: s = \"abcaba\"\nOutput: 1\nExplanation: The longest special substring which occurs thrice is \"a\": substrings \"abcaba\", \"abcaba\", and \"abcaba\".\nIt can be shown that the maximum length achievable is 1.\n\n \nConstraints:\n\n3 <= s.length <= 50\ns consists of only lowercase English letters.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef longest_special_substring(s):\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the length of the longest special substring of s that occurs at least thrice.\n\n    A string is special if it is made up of only a single character.\n    A substring is a contiguous non-empty sequence of characters within a string.\n\n    Args:\n        s: The input string consisting of lowercase English letters.\n\n    Returns:\n        The length of the longest special substring that occurs at least thrice,\n        or -1 if no such substring exists.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(s)\n    max_len = -1\n\n    # Iterate through all possible characters\n    for char_code in range(ord('a'), ord('z') + 1):\n        char = chr(char_code)\n        \n        # Find all occurrences of the current character\n        indices = [i for i, c in enumerate(s) if c == char]\n        \n        if len(indices) < 1:\n            continue\n        \n        # Iterate through possible lengths of special substrings of the current character\n        # The maximum possible length of a special substring with character 'char'\n        # cannot exceed the number of consecutive occurrences of 'char'.\n        # We can determine the maximum consecutive occurrences of 'char'.\n        max_consecutive_char = 0\n        current_consecutive = 0\n        for c in s:\n            if c == char:\n                current_consecutive += 1\n            else:\n                max_consecutive_char = max(max_consecutive_char, current_consecutive)\n                current_consecutive = 0\n        max_consecutive_char = max(max_consecutive_char, current_consecutive)\n\n\n        for length in range(1, max_consecutive_char + 1):\n            special_substring = char * length\n            count = 0\n            # Check for occurrences of the special_substring\n            # We only need to check if the special_substring can be formed.\n            # Since `length` is at most `max_consecutive_char` we know that\n            # `char * length` will appear as a substring at least once if `length` > 0.\n            # The problem is to check if it appears *at least thrice*.\n            \n            # A more direct approach is to count the occurrences.\n            # We can iterate through the string and count substrings.\n            temp_count = 0\n            for i in range(n - length + 1):\n                if s[i:i+length] == special_substring:\n                    temp_count += 1\n            \n            if temp_count >= 3:\n                max_len = max(max_len, length)\n\n    return max_len\n```",
