    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport sys\n\ndef solve():\n    N, D, P = map(int, sys.stdin.readline().split())\n    F = list(map(int, sys.stdin.readline().split()))\n\n    F.sort(reverse=True)\n\n    total_cost = sum(F)\n    min_cost = total_cost\n    \n    current_fare_sum = 0\n    for i in range(0, N, D):\n        # Cost of buying D passes\n        pass_cost = P\n        \n        # Sum of fares for the next D days (or fewer if at the end of the trip)\n        fare_sum_for_pass = sum(F[i:min(i+D, N)])\n        \n        # If the sum of fares is greater than the cost of passes, it's cheaper to buy passes\n        if fare_sum_for_pass > pass_cost:\n            # Deduct the fare sum that will be covered by passes\n            total_cost -= fare_sum_for_pass\n            # Add the cost of the passes\n            total_cost += pass_cost\n            # Update the minimum cost\n            min_cost = min(min_cost, total_cost)\n        else:\n            # If the sum of fares for the next D days is less than or equal to the pass cost,\n            # it's not cheaper to buy passes for these days.\n            # Since we sorted F in reverse, any subsequent group of D days will also have fares\n            # less than or equal to the pass cost. So we can break.\n            break\n\n    print(min_cost)\n\nsolve()\n```",
