      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport sys\n\ndef solve():\n    B = int(sys.stdin.readline())\n\n    # Since B <= 10^18, A^A <= 10^18.\n    # We can estimate the maximum possible value of A.\n    # If A = 10, 10^10 = 10^10\n    # If A = 20, 20^20 is very large.\n    # If A = 18, 18^18 is roughly (1.8 * 10^1)^18 = 1.8^18 * 10^18.\n    # 1.8^2 approx 3.24, 1.8^4 approx 10.5, 1.8^8 approx 110, 1.8^16 approx 12100.\n    # So 18^18 is much larger than 10^18.\n    # This suggests A will not be very large.\n    # Let's try an upper bound for A. If A=20, 20^20 >> 10^18.\n    # A quick test: 15^15 is already quite large.\n    # 10^10 = 10^10\n    # 11^11 = 285,311,670,611\n    # 12^12 = 8,916,100,448,256\n    # 13^13 = 302,875,106,592,253\n    # 14^14 = 1,111,203,947,314,401\n    # 15^15 = 43,789,389,038,085,9375\n    # 16^16 = 1,844,674,407,370,955,1616 (> 10^18)\n    # So A cannot be larger than 15 if B <= 10^18.\n    # A more careful upper bound: log_A(B) = A.\n    # If A=18, 18^18 ~ 3.9 * 10^22 >> 10^18.\n    # If A=17, 17^17 ~ 1.3 * 10^21 >> 10^18.\n    # If A=16, 16^16 ~ 1.8*10^19 > 10^18.\n    # If A=15, 15^15 ~ 4.3*10^17 < 10^18.\n    # So A is at most 15. For B=10^18, 15^15 < 10^18 < 16^16.\n    # Thus, we only need to check A from 1 up to around 18 or 19 to be safe.\n\n    for A in range(1, 20): # A sufficiently large upper bound ensures we don't miss any.\n        # Calculate A^A. We need to be careful about potential overflow if B was larger,\n        # but for B <= 10^18, Python's arbitrary precision integers handle this.\n        try:\n            a_pow_a = pow(A, A)\n        except OverflowError:\n            # If A^A overflows Python's capabilities (highly unlikely for B<=10^18),\n            # it's definitely greater than B.\n            break\n\n        if a_pow_a == B:\n            print(A)\n            return\n        if a_pow_a > B:\n            # Since A^A is monotonically increasing for positive A,\n            # if A^A is already greater than B,