    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef check_array_transformation(nums, k):\n    n = len(nums)\n    if k == 1:\n        return all(x == 0 for x in nums)\n\n    # Use a difference array or track pending subtractions\n    # pending_subtractions[i] will store how many times a subtraction\n    # operation starting at index i is still active at the current index.\n    # This is equivalent to tracking the \"net_reduction\" that affects elements\n    # from the current index due to operations started k-1 indices ago.\n    pending_subtractions = [0] * n\n    net_reduction_at_current_element = 0\n\n    for i in range(n):\n        # Remove the effect of operations that ended k elements ago\n        if i >= k:\n            net_reduction_at_current_element -= pending_subtractions[i - k]\n\n        current_val = nums[i] - net_reduction_at_current_element\n\n        if current_val < 0:\n            # We tried to subtract too much, impossible to reach zero\n            return False\n        elif current_val > 0:\n            # We need to reduce current_val to 0.\n            # This requires starting a new operation of size k here.\n            # This operation will reduce elements from i to i + k - 1.\n            # We need to reduce by current_val.\n            if i + k > n:\n                # If we need to start an operation but it extends beyond the array,\n                # then it's impossible to make current_val zero without affecting\n                # elements outside the array (which is not allowed) or without a full subarray.\n                return False\n            pending_subtractions[i] = current_val\n            net_reduction_at_current_element += current_val\n\n    # After iterating through the entire array, all elements should have effectively become zero.\n    # The net_reduction_at_current_element should be zero by the end if all operations\n    # were fully accounted for. Also, if there are any pending subtractions that extend\n    # beyond the array (which should have been caught earlier), or if any elements\n    # after the loop are unexpectedly non-zero due to reductions not accounted for.\n    # However, the loop structure already checks for this.\n    # The final check ensures no left-over positive values from operations that haven't expired.\n    # Since we ensured `i + k > n` leads to False for `current_val > 0`,\n    # all elements up to n-k should have been reduced to zero.\n    # For elements from n-k+1 to n-1, if they are not zero, and\n    # they cannot be part of a full k-size subarray, then it's impossible.\n    # Our current logic handles this: `if i + k > n and current_val > 0` returns False.\n    # So, by the end of the loop, if we haven't returned False, it means\n    # all elements were successfully reduced to zero.\n    return True\n\n```",
