m for every subsequence S.\n\n    # Consider the contribution of each element nums[i].\n    # When nums[i] is included in a subsequence S, and that subsequence S has\n    # an inner subsequence that sums to k, how does it contribute?\n\n    # Let's consider a different DP state.\n    # dp[i][j] = sum of powers for all subsequences formed using elements\n    # from nums[0...i-1] whose current sum (of the subsequence itself) is j.\n    # This is also not directly correct, as the \"power\" is defined for subsequences whose sum is k.\n\n    # Let dp[i][j] be the number of ways to select a subsequence from nums[0...i-1]\n    # such that the count of inner subsequences summing to 'k' for this selected\n    # subsequence is 'j'. This would be too big.\n\n    # The problem asks for the sum of power of all subsequences of `nums`.\n    # Let `P(S)` be the power of a subsequence `S`. We want to calculate `sum(P(S))`\n    # for all subsequences `S` of `nums`.\n\n    # `P(S)` is the number of subsequences of `S` that sum to `k`.\n    # This means we are actually counting, for every possible sub-subsequence `T` of `nums`\n    # (where `T` is a subsequence of some subsequence `S`), if `sum(T) == k`,\n    # then `T` contributes to the `power` of all subsequences `S` that contain `T`.\n\n    # If `T` is a sub-subsequence of `nums` and `sum(T) == k`,\n    # then `T` is a subsequence of `S` if `T` is a sub-subsequence of `S`.\n    # For a fixed `T` such that `sum(T) = k`, how many subsequences `S` of `nums` contain `T`?\n    # If `T` has `m` elements, and `nums` has `n` elements.\n    # The elements of `T` must be present in `S`.\n    # The remaining `n-m` elements of `nums` (that are not in `T`) can either be in `S` or not.\n    # There are `2^(n-m)` such subsequences `S` that contain `T`.\n\n    # So, the total sum of power is `sum_{T: sum(T)=k} 2^(n - |T|)`.\n\n    # Let's verify with Example 1: nums = [1,2,3], k = 3\n    # Sub-subsequences T with sum k=3:\n    # 1. T = [1,2]. |T| 