    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef minMovesToGroup(s: str) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the minimum number of steps to group all black balls to the right\n    and all white balls to the left by swapping adjacent balls.\n\n    Args:\n        s: A binary string representing the colors of the balls, where '1' is black\n           and '0' is white.\n\n    Returns:\n        The minimum number of steps required.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(s)\n    black_balls = []\n    for i in range(n):\n        if s[i] == '1':\n            black_balls.append(i)\n\n    if not black_balls:\n        return 0\n\n    # The target positions for the black balls will be consecutive, ending at n-1.\n    # The exact positions depend on the number of black balls.\n    # If there are k black balls, their target positions will be n-k, n-k+1, ..., n-1.\n\n    # To minimize swaps, we want to move the black balls to their nearest suitable\n    # target positions. Since we're grouping to the right, the black balls will\n    # occupy the last 'num_black' positions.\n    # The cost of moving a black ball from index 'i' to index 'j' is |i - j|.\n    # We want to find a permutation of target positions that minimizes the sum\n    # of absolute differences.\n\n    # Consider the last k positions as potential target spots for the k black balls.\n    # The k black balls are at indices b_0, b_1, ..., b_{k-1}.\n    # The k target spots are at indices n-k, n-k+1, ..., n-1.\n    # The minimum number of swaps to move elements to their sorted positions is\n    # achieved by moving them to their median-aligned target positions. This is\n    # related to the idea that the sum of absolute differences is minimized when\n    # items are moved towards the median.\n\n    # Let's rephrase the problem: We want to move all '1's to the right of all '0's.\n    # This is equivalent to moving all '1's to the last 'num_ones' positions.\n    # The cost to move a '1' at index `i` to the its sorted final position `j` is `abs(i - j)`.\n    # The total cost is the sum of `abs(i - j)` for all '1's.\n\n    # Let the indices of black balls be `b_0, b_1, ..., b_{k-1}`.\n    # The target indices are `n-k, n-k+1, ..., n-1`.\n    # We want to find a matching between `b_i` and target indices that minimizes\n    # sum of distances. This is achieved by matching `b_i` to `n-k+i`.\n\n    # The median trick is applicable here. If we have elements at positions `p_0, p_1, ..., p_{k-1}`\n    # and want to move them to target positions `t_0, t_1, ..., t_{k-1}` such that\n    # `t_i = t_0 + i`, the minimum cost is `sum(abs(p_i - (p_0 + i + c)))` for some offset `c`.\n    # This simplifies to minimizing `sum(abs((p_i - i) - (p_0 + c)))`.\n    # Let `q_i = p_i - i`. We need to find `c` that minimizes `sum(abs(q_i - (p_0 + c)))`.\n    # This is minimized when `p_0 + c` is the median of `q_i`.\n\n    # So, we can calculate `p_i - i` for each black ball.\n    # `p_i` is the original index of the i-th black ball (0-indexed).\n    # `i` is its rank among black balls (0-indexed).\n    # We are essentially aligning the relative positions.\n\n    num_black = len(black_balls)\n    # Calculate the adjusted positions: original_index - rank_among_black_balls\n    adjusted_positions = [black_balls[i] - i for i in range(num_black)]\n\n    # Find the median of the adjusted positions\n    adjusted_positions.sort()\n    median_adjusted_pos = adjusted_positions[num_black // 2]\n\n    # Calculate the total moves: sum of absolute differences between each adjusted position and the median\n    min_moves = 0\n    for pos in adjusted_positions:\n        min_moves += abs(pos - median_adjusted_pos)\n\n    return min_moves\n```",
