    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_127",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. Return the function body without invoking it at the final solution. Question:\nGiven three integers a, b, and n, return the maximum value of (a XOR x) * (b XOR x) where 0 <= x < 2^n.\nSince the answer may be too large, return it modulo 10^9 + 7.\nNote that XOR is the bitwise XOR operation.\n \nExample 1:\n\nInput: a = 12, b = 5, n = 4\nOutput: 98\nExplanation: For x = 2, (a XOR x) = 14 and (b XOR x) = 7. Hence, (a XOR x) * (b XOR x) = 98. \nIt can be shown that 98 is the maximum value of (a XOR x) * (b XOR x) for all 0 <= x < 2^n.\n\nExample 2:\n\nInput: a = 6, b = 7 , n = 5\nOutput: 930\nExplanation: For x = 25, (a XOR x) = 31 and (b XOR x) = 30. Hence, (a XOR x) * (b XOR x) = 930.\nIt can be shown that 930 is the maximum value of (a XOR x) * (b XOR x) for all 0 <= x < 2^n.\nExample 3:\n\nInput: a = 1, b = 6, n = 3\nOutput: 12\nExplanation: For x = 5, (a XOR x) = 4 and (b XOR x) = 3. Hence, (a XOR x) * (b XOR x) = 12.\nIt can be shown that 12 is the maximum value of (a XOR x) * (b XOR x) for all 0 <= x < 2^n.\n\n \nConstraints:\n\n0 <= a, b < 2^50\n0 <= n <= 50",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef solve():\n    MOD = 10**9 + 7\n\n    class Node:\n        def __init__(self):\n            self.children = {}\n            self.cnt = 0\n\n    def insert(root, num):\n        curr = root\n        for i in range(50, -1, -1):\n            bit = (num >> i) & 1\n            if bit not in curr.children:\n                curr.children[bit] = Node()\n            curr = curr.children[bit]\n            curr.cnt += 1\n\n    def query(root, a, b, n):\n        ans = 0\n        for i in range(n - 1, -1, -1):\n            bit_a = (a >> i) & 1\n            bit_b = (b >> i) & 1\n\n            # Try to make the current bit of x such that a XOR x and b XOR x have different bits at this level\n            # This maximizes the product at higher bits.\n\n            # Case 1: current bit of x is 0\n            #  a ^ x will have bit_a\n            #  b ^ x will have bit_b\n            # If bit_a != bit_b, this is good.\n            # If bit_a == bit_b, we have to consider the next bit.\n\n            # Case 2: current bit of x is 1\n            #  a ^ x will have 1 - bit_a\n            #  b ^ x will have 1 - bit_b\n            # If (1 - bit_a) != (1 - bit_b), this is good. (which is equivalent to bit_a != bit_b)\n            # If (1 - bit_a) == (1 - bit_b), we have to consider the next bit.\n\n            # The goal is to make the most significant bits of (a XOR x) and (b XOR x) different.\n            # Let the current bit of x be `cur_x_bit`.\n            # The current bit of (a XOR x) is `bit_a ^ cur_x_bit`.\n            # The current bit of (b XOR x) is `bit_b ^ cur_x_bit`.\n\n            # We want `(bit_a ^ cur_x_bit) != (bit_b ^ cur_x_bit)`. This simplifies to `bit_a != bit_b`.\n            # If `bit_a != bit_b`:\n            #  If `cur_x_bit = 0`: a^x has bit_a, b^x has bit_b. One is 0, one is 1.\n            #  If `cur_x_bit = 1`: a^x has 1-bit_a, b^x has 1-bit_b. One is 0, one is 1.\n            # In this scenario (`bit_a != bit_b`), we can always make the current bit of the product 1 by choosing `cur_x_bit`.\n            # Specifically, if `bit_a = 0, bit_b = 1`:\n            #  If `cur_x_bit = 0`: a^x has 0, b^x has 1.\n            #  If `cur_x_bit = 1`: a^x has 1, b^x has 0.\n            # In both cases, the product of the current bits is 0. So this doesn't directly contribute to the ans.\n            # However, this also means that at some point, one of the numbers will have a 0 and the other a 1 at this bit position,\n            # which is ideal for maximizing the product.\n\n            # Let's analyze the contribution to the answer based on the most significant bit.\n            # We are building `x` bit by bit from MSB to LSB.\n            # At bit `i`, we are deciding `x_i`.\n            # `a_i ^ x_i` and `b_i ^ x_i`.\n            # We want to maximize `ans = (A * B) % MOD`.\n\n            # Consider the contribution to the product at bit `i`.\n            # If at bit `i`, `a_i ^ x_i` is 1 and `b_i ^ x_i` is 1, then this contributes `(1 << i) * (1 << i)` to the product if these are the most significant differing bits.\n            # But we are interested in the actual values of A and B.\n\n            # Dynamic programming approach:\n            # `dp[i][state]` where `i` is the bit position and `state` encodes information about the prefix of `x` formed so far.\n            # What information do we need to carry over?\n            # We need to know the current prefix of `a ^ x` and `b ^ x`.\n            # This seems too complex due to the range of `a` and `b`.\n\n            # Let's consider the bits of `x` from MSB to LSB (from `n-1` down to 0).\n            # At bit `i`, let the current bit of `a` be `bit_a` and `b` be `bit_b`.\n            # We are choosing the `i`-th bit of `x`, let it be `x_i`.\n            # The resulting `i`-th bit of `a ^ x` is `a_i = bit_a ^ x_i`.\n            # The resulting `i`-th bit of `b ^ x` is `b_i = bit_b ^ x_i`.\n\n            # We want to try to make `a_i` and `b_i` different.\n            # If `bit_a != bit_b`:\n            #  If we choose `x_i = 0`, then `a_i = bit_a`, `b_i = bit_b`. They remain different.\n            #  If we choose `x_i = 1`, then `a_i = 1 - bit_a`, `b_i = 1 - bit_b`. They also remain different.\n            # In this case (`bit_a != bit_b`), we have flexibility. At bit `i`, one of `a_i`, `b_i` will be 0 and the other 1.\n            #  This means that the prefix up to bit `i` for one number will have a 0 at bit `i` and the other will have a 1.\n            # So, the product of the prefixes will have a 0 at bit `i`.\n\n            # If `bit_a == bit_b`:\n            #  If we choose `x_i = 0`, then `a_i = bit_a`, `b_i = bit_b`. They remain the same. Both 0 or both 1.\n            #  If we choose `x_i = 1`, then `a_i = 1 - bit_a`, `b_i = 1 - bit_b`. They also remain the same. Both 1 or both 0.\n            # In this case (`bit_a == bit_b`), `a_i` will always be equal to `b_i`.\n            #  This means the prefix up to bit `i` will have the same bit for both numbers.\n            #  If this is the most significant differing bit, the product will get a contribution from these bits.\n\n            # Let's reframe. We are constructing `x` bit by bit.\n            # At each bit `i`, we need to decide `x_i`.\n            # This decision affects the `i`-th bits of `a^x` and `b^x`.\n            # Let `A_prefix` and `B_prefix` be the values of `a^x` and `b^x` formed by bits greater than `i`.\n            # We are trying to append `x_i`.\n            # The new `a^x` prefix becomes `A_prefix | ((bit_a ^ x_i) << i)`.\n            # The new `b^x` prefix becomes `B_prefix | ((bit_b ^ x_i) << i)`.\n\n            # This is a greedy approach. At each bit `i`, we try to make `a^x` and `b^x` differ as much as possible at this bit.\n            # This means trying to make one bit 1 and the other 0.\n            # If `bit_a != bit_b`:\n            #   We can choose `x_i` such that `a_i = 0, b_i = 1` or `a_i = 1,