1`. `res_a_i=0, res_b_i=0`. Not 1.\n                #   My interpretation of setting bits to 1 is wrong.\n\n                # We are trying to maximize `( (a XOR x) * (b XOR x) ) % MOD`.\n                # Let `A = a XOR x` and `B = b XOR x`.\n                # We want to maximize `A * B`.\n                # This is maximized when `A` and `B` are close to each other, or when one is very large and the other is also large.\n                # The maximum value of `A` or `B` is `2^n - 1`.\n\n                # Consider bit `i` from `n-1` down to `0`.\n                # `bit_a = (a >> i) & 1`\n                # `bit_b = (b >> i) & 1`\n\n                # We are choosing `x_i`.\n                # `res_a_i = bit_a ^ x_i`\n                # `res_b_i = bit_b ^ x_i`\n\n                # Scenario 1: We want to make `res_a_i` and `res_b_i` different.\n                # This is achievable if `bit_a != bit_b`.\n                # If `bit_a = 0, bit_b = 1`:\n                #   If `x_i = 0`: `res_a_i = 0`, `res_b_i = 1`.\n                #   If `x_i = 1`: `res_a_i = 1`, `res_b_i = 0`.\n                # In either case, the `i`-th bit of the product `A*B` (if it's the most significant differing bit) is 0.\n                # If `bit_a == bit_b`:\n                #   If `x_i = 0`: `res_a_i = bit_a`, `res_b_i = bit_b`. They are the same.\n                #   If `x_i = 1`: `res_a_i = 1 - bit_a`, `res_b_i = 1 - bit_b`. They are the same.\n                # So, if `bit_a == bit_b`, then `res_a_i` will always be equal to `res_b_i`.\n\n                # Strategy: At each bit `i`, we try to make `res_a_i` and `res_b_i` different if possible to maximize the split.\n                # This happens when `bit_a != bit_b`.\n                # If `bit_a != bit_b`:\n                #   We can choose `x_i` to make `res_a_i = 1` and `res_b_i = 0`, or vice versa.\n                #   This doesn't directly add to the product at this bit position's magnitude.\n                #   However, it means that for