     # `cur_b_xor_x = 0`\n                # `ans = 0`\n\n                # For bit `i` from `n-1` down to `0`:\n                # `bit_a = (a >> i) & 1`\n                # `bit_b = (b >> i) & 1`\n\n                # We want to decide `x_i`.\n                # If `bit_a == bit_b`:\n                #   If we set `x_i = 0`:\n                #     `next_a_i = bit_a`\n                #     `next_b_i = bit_b` (same as `bit_a`)\n                #     We continue with the same bits, potentially `0*0` or `1*1` at this position.\n                #     The contribution to the current prefix of `cur_a_xor_x` and `cur_b_xor_x` means they stay the same at bit `i`.\n                #   If we set `x_i = 1`:\n                #     `next_a_i = 1 - bit_a`\n                #     `next_b_i = 1 - bit_b` (same as `1 - bit_a`)\n                #     We flip bits. The contribution to `cur_a_xor_x` and `cur_b_xor_x` flips the `i`-th bit.\n                #   To maximize the product, if `bit_a == bit_b`, we prefer that `next_a_i` and `next_b_i` are `1`.\n                #   If `bit_a = 0, bit_b = 0`: choose `x_i = 1`. `next_a_i=1`, `next_b_i=1`. Add `1 << i` to `cur_a_xor_x` and `cur_b_xor_x`.\n                #   If `bit_a = 1, bit_b = 1`: choose `x_i = 0`. `next_a_i=1`, `next_b_i=1`. Add `1 << i` to `cur_a_xor_x` and `cur_b_xor_x`.\n                #   So if `bit_a == bit_b`, we set `x_i` to flip the bits if `bit_a == 0`, and keep them as they are if `bit_a == 1`, all to make the i-th bits 1.\n                #   This means `x_i = 1 - bit_a`.\n                #   `cur_a_xor_x |= (1 << i)`\n                #   `cur_b_xor_x |= (1 << i)`\n\n                # If `bit_a != bit_b`:\n                #   We can choose `x_i` to make `next_a_i` and `next_b_i` different.\n                #   Choice 1: `x_i = 0`. `next_a_i = bit_a`, `next_b_i = bit_b`.\n                #     If `bit_a=0, bit_b=1`: `next_a_i=0`, `next_b_i=1`. Current values: `(cur_a_xor_x)` and `(cur_b_xor_x | (1<<i))`.\n                #     If `bit_a=1, bit_b=0`: `next_a_i=1`, `next_b_i=0`. Current values: `(cur_a_xor_x | (1<<i))` and `(cur_b_xor_x)`.\n                #   Choice 2: `x_i = 1`. `next_a_i = 1 - bit_a`, `next_b_i = 1 - bit_b`.\n                #     If `bit_a=0, bit_b=1`: `next_a_i=1`, `next_b_i=0`. Current values: `(cur_a_xor_x | (1<<i))` and `(cur_b_xor_x)`.\n                #     If `bit_a=1, bit_b=0`: `next_a_i=0`, `next_b_i=1`. Current values: `(cur_a_xor_x)` and `(cur_b_xor_x | (1<<i))`.\n                #   Here, we have two branches. We need to see which branch leads to a larger final product.\n                #   This suggests a recursive approach or a Trie.\n\n                # Let's use the Trie of `x` values.\n                # `root = Node()`\n                # `insert(root, 0)` # Base case for x=0\n\n                # This problem can be solved with a greedy approach on bits.\n                # At bit `i` from `n-1` down to `0`.\n                # `bit_a = (a >> i) & 1`\n                # `bit_b = (b >> i) & 1`\n\n                # We are building the final values `A = a^x` and `B = b^x`.\n                # `cur_A = 0`, `cur_B = 0`.\n\n                # Try to make `cur_A` and `cur_B` have the same bit at position `i` if possible, and that bit is 1.\n                # This occurs if `a_i = b_i`, and we can make `(a^x)_i = 1` and `(b^x)_i = 1`.\n                # For `(a^x)_i = 1`, we need `a_i ^ x_i = 1`. So `x_i = 1 - a_i`.\n                # For `(b^x)_i = 1`, we need `b_i ^ x_i = 1`. So `x_i = 1 - b_i`.\n                # If `a_i == b_i`, then these `x_i` requirements are the same.\n                # So if `a_i == b_i`, we can choose `x_i = 1 - a_i` to make both resulting bits 1.\n                # If `bit_a == bit_b`:\n                #   We choose `x_i = 1 - bit_a`.\n                #   `res_a_i = bit_a ^ (1 - bit_a) = 1`\n                #   `res_b_i = bit_b ^ (1 - bit_b) = 1`\n                #   `cur_A |= (1 << i)`\n                #   `cur_B |= (1 << i)`\n                #   This choice is greedy and aims to set the highest possible bits of A and B to 1.\n\n                # If `bit_a != bit_b`:\n                #   We cannot make both `res_a_i` and `res_b_i` be 1 by a single choice of `x_i`.\n                #   One will be 0, and the other will be 1.\n                #   This implies the `i`-th bit of `A*B` (if these are the most significant differing bits) is 0.\n                #   We have two choices for `x_i` that lead to different `(a^x)_i` and `(b^x)_i`.\n                #   Choice 1: Make `(a^x)_i = 1`, `(b^x)_i = 0`.\n                #     This requires `bit_a ^ x_i = 1` and `bit_b ^ x_i = 0`.\n                #     If `bit_a=0, bit_b=1`: `x_i` must be 1 for `a^x`, needs `x_i=0` for `b^x`. Impossible.\n                #     Ah, the issue is that `x_i` must be the same for both.\n                #     Let's re-examine `bit_a != bit_b`.\n                #     If `bit_a=0, bit_b=1`:\n                #       If `x_i=0`: `(a^x)_i = 0`, `(b^x)_i = 1`.\n                #       If `x_i=1`: `(a^x)_i = 1`, `(b^x)_i = 0`.\n                #     In both cases, one bit is 0 and the other is 1.\n                #     We want to choose `x_i` to maximize the FINAL product.\n                #     This means we should explore both possibilities for `x_i` when `bit_a != bit_b`.\n                #     This is where the Trie comes in.\n\n                # Let's build a Trie of `x` that results in `(a^x)` and `(b^x)`.\n                # The Trie will store prefixes of `x`.\n                # For each bit `i` from `n-1` down to 0:\n                # `bit_a = (a >> i) & 1`\n                # `bit_b = (b >> i) & 1`\n                #\n                # We are at a node in the Trie representing the prefix of `x` determined by bits `n-1` down to `i+1`.\n                # Let the current prefix of `a^x` be `A_prefix` and `b^x` be `B_prefix`.\n                #\n                # Consider the possible next bits of `x`, which are `0` and `1`.\n\n                # State for recursion/Trie: (bit_pos, current_a_val, current_b_val)\n                # `bit_pos`: the current bit we are considering (from n-1 down to 0)\n                # `current_a_val`: the prefix of `a^x` built so far.\n                # `current_b_val`: the prefix of `b^x` built so far.\n\n                # `solve_recursive(bit_pos, current_a_val, current_b_val)`\n\n                # Base case: `bit_pos < 0`. Return `(current_a_val * current_b_val) % MOD`.\n\n                # Transition:\n                # `bit_a = (a >> bit_pos) & 1`\n                # `bit_b = (b >> bit_pos) & 1`\n\n                # Option 1: Try `x_i = 0`.\n                #   `next_a_i = bit_a ^ 0 = bit_a`\n                #   `next_b_i = bit_b ^ 0 = bit_b`\n                #   `res1 = solve_recursive(bit_pos - 1, current_a_val | (next_a_i << bit_pos), current_b_val | (next_b_i << bit_pos))`\n\n                # Option 2: Try `x_i = 1`.\n                #   `next_a_i = bit_a ^ 1`\n                #   `next_b_i = bit_b ^ 1`\n                #   `res2 = solve_recursive(bit_pos - 1, current_a_val | (next_a_i << bit_pos), current_b_val | (next_b_i << bit_pos))`\n\n                # Return `max(res1, res2)`.\n\n                # Memoization: `memo[bit_pos][current_a_val][current_b_val]`\n                # `current_a_val` and `current_b_val` can be up to `2^n`. This is too much.\n                # The state needs to be more compact.\n\n                # The state can be related to the prefixes contributed by bits >= `bit_pos`.\n                # `(a >> (bit_pos+1))` and `(b >> (bit_pos+1))`.\n\n                # Let's consider the constraint `n <= 50`.\n                # The crucial observation might be that we only care about the relative differences of bits of `a` and `b` up to `n`.\n\n                # If `bit_a == bit_b`:\n                #   Both `x_i=0` and `x_i=1` result in `(a^x)_i == (b^x)_i`.\n                #   If `bit_a = 0`, then `(a^x)_i` and `(b^x)_i` will