erge. A good approach is to iterate\n    # through sorted available sizes, perform merges, and add new sizes to the set.\n    # Repeat until no more sizes can be processed (i.e., no more mergable pairs).\n\n    # The problem can be rephrased: for each slime size, we take as many pairs as possible\n    # and form new slimes of size 2X. The remaining single slimes (or those that cannot form pairs)\n    # contribute to the final count. We need to carry over the `carry` to 2X.\n\n    # A more efficient way to handle this is to use a dictionary and iterate on its sorted keys.\n    # When a new size 2X is formed, add its count to the dictionary.\n    \n    # We need to make sure we process all sizes, including those generated.\n    # Convert slimes to a list of (size, count) and sort it initially.\n    # It's better to put all into a dictionary and handle it iteratively.\n    \n    # Use a dictionary as a \"bucket\" for counts for each size.\n    # Iterate through the sizes, and for each size:\n    # 1. Calculate how many pairs can be formed. These contribute to 2*size.\n    # 2. Add the remainder to the final count.\n    \n    # We need to process sizes in increasing order.\n    # We can do this by repeatedly getting the smallest key from the dictionary.\n    \n    # A convenient way to handle this is to maintain a dictionary where keys are sizes\n    # and values are counts. When a size `X` is processed, `count[X] // 2` is carried\n    # over to `count[2X]`, and `count[X] % 2` is kept.\n    # We need to ensure `2X` is processed *after* `X`.\n    \n    # The maximum possible size can be very large (max S_i * 2^(something)).\n    # However, we only care about sizes that actually exist or are generated.\n    \n    # Let's use a dictionary `current_slime_counts`.\n    current_slime_counts = {}\n    for s, c in slimes.items():\n        current_slime_counts[s] = c\n        \n    final_slime_count = 0\n\n    # Keep track of sizes that need to be processed.\n    # A sorted list of unique sizes ensures we process smaller ones first.\n    # Since new sizes (2X) can appear, we need to efficiently update this.\n    # A `while` loop that takes the smallest element, processes it, and adds new elements\n    # to the set of available sizes to be processed is a good approach.\n    \n    # Get all initial keys.\n    keys_to_process = sorted(list(current_slime_counts.keys()))\n    \n    # Use an index to iterate through potentially growing keys_to_process\n    i = 0\n    while i < len(keys_to_process):\n        current_s = keys_to_process[i]\n        \n        if current_s not in current_slime_counts or current_slime_counts[current_s] == 0:\n            i += 1\n            continue\n            \n        count = current_slime_counts[current_s]\n        \n        # How many pairs can be formed?\n        pairs = count // 2\n        \n        # Remaining slimes of size current_s\n        remainder = count % 2\n        \n        if remainder == 1:\n            final_slime_count += 1\n            \n        if pairs > 0:\n            next_s = current_s * 2\n            current_slime_counts[next_s] = current_slime_counts.get(next_s, 0) + pairs\n            \n            # If next_s is a new size, add it to keys_to_process and keep it sorted.\n            # This is the tricky part with list.insert or rebuilding.\n            # A more robust way might be to keep keys_to_process as a sorted list\n            # and re-insert elements efficiently.\n            \n            # For simplicity, if next_s > current_s, it will be handled later.\n            # We don't need to re-sort all the time, just ensure it eventually gets processed.\n            # The way it's implemented now, `keys_to_process` is initialized once.\n            # This means `next_s` might not be in `keys_to_process`.\n            # We need to make sure all generated primes eventually get processed.\n            \n            # Better approach:\n            # Add new generated sizes to primary `slimes_data` dictionary.\n            # Re-sort `keys_to_process` if a new key is added that is smaller than current.\n            # But we are iterating in increasing order. So `2*current_s` will always be\n            # larger than `current_s`. If `2*current_s` is a new size, we just need to make\n            # sure it gets added to the `keys_to_process` list in its correct sorted position.\n            \n            # Efficiently insert `next_s` into `keys_to_process` if it's new.\n            if next_s not in current_slime_counts or Slimes[next_s] is None:\n                # Add to keys_to_process if it's a new size.\n                # Find its insertion point and insert.\n                # This could be expensive for large N repeatedly.\n                # However, the number of distinct sizes (S_i and their powers of 2)\n                # is log(max S_i / min S_i) roughly.\n                # A more optimized way for `keys_to_process` is to use a min-heap or\n                # simply iterate through a `sorted(current_slime_counts.keys())` in each outer loop\n                # if there are changes.\n                \n                # Let's rebuild the `keys_to_process` list after new values might be added.\n                # This might look like quadratic complexity but distinct values of S and 2S...\n                # The total number of distinct sizes produced is limited.\n                # Each S_i can generate S_i*2, S_i*4, ... S_i*2^k. Max k is around log2(10^9).\n                # N * log(S_max) new distinct sizes. Total distinct sizes ~ N * 30.\n                # Sorting N log N.\n                \n                pass # The current approach of rebuild `keys_to_process` in an outer loop.\n                \n        # Remove current_s from consideration for further processing in this step.\n        del current_slime_counts[current_s]\n        \n        i += 1\n        \n    print(final_slime_count + sum(current_slime_counts.values()))\n\n\n# Optimized approach using a dictionary that is dynamically updated.\ndef solve_optimized():\n    N = int(input())\n    slimes_map = {}\n    for _ in range(N):\n        S, C = map(int, input().split())\n        slimes_map[S] = C\n\n    # Use a sorted list of unique sizes encountered, which will grow over time.\n    # Keep track of a pointer into this list.\n    \n    # We will process keys. When a 2X key is generated, we add it to the map.\n    # Then we re-sort the keys and find the new index. This is inefficient.\n    \n    # A single pass using sorted keys of the current map.\n    # The crucial observation for optimality is that processing smaller sizes first\n    # is always optimal. If we merge X, X to get 2X, then at a later point two 2X's can\n    # merge to 4X. The order matters only that we process X before 2X if they both exist.\n    # Example: 4 slimes of size 1. (1,4) -> (2,2) -> (4,1). Final count 1.\n    # If we had (1,2), (2,1). -> (2,1)+(2,1) -> (4,1). Final count 1.\n    \n    # Let's process the sizes from smallest to largest.\n    # The `slimes_map` accumulates counts.\n    \n    # Get all initial sizes.\n    sizes = sorted(slimes_map.keys())\n    \n    # We use a `current_total_count` to keep track of the final slimes.\n    final_slimes = 0\n    \n    # Example dry run: {3:3, 5:1, 6:1}\n    # sizes = [3, 5, 6]\n    \n    # Current slimes map: {3:3, 5:1, 6:1}\n    \n    # Iterate through keys, but handle dynamically created keys\n    # The approach is to store all values in a dictionary.\n    # Iterate through sorted keys, perform merges, and add generated slimes to dictionary.\n    # Then we NEED to re-sort or use a data structure which keeps keys sorted.\n    \n    # Using `collections.defaultdict` for easier handling of new keys.\n    # `sizes_to_process` list will store all distinct sizes that might have slimes.\n    # Initially this is the input `S_i`.\n    # As we produce `2*S_i`, we add `2*S_i` to this list if it's new.\n    # Then sort `sizes_to_process` list.\n    # This involves re-sorting inside a loop, which can be `O(N*logN * logS_max)`.\n    # N is distinct initial sizes, logS_max is max products `2^k * S_i`.\n    # So `N * logS_max` is total distinct sizes.\n    # Total complexity: `N*logS_max * N*logS_max * log(N*logS_max)` roughly. Okay for N = 10^5\n    \n    # Let's use a dictionary to hold ALL slimes and iterate through its sorted keys.\n    # This ensures that we process smaller slimes first.\n    \n    # Start with initial slimes_map.\n    # Loop over sorted keys.\n    # Example: {3:3, 5:1, 6:1}\n    # Initial sorted keys: [3, 5, 6]\n    \n    all_sizes = sorted(list(slimes_map.keys()))\n    \n    for current_size in all_sizes:\n        if current_size not in slimes_map:\n            continue # This size might have been entirely consumed by a previous merge (e.g. if we processed 3 and removed 3)\n        \n        count = slimes_map[current_size]\n        \n        # How many slimes of `current_size` can be paired up?\n        pairs = count // 2\n        \n        # How many `current_size` slimes remain after pairing?\n        remainder = count % 2\n        \n        # If there's a remainder, it contributes to the final slime count.\n        if remainder == 1:\n            final_slimes += 1\n            \n        # If pairs were formed, create new slimes of size `2 * current_size`.\n        if pairs > 0:\n            next_size = current_size * 2\n            \n            # Add these new slimes to the map. Handle if `next_size` is new or existing.\n            slimes_map[next_size] = slimes_map.get(next_size, 0) + pairs\n            \n            # Critically, if `next_size` is a new size that is larger than any size\n            # we've already seen or added to `all_sizes`, it needs to be added for future iteration.\n            # To handle this efficiently, we must ensure `all_sizes` is always sorted correctly\n            # and includes all possible generated sizes.\n            # A simple way without re-sorting `all_sizes` constantly:\n            # We add `next_size` to a temporary list `newly_generated_sizes`.\n            # After processing all `all_sizes` from current iteration, combine\n            # `newly_generated_sizes` with `all_sizes` and re-sort for the next iteration.\n            # But this is not robust because `next_size` could be smaller than a later element in `all_sizes`.\n            \n            # The correct way is to process from min to max:\n            # Use a `dict` as the primary storage. After each iteration step (for a `current_size`),\n            # `slimes_map[current_size]` effectively becomes `remainder`. However, we don't need to\n            # keep `remainder` in the map, as it's already counted in `final_slimes`.\n            \n    # The previous loop structure is flawed because new sizes `2*current_size` are added to a map,\n    # but the `all_sizes` list, which controls iteration, is static after initialization.\n    \n    # Corrected approach:\n    # Use a dictionary `slimes_data` to store `size:count` dynamically.\n    # Iterate by repeatedly finding the smallest key in `slimes_data`.\n    # When processing a key:\n    #   1. Take its count.\n    #   2. Add `count % 2` to `final_slimes`.\n    #   3. Add `count // 2` to `2 * key` in `slimes_data`.\n    #   4. Remove `key` from `slimes_data` (or set its count to 0).\n    # Repeat until `slimes_data` is empty.\n    \n    slimes_data = {}\n    for S, C in slimes_map.items():\n        slimes_data[S] = C\n        \n    final_slimes_count = 0\n    \n    # We need to process sizes in increasing order.\n    # An `OrderedDict` or continuously sorting `keys` is slow.\n    # A min-heap (priority queue) of keys is ideal here.\n    # `heapq` module provides min-heap functionality.\n    import heapq\n    \n    # Initialize the min-heap with all initial sizes.\n    min_heap_sizes = list(slimes_data.keys())\n    heapq.heapify(min_heap_sizes)\n    \n    while min_heap_sizes:\n        current_s = heapq.heappop(min_heap_sizes)\n        \n        # If this size was already processed or its count became 0, skip.\n        # This can happen if a larger size generated a smaller one (not possible here, 2X > X),\n        # or if an entry was added and then removed.\n        # More likely scenario: `current_s` may have been `current_s` then processed,\n        # and then `2*other_s == current_s` adds to `current_s` *again*.\n        # We need to make sure we don't re-process `current_s` if its count was already taken.\n        # The `slimes_data` map holds the current valid counts.\n        \n        if current_s not in slimes_data or slimes_data[current_s] == 0:\n            continue\n            \n        count = slimes_data[current_s]\n        \n        final_slimes_count += (count % 2)\n        \n        pairs = count // 2\n        \n        if pairs > 0:\n            next_s = current_s * 2\n            \n            # If `next_s` already has slimes, update its count.\n            # If `next_s` is new, add it to `slimes_data` and the heap.\n            if next_s not in slimes_data or slimes_data[next_s] == 0:\n                heapq.heappush(min_heap_sizes, next_s)\n            \n            slimes_data[next_s] = slimes_data.get(next_s, 0) + pairs\n            \n        # The slimes of `current_s` are now fully processed (remainder counted, pairs moved).\n        del slimes_data[current_s]\n        \n    print(final_slimes_count)\n\n\nsolve_optimized()\n```",
