    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef count_happy_students(nums: list[int]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the number of ways to select a group of students so that everyone remains happy.\n\n    Args:\n        nums: A list of integers where nums[i] represents a condition for the i-th student.\n\n    Returns:\n        The number of ways to select a group of students so that everyone remains happy.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(nums)\n    nums.sort()  # Sort the array to efficiently check conditions\n\n    count = 0\n\n    # Case 1: Select 0 students\n    # All students must be unhappy if not selected (i.e., total_selected < nums[i])\n    # For 0 selected students, total_selected = 0.\n    # So, for all students, 0 < nums[i] must be true.\n    # If nums[0] is 0, then the first student would be unhappy.\n    if nums[0] > 0:\n        count += 1\n\n    # Iterate through possible numbers of selected students from 1 to n (inclusive)\n    for k in range(1, n + 1):\n        # We are considering selecting 'k' students.\n        # When nums is sorted, if we select 'k' students,\n        # it implies we are considering the first 'k' students in the sorted array\n        # as potentially selected, and the remaining 'n - k' students as not selected.\n\n        # Condition for selected students (first k students in sorted array):\n        # selected = k\n        # For a selected student i, k > nums[i] must be true.\n        # Since nums is sorted, if k > nums[k-1] (the largest of the first k values),\n        # then k will be greater than all nums[j] where j < k.\n        \n        # Condition for not selected students (remaining n-k students):\n        # not_selected = n - k\n        # For a not selected student j, k < nums[j] must be true.\n        # Since nums is sorted, if k < nums[k] (the smallest of the remaining n-k values),\n        # then k will be less than all nums[j] where j >= k.\n\n        # Combined conditions:\n        # 1. k > nums[k-1] (for chosen students)\n        # 2. k < nums[k] (for not chosen students) - This condition applies only if k < n\n\n        # Check conditions based on k\n        # Case when k students are selected from the sorted array:\n        # All selected students (with values nums[0] to nums[k-1]) must satisfy k > nums[i].\n        # This is equivalent to checking k > nums[k-1] since nums is sorted.\n        if k > nums[k - 1]:\n            # All not selected students (with values nums[k] to nums[n-1]) must satisfy k < nums[i].\n            # This is equivalent to checking k < nums[k] since nums is sorted.\n            # This check is only relevant if there are students not selected (i.e., k < n).\n            if k < n:\n                if k < nums[k]:\n                    count += 1\n            else:  # All students are selected (k == n)\n                # No students are not selected, so the second condition is trivially met.\n                count += 1\n\n    return count\n```",
