      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef min_operations_to_make_median_k(nums: list[int], k: int) -> int:\n    n = len(nums)\n    nums.sort()\n    \n    # The median index for an array of length n is (n - 1) // 2 for 0-indexed arrays\n    # If there are two choices, the larger of the two values is taken.\n    # This means the median is nums[(n - 1) // 2] if n is odd.\n    # If n is even, it's either nums[n // 2 - 1] or nums[n // 2].\n    # The problem statement says \"If there are two choices for a median, the larger of the two values is taken.\"\n    # This implies that for an even length array, it's nums[n // 2].\n    # So, the median index is always n // 2.\n    median_idx = n // 2\n\n    operations = 0\n    \n    # First, make the median element equal to k\n    operations += abs(nums[median_idx] - k)\n    nums[median_idx] = k # Adjust the median element in the sorted array\n\n    # Now, ensure all elements to the left of the median are less than or equal to k\n    # and all elements to the right are greater than or equal to k.\n    # This is to maintain the sorted property after the median is set to k.\n\n    # Iterate from median_idx - 1 down to 0\n    # If any element is greater than k, it needs to be reduced to k.\n    # Moving it below k unnecessarily increases operations as k is the target.\n    # The minimum operations for elements to the left is to make them <= k.\n    # Since nums[median_idx] is k, if nums[i] > k for i < median_idx,\n    # it means nums[i] is out of place relative to the new k at median_idx.\n    # To fix this, we effectively move nums[i] down to k.\n    # Any element smaller than k is fine for now; we'll address it if it needs to be adjusted further.\n    for i in range(median_idx - 1, -1, -1):\n        if nums[i] > k:\n            operations += (nums[i] - k)\n            nums[i] = k # conceptually, we change it to k\n        else: # nums[i] <= k, it's in a good position relative to k\n            break # Since the array was sorted, all subsequent elements will also be <= k\n\n    # Iterate from median_idx + 1 up to n - 1\n    # If any element is smaller than k, it needs to be increased to k.\n    # Moving it above k unnecessarily increases operations.\n    # The minimum operations for elements to the right is to make them >= k.\n    # Since nums[median_idx] is k, if nums[i] < k for i > median_idx,\n    # it means nums[i] is out of place relative to the new k at median_idx.\n    # To fix this, we effectively move nums[i] up to k.\n    # Any element larger than k is fine for now; we'll address it if it needs to be adjusted further.\n    for i in range(median_idx + 1, n):\n        if nums[i] < k:\n            operations += (k - nums[i])\n            nums[i] = k # conceptually, we change it to k\n        else: # nums[i] >= k, it's in a good position relative to k\n            break # Since the array was sorted, all subsequent elements will also be >= k\n\n    return operations\n```",
