l operation count.\n    # If it places pieces on S, it effectively \"propagates\" this operation.\n    #\n    # The problem is about maximizing the total operations.\n    # Let d[i] be the maximum number of new pieces that can be generated if a single\n    # piece is placed on vertex i and then removed.\n    # It turns out that for optimal strategy, for any vertex x, we should choose to place pieces\n    # on all adjacent vertices y such that W_y < W_x. This decision is greedy and locally optimal.\n    # Why? Because each such piece on y can then be removed, contributing 1 to total operations,\n    # and potentially generating more pieces on *its* neighbors.\n    #\n    # Let D_i be the maximum number of times a piece initially on vertex i can contribute\n    # to the total operation count (directly by being removed, and indirectly by generating\n    # pieces on adjacent vertices that are subsequently removed).\n    #\n    # Sort vertices by W_i in ascending order.\n    # This allows us to compute D_i for vertices with smaller W_i first.\n    # If W_x is small, then it's easier to put pieces on x's neighbors y when W_y > W_x.\n    #\n    # Let's consider the reverse: sort by W_i in descending order.\n    # If we process vertices in decreasing order of W_i (let's say x_1, x_2, ..., x_N where W_{x_1} >= W_{x_2} >= ...),\n    # when we compute dp[x_k]:\n    # dp[x_k] = 1 + sum(dp[y]) for all y in adj[x_k] such that W_y < W_{x_k}.\n    # The sum condition sum(W_y) < W_x_k is a critical constraint.\n    # This means not all neighbors with smaller weights can always be chosen.\n    #\n    # However, the problem \"It can be proved that, regardless of how the operation is performed,\n    # there will be no pieces on the graph after a finite number of iterations.\" implies that\n    # the total \"work\" extractable from a given configuration of pieces is well-defined and finite.\n    #\n    # This generally points to a potential function argument or a state space exploration