final_row_colors[r]` and `c` has a `final_col_colors[c]`:\n    # The color of (r, c) is determined by the operation that happened LATER in the original sequence.\n    # In the reversed sequence, this means if `r` is in `final_row_colors` and `c` is in `final_col_colors`, we need to know which one was the \"final\" setter.\n    # The `rows_finalized` and `cols_finalized` indicate if that row/col was ever touched by an operation that turned out to be the LAST for that row/col.\n\n    # The correct accounting:\n    # Iterate through the cells that are colored by the FINAL row operations.\n    # For each `(r, color)` in `final_row_colors.items()`:\n    #   This row `r` will be filled with `color` for all columns `c` that are NOT in `final_col_colors`.\n    #   `num_cols_not_in_final_col_colors = W - len(final_col_colors)`\n    #   Add `num_cols_not_in_final_col_colors` to `color_counts[color]`.\n\n    # Iterate through the cells that are colored by the FINAL column operations.\n    # For each `(c, color)` in `final_col_colors.items()`:\n    #   This column `c` will be filled with `color` for all rows `r` that are NOT in `final_row_colors`.\n    #   `num_rows_not_in_final_row_colors = H - len(final_row_colors)`\n    #   Add `num_rows_not_in_final_row_colors` to `color_counts[color]`.\n\n    # What about the intersections? Cells (r, c) where `r` is in `final_row_colors` AND `c` is in `final_col_colors`.\n    # The color of such a cell is determined by the operation that happened LATER in the original sequence.\n    # This is implicitly handled if we iterate carefully.\n\n    # Let's try a concrete simulation for the intersections.\n    # We need to iterate through all cells that are affected by both a final row color and a final column color.\n    # This is the set of cells `(r, c)` where `r` is a key in `final_row_colors` and `c` is a key in `final_col_colors`.\n    # For each such cell, we need to determine its color.\n    # We can iterate through the operations *again*