    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_293",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. The function should take stdin as input and print the output. Simply call the function after the definition. Question:\nAtCoder Inc. is planning to develop a product. The product has K parameters, whose values are currently all zero. The company aims to raise all parameter values to at least P.\nThere are N development plans. Executing the i-th development plan (1 \\le i \\le N) increases the value of the j-th parameter by A_{{i,j}} for every integer j such that 1 \\le j \\le K, at the cost of C_i.\nA development plan cannot be executed more than once. Determine whether the company can achieve its goal, and if it can, find the minimum total cost required to achieve the goal.\n\nInput\n\nThe input is given from Standard Input in the following format:\nN K P\r\nC_1 A_{{1,1}} A_{{1,2}} \\dots A_{{1,K}}\r\nC_2 A_{{2,1}} A_{{2,2}} \\dots A_{{2,K}}\r\n\\dots\r\nC_N A_{{N,1}} A_{{N,2}} \\dots A_{{N,K}}\n\nOutput\n\nIf AtCoder Inc. can achieve its goal, print the minimum total cost required to achieve the goal; otherwise, print -1.\n\nConstraints\n\n\n- 1 \\le N \\le 100\n- 1 \\le K,P \\le 5\n- 0 \\le A_{{i,j}} \\le P(1 \\le i \\le N,1 \\le j \\le K)\n- 1 \\le C_i \\le 10^9(1 \\le i \\le N)\n- All input values are integers.\n\nSample Input 1\n\n4 3 5\r\n5 3 0 2\r\n3 1 2 3\r\n3 2 4 0\r\n1 0 1 4\n\nSample Output 1\n\n9\r\n\nIf you execute the first, third, and fourth development plans, each parameter will be 3+2+0=5,0+4+1=5,2+0+4=6, all of which are at least 5, so the goal is achieved. The total cost in this case is 5 + 3 + 1 = 9.\nIt is impossible to achieve the goal at a total cost of 8 or less. Thus, the answer is 9.\n\nSample Input 2\n\n7 3 5\r\n85 1 0 1\r\n37 1 1 0\r\n38 2 0 0\r\n45 0 2 2\r\n67 1 1 0\r\n12 2 2 0\r\n94 2 2 1\n\nSample Output 2\n\n-1\r\n\nYou cannot achieve the goal no matter what you do. Thus, print -1.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport sys\n\ndef solve():\n    N, K, P = map(int, sys.stdin.readline().split())\n    plans = []\n    for _ in range(N):\n        line = list(map(int, sys.stdin.readline().split()))\n        cost = line[0]\n        params = tuple(line[1:])\n        plans.append((cost, params))\n\n    # Since P and K are small, we can use dynamic programming.\n    # The state will be a tuple representing the current values of the K parameters.\n    # The maximum value any parameter can reach to satisfy the condition is P.\n    # If a parameter value exceeds P, it's effectively P for our goal.\n    # So, the state will be a tuple of K integers, where each integer is between 0 and P.\n    \n    # dp[param_tuple] = minimum cost to achieve at least param_tuple for each parameter.\n    # We use a dictionary for DP table because not all combinations might be reachable\n    # or we might not need to store states where parameters already exceed P.\n    \n    dp = {}\n    \n    # Initial state: all parameters are 0, cost is 0.\n    initial_state = tuple([0] * K)\n    dp[initial_state] = 0\n    \n    # Iterate through each development plan\n    for cost, plan_params in plans:\n        # Create a new DP table for the current plan to avoid modifying\n        # it while iterating over the previous DP table.\n        new_dp = dp.copy()\n        \n        # Iterate over existing states in the DP table\n        for state, current_cost in dp.items():\n            \n            # For each existing state, consider applying the current plan.\n            new_state_list = []\n            for i in range(K):\n                # The new value for the i-th parameter is the maximum of its current value\n                # and the value from the plan, capped at P.\n                new_val = min(state[i] + plan_params[i], P)\n                new_state_list.append(new_val)\n            new_state = tuple(new_state_list)\n            \n            # Calculate the total cost if we apply this plan to this state.\n            total_cost_with_plan = current_cost + cost\n            \n            # Update the new DP table if this path is cheaper or if the state is new.\n            if new_state not in new_dp or total_cost_with_plan < new_dp[new_state]:\n                new_dp[new_state] = total_cost_with_plan\n        \n        # Update the main DP table after considering all existing states with the current plan.\n        dp = new_dp\n        \n    # The target state is when all parameters are at least P.\n    # In our DP state representation, this means the tuple (P, P, ..., P).\n    target_state = tuple([P] * K)\n    \n    # Check if the target state is reachable.\n    if target_state in dp:\n        print(dp[target_state])\n    else:\n        # If the target state is not in the DP table, it means it's impossible to reach.\n        print(-1)\n\ndef main():\n    solve()\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n```",
