    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. The function should take stdin as input and print the output. Simply call the function after the definition. Question:\nA subarray is a continuous part of array.\n\nYarik recently found an array $a$ of $n$ elements and became very interested in finding the maximum sum of a non empty subarray. However, Yarik doesn't like consecutive integers with the same parity, so the subarray he chooses must have alternating parities for adjacent elements.\n\nFor example, $[1, 2, 3]$ is acceptable, but $[1, 2, 4]$ is not, as $2$ and $4$ are both even and adjacent.\n\nYou need to help Yarik by finding the maximum sum of such a subarray.\n\nInput\n\nThe first line contains an integer $t$ $(1 \\le t \\le 10^4)$ — number of test cases. Each test case is described as follows.\n\nThe first line of each test case contains an integer $n$ $(1 \\le n \\le 2 \\cdot 10^5)$ — length of the array.\n\nThe second line of each test case contains $n$ integers $a_1, a_2, \\dots, a_n$ $(-10^3 \\le a_i \\le 10^3)$ — elements of the array.\n\nIt is guaranteed that the sum of $n$ for all test cases does not exceed $2 \\cdot 10^5$.\n\nOutput\n\nFor each test case, output a single integer — the answer to the problem.Sample Input 1:\n7\n\n5\n\n1 2 3 4 5\n\n4\n\n9 9 8 8\n\n6\n\n-1 4 -1 0 5 -4\n\n4\n\n-1 2 4 -3\n\n1\n\n-1000\n\n3\n\n101 -99 101\n\n20\n\n-10 5 -8 10 6 -10 7 9 -2 -6 7 2 -4 6 -1 7 -6 -7 4 1\n\n\n\nSample Output 1:\n\n15\n17\n8\n4\n-1000\n101\n10\n",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The user wants a Python function that finds the maximum sum of a subarray with alternating parities.\n\n```python\ndef solve():\n    n = int(input())\n    a = list(map(int, input().split()))\n\n    max_overall_sum = -float('inf')\n\n    for i in range(n):\n        current_sum = a[i]\n        max_overall_sum = max(max_overall_sum, current_sum)\n        current_parity = a[i] % 2\n\n        for j in range(i + 1, n):\n            next_parity = a[j] % 2\n            if next_parity != current_parity:\n                current_sum += a[j]\n                max_overall_sum = max(max_overall_sum, current_sum)\n                current_parity = next_parity\n            else:\n                # Parity constraint violated, break this subarray\n                break\n    print(max_overall_sum)\n\ndef solve_kadane():\n    n = int(input())\n    a = list(map(int, input().split()))\n\n    max_so_far = -float('inf')\n    current_max = -float('inf')\n    last_parity = None\n\n    for x in a:\n        if current_max == -float('inf'):  # Start a new subarray\n            current_max = x\n            last_parity = x % 2\n        else:\n            if x % 2 != last_parity:\n                current_max += x\n                last_parity = x % 2\n            else:\n                # Parity mismatch, restart the subarray\n                max_so_far = max(max_so_far, current_max)\n                current_max = x\n                last_parity = x % 2\n        max_so_far = max(max_so_far, current_max)\n\n    print(max_so_far)\n\ndef solve_optimized():\n    n = int(input())\n    a = list(map(int, input().split()))\n\n    max_so_far = -float('inf')\n    current_max_ending_here = -float('inf') # Represents the max sum ending at current element following parity rules\n\n    for x in a:\n        if current_max_ending_here == -float('inf'):\n            # This is the first element of a potential subarray\n            current_max_ending_here = x\n        else:\n            # Check parity with the last element of the subarray that formed current_max_ending_here\n            # We need to know the parity of the *last element* that contributed to current_max_ending_here.\n            # A simpler approach is to treat each element as a potential start of a new subarray\n            # or an extension of the previous one.\n\n            # Option 1: Start a new subarray with just 'x'\n            # Option 2: Extend the previous valid subarray with 'x'\n            # This requires knowing the parity of the immediate *previous* element in the array `a`\n            # and matching it to the `x`.\n\n            # Let's re-think the Kadane-like approach for alternating parity.\n            # `current_max` will store the max sum of an alternating subarray ending at the current position.\n            # If current element `a[i]` is considered:\n            #   - It can form a new subarray `[a[i]]`.\n            #   - It can extend an existing subarray `[..., a[i-1]]` if `a[i-1]` and `a[i]` have different parities.\n            # We want to choose the maximum of these.\n            # If we extend, `current_max = previous_current_max + a[i]`.\n            # If we start new, `current_max = a[i]`.\n            # So, `current_max = max(a[i], previous_current_max + a[i])` WITH PARITY CONSTRAINT.\n\n            # The key is that `previous_current_max` must have ended with the *correct* parity for `x`.\n\n            # Let's adjust for parity:\n            # If current_max_ending_here is not -inf, it means it contains some valid alternating sequence.\n            # Its last element's parity is known (implicitly through the loop, based on a[i-1]).\n            # Let's instead keep track of the *last added number's parity* explicitly.\n\n            # Resetting for each `x` to avoid complex state:\n            # We iterate `j` from `i` to `n-1` for every `i`. This is O(N^2).\n            # The prompt constraint N=2*10^5 implies O(N) or O(N log N).\n\n            # Let's retry the Kadane-style.\n            # Initialize max_so_far = a[0], current_max = a[0].\n            # For i from 1 to n-1:\n            #   If a[i] and a[i-1] have different parities:\n            #     current_max = max(a[i], current_max + a[i])\n            #   Else (same parity):\n            #     current_max = a[i] # Must start a new subarray\n            #   max_so_far = max(max_so_far, current_max)\n            pass # The loop below implements this more directly\n\n    max_so_far = -float('inf')\n    current_max = -float('inf')\n\n    for i in range(n):\n        if i == 0:\n            current_max = a[0]\n        else:\n            if (a[i] % 2) != (a[i-1] % 2):\n                current_max = max(a[i], current_max + a[i])\n            else:\n                current_max = a[i]\n        \n        max_so_far = max(max_so_far, current_max)\n\n    print(max_so_far)\n\n\ndef solve_final_kadane():\n    n = int(input())\n    a = list(map(int, input().split()))\n\n    max_overall_sum = -float('inf') # Stores the maximum sum found across all subarrays\n    current_max_sum = -float('inf')  # Stores the maximum sum of a valid alternating subarray ending at the current position\n\n    for i in range(n):\n        if current_max_sum == -float('inf'):\n            # This is the very first element or the start of a new valid sequence after a break\n            current_max_sum = a[i]\n        elif (a[i] % 2) != (a[i-1] % 2):\n            # Elements have alternating parity, extend the current subarray\n            current_max_sum = max(a[i], current_max_sum + a[i])\n        else:\n            # Parity breaks, start a new subarray with the current element\n            current_max_sum = a[i]\n        \n        # In each step, current_max_sum is the maximum sum of an alternating subarray ending at a[i].\n        # We update the overall maximum.\n        max_overall_sum = max(max_overall_sum, current_max_sum)\n\n    print(max_overall_sum)\n\n\ndef solve_final():\n    n = int(input())\n    a = list(map(int, input().split()))\n\n    max_overall_sum = -float('inf')  # Stores the maximum sum across all valid alternating subarrays\n    current_max_ending_here = -float('inf') # Stores the maximum sum of a valid alternating subarray *ending at the current element*\n\n    for i in range(n):\n        if i == 0:\n            # For the first element, it's always the start of a potential subarray\n            current_max_ending_here = a[i]\n        else:\n            # Check if current element 'a[i]' can extend the subarray ending at 'a[i-1]'\n            if (a[i] % 2) != (a[i-1] % 2):\n                # Parity matches for extension. We have two options for current_max_ending_here:\n                # 1. Start a new subarray with just a[i].\n                # 2. Extend the previous best alternating subarray: current_max_ending_here_previous_step + a[i].\n                # We take the maximum of these.\n                current_max_ending_here = max(a[i], current_max_ending_here + a[i])\n            else:\n                # Parity does not match. We *must* start a new subarray with a[i].\n                current_max_ending_here = a[i]\n        \n        # After updating current_max_ending_here for a[i], update the overall maximum\n        max_overall_sum = max(max_overall_sum, current_max_ending_here)\n\n    print(max_overall_sum)\n\n# Read the number of test cases\nt = int(input())\n\n# Loop through each test case\nfor _ in range(t):\n    solve_final()\n\n```",
