    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_194",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. The function should take stdin as input and print the output. Simply call the function after the definition. Question:\nWe have a grid with H rows and W columns.\r\nLet (i,j) denote the square at the i-th row from the top and j-th column from the left.\r\nEach square in the grid is one of the following: the start square, the goal square, an empty square, a wall square, and a candy square.\r\n(i,j) is represented by a character A_{{i,j}}, and is the start square if A_{{i,j}}= S, the goal square if A_{{i,j}}= G, an empty square if A_{{i,j}}= ., a wall square if A_{{i,j}}= #, and a candy square if A_{{i,j}}= o.\r\nHere, it is guaranteed that there are exactly one start, exactly one goal, and at most 18 candy squares.\nTakahashi is now at the start square.\r\nHe can repeat moving to a vertically or horizontally adjacent non-wall square.\r\nHe wants to reach the goal square in at most T moves.\r\nDetermine whether it is possible.\r\nIf it is possible, find the maximum number of candy squares he can visit on the way to the goal square, where he must finish.\r\nEach candy square counts only once, even if it is visited multiple times.\n\nInput\n\nThe input is given from Standard Input in the following format:\nH W T\r\nA_{{1,1}}A_{{1,2}}\\dots A_{{1,W}}\r\n\\vdots\r\nA_{{H,1}}A_{{H,2}}\\dots A_{{H,W}}\n\nOutput\n\nIf it is impossible to reach the goal square in at most T moves, print -1.\r\nOtherwise, print the maximum number of candy squares that can be visited on the way to the goal square, where Takahashi must finish.\n\nConstraints\n\n\n- 1\\leq H,W \\leq 300\n- 1 \\leq T \\leq 2\\times 10^6\n- H, W, and T are integers.\n- A_{{i,j}} is one of S, G, ., #, and o.\n- Exactly one pair (i,j) satisfies A_{{i,j}}= S.\n- Exactly one pair (i,j) satisfies A_{{i,j}}= G.\n- At most 18 pairs (i,j) satisfy A_{{i,j}}= o.\n\nSample Input 1\n\n3 3 5\r\nS.G\r\no#o\r\n.#.\n\nSample Output 1\n\n1\r\n\nIf he makes four moves as (1,1) \\rightarrow (1,2) \\rightarrow (1,3) \\rightarrow (2,3) \\rightarrow (1,3), he can visit one candy square and finish at the goal square.\r\nHe cannot make five or fewer moves to visit two candy squares and finish at the goal square, so the answer is 1.\nNote that making five moves as (1,1) \\rightarrow (2,1) \\rightarrow (1,1) \\rightarrow (1,2) \\rightarrow (1,3) \\rightarrow (2,3) to visit two candy squares is invalid since he would not finish at the goal square.\n\nSample Input 2\n\n3 3 1\r\nS.G\r\n.#o\r\no#.\n\nSample Output 2\n\n-1\r\n\nHe cannot reach the goal square in one or fewer moves.\n\nSample Input 3\n\n5 10 2000000\r\nS.o..ooo..\r\n..o..o.o..\r\n..o..ooo..\r\n..o..o.o..\r\n..o..ooo.G\n\nSample Output 3\n\n18",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport heapq\n\ndef solve():\n    H, W, T = map(int, input().split())\n    grid = [input() for _ in range(H)]\n\n    start_row, start_col = -1, -1\n    goal_row, goal_col = -1, -1\n    candy_locations = []\n\n    for r in range(H):\n        for c in range(W):\n            if grid[r][c] == 'S':\n                start_row, start_col = r, c\n            elif grid[r][c] == 'G':\n                goal_row, goal_col = r, c\n            elif grid[r][c] == 'o':\n                candy_locations.append((r, c))\n\n    num_candies = len(candy_locations)\n    all_locations = [(start_row, start_col)] + candy_locations + [(goal_row, goal_col)]\n    num_points = len(all_locations)\n\n    # Precompute distances between all important points (S, G, candies) using BFS\n    dist_matrix = [[float('inf')] * num_points for _ in range(num_points)]\n\n    def bfs(start_r, start_c):\n        q = [(start_r, start_c, 0)]\n        visited = [[False] * W for _ in range(H)]\n        visited[start_r][start_c] = True\n        distances = {}\n\n        while q:\n            r, c, d = q.pop(0)\n            distances[(r, c)] = d\n\n            for dr, dc in [(0, 1), (0, -1), (1, 0), (-1, 0)]:\n                nr, nc = r + dr, c + dc\n                if 0 <= nr < H and 0 <= nc < W and grid[nr][nc] != '#' and not visited[nr][nc]:\n                    visited[nr][nc] = True\n                    q.append((nr, nc, d + 1))\n        return distances\n\n    point_of_interest_distances = []\n    for i, (sr, sc) in enumerate(all_locations):\n        point_of_interest_distances.append(bfs(sr, sc))\n\n    for i in range(num_points):\n        for j in range(num_points):\n            target_r, target_c = all_locations[j]\n            if (target_r, target_c) in point_of_interest_distances[i]:\n                dist_matrix[i][j] = point_of_interest_distances[i][(target_r, target_c)]\n\n    # If direct path from S to G is impossible or too long, output -1.\n    if dist_matrix[0][num_points - 1] == float('inf') or dist_matrix[0][num_points - 1] > T:\n        # Check if any candy can be collected before time runs out for S to G\n        max_candies_no_dist_cost = -1\n        if dist_matrix[0][num_points - 1] != float('inf'):\n            max_candies_no_dist_cost = 0\n\n        for k in range(1, num_points - 1): # Iterate through candies\n            dist_s_to_candy = dist_matrix[0][k]\n            dist_candy_to_g = dist_matrix[k][num_points - 1]\n            if dist_s_to_candy != float('inf') and dist_candy_to_g != float('inf'):\n                if dist_s_to_candy + dist_candy_to_g <= T:\n                    max_candies_no_dist_cost = max(max_candies_no_dist_cost, 1)\n\n        if max_candies_no_dist_cost == -1:\n            print(-1)\n            return\n\n        # If S to G path is impossible or too long, we can still potentially visit candies\n        # if the total time to visit some candies and reach G is within T.\n        # This part requires TSP-like dynamic programming.\n\n        # dp[mask][i] = minimum time to visit candies represented by 'mask' and end at candy 'i'\n        # 'mask' is a bitmask of length num_candies\n        # 'i' is the index of the candy (0 to num_candies-1), corresponding to all_locations[1] to all_locations[num_candies]\n        dp = [[float('inf')] * num_candies for _ in range(1 << num_candies)]\n\n        # Base cases: Starting from S and visiting the first candy\n        for i in range(num_candies):\n            dist_s_to_candy_i = dist_matrix[0][i + 1]\n            if dist_s_to_candy_i != float('inf'):\n                dp[1 << i][i] = dist_s_to_candy_i\n\n        # Iterate through increasing number of set bits in mask\n        for mask in range(1 << num_candies):\n            for i in range(num_candies):\n                if (mask >> i) & 1:  # If candy 'i' is in the current mask\n                    for j in range(num_candies):\n                        if not ((mask >> j) & 1): # If candy 'j' is not yet in the mask\n                            new_mask = mask | (1 << j)\n                            dist_candy_i_to_j = dist_matrix[i + 1][j + 1]\n                            if dp[mask][i] != float('inf') and dist_candy_i_to_j != float('inf'):\n                                dp[new_mask][j] = min(dp[new_mask][j], dp[mask][i] + dist_candy_i_to_j)\n\n        max_collected_candies = -1\n        if max_candies_no_dist_cost != -1:\n            max_collected_candies = max_candies_no_dist_cost\n\n        # Check paths that end at G\n        for mask in range(1 << num_candies):\n            for i in range(num_candies):\n                if dp[mask][i] != float('inf'):\n                    dist_last_candy_to_g = dist_matrix[i + 1][num_points - 1]\n                    if dp[mask][i] + dist_last_candy_to_g <= T:\n                        num_collected = bin(mask).count('1')\n                        max_collected_candies = max(max_collected_candies, num_collected)\n        print(max_collected_candies)\n        return\n\n\n    # Dijkstra to solve the TSP-like problem on candies\n    # State: (number of candies collected, last candy visited, mask of visited candies)\n    # We want to maximize the number of candies collected within time T.\n    # Since we want to *maximize* candies, and DP state usually minimizes cost,\n    # we can reframe this: find the minimum time to achieve a certain number of candies.\n    # Or, use Dijkstra where cost is negative time to explore states that *can* lead to more candies.\n\n    # Let's use Dijkstra to find minimum time to reach end state for each candy mask.\n    # State in heap: (current_time, current_node_idx, mask)\n    # current_node_idx: 0 for S, 1 to num_candies for candies, num_points-1 for G.\n    # We are interested in reaching G.\n\n    # We will use Dijkstra to find the minimum time to reach any state (candy_mask, last_candy_index)\n    # State: (mask, last_candy_bitmask_index) where last_candy_bitmask_index is the index of the last candy visited (0 to num_candies-1)\n    # dp[mask][last_candy_idx] = minimum time to achieve this state.\n    # Initial state is before visiting any candy, from S.\n    dp = [[float('inf')] * num_candies for _ in range(1 << num_candies)]\n\n    # Start from S to each candy\n    for i in range(num_candies):\n        dist_s_to_candy_i = dist_matrix[0][i + 1]\n        if dist_s_to_candy_i != float('inf'):\n            dp[1 << i][i] = dist_s_to_candy_i\n\n    # Use Dijkstra's or BFS-like approach for DP transitions\n    # We can iterate through masks by number of set bits.\n    # The number of candies is at most 18, so 2^18 is manageable.\n\n    pq = []\n    for i in range(num_candies):\n        if dp[1 << i][i] != float('inf'):\n            heapq.heappush(pq, (dp[1 << i][i], i, 1 << i)) # (time, last_candy_index, mask)\n\n    while pq:\n        current_time, last_candy_idx, mask = heapq.heappop(pq)\n\n        if current_time > dp[mask][last_candy_idx]:\n            continue\n\n        # Transition to the next candy\n        for next_candy_idx in range(num_candies):\n            if not ((mask >> next_candy_idx) & 1): # If next candy is not yet visited\n                dist_between_candies = dist_matrix[last_candy_idx + 1][next_candy_idx + 1]\n                if dist_between_candies != float('inf'):\n                    new_time = current_time + dist_between_candies\n                    new_mask = mask | (1 << next_candy_idx)\n                    if new_time < dp[new_mask][next_candy_idx]:\n                        dp[new_mask][next_candy_idx] = new_time\n                        heapq.heappush(pq, (new_time, next_candy_idx, new_mask))\n\n    max_collected = -1\n\n    # Check paths that reach G after visiting candies\n    for mask in range(1 << num_candies):\n        for last_candy_idx in range(num_candies):\n            if dp[mask][last_candy_idx] != float('inf'):\n                dist_last_candy_to_g = dist_matrix[last_candy_idx + 1][num_points - 1]\n                if dist_last_candy_to_g != float('inf'):\n                    total_time = dp[mask][last_candy_idx] + dist_last_candy_to_g\n                    if total_time <= T:\n                        num_collected = bin(mask).count('1')\n                        max_collected = max(max_collected, num_collected)\n\n    # Consider the case where no candies are collected but S can reach G within T.\n    dist_s_to_g = dist_matrix[0][num_points - 1]\n    if dist_s_to_g != float('inf') and dist_s_to_g <= T:\n        max_collected = max(max_collected, 0)\n\n\n    print(max_collected)\n\nsolve()\n```",
