`odd_counts % 2 == 1`, we have one character type left with an odd frequency. This character type can be the center of one palindrome.\n    #\n    # So, the total number of palindromes we can form from the characters is `odd_counts // 2` + `odd_counts % 2`.\n    # This simplifies to `odd_counts`.\n    #\n    # This is still leading to `odd_counts`. The key is how many `odd_counts` we can actually use.\n    #\n    # A palindrome can have at most one character type with an odd count.\n    # If we have `odd_counts` character types with odd frequencies.\n    # We can form `odd_counts // 2` pairs. These pairs allow us to form palindromes where all character counts are even.\n    # We are left with `odd_counts % 2` characters that *must* be centers of palindromes.\n    #\n    # The number of palindromes we can form is `odd_counts // 2` (from balanced character sets) plus `odd_counts % 2` (if there's a single center).\n    # This is `odd_counts`.\n    #\n    # The number of palindromes is limited by the number of words.\n    #\n    # The correct logic seems to be:\n    # If `odd_counts` is the number of character types with odd frequencies.\n    # We can form `odd_counts // 2` pairs. These pairs can be used to construct palindromes.\n    # We are left with `odd_counts % 2` characters that must be centers.\n    # If `odd_counts % 2 == 1`, we can form one final palindrome with a center.\n    #\n    # The number of palindromes is `odd_counts // 2` (from balanced pairs) + `odd_counts % 2` (if there's a center).\n    # This is `odd_counts`.\n    #\n    # The number of palindromes is limited by the number of words.\n    #\n    # The crucial point is how many of these odd characters can coexist in palindromes.\n    # Each palindrome can have at most one character type with an odd count.\n    # If we have `k` character types with odd frequencies.\n    # We can pair up `k // 2` of them. These pairs can be used to form palindromes with all even counts.\n    # We are left with `k % 2