    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef minOperations(nums):\n    \"\"\"\n    Given a 0-indexed permutation of n integers nums.\n    A permutation is called semi-ordered if the first number equals 1 and the last number equals n.\n    You can perform the below operation as many times as you want until you make nums a semi-ordered permutation:\n\n    Pick two adjacent elements in nums, then swap them.\n\n    Return the minimum number of operations to make nums a semi-ordered permutation.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(nums)\n    \n    # Find the index of 1 and n\n    index_of_1 = nums.index(1)\n    index_of_n = nums.index(n)\n    \n    # The number of operations to move 1 to the first position\n    # is its current index because each swap with an adjacent element\n    # moves it one step to the left.\n    operations_for_1 = index_of_1\n    \n    # The number of operations to move n to the last position\n    # is n - 1 - its current index because each swap with an adjacent element\n    # moves it one step to the right.\n    operations_for_n = (n - 1) - index_of_n\n    \n    # If 1 is to the right of n, moving n to the end will displace 1 one step\n    # further from its target position. So we subtract 1 from the total\n    # operations_for_1 because one swap for n will also advance 1 one position\n    # towards its target.\n    if index_of_1 > index_of_n:\n        return operations_for_1 + operations_for_n - 1\n    else:\n        return operations_for_1 + operations_for_n\n\n```",
