      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef solve():\n    N = int(input())\n    cards = []\n    for i in range(N):\n        A, C = map(int, input().split())\n        cards.append((A, C, i + 1))  # Store A, C, and original index\n\n    # Sort cards by strength A in descending order\n    # If A is the same, sort by cost C in ascending order (though constraints say A_i are distinct)\n    cards.sort(key=lambda x: (-x[0], x[1]))\n\n    remaining_cards = []\n    min_cost_so_far = float('inf')\n\n    for card in cards:\n        strength, cost, original_idx = card\n        # A card y is discarded if there exists an x such that A_x > A_y and C_x < C_y.\n        # Since we sorted by A in descending order, for any card currently being considered (y),\n        # all previously processed cards (x) have A_x > A_y.\n        # So we only need to check the minimum cost seen among the previously processed cards.\n        # If the current card's cost (C_y) is greater than the minimum cost seen so far (min_cost_so_far = C_x),\n        # then this card (y) can be discarded.\n        # Otherwise, this card (y) remains, and it might become an 'x' for future cards,\n        # so we update min_cost_so_far if its cost is lower.\n\n        if cost < min_cost_so_far:\n            remaining_cards.append(original_idx)\n            min_cost_so_far = cost\n\n    remaining_cards.sort()\n\n    print(len(remaining_cards))\n    print(*remaining_cards)\n\nsolve()\n```",
