    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport sys\n\nsys.setrecursionlimit(2000)\n\ndef solve():\n    N, H, W = map(int, sys.stdin.readline().split())\n    tiles = []\n    for _ in range(N):\n        tiles.append(list(map(int, sys.stdin.readline().split())))\n\n    grid_area = H * W\n    tile_areas = []\n    for a, b in tiles:\n        tile_areas.append(a * b)\n\n    def backtrack(current_grid, remaining_tiles_mask):\n        # Find the first empty cell\n        empty_cell = None\n        for r in range(H):\n            for c in range(W):\n                if current_grid[r][c] == 0:\n                    empty_cell = (r, c)\n                    break\n            if empty_cell:\n                break\n\n        # If no empty cell, the grid is filled\n        if not empty_cell:\n            return True\n\n        r, c = empty_cell\n\n        # Try placing each remaining tile\n        for i in range(N):\n            if (remaining_tiles_mask >> i) & 1:\n                a, b = tiles[i]\n                orientations = [(a, b)]\n                if a != b:\n                    orientations.append((b, a))\n\n                for tile_h, tile_w in orientations:\n                    # Check if the tile fits at (r, c)\n                    if r + tile_h <= H and c + tile_w <= W:\n                        can_place = True\n                        for dr in range(tile_h):\n                            for dc in range(tile_w):\n                                if current_grid[r + dr][c + dc] == 1:\n                                    can_place = False\n                                    break\n                            if not can_place:\n                                break\n\n                        if can_place:\n                            # Place the tile\n                            new_grid = [row[:] for row in current_grid]\n                            for dr in range(tile_h):\n                                for dc in range(tile_w):\n                                    new_grid[r + dr][c + dc] = 1\n\n                            # Recurse with the new grid and one less tile\n                            if backtrack(new_grid, remaining_tiles_mask ^ (1 << i)):\n                                return True\n        return False\n\n    # Initial empty grid\n    initial_grid = [[0 for _ in range(W)] for _ in range(H)]\n\n    # If there are enough tiles such that their total area is at least grid_area\n    # This is a necessary but not sufficient condition if we only try to cover\n    # But here we try to cover exactly\n    \n    # Since N is small (<= 7), we can iterate through all subsets of tiles\n    # and check if any subset can perfectly tile the grid.\n    \n    for i in range(1 << N): # Iterate through all possible subsets of tiles\n        current_subset_area = 0\n        current_subset_tiles = []\n        subset_tile_indices = []\n        \n        for j in range(N):\n            if (i >> j) & 1:\n                current_subset_area += tile_areas[j]\n                current_subset_tiles.append(tiles[j])\n                subset_tile_indices.append(j)\n        \n        if current_subset_area == grid_area:\n            # Try to tile the grid with this exact subset of tiles\n            \n            # We need a way to know WHICH tiles from the original list are in the subset\n            # We can pass the bitmask of used tiles to the backtrack function.\n            \n            # `remaining_tiles_mask` will represent the available tiles from the N input tiles\n            # initially, it's the mask `i` for the current subset\n            \n            if backtrack(initial_grid, i): # Start backtrack with the mask of the current subset\n                print(\"Yes\")\n                return\n\n    print(\"No\")\n\n\ndef main():\n    solve()\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n```",
