    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport sys\n\nsys.setrecursionlimit(10**6)\n\ndef solve():\n    N, T, M = map(int, sys.stdin.readline().split())\n    incompatible_pairs = []\n    for _ in range(M):\n        u, v = map(int, sys.stdin.readline().split())\n        incompatible_pairs.append((u - 1, v - 1)) # Adjust to 0-indexed\n\n    # Memoization table\n    memo = {}\n\n    def is_valid_team(team):\n        for u, v in incompatible_pairs:\n            if u in team and v in team:\n                return False\n        return True\n\n    def count_ways_recursive(player_idx, current_teams):\n        # Base case: All players are assigned\n        if player_idx == N:\n            # Check if all T teams have at least one player\n            if len(current_teams) == T and all(len(team) > 0 for team in current_teams):\n                return 1\n            return 0\n\n        # Memoization key: tuple of frozensets (for canonical representation of teams)\n        # Sort the frozensets to handle team order invariance\n        key = (player_idx, tuple(sorted(frozenset(team) for team in current_teams)))\n        if key in memo:\n            return memo[key]\n\n        ways = 0\n\n        # Option 1: Assign to an existing team\n        for i in range(len(current_teams)):\n            temp_team = current_teams[i] + (player_idx,)\n            if is_valid_team(temp_team):\n                # Create a new list for the next recursive call to avoid modifying current_teams directly\n                next_teams = list(current_teams)\n                next_teams[i] = temp_team\n                ways += count_ways_recursive(player_idx + 1, tuple(next_teams))\n\n        # Option 2: Assign to a new team if we haven't reached T teams yet\n        if len(current_teams) < T:\n            new_team = (player_idx,)\n            # Create a new list and append the new team\n            next_teams = list(current_teams)\n            next_teams.append(new_team)\n            ways += count_ways_recursive(player_idx + 1, tuple(next_teams))\n\n        memo[key] = ways\n        return ways\n\n    ans = count_ways_recursive(0, ())\n\n    # The problem statement says \"two divisions are considered different when there are two players\n    # who belong to the same team in one division and different teams in the other.\"\n    # This implies that the order of teams does not matter.\n    # Our recursive function implicitly treats teams as ordered when adding them to current_teams.\n    # We need to divide by T! to account for the permutations of identical team configurations.\n\n    # Calculate T!\n    import math\n    factorial_T = math.factorial(T)\n\n    print(ans // factorial_T)\n\nsolve()\n```",
