    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef solve(nums: list[int], m: int) -> bool:\n    n = len(nums)\n\n    # dp[i][j] will be True if the subarray nums[i:j+1] can be split into\n    # (j - i + 1) individual elements, respecting the splitting rules.\n    dp = [[False] * n for _ in range(n)]\n\n    # Base case: Any single-element subarray can be considered \"split\" into itself.\n    for i in range(n):\n        dp[i][i] = True\n\n    # Fill the DP table for increasing lengths of subarrays\n    for length in range(2, n + 1):\n        for i in range(n - length + 1):\n            j = i + length - 1  # End index of the current subarray nums[i:j+1]\n\n            # Try all possible split points k\n            for k in range(i, j):  # Splitting into nums[i:k+1] and nums[k+1:j+1]\n                left_sub_len = k - i + 1\n                right_sub_len = j - (k + 1) + 1\n\n                left_sum = sum(nums[i : k + 1])\n                right_sum = sum(nums[k + 1 : j + 1])\n\n                # Check conditions for the left subarray\n                left_valid = (left_sub_len == 1) or (left_sum >= m)\n                # Check conditions for the right subarray\n                right_valid = (right_sub_len == 1) or (right_sum >= m)\n\n                # If both splits are valid and both resulting subarrays\n                # can eventually be split into single elements, then\n                # the current subarray nums[i:j+1] can also be fully split.\n                if left_valid and right_valid:\n                    if dp[i][k] and dp[k + 1][j]:\n                        dp[i][j] = True\n                        break  # Found a valid split, no need to check other k's for this (i, j)\n\n    return dp[0][n - 1]\n```",
