    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport sys\n\ndef solve():\n    N, M, L = map(int, sys.stdin.readline().split())\n    a = list(map(int, sys.stdin.readline().split()))\n    b = list(map(int, sys.stdin.readline().split()))\n    \n    unsuitable = set()\n    for _ in range(L):\n        c, d = map(int, sys.stdin.readline().split())\n        unsuitable.add((c - 1, d - 1)) # 0-indexed\n\n    max_price = 0\n\n    # Sort main dishes in descending order of price, keeping track of original index\n    sorted_a = sorted([(a[i], i) for i in range(N)], key=lambda x: x[0], reverse=True)\n    # Sort side dishes in descending order of price, keeping track of original index\n    sorted_b = sorted([(b[i], i) for i in range(M)], key=lambda x: x[0], reverse=True)\n\n    # We only need to check a limited number of the most expensive dishes\n    # A set meal's price is the sum of main dish and side dish.\n    # The most expensive set meal will likely involve one of the most expensive main dishes\n    # and one of the most expensive side dishes.\n    # The maximum number of combinations we might need to check is bounded by L+1 (if all combinations with the most expensive main/side are unsuitable)\n    # or some small constant, as N and M can be up to 10^5\n    \n    # Iterate through the most expensive main dishes\n    for i in range(min(N, L + 1)): # Try checking up to L+1 most expensive main dishes\n        main_dish_price, main_dish_idx = sorted_a[i]\n        \n        # For each main dish, iterate through the most expensive side dishes\n        for j in range(min(M, L + 1)): # Try checking up to L+1 most expensive side dishes\n            side_dish_price, side_dish_idx = sorted_b[j]\n            \n            if (main_dish_idx, side_dish_idx) not in unsuitable:\n                max_price = max(max_price, main_dish_price + side_dish_price)\n            \n    print(max_price)\n\nsolve()\n```",
