t 'd' (idx 2).\n    # Remove these. Original string without 0 and 2. String becomes \"daaa\".\n    \n    # Now string is \"daaa\".\n    # 'd': [0]\n    # 'a': [1, 2, 3] (indices relative to \"daaa\")\n    \n    # Choose 'a' at index 2 (relative to \"daaa\", this is the 'a' originally at index 4).\n    # Closest left 'a' (idx 1), closest right 'a' (idx 3).\n    # Remove these. String becomes \"da\".\n    \n    # Now string is \"da\".\n    # 'd': [0]\n    # 'a': [1]\n    \n    # No more duplicate characters. Minimized length is 2.\n    \n    # This process always reduces the count of a character by 2 (if both sides exist),\n    # or by 1 (if only one side exists).\n    # The original character 'c' at index 'i' is NOT removed.\n    # So if we have 'k' occurrences of a character:\n    # - If k is 1, it cannot be removed.\n    # - If k is 2, say \"xyx\". Pick 'y'. Both 'x's are removed. String becomes \"y\".\n    #   No, this is wrong. \"xyx\". Choose 'x' at index 0. No left 'x'. Right 'x' at index 2 is removed. String becomes \"xy\".\n    #   Choose 'x' at index 2. Left 'x' at index 0 is removed. String becomes \"yx\".\n    #   Choose 'y' at index 1. No 'y' to left. No 'y' to right. No change.\n    \n    # Let's re-evaluate \"Delete the closest occurrence of c to the left of i (if any) and the closest occurrence of c to the right of i (if any).\"\n    # If s = \"aaabc\"\n    # Choose 'a' at i=1. Left 'a' is at 0. Right 'a' is at 2.\n    # We remove s[0] and s[2]. The characters are deleted.\n    # The string becomes \"abc\". The 'a' that *was* at index 1 is inherently removed\n    # because the *string itself* changes, and its original index 1 is now\n    # filled by the character 'b' (which was at index 3). This effectively means\n    # the character at index 'i' in the *original* string also disappears from its position,\n    # as the string collapses.\n\n    # This seems like a problem where ultimately, the only characters that can remain\n    # are unique characters