   # Any string reachable from `s` is a cyclic shift of `s`.\n    # Let `x` be a reachable string, `x = rotate(s, p)`.\n    # Number of `l` such that `rotate(x, l) == t` is 1 if `(m-p) mod n != 0` and 0 if `(m-p) mod n == 0`.\n    #\n    # Let `N_match_t_from_any_different_state`.\n    # If `s == t`:\n    #   From `s` (p=0), `l=(0-0) mod n = 0`, so 0 ops.\n    #   From `x != s` (p!=0), `l=(0-p) mod n != 0`, so 1 op.\n    #   Number of strings `x != s` is `count_distinct_cyclic_shifts - 1`.\n    #   Let num_distinct_shifts be `D`. If `s` has no repetition, `D=n`.\n    #   If `s == t`, `c = 1` (ops from s to t -> `l=2` for \"abcd\").\n    #   Number of operations on states `!= t` to get to `t`.\n    #\n    # Let `N_ways_to_t_from_t_state` = `count`\n    # Let `N_ways_to_t_from_not_t_state` = `count`\n    #\n    # This assumes that the number of operations that yield `t` is the SAME for ANY starting string. This is only true if all strings are identical or if `t` is identical to `s`.\n\n    # If `t` is indeed a cyclic shift of `s`, then all reachable strings from `s` are cyclic shifts of `s`.\n    # Let `m` be the shift of `t` w.r.t `s`.\n    # Let `x` be a reachable string, `x = rotate(s, p)`.\n    # Number of ops from `x` to `t` is 1 if `(m-p) mod n != 0`, and 0 if `(m-p) mod n == 0`.\n    #\n    # Let `k` be the number of operations.\n    # Consider the total number of operations that contribute to the target `t`.\n    #\n    # Two types of operations: type A lands on `t`, type B lands elsewhere.\n    #\n    # If `s == t` initially:\n    #  Number of ways to end up at `t`.\n    #  We can have `i` steps not landing on `t`, and `k-i` steps landing on `t`.\n    #  Let `S_i` be the number of ways to perform `i` operations such that the net shift is not zero.\n    #  Let `Z_i` be the number of ways to perform `i` operations such that the net shift IS zero.\n    #\n    #  If `k` is the total operations.\n    #  If `s == t`, we need the final shift to be 0.\n    #  If s !=