be implemented.\n    #\n    # The term `pow(-1, k, MOD)` is `1` if `k` is even, `MOD - 1` if `k` is odd.\n    # `modInverse(n, MOD)` is `pow(n, MOD-2, MOD)` if `MOD` is prime.\n    # `MOD = 10^9 + 7` is prime.\n\n    # Need to correctly calculate `m`: the shift of `t` w.r.t `s`.\n    # `t` is obtained from `s` by `s[n-l:] + s[:n-l]`.\n    # The shift amount is `l`.\n    # E.g., s=\"abcd\", t=\"cdab\". `t` is `s` shifted by 2. So `m=2`.\n    # `double_s.find(t)` gives the minimum shift.\n\n    MOD = 10**9 + 7\n\n    def power(base, exp):\n        res = 1\n        base %= MOD\n        while exp > 0:\n            if exp % 2 == 1:\n                res = (res * base) % MOD\n            base = (base * base) % MOD\n            exp //= 2\n        return res\n\n    def modInverse(n_val):\n        return power(n_val, MOD - 2)\n\n    n = len(s)\n\n    # Find m, the shift of t relative to s.\n    # t = s[m:] + s[:m]\n    double_s = s + s\n    m = double_s.find(t) # This finds the smallest shift.\n\n    # If s == t, then m=0.\n    # If t is not a cyclic shift of s, this find will not work or will return -1. But problem says t is achievable.\n\n    # Calculate `n_minus_1_pow_k = power(n - 1, k)`\n    n_minus_1 = n - 1\n    if n_minus_1 == 0: # n=1, unlikely given constraints n>=2\n        n_minus_1_pow_k = 0 if k > 0 else 1\n    else:\n        n_minus_1_pow_k = power(n_minus_1, k)\n\n    # Calculate `minus_1_pow_k`\n    minus_1_pow_k = 1 if k % 2 == 0 else (MOD - 1)\n\n    # Calculate `count_term` which depends on `m` and `k`.\n    # This is the number of ways to have final shift `m` in `k` steps, each step being a shift `l` in `[1, n-1]`.\n\n    # Let `C(n, k, m)` be this count.\n    # The formula for `C(n, k, m)` is `(1/n) * sum_{j=0 to n-1} (-omega_j^m) * (P(omega_j))^k`.\n    # `P(omega_j)` is `n-1` if `j=0`, and `-1` if `j > 0`.\n    # `omega_j = exp(2*pi*i*j/n)`.\n    #\n    # `C(n, k, m) = (1/n) * [ (n-1)^k + sum_{j=1 to n-1} (-omega_j^m) * (-1)^k ]`\n    # `C(n, k, m) = (