         `dp[7][2] = (dp[7][2] + dp[3][1]) % MOD = (0 + 0) % MOD = 0`. (`dp[3][1]` was 0)\n\n**Final answer**:\n`total = dp[7][0] + dp[7][1] + dp[7][2] = 1 + 1 + 0 = 2`. (Correct for `[2,3,6]`)\n\nThe logic seems solid. The memoization table stores intermediate counts correctly.\n\n```python\nimport collections\n\ndef specialPermutations(nums: list[int]) -> int:\n    n = len(nums)\n    MOD = 10**9 + 7\n\n    # dp[mask][last_idx] stores the number of special permutations\n    # using elements represented by 'mask', ending with nums[last_idx].\n    # The mask is an integer where the i-th bit is set if nums[i] is included.\n    dp = [[0] * n for _ in range(1 << n)]\n\n    # Base cases: A single element forms a special permutation of length 1.\n    for i in range(n):\n        dp[1 << i][i] = 1\n\n    # Iterate through all possible masks (from masks with 2 elements up to all elements)\n    # The number of elements in a mask can be found by counting set bits.\n    # The smallest mask for two elements will be 3 (011 binary).\n    # We can iterate from 1 to (1 << n) - 1, and the inner logic will handle it correctly.\n    for mask in range(1, 1 << n):\n        # Iterate through all possible last elements for the current mask\n        for i in range(n):\n            # If nums[i] is not part of the current mask, skip it.\n            if not (mask & (1 << i)):\n                continue\n\n            # If this is a base case (mask represents only one element, nums[i]),\n            # its dp value is already set to 1. We don't need to build upon it from smaller masks.\n            if mask == (1 << i):\n                continue\n\n            # Calculate the previous mask by removing nums[i]\n            prev_mask = mask ^ (1 << i)\n\n            # Iterate through all possible previous elements (nums[j])\n            for j in range(n):\n                # If nums[j] was part of the previous mask\n                if prev_mask & (1 << j):\n                    # Check the divisibility condition\n                    if nums[i] % nums[j] == 0 or nums[j] % nums[i] == 0:\n                        # Add the number of permutations ending with nums[j] using prev_mask\n                        # to the current count for dp[mask][i]\n                        dp[mask][i] = (dp[mask][i] + dp[prev_mask][j]) % MOD\n\n    # Sum up all special permutations that use all n elements\n    total_special_permutations = 0\n    full_mask = (1 << n) - 1 # Mask where all bits are set\n    for i in range(n):\n        total_special_permutations = (total_special_permutations + dp[full_mask][i]) % MOD\n\n    return total_special_permutations\n\n```",
