    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\nfrom collections import Counter\n\ndef minOperations(nums: list[int]) -> int:\n    counts = Counter(nums)\n    operations = 0\n\n    for count in counts.values():\n        if count == 1:\n            return -1\n        \n        # We need to find the minimum number of operations to remove 'count' items.\n        # We prefer to use 3-element operations as much as possible to reduce the total number of operations.\n        # This is because 3-element operations remove more elements per operation than 2-element operations.\n\n        # If count % 3 == 0, we can use only 3-element operations.\n        # For example, if count = 6, we use 6 // 3 = 2 operations.\n        # If count = 3, we use 3 // 3 = 1 operation.\n        operations += (count + 2) // 3\n        # This formula (count + 2) // 3 works for all cases:\n        # If count % 3 == 0: (e.g., count = 6) (6 + 2) // 3 = 8 // 3 = 2. Correct.\n        # If count % 3 == 1: (e.g., count = 4) (4 + 2) // 3 = 6 // 3 = 2.\n        #    This is equivalent to one 3-element op and one 2-element op (3 + 1 -> 3 + (2,2) - impossible)\n        #    Better: take one 2-element op, remaining 2. Take another 2-element op.\n        #    If count = 4, remove 2, then remove 2. (2 operations)\n        #    (4+2)//3 = 2. Correct.\n        #    If count = 7, remove 3, remove 2, remove 2. (3 operations)\n        #    (7+2)//3 = 3. Correct.\n        # If count % 3 == 2: (e.g., count = 5) (5 + 2) // 3 = 7 // 3 = 2.\n        #    This is equivalent to one 3-element op and one 2-element op (2 operations)\n        #    (5+2)//3 = 2. Correct.\n\n    return operations\n\n```",
