    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_281",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. The function should take stdin as input and print the output. Simply call the function after the definition. Question:\nThis problem is an easier version of Problem G.\n\nThere is a slot machine with three reels.\r\nThe arrangement of symbols on the i-th reel is represented by the string S_i. Here, S_i is a string of length M consisting of digits.\nEach reel has a corresponding button. For each non-negative integer t, Takahashi can either choose and press one button or do nothing exactly t seconds after the reels start spinning.\r\nIf he presses the button corresponding to the i-th reel exactly t seconds after the reels start spinning, the i-th reel will stop and display the ((t \\bmod M)+1)-th character of S_i.\r\nHere, t \\bmod M denotes the remainder when t is divided by M.\nTakahashi wants to stop all the reels so that all the displayed characters are the same.\r\nFind the minimum possible number of seconds from the start of the spin until all the reels are stopped so that his goal is achieved.\r\nIf this is impossible, report that fact.\n\nInput\n\nThe input is given from Standard Input in the following format:\nM\r\nS_1\r\nS_2\r\nS_3\n\nOutput\n\nIf it is impossible to stop all the reels so that all the displayed characters are the same, print -1.\r\nOtherwise, print the minimum possible number of seconds from the start of the spin until such a state is achieved.\n\nConstraints\n\n\n- 1 \\leq M \\leq 100\n- M is an integer.\n- S_i is a string of length M consisting of digits.\n\nSample Input 1\n\n10\r\n1937458062\r\n8124690357\r\n2385760149\n\nSample Output 1\n\n6\r\n\nTakahashi can stop each reel as follows so that 6 seconds after the reels start spinning, all the reels display 8.\n\n- Press the button corresponding to the second reel 0 seconds after the reels start spinning. The second reel stops and displays 8, the ((0 \\bmod 10)+1=1)-st character of S_2.\n- Press the button corresponding to the third reel 2 seconds after the reels start spinning. The third reel stops and displays 8, the ((2 \\bmod 10)+1=3)-rd character of S_3.\n- Press the button corresponding to the first reel 6 seconds after the reels start spinning. The first reel stops and displays 8, the ((6 \\bmod 10)+1=7)-th character of S_1.\n\nThere is no way to make the reels display the same character in 5 or fewer seconds, so print 6.\n\nSample Input 2\n\n20\r\n01234567890123456789\r\n01234567890123456789\r\n01234567890123456789\n\nSample Output 2\n\n20\r\n\nNote that he must stop all the reels and make them display the same character.\n\nSample Input 3\n\n5\r\n11111\r\n22222\r\n33333\n\nSample Output 3\n\n-1\r\n\nIt is impossible to stop the reels so that all the displayed characters are the same.\r\nIn this case, print -1.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef solve():\n    M = int(input())\n    S = [input() for _ in range(3)]\n\n    min_time = float('inf')\n\n    # Iterate through all possible digits (0-9) that could be displayed\n    for target_digit in \"0123456789\":\n        # Find all possible times 't' for each reel to display the target_digit\n        times_per_reel = []\n        for i in range(3):\n            reel_times = []\n            for t_val in range(2 * M): # Search up to 2M to cover all possibilities for a full cycle for M=100, 200 checks\n                if S[i][t_val % M] == target_digit:\n                    reel_times.append(t_val)\n            times_per_reel.append(reel_times)\n        \n        # If any reel cannot display the target_digit, skip this digit\n        if any(not reel_times for reel_times in times_per_reel):\n            continue\n\n        # Try all combinations of times for each reel\n        # We need to pick one time for each reel.\n        # This is a bit tricky: we need to pick t1, t2, t3 such that:\n        # 1. t1, t2, t3 are distinct\n        # 2. Each reel displays the target_digit at its chosen time\n        # 3. max(t1, t2, t3) is minimized\n\n        # The approach below iterates through possible final display times\n\n        for t1 in times_per_reel[0]:\n            for t2 in times_per_reel[1]:\n                for t3 in times_per_reel[2]:\n                    # To ensure distinct times are *initially* chosen for stopping\n                    # but we are looking for the maximum of the chosen times\n                    # and the *actual* times must be distinct for pressing the buttons.\n                    \n                    # Store distinct times to press buttons\n                    button_press_times = sorted([t1, t2, t3])\n                    \n                    # Check for distinctness of the button press times\n                    if button_press_times[0] != button_press_times[1] and \\\n                       button_press_times[1] != button_press_times[2]:\n                        \n                        current_max_time = button_press_times[2]\n                        min_time = min(min_time, current_max_time)\n                    \n                    # Handle cases where some button press times are the same\n                    # If two reels are stopped at the same time T, the reels would be 0, 1, 2.\n                    # The first press is at time T, the second at time T+1, the third at time T+2...\n                    # So we need to re-evaluate the actual stopping times if there are collisions.\n                    \n                    # To resolve collisions, we can consider the time resolution of buttons.\n                    # If reels stop at T1, T2, T3 (possibly not unique for now)\n                    # And these are the *earliest times* they can show the target character at that position.\n                    # We want to find a set of times (t_a, t_b, t_c) where each t_x is a valid time\n                    # for reel x to show the character, and t_a, t_b, t_c are distinct.\n                    # The maximum of these chosen distinct times is what we want to minimize.\n\n                    # Let's consider the scenario from the sample explanation:\n                    # R2 at 0s, R3 at 2s, R1 at 6s. Max is 6. All distinct.\n                    \n                    # Another way to think about it: for a given target digit,\n                    # what are the stopping times for each reel?\n                    # Reel 0: t_0_k values\n                    # Reel 1: t_1_k values\n                    # Reel 2: t_2_k values\n                    \n                    # We need to pick one t from each set, say T0, T1, T2.\n                    # All T0, T1, T2 must be distinct. The maximum of these is the total time.\n                    # We can iterate through all combinations of (t0, t1, t2)\n                    # and if they are distinct, update the min_time.\n                    \n                    # The previous nested loop already does this.\n                    # However, if t1=t2, they are not distinct. The sample explains\n                    # \"Press the button corresponding to the second reel 0 seconds after the reels start spinning.\"\n                    # \"Press the button corresponding to the third reel 2 seconds after the reels start spinning.\"\n                    # \"Press the button corresponding to the first reel 6 seconds after the reels start spinning.\"\n                    # These are the *actual* button press times, which must be distinct.\n                    # The question is how to get those distinct times if multiple candidate reels\n                    # can show the character at the same minimal time.\n                    \n                    # Let's refine the logic for resolving collisions.\n                    \n                    # For a given target digit, collect all (time, reel_index) pairs\n                    all_stop_events = []\n                    for r_idx in range(3):\n                        for t_val_for_reel in times_per_reel[r_idx]:\n                            all_stop_events.append((t_val_for_reel, r_idx))\n                    \n                    # Sort by time\n                    all_stop_events.sort()\n                    \n                    # This problem is a bit like finding 3 distinct times to stop 3 different reels\n                    # such that the number displayed at that time (t % M) is the target digit.\n                    # And we want to minimize the maximum of these 3 distinct times.\n                    \n                    # Example: target_digit = '8'\n                    # Reel 0: 6, 16, 26, ...\n                    # Reel 1: 0, 10, 20, ...\n                    # Reel 2: 2, 12, 22, ...\n                    \n                    # We can pick (6, 0, 2). Max is 6. All distinct.\n                    # This is what the nested loops achieve.\n\n    # If min_time is still infinity, no solution found\n    if min_time == float('inf'):\n        print(-1)\n    else:\n        print(min_time)\n\nsolve()\n```",
