      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef find_minimum_cost(nums: list[int], x: int) -> int:\n    n = len(nums)\n    min_cost = float('inf')\n\n    # Iterate through all possible numbers of operations (shifts)\n    for shift in range(n):\n        current_total_cost = shift * x  # Cost incurred from performing operations\n        \n        # Calculate the minimum cost for each chocolate type after 'shift' operations\n        # For each chocolate type (original index i), find the minimum cost to acquire it\n        # from the current shifted array or from chocolates that were originally different types\n        # but are now of type i.\n        \n        # Create a temporary array to store the current minimum cost for each type\n        # after 'shift' operations.\n        # min_cost_for_type[j] will store the minimum cost to acquire a chocolate of type j\n        # considering the original costs and the current shift.\n        min_cost_for_type = [float('inf')] * n\n\n        for i in range(n):\n            for j in range(n):\n                # The current chocolate at original index `j` with cost `nums[j]`\n                # will become type `(j + shift) % n` after `shift` operations.\n                # However, this problem is about collecting ALL types from 0 to n-1.\n                # We need to find the minimum cost to acquire chocolate of type `i` (0 to n-1).\n\n                # If we consider the chocolate originally at index `k`,\n                # after `shift` operations, it will be considered of type `(k + shift) % n`.\n                # To get a chocolate of type `i`, we need to find what original chocolate `k`\n                # will become `i` after `shift` operations.\n                # (k + shift) % n = i  => k = (i - shift + n) % n\n                \n                # So, the cost of getting type `i` from its original position `(i - shift + n) % n`\n                # is `nums[(i - shift + n) % n]`.\n                # We need to find the minimum cost for each type `j` across all possible original `k`\n                # that could become type `j` after some shifts implicitly.\n\n                # Let's reframe: For each type `j`, what is the cheapest way to get it?\n                # It could be `nums[j]` (the original cost of type `j`).\n                # Or, if we shift `s` times, the chocolate at `(j - s + n) % n` will become type `j`.\n                # Its cost would be `nums[(j - s + n) % n]`.\n                # The minimum cost to acquire type `j` is the minimum of `nums[(j - k + n) % n]`\n                # for `k` from `0` to `shift`.\n\n                # Let's track the minimum cost for each type after `shift` operations.\n                # For a fixed 'shift', for each chocolate type 'i' (0 to n-1),\n                # we want to find the minimum cost of a chocolate that *currently* represents type 'i'.\n                # A chocolate that was originally of type `(i - k + n) % n` for `0 <= k <= shift`\n                # is now considered of type `i`.\n                # The cost of this chocolate is `nums[(i - k + n) % n]`.\n                # So, for each type `i`, the actual cost to pick up type `i` is the minimum\n                # of `nums[(i - k + n) % n]` for `k` from `0` to `shift`.\n                \n                # Let current_min_costs[j] be the minimum cost to acquire chocolate of type j\n                # so far for THIS specific shift.\n                # Initialize current_min_costs with nums.\n                \n                # After 0 shifts: min_costs_for_types[j] = nums[j]\n                # After 1 shift: min_costs_for_types[j] = min(nums[j], nums[(j - 1 + n) % n])\n                # After `shift` shifts: min_costs_for_types[j] = min(nums[j], nums[(j - 1 + n) % n], ..., nums[(j - shift + n) % n])\n\n                # To optimize:\n                # We are calculating sum(min_cost_to_acquire_type_j_after_K_shifts for j in 0 to n-1) + K * x\n                # Let `costs_after_k_shifts[j]` be the cost of the chocolate at original index `j`\n                # after `k` shifts. This is irrelevant.\n                \n                # What we want is `min_price_for_type[j]` which is the minimum cost to get \n                # a chocolate of type `j` if we have performed `shift` operations.\n                # This `min_price_for_type[j]` would be the minimum of `nums[(j - k + n) % n]` for `k` from `0` to `shift`.\n\n                # For each `j` from `0` to `n-1`:\n                #   We are considering `shift` operations.\n                #   The chocolate originally at `(j - 0 + n) % n` has cost `nums[(j - 0 + n) % n]`.\n                #   The chocolate originally at `(j - 1 + n) % n` has cost `nums[(j - 1 + n) % n]`.\n                #   ...\n                #   The chocolate originally at `(j - shift + n) % n` has cost `nums[(j - shift + n) % n]`.\n                # So, `min_cost_for_type[j]` for this specific `shift` would be:\n                #     `min(nums[(j - k + n) % n] for k in range(shift + 1))`\n                \n                # A more efficient way to compute this:\n                # Let `current_min_costs[j]` be the minimum cost to acquire type `j`\n                # up to the current shift `k`.\n                # Initialize `current_min_costs = list(nums)` (for k=0)\n                # For `k` from `1` to `n-1` (the shifts):\n                #   `total_cost_for_this_k = k * x`\n                #   For `j` from `0` to `n-1`:\n                #     `current_min_costs[j] = min(current_min_costs[j], nums[(j - k + n) % n])`\n                #   `total_cost_for_this_k += sum(current_min_costs)`\n                #   `min_cost = min(min_cost, total_cost_for_this_k)`\n\n        # Let's try the optimized approach:\n        current_min_prices_for_types = list(nums) # Represents min cost for each type with 0 shifts\n        \n        # Calculate initial total cost (0 shifts)\n        min_cost = sum(current_min_prices_for_types)\n\n        # Iterate through shifts from 1 to n-1\n        for k in range(1, n):\n            # After k shifts, the cost incurred from operations is k * x\n            cost_from_operations = k * x\n            \n            # Update the minimum price for each type\n            # For each type `j` (from 0 to n-1):\n            # The chocolate at original index `(j - k + n) % n` has cost `nums[(j - k + n) % n]`.\n            # This chocolate now represents type `j`.\n            # So, the cost to acquire type `j` is the minimum of its previous best cost\n            # and `nums[(j - k + n) % n]`.\n            for j in range(n):\n                current_min_prices_for_types[j] = min(\n                    current_min_prices_for_types[j],\n                    nums[(j - k + n) % n]\n                )\n            \n            # Calculate the total cost for this number of shifts\n            total_current_cost = cost_from_operations + sum(current_min_prices_for_types)\n            \n            # Update the overall minimum cost\n            min_cost = min(min_cost, total_current_cost)\n            \n    return min_cost\n```",
