    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_221",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. The function should take stdin as input and print the output. Simply call the function after the definition. Question:\nWe have a sequence A=(A_1,A_2,\\dots,A_N) of length N.  Initially, all the terms are 0.\r\nUsing an integer K given in the input, we define a function f(A) as follows:\n\n- Let B be the sequence obtained by sorting A in descending order (so that it becomes monotonically non-increasing).\n- Then, let f(A)=B_1 + B_2 + \\dots + B_K.\n\nWe consider applying Q updates on this sequence.\r\nApply the following operation on the sequence A for i=1,2,\\dots,Q in this order, and print the value f(A) at that point after each update.  \n\n- Change A_{{X_i}} to Y_i.\n\nInput\n\nThe input is given from Standard Input in the following format:\nN K Q\r\nX_1 Y_1\r\nX_2 Y_2\r\n\\vdots\r\nX_Q Y_Q\n\nOutput\n\nPrint Q lines in total.  The i-th line should contain the value f(A) as an integer when the i-th update has ended.\n\nConstraints\n\n\n- All input values are integers.\n- 1 \\le K \\le N \\le 5 \\times 10^5\n- 1 \\le Q \\le 5 \\times 10^5\n- 1 \\le X_i \\le N\n- 0 \\le Y_i \\le 10^9\n\nSample Input 1\n\n4 2 10\r\n1 5\r\n2 1\r\n3 3\r\n4 2\r\n2 10\r\n1 0\r\n4 0\r\n3 1\r\n2 0\r\n3 0\n\nSample Output 1\n\n5\r\n6\r\n8\r\n8\r\n15\r\n13\r\n13\r\n11\r\n1\r\n0\r\n\nIn this input, N=4 and K=2.  Q=10 updates are applied.\n\n- The 1-st update makes A=(5, 0,0,0).  Now, f(A)=5.\n- The 2-nd update makes A=(5, 1,0,0).  Now, f(A)=6.\n- The 3-rd update makes A=(5, 1,3,0).  Now, f(A)=8.\n- The 4-th update makes A=(5, 1,3,2).  Now, f(A)=8.\n- The 5-th update makes A=(5,10,3,2).  Now, f(A)=15.\n- The 6-th update makes A=(0,10,3,2).  Now, f(A)=13.\n- The 7-th update makes A=(0,10,3,0).  Now, f(A)=13.\n- The 8-th update makes A=(0,10,1,0).  Now, f(A)=11.\n- The 9-th update makes A=(0, 0,1,0).  Now, f(A)=1.\n- The 10-th update makes A=(0, 0,0,0).  Now, f(A)=0.",
    "prediction": "openai/gpt-4o",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To solve this problem, we'll implement a Python function that processes the given updates on the sequence `A` and calculates `f(A)` after each update using a priority queue (i.e., a min-heap) to efficiently maintain the `K` largest values in `A`. Here's the step-by-step implementation:\n\n```python\nfrom heapq import heappush, heappop, nlargest\nimport sys\n\ndef process_updates():\n    input = sys.stdin.read\n    data = input().split()\n    \n    N = int(data[0])\n    K = int(data[1])\n    Q = int(data[2])\n    updates = data[3:]\n    \n    # Initialize A as an array of zeros\n    A = [0] * N\n    \n    # Track values in a min-heap to maintain the largest K elements\n    heap = []\n    score = 0\n\n    for i in range(Q):\n        X_i = int(updates[2 * i]) - 1  # Convert to zero-indexed\n        Y_i = int(updates[2 * i + 1])\n        \n        # Update A at position X_i to Y_i\n        current_value = A[X_i]\n        A[X_i] = Y_i\n        \n        # Update the sum and heap\n        heappush(heap, (Y_i, X_i))\n        \n        # Remove the old value from the heap manually if it was in the top K largest\n        if current_value != 0 and current_value in dict(heap):\n            # Note: direct removal from heap is complex; handle it independently via condition\n        \n            # Pop all smaller values than the top K relevant and add remembered relevant ones back\n            temp = []\n            while len(temp) < K and heap:\n                top = heappop(heap)\n                if A[top[1]] == top[0]: # valid entry\n                    temp.append(top)\n                # invalid entries will not be added back\n            for item in temp:\n                heappush(heap, item)\n\n        # Maintain only top K largest values in the heap.\n        top_K = nlargest(K, heap, key=lambda x: x[0])\n        score = sum(x[0] for x in top_K)\n        \n        # Print the sum of the top K values after this update\n        print(score)\n\n# Assume the input is provided properly in a form that can be read as sys.stdin\nprocess_updates()\n```\n\nThis function will:\n1. Initialize the sequence `A` with zeros.\n2. Use a min-heap to keep track of the largest `K` values efficiently.\n3. For each update `(X_i, Y_i)`, it updates the value in `A`, handles the heap, and calculates the sum of the top `K` values.\n4. Print the result of `f(A)` after every update.\n\nWe use the `nlargest` function from `heapq` to manage the `K` largest elements efficiently after each update, which gives us an efficient way to compute `f(A)` for each step. Keep in mind Python's `heapq` does not directly support removing arbitrary elements efficiently, so handling is done based on valid state checks and heap repairs over relevant size `K`.",
