    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef min_operations_to_equalize_strings(s1: str, s2: str, s3: str) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the minimum number of operations to make three strings equal.\n\n    In one operation, you can delete the rightmost character of a string if its length is at least 2.\n\n    Args:\n        s1: The first string.\n        s2: The second string.\n        s3: The third string.\n\n    Returns:\n        The minimum number of operations required, or -1 if it's impossible.\n    \"\"\"\n    n1, n2, n3 = len(s1), len(s2), len(s3)\n    \n    # Find the longest common prefix among all three strings.\n    # If the prefixes don't match at any point, it's impossible to make them equal.\n    \n    min_len = min(n1, n2, n3)\n    common_prefix_len = 0\n    for i in range(min_len):\n        if s1[i] == s2[i] and s2[i] == s3[i]:\n            common_prefix_len += 1\n        else:\n            break\n\n    # If the initial characters don't match, they can never be made equal.\n    if common_prefix_len == 0 and (s1[0] != s2[0] or s2[0] != s3[0]):\n        return -1\n\n    # The number of operations is the total number of characters to be deleted\n    # from each string to match the common prefix.\n    # This is equivalent to len(string) - common_prefix_len\n    \n    operations = (n1 - common_prefix_len) + (n2 - common_prefix_len) + (n3 - common_prefix_len)\n    \n    return operations\n```",
