    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_254",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. The function should take stdin as input and print the output. Simply call the function after the definition. Question:\nThere are N items.\r\nEach of these is one of a pull-tab can, a regular can, or a can opener.\r\nThe i-th item is described by an integer pair (T_i, X_i) as follows:  \n\n- If T_i = 0, the i-th item is a pull-tab can; if you obtain it, you get a happiness of X_i.\n- If T_i = 1, the i-th item is a regular can; if you obtain it and use a can opener against it, you get a happiness of X_i.\n- If T_i = 2, the i-th item is a can opener; it can be used against at most X_i cans.\n\nFind the maximum total happiness that you get by obtaining M items out of N.\n\nInput\n\nThe input is given from Standard Input in the following format:\nN M\r\nT_1 X_1\r\nT_2 X_2\r\n\\vdots\r\nT_N X_N\n\nOutput\n\nPrint the answer as an integer.\n\nConstraints\n\n\n- 1 \\leq M \\leq N \\leq 2 \\times 10^5\n- T_i is 0, 1, or 2.\n- 1 \\leq X_i \\leq 10^9\n- All input values are integers.\n\nSample Input 1\n\n8 4\r\n0 6\r\n0 6\r\n1 3\r\n1 5\r\n1 15\r\n2 1\r\n2 10\r\n2 100\n\nSample Output 1\n\n27\r\n\nIf you obtain the 1-st, 2-nd, 5-th, and 7-th items, and use the 7-th item (a can opener) against the 5-th item, you will get a happiness of 6 + 6 + 15 = 27.\r\nThere are no ways to obtain items to get a happiness of 28 or greater, but you can still get a happiness of 27 by obtaining the 6-th or 8-th items instead of the 7-th in the combination above.\n\nSample Input 2\n\n5 5\r\n1 5\r\n1 5\r\n1 5\r\n1 5\r\n1 5\n\nSample Output 2\n\n0\n\nSample Input 3\n\n12 6\r\n2 2\r\n0 1\r\n0 9\r\n1 3\r\n1 5\r\n1 3\r\n0 4\r\n2 1\r\n1 8\r\n2 1\r\n0 1\r\n0 4\n\nSample Output 3\n\n30",
    "prediction": "openai/gpt-4o",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To solve the problem, we need to maximize total happiness by strategically selecting M items out of the given N items. The items can be one of three types: pull-tab cans, regular cans, or can openers. Here's the strategy we'll follow:\n\n1. **Pull-Tab Cans:** We can always directly take these to gain the happiness specified without any conditions.\n2. **Regular Cans:** These need a can opener to gain happiness. Therefore, we need to ensure that if we pick one of these, we have a can opener available.\n3. **Can Openers:** These can be used to open regular cans. The number specifies how many cans a single can opener can open.\n\nWe will approach the problem as follows:\n\n- Separate items into pull-tab cans, regular cans, and can openers.\n- Sort the pull-tab cans and regular cans in descending order of happiness, and the can openers in descending order of the number they can open.\n- Use a greedy strategy to pick items:\n  - Prioritize picking pull-tab cans as they directly contribute to happiness.\n  - Track available can openers, and use them to unlock the benefit of regular cans.\n  - Add regular cans to the picked list only if there are can openers available.\n- Ensure that only M items are picked, and calculate the total happiness.\n\nHere's the code implementing this strategy:\n\n```python\ndef max_happiness():\n    import sys\n    input = sys.stdin.read\n    from heapq import heappush, heappop\n    \n    data = input().strip().split()\n    \n    N = int(data[0])\n    M = int(data[1])\n    \n    items = []\n    for i in range(N):\n        T_i = int(data[2 + 2 * i])\n        X_i = int(data[2 + 2 * i + 1])\n        items.append((T_i, X_i))\n    \n    pull_tab_cans = []\n    regular_cans = []\n    can_openers = []\n    \n    # Separate the items\n    for T_i, X_i in items:\n        if T_i == 0:\n            heappush(pull_tab_cans, -X_i)  # we use negative to simulate max heap\n        elif T_i == 1:\n            heappush(regular_cans, -X_i)  # we use negative to simulate max heap\n        elif T_i == 2:\n            # In this case, we take openers with higher capacity first\n            heappush(can_openers, -X_i)\n    \n    selected_items = []\n    \n    while len(selected_items) < M:\n        # Always prefer pull-tab cans since they provide happiness immediately\n        if pull_tab_cans:\n            heappush(selected_items, -heappop(pull_tab_cans))\n        elif regular_cans and can_openers:\n            # We can only use regular cans if there's a can opener\n            can_opener_capacity = -heappop(can_openers)\n            regular_can_happiness = -heappop(regular_cans)\n            can_opener_capacity -= 1\n            \n            # Use one can opener capacity to gain the benefit of a regular can\n            heappush(selected_items, regular_can_happiness)\n            \n            if can_opener_capacity > 0:\n                heappush(can_openers, -can_opener_capacity)\n        else:\n            if can_openers:\n                # If only can openers are left, take the opener if possible\n                heappop(can_openers)\n            else:\n                # If no more useful items can be picked, we might need to break the loop\n                break\n    \n    # Sum up the happiness from selected items\n    total_happiness = sum(selected_items)\n    \n    print(total_happiness)\n\nmax_happiness()\n```\n\nThis function will read the input from `stdin`, process the data according to the outlined strategy, and print the maximum achievable happiness. The use of heaps (via the `heapq` library) allows efficient selection of the top items in terms of their contribution to happiness.",
