    "global index": "MMLUPro_computer science_9202",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Statement 1| The original ResNets and Transformers are feedforward neural networks. Statement 2| The original Transformers use self-attention, but the original ResNet does not.\n\nOptions: \nA. True, True\nB. True, False\nC. False, False\nD. False, True\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The correct answer is **D**.\n\nStatement 1 is False. ResNets, while primarily feedforward in their main data flow, incorporate residual connections that skip layers, which technically makes them not *purely* feedforward in the strictest sense as information can bypass layers. Transformers, on the other hand, are undeniably feedforward networks within each layer and when considering the overall flow from input to output. However, the \"original ResNets\" reference makes the first part of the statement questionable enough to deem the whole statement False when contrasted with how Transformers are generally classified.\n\nStatement 2 is True. The original Transformer architecture introduced and heavily relied on multi-headed self-attention mechanisms. The original ResNet architecture, published in 2015, predates Transformers (2017) and did not utilize self-attention; its core innovation was the residual block.",
