      "generated_answer": "The question asks about conditional branching in APL. A conditional branch in APL is achieved using the \"branch\" operator, represented by a right-pointing arrow ($\\to$). This operator, when used with a conditional expression, determines the next instruction to execute. Specifically, the expression to the left of the arrow is evaluated, and if it results in a non-zero value, the program branches to the expression on the right of the arrow, which specifies the target.\n\nOption G accurately describes this mechanism, stating \"A conditional branch instruction is specified in APL by means of a right-pointing arrow followed on its right by an expression. It can also be specified using a label or the compression function.\" While labels and compression are advanced or alternative ways to achieve branching behavior, the core mechanism of the right-pointing arrow is correctly identified.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{G}$.",
