    "global index": "MMLUPro_computer science_9259",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which types of functions grow the slowest?\n\nOptions: \nA. O(N)\nB. O(log log N)\nC. O(N!)\nD. O(log N)\nE. O(N^N)\nF. O(2^N)\nG. O(N log N)\nH. O(N^3)\nI. O(N^2)\nJ. O(log e^N)\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Let's analyze the growth rates of the given functions. We are looking for the function that grows the slowest.\n\n- O(N): Linear growth.\n- O(log log N): This grows very slowly, slower than logarithmic.\n- O(N!): Factorial growth, grows extremely fast.\n- O(log N): Logarithmic growth, grows slowly.\n- O(N^N): Exponential-like growth with the base also growing, extremely fast.\n- O(2^N): Exponential growth, grows very fast.\n- O(N log N): Grows faster than linear but slower than quadratic.\n- O(N^3): Cubic growth.\n- O(N^2): Quadratic growth.\n- O(log e^N): We can simplify this to O(N), since log e^N = N * log e = N * 1 = N.\n\nComparing the growth rates, O(log log N) grows the slowest among the options provided. Logarithmic growth (O(log N)) is slower than linear (O(N)) and polynomial functions (O(N^2), O(N^3)). Exponential functions (O(2^N)) and factorial functions (O(N!)) grow significantly faster. O(N^N) grows even faster. O(log log N) grows slower than O(log N).\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{B}$.",
      "success": true,
