    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The Singular Value Decomposition (SVD) of a data matrix $X$ can be decomposed into $X = U \\Sigma V^T$. Principal Component Analysis (PCA) aims to find a linear projection that maximizes variance. If the data is standardized to have zero mean and unit variance, the covariance matrix is directly related to the Gram matrix $X^T X$. The eigenvectors of the covariance matrix (which define the PCA directions) are the right singular vectors ($V$) of $X$, and the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix are proportional to the squared singular values. Thus, to achieve the same projection as SVD, the data needs to be centered.\n\nThe correct answer is \\boxed{I}.",
