    "global index": "MMLUPro_computer science_9077",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A particular disk unit uses a bit string to record the occupancy or vacancy of its tracks, with O denoting vacant and 1 denoting occupied. A 32-bit segment of this string has the hexadecimal value D4FE2003. The percentage of occupied tracks for the corresponding part of the disk, to the nearest percent, is\n\nOptions: \nA. 69%\nB. 38%\nC. 12%\nD. 50%\nE. 25%\nF. 75%\nG. 44%\nH. 62%\nI. 56%\nJ. 81%\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The hexadecimal value D4FE2003 needs to be converted to its binary representation.\nD in hex is 1101 in binary.\n4 in hex is 0100 in binary.\nF in hex is 1111 in binary.\nE in hex is 1110 in binary.\n2 in hex is 0010 in binary.\n0 in hex is 0000 in binary.\n0 in hex is 0000 in binary.\n3 in hex is 0011 in binary.\n\nConcatenating these binary representations, we get:\n1101 0100 1111 1110 0010 0000 0000 0011\n\nThis is a 32-bit string. Now we count the number of '1's (occupied tracks) in this binary string.\n1101 (3 ones)\n0100 (1 one)\n1111 (4 ones)\n1110 (3 ones)\n0010 (1 one)\n0000 (0 ones)\n0000 (0 ones)\n0011 (2 ones)\n\nTotal number of ones = 3 + 1 + 4 + 3 + 1 + 0 + 0 + 2 = 14\n\nThe total number of bits in the segment is 32.\nThe percentage of occupied tracks is (Number of occupied tracks / Total number of tracks) * 100.\nPercentage of occupied tracks = (14 / 32) * 100\n\nCalculating the percentage:\n(14 / 32) * 100 = 0.4375 * 100 = 43.75%\n\nRounding to the nearest percent, we get 44%.\n\nComparing this with the given options:\nA. 69%\nB. 38%\nC. 12%\nD. 50%\nE. 25%\nF. 75%\nG. 44%\nH. 62%\nI. 56%\nJ. 81%\n\nThe calculated percentage of 44% matches option G.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{G}$.",
