  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_computer science_9122",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: An online store uses 6-bit binary sequences to identify each unique item for sale. The store plans to increase the number of items it sells and is considering using 7-bit binary sequences. Which of the following best describes the result of using 7-bit sequences instead of 6- bit sequences?\n\nOptions: \nA. 10 times as many items can be uniquely identified.\nB. 2 more items can be uniquely identified.\nC. 2 times as many items can be uniquely identified.\nD. The number of uniquely identified items will remain the same.\nE. 3 times as many items can be uniquely identified.\nF. Half as many items can be uniquely identified.\nG. 128 more items can be uniquely identified.\nH. 10 more items can be uniquely identified.\nI. 4 times as many items can be uniquely identified.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "A binary sequence of length $n$ bits can uniquely identify $2^n$ unique items.\nFor 6-bit sequences, the number of unique items is $2^6 = 64$.\nFor 7-bit sequences, the number of unique items is $2^7 = 128$.\n\nThe difference in the number of uniquely identified items is $128 - 64 = 64$.\nThe ratio of the number of unique items with 7-bit sequences to 6-bit sequences is $\\frac{2^7}{2^6} = 2$.\nTherefore, using 7-bit sequences instead of 6-bit sequences allows for 2 times as many items to be uniquely identified.\n\nLet's check the options:\nA. 10 times as many items can be uniquely identified. (Incorrect, $128/64 = 2$)\nB. 2 more items can be uniquely identified. (Incorrect, $128 - 64 = 64$ more items, not 2)\nC. 2 times as many items can be uniquely identified. (Correct, $128 = 2 \\times 64$)\nD. The number of uniquely identified items will remain the same. (Incorrect)\nE. 3 times as many items can be uniquely identified. (Incorrect)\nF. Half as many items can be uniquely identified. (Incorrect)\nG. 128 more items can be uniquely identified. (Incorrect, it's 64 more items)\nH. 10 more items can be uniquely identified. (Incorrect)\nI. 4 times as many items can be uniquely identified. (Incorrect)\n\nThe correct option is C.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{C}$.",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 247,
        "output_tokens": 384,
        "total_tokens": 631
