  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_computer science_9137",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Given two Boolean random variables, A and B, where P(A) = 1/2, P(B) = 1/3, and P(A | ¬B) = 1/4, what is P(A | B)?\n\nOptions: \nA. 1/6\nB. 0\nC. 1/3\nD. 1\nE. 1/2\nF. 5/6\nG. 1/4\nH. 3/4\nI. 2/3\nJ. 4/5\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To solve this problem, we'll use the definition of conditional probability and Bayes' theorem.\nWe are given:\nP(A) = 1/2\nP(B) = 1/3\nP(A | ¬B) = 1/4\n\nWe want to find P(A | B).\n\nFirst, let's find P(¬B):\nP(¬B) = 1 - P(B) = 1 - 1/3 = 2/3\n\nNow, let's use the definition of conditional probability for P(A | ¬B):\nP(A | ¬B) = P(A ∩ ¬B) / P(¬B)\n1/4 = P(A ∩ ¬B) / (2/3)\nP(A ∩ ¬B) = (1/4) * (2/3) = 2/12 = 1/6\n\nWe know that the probability of A can be broken down into two mutually exclusive cases: A occurring with B, and A occurring with ¬B.\nP(A) = P(A ∩ B) + P(A ∩ ¬B)\n1/2 = P(A ∩ B) + 1/6\nP(A ∩ B) = 1/2 - 1/6 = 3/6 - 1/6 = 2/6 = 1/3\n\nFinally, we can find P(A | B) using the definition of conditional probability:\nP(A | B) = P(A ∩ B) / P(B)\nP(A | B) = (1/3) / (1/3)\nP(A | B) = 1\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{D}$.",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 181,
        "output_tokens": 386,
        "total_tokens": 567
      },
      "provider": "openrouter",
