    "global index": "MMLUPro_computer science_9143",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In a Gigabit Ethernet LAN, the average size of a frame is 1000 bytes. If a noise of 2ms occurs on the LAN, how many frames are destroyed?\n\nOptions: \nA. 750\nB. 500\nC. 1000\nD. 600\nE. 150\nF. 100\nG. 200\nH. 400\nI. 125\nJ. 250\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To determine the number of frames destroyed, we need to calculate how many frames are being transmitted during the 2ms noise period. A Gigabit Ethernet transmits data at a rate of 1 billion bits per second.\n\nFirst, convert the noise duration to seconds: 2 ms = 0.002 seconds.\nNext, calculate the total bits transmitted during this period:\nData rate = 1 Gbps = 1,000,000,000 bits/sec\nTotal bits transmitted = Data rate * Time duration\nTotal bits transmitted = 1,000,000,000 bits/sec * 0.002 sec = 2,000,000 bits\n\nNow, convert the frame size from bytes to bits:\nFrame size = 1000 bytes\n1 byte = 8 bits\nFrame size in bits = 1000 bytes * 8 bits/byte = 8000 bits\n\nFinally, calculate the number of frames destroyed by dividing the total bits transmitted during the noise period by the size of each frame:\nNumber of frames destroyed = Total bits transmitted / Frame size in bits\nNumber of frames destroyed = 2,000,000 bits / 8000 bits/frame = 250 frames\n\nSo, 250 frames are destroyed.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{J}$.",
