      "generated_answer": "A labelled rooted forest on $n$ vertices is a collection of rooted trees on a set of $n$ labelled vertices.\nLet $F_n$ be the number of labelled rooted forests on $n$ vertices.\nThe number of labelled rooted trees on $n$ vertices is given by Cayley's formula for labelled trees multiplied by $n$ (since any vertex can be chosen as the root). The number of labelled trees on $n$ vertices is $n^{n-2}$. Thus, the number of labelled rooted trees on $n$ vertices is $n \\cdot n^{n-2} = n^{n-1}$.\n\nA labelled rooted forest on $n$ vertices can be viewed as a structure where each vertex belongs to a rooted tree, and each vertex is either a root or connected to another vertex.\nAlternatively, consider a set of $n$ labelled vertices. For each vertex, we can decide which vertex it points to, or it can be a root in its own tree.\nConsider the structure of a labelled rooted forest. We can also think of this in terms of the number of ways to form such forests.\nLet's consider a different approach using exponential generating functions.\nThe exponential generating function for labelled rooted trees is $T(x) = \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty n^{n-1} \\frac{x^n}{n!} = \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\frac{(nx)^{n-1}}{n!} n$. This is not straightforward.\n\nLet's consider a simpler problem. The number of labelled forests (unrooted) on $n$ vertices is known to be related to the number of trees.\nA labelled rooted forest on $n$ vertices can be constructed by considering that each vertex must belong to exactly one rooted tree.\nConsider another perspective. For a labelled rooted forest on $n$ vertices, each vertex can either be a root or can be a child of another vertex.\nLet's revisit the number of labelled rooted trees on $n$ vertices, which is $n^{n-1}$.\n\nA labelled rooted forest is a collection of disjoint labelled rooted trees.\nHowever, the problem phrasing \"number of labelled rooted forests on 6 vertices\" suggests a specific definition.\nA different way to count labelled roo