    "global index": "MMLUPro_computer science_9150",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In an IPv4 datagram, the value of the total-length field is $(00 \\mathrm{{~A}} 0)_{{16}}$ and the value of the headerlength (HLEN) is (5) $1_{{16}}$. How many bytes of payload are being carried by the datagram?\n\nOptions: \nA. 220\nB. 240\nC. 150\nD. 180\nE. 100\nF. 140\nG. 160\nH. 200\nI. 260\nJ. 120\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The `total-length` field in an IPv4 datagram indicates the total size of the datagram in bytes, including the header and the payload. The `header-length` (HLEN) field indicates the size of the IP header in units of 32-bit words. To find the number of payload bytes, we subtract the IP header size from the total length.\n\nGiven:\nTotal Length = $(00 \\mathrm{~A} 0)_{{16}}$ bytes\nHeader Length (HLEN) = $(5)_{{16}}$ 32-bit words\n\nFirst, convert the total length from hexadecimal to decimal:\n$(00 \\mathrm{~A} 0)_{{16}} = 0 \\times 16^3 + 0 \\times 16^2 + 10 \\times 16^1 + 0 \\times 16^0 = 10 \\times 16 = 160$ bytes.\n\nNext, convert the header length from 32-bit words to bytes:\nHeader Size = HLEN $\\times$ 4 bytes/word\nHeader Size = $(5)_{{16}} \\times 4 = 5 \\times 4 = 20$ bytes.\n\nNow, calculate the payload size by subtracting the header size from the total length:\nPayload Size = Total Length - Header Size\nPayload Size = 160 bytes - 20 bytes = 140 bytes.\n\nTherefore, there are 140 bytes of payload being carried by the datagram.\n\nComparing this to the given options:\nA. 220\nB. 240\nC. 150\nD. 180\nE. 100\nF. 140\nG. 160\nH. 200\nI. 260\nJ. 120\n\nThe calculated payload size of 140 bytes matches option F.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{F}$.",
      "success": true,
