    "global index": "MMLUPro_computer science_9211",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: How many distinct directed trees can be constructed from a undirected tree with 100 nodes?\n\nOptions: \nA. 9900\nB. 10000\nC. 250\nD. 1000\nE. 150\nF. 99\nG. 50\nH. 500\nI. 200\nJ. 100\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The number of distinct directed trees that can be constructed from an undirected tree with $n$ nodes is equal to the number of distinct rooted directed trees, where each node has exactly one incoming edge in the directed tree, except for the root which has zero incoming edges. For an undirected tree with $n$ nodes, we can choose any of the $n$ nodes as the root. Once a root is chosen, an undirected tree can be oriented into a directed tree by directing each edge away from the root. Therefore, for an undirected tree with $n$ nodes, there are $n$ distinct directed trees that can be constructed.\n\nIn this question, we have an undirected tree with 100 nodes. By the reasoning above, we can construct 100 distinct directed trees by choosing each of the 100 nodes as the root and orienting the edges away from the root.\n\nTherefore, for an undirected tree with 100 nodes, there are 100 distinct directed trees that can be constructed.\n\nComparing this with the given options:\nA. 9900\nB. 10000\nC. 250\nD. 1000\nE. 150\nF. 99\nG. 50\nH. 500\nI. 200\nJ. 100\n\nThe correct answer is 100, which corresponds to option J.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{J}$.",
