    "global index": "MMLUPro_computer science_9213",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Consider that the following two signals: $x(t)$ and $v(t)$ $$ x(t)=\\left\\{{\\begin{{array}}{{cc}} 1 & 0 \\leq t \\leq 3 \\\\ 0 & \\text {{ otherwise }} \\end{{array}} \\quad v(t)=\\left\\{{\\begin{{array}}{{cc}} 1 & 0 \\leq t \\leq 2 \\\\ 0 & \\text {{ otherwise }} \\end{{array}}\\right.\\right. $$ Let $y(\\tau)=\\int_{{-\\infty}}^{{\\infty}} x(\\tau-t) v(t) d t$. Let $\\tau=2.5$. Determine $y(\\tau)$.\n\nOptions: \nA. 3\nB. 2.5\nC. 2\nD. 1.75\nE. 0.5\nF. 3.5\nG. 0\nH. 1\nI. 1.5\nJ. 4\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The integral $y(\\tau)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} x(\\tau-t) v(t) dt$ represents the convolution of $x(t)$ and $v(t)$.\nThe signal $v(t)$ is a rectangular pulse of width 2, from 0 to 2. The signal $x(t)$ is a rectangular pulse of width 3, from 0 to 3.\nThe convolution of two rectangular pulses of widths $W_1$ and $W_2$ results in a trapezoidal pulse. For $\\tau=2.5$, we need to find the overlap between $x(\\tau-t)$ (which is $x(2.5-t)$) and $v(t)$.\nThe function $x(2.5-t)$ is a rectangular pulse of width 3, located such that its end is at $2.5$. Thus, $x(2.5-t)$ is 1 for $0 \\leq 2.5-t \\leq 3$, which means $-2.5 \\leq -t \\leq 0.5$, or $-0.5 \\leq t \\leq 2.5$.\nThe function $v(t)$ is 1 for $0 \\leq t \\leq 2$.\nThe integral $y(2.5) = \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} x(2.5-t) v(t) dt$ is the integral of the product of $x(2.5-t)$ and $v(t)$. The product is 1 only in the region where both functions are 1. This region is $0 \\leq t \\leq 2.5$ and $0 \\leq t \\leq 2$. The intersection of these intervals is $0 \\leq t \\leq 2$.\nTherefore, $y(2.5) = \\int_{0}^{2} 1 \\cdot 1 dt = \\int_{0}^{2} 1 dt = [t]_0^2 = 2 - 0 = 2$.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{C}$.",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 268,
        "output_tokens": 447,
        "total_tokens": 715
      },
      "provider": "openrouter",
      "error": null
