    "global index": "MMLUPro_computer science_9252",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Let $X \\sim N(0,1)$ and let the distortion measure be squared error. Here we do not allow block descriptions. Compute the minimum expected distortion for one bit quantization of $X$ using a squared error distortion measure.\n\nOptions: \nA. 0.625\nB. 0.212\nC. 0.411\nD. 0.363\nE. 0.1\nF. 0.75\nG. 0.498\nH. 0.333\nI. 0.256\nJ. 0.125\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The problem asks for the minimum expected squared error distortion for one-bit quantization of a standard normal random variable $X \\sim N(0,1)$. For one-bit quantization, we partition the real line into two regions, say $R_1$ and $R_2$, and assign a representative value $y_1$ to all $x \\in R_1$ and $y_2$ to all $x \\in R_2$. The distortion is given by $d(x,y) = (x-y)^2$. We want to minimize $E[d(X, Y)]$ over choices of $R_1, R_2, y_1, y_2$.\n\nFor a single bit quantizer, the optimal quantization levels $y_1$ and $y_2$ are such that $y_1 = E[X|X \\in R_1]$ and $y_2 = E[X|X \\in R_2]$. The optimal partition is achieved when the threshold between the two regions is 0. So, we have two regions, $(-\\infty, 0]$ and $(0, \\infty)$.\nLet $y_1 = E[X|X \\le 0]$ and $y_2 = E[X|X > 0]$. For a standard normal distribution, due to symmetry about 0, $E[X|X \\le 0] = -E[X|X > 0]$. Furthermore, since the mean of the distribution is 0, $E[X] = \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} x f(x) dx = \\int_{-\\infty}^{0} x f(x) dx + \\int_{0}^{\\infty} x f(x) dx = 0$.\nSince $f(x)$ is an even function, $\\int_{-\\infty}^{0} x f(x) dx = -\\int_{0}^{\\infty} x f(x) dx$.\nThus, $E[X|X \\le 0] = \\frac{\\int_{-\\infty}^{0} x f(x) dx}{P(X \\le 0)} = \\frac{\\int_{-\\infty}^{0} x f(x) dx}{1/2} = 2 \\int_{-\\infty}^{0} x f(x) dx$ and $E[X|X > 0] = \\frac{\\int_{0}^{\\infty} x f(x) dx}{P(X > 0)} = \\frac{\\int_{0}^{\\infty} x f(x) dx}{1/2} = 2 \\int_{0}^{\\infty} x f(x) dx$.\nFrom $E[X] = 0$, we have $\\int_{-\\infty}^{0} x f(x) dx + \\int_{0}^{\\infty} x f(x) dx = 0$, which implies $\\int_{-\\infty}^{0} x f(x) dx = -\\int_{0}^{\\infty} x f(x) dx$.\nTherefore, $E[X|X \\le 0] = -E[X|X > 0]$. Let this value be $-a$ and $a$ respectively, where $a = 2 \\int_{0}^{\\infty} x f(x) dx$.\nWe need to compute $a$. Using integration by parts, $\\int_{0}^{\\infty} x \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi}} e^{-x^2/2} dx = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi}} \\int_{0}^{\\infty} x e^{-x^2/2} dx$.\nLet $u = -x^2/2$, so $du = -x dx$. When $x=0$, $u=0$. When $x \\to \\infty$, $u \\to -\\infty$.\n$\\int_{0}^{\\infty} x e^{-x^2/2} dx = \\int_{0}^{-\\infty} -e^u du = \\int_{-\\infty}^{0} e^u du = [e^u]_{-\\infty}^{0} = e^0 - \\lim_{u \\to -\\infty} e^u = 1 - 0 = 1$.\nSo, $a = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi}} (1) = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi}}$.\nThus, $y_1 = -1/\\sqrt{2\\pi}$ and $y_2 = 1/\\sqrt{2\\pi}$.\n\nThe expected distortion is:\n$D_{min} = \\int_{-\\infty}^{0} (x - y_1)^2 f(x) dx + \\int_{0}^{\\infty} (x - y_2)^2 f(x) dx$\n$D_{min} = \\int_{-\\infty}^{0} (x + 1/\\sqrt{2\\pi})^2 f(x) dx + \\int_{0}^{\\infty} (x - 1/\\sqrt{2\\pi})^2 f(x) dx$\nDue to symmetry, $\\int_{-\\infty}^{0} (x + 1/\\sqrt{2\\pi})^2 f(x) dx = \\int_{0}^{\\infty} (x + 1/\\sqrt{2\\pi})^2 f(-x) dx = \\int_{0}^{\\infty} (x + 1/\\sqrt{2\\pi})^2 f(x) dx$.\nSo, $D_{min} = 2 \\int_{0}^{\\infty} (x - 1/\\sqrt{2\\pi})^2 f(x) dx$\n$D_{min} = 2 \\int_{0}^{\\infty} (x^2 - 2x/\\sqrt{2\\pi} + 1/(2\\pi)) f(x) dx$\n$D_{min} = 2 \\left( \\int_{0}^{\\infty} x^2 f(x) dx - \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2\\pi}} \\int_{0}^{\\infty} x f(x) dx + \\frac{1}{2\\pi} \\int_{0}^{\\infty} f(x) dx \\right)$\nWe know that $\\int_{0}^{\\infty} x f(x) dx = \\frac{1}{2} a = \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2\\pi}}$.\nAnd $\\int_{0}^{\\infty} f(x) dx = P(X > 0) = 1/2$.\nFor a standard normal distribution, $E[X^2] = Var(X) + (E[X])^2 = 1 + 0^2 = 1$.\nAlso, $E[X^2] = \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} x^2 f(x) dx = \\int_{-\\infty}^{0} x^2 f(x) dx + \\int_{0}^{\\infty} x^2 f(x) dx$.\nSince $x^2 f(x)$ is an even function, $\\int_{-\\infty}^{0} x^2 f(x) dx = \\int_{0}^{\\infty} x^2 f(x) dx$.\nSo, $\\int_{0}^{\\infty} x^2 f(x) dx = \\frac{1}{2} E[X^2] = \\frac{1}{2}$.\n\nSubstituting these values:\n$D_{min} = 2 \\left( \\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2\\pi}} \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2\\pi}} + \\frac{1}{2\\pi} \\frac{1}{2} \\right)$\n$D_{min} = 2 \\left( \\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{1}{2\\pi} + \\frac{1}{4\\pi} \\right)$\n$D_{min} = 2 \\left( \\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{1}{4\\pi} \\right)$\n$D_{min} = 1 - \\frac{1}{2\\pi}$\nUsing $\\pi \\approx 3.14159$,\n$D_{min} = 1 - \\frac{1}{2 \\times 3.14159} = 1 - \\frac{1}{6.28318} \\approx 1 - 0.15915 = 0.84085$.\n\nLet's recheck the calculation of $y_1$ and $y_2$.\nFor a standard normal distribution, $y_1 = E[X | X \\le 0]$ and $y_2 = E[X | X > 0]$.\n$y_2 = \\frac{\\int_0^\\infty x \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi}} e^{-x^2/2} dx}{\\int_0^\\infty \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi}} e^{-x^2/2} dx} = \\frac{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi}} \\int_0^\\infty x e^{-x^2/2} dx}{1/2} = \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2\\pi}} \\int_0^\\infty x e^{-x^2/2} dx$.\nAs calculated before, $\\int_0^\\infty x e^{-x^2/2} dx = 1$.\nSo, $y_2 = \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2\\pi}} \\times 1 = \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{\\pi}}$.\nAnd $y_1 = -y_2 = -\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{\\pi}}$.\n\nThe minimum distortion is given by:\n$D_{min} = \\int_{-\\infty}^0 (x - y_1)^2 f(x) dx + \\int_0^\\infty (x - y_2)^2 f(x) dx$\n$D_{min} = \\int_{-\\infty}^0 (x + \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{\\pi}})^2 f(x) dx + \\int_0^\\infty (x - \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{\\pi}})^2 f(x) dx$\nUsing symmetry, $D_{min} = 2 \\int_0^\\infty (x - \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{\\pi}})^2 f(x) dx$\n$D_{min} = 2 \\int_0^\\infty (x^2 - 2x\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{\\pi}} + \\frac{2}{\\pi}) f(x) dx$\n$D_{min} = 2 \\left( \\int_0^\\infty x^2 f(x) dx - 2\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{\\pi}} \\int_0^\\infty x f(x) dx + \\frac{2}{\\pi} \\int_0^\\infty f(x) dx \\right)$\nWe have $\\int_0^\\infty x^2 f(x) dx = 1/2$, $\\int_0^\\infty x f(x) dx = \\frac{1}{2} \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{\\pi}} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi}}$, and $\\int_0^\\infty f(x) dx = 1/2$.\n$D_{min} = 2 \\left( \\frac{1}{2} - 2\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{\\pi}} \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi}} + \\frac{2}{\\pi} \\frac{1}{2} \\right)$\n$D_{min} = 2 \\left( \\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{2}{\\pi} + \\frac{1}{\\pi} \\right)$\n$D_{min} = 2 \\left( \\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{1}{\\pi} \\right) = 1 - \\frac{2}{\\pi}$\n$D_{min} = 1 - \\frac{2}{3.14159} \\approx 1 - 0.63662 = 0.36338$.\n\nThis value is close to option D.\n\nLet's check a known result for one-bit quantization of a Gaussian random variable.\nThe minimum expected squared error distortion for one-bit quantization of $N(\\mu, \\sigma^2)$ is $\\sigma^2 (1 - \\frac{2}{\\pi})$.\nHere, $X \\sim N(0,1)$, so $\\mu=0$ and $\\sigma^2=1$.\nThe minimum expected distortion is $1 \\times (1 - \\frac{2}{\\pi}) = 1 - \\frac{2}{\\pi}$.\n\n$1 - \\frac{2}{\\pi} \\approx 1 - \\frac{2}{3.14159265} \\approx 1 - 0.63661977 \\approx 0.36338023$.\n\nThe closest option is 0.363.\n\nFinal check of the calculation.\n$y_2 = E[X|X>0] = \\frac{\\int_0^\\infty x \\phi(x) dx}{P(X>0)} = \\frac{\\int_0^\\infty x \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi}} e^{-x^2/2} dx}{1/2} = \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{\\pi}}$.\n$D = P(X>0) E[(X-y_2)^2 | X>0] + P(X \\le 0) E[(X-y_1)^2 | X \\le 0]$\n$D = \\frac{1}{2} E[(X-\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{\\pi}})^2 | X>0] + \\frac{1}{2} E[(X+\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{\\pi}})^2 | X \\le 0]$\nBy symmetry, the two terms are equal.\n$D = E[(X-\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{\\pi}})^2 | X>0] = E[X^2 - 2X\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{\\pi}} + \\frac{2}{\\pi} | X>0]$\n$D = E[X^2 | X>0] - 2\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{\\pi}} E[X | X>0] + \\frac{2}{\\pi}$\n$E[X | X>0] = \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{\\pi}}$\n$E[X^2 | X>0] = \\frac{\\int_0^\\infty x^2 \\phi(x) dx}{1/2} = 2 \\int_0^\\infty x^2 \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi}} e^{-x^2/2} dx = \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2\\pi}} \\int_0^\\infty x^2 e^{-x^2/2} dx$.\nUsing $\\int_0^\\infty x^2 e^{-ax^2} dx = \\frac{1}{4a}\\sqrt{\\frac{\\pi}{a}}$. Here $a = 1/2$.\n$\\int_0^\\infty x^2 e^{-x^2/2} dx = \\frac{1}{4(1/2)}\\sqrt{\\frac{\\pi}{1/2}} = \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{2\\pi}$.\nSo, $E[X^2 | X>0] = \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2\\pi}} \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{2\\pi} = 1$.\n$D = 1 - 2\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{\\pi}} \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{\\pi}} + \\frac{2}{\\pi} = 1 - 2 \\frac{2}{\\pi} + \\frac{2}{\\pi} = 1 - \\frac{4}{\\pi} + \\frac{2}{\\pi} = 1 - \\frac{2}{\\pi}$.\nSo the formula $1 - \\frac{2}{\\pi}$ is correct.\n\nThe minimum expected distortion for one-bit quantization of $N(0,1)$ is $1 - \\frac{2}{\\pi} \\approx 0.36338$.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{D}$.",
