resent connections between nodes. We can consider <A,B> and <B,A> to represent the same undirected edge.\n\nThe available edges are:\n1 is connected to 2.\n2 is connected to 4.\n4 is connected to 5.\n5 is connected to 6.\n\nLet's form the adjacency list for clarity:\n1: [2]\n2: [1, 4]\n4: [2, 5]\n5: [4, 6]\n6: [5]\n\nNow let's find the shortest path from 1 to 6 using a Breadth-First Search (BFS) approach.\n\nStart at 1.\nQueue: [1]\nVisited: {1}\nPath: {1: None}\n\nDequeue 1. Neighbors of 1: [2].\nEnqueue 2. Visited: {1, 2}. Path: {2: 1}.\nQueue: [2]\n\nDequeue 2. Neighbors of 2: [1, 4]. 1 is visited.\nEnqueue 4. Visited: {1, 2, 4}. Path: {4: 2}.\nQueue: [4]\n\nDequeue 4. Neighbors of 4: [2, 5]. 2 is visited.\nEnqueue 5. Visited: {1, 2, 4, 5}. Path: {5: 4}.\nQueue: [5]\n\nDequeue 5. Neighbors of 5: [4, 6]. 4 is visited.\nEnqueue 6. Visited: {1, 2, 4, 5, 6}. Path: {6: 5}.\nQueue: [6]. We found the target node 6.\n\nNow, reconstruct the path from 6 back to 1 using the parent pointers in 'Path':\n6 <- 5 <- 4 <- 2 <- 1\n\nSo the path is [1, 2, 4, 5, 6]. This path has a length of 4 edges.\n\nLet's review the options again:\nA. [1, 5, 4, 6] - Not a valid path as 1 is not connected to 5.\nB. [1, 5, 4, 2, 6] - Not a valid path as 1 is not connected to 5.\nC. [1, 2, 4, 5, 6] - Valid path, length 4.\nD. [1, 2, 5, 4, 6] - Not a valid path as 2 is not connected to 5.\nE. [1, 2, 5, 6] - Not a valid path as 2 is not connected to 5.\nF. [1, 2, 4, 5] - Does not reach node 6.\nG. [1, 2, 5, 4, 5, 6] - Not a valid path as 2 is not connected to 5.\nH. [1, 5, 6] - Not a valid path as 1 is not connected to 5.\nI. [1, 2, 6] - Not a valid path as 2 is not connected to 6.\nJ. [1, 2, 4, 6] - Not a valid path as 4 is not connected to 6.\n\nBased on the edges provided, the only valid path from 1 to 6 given in the options is [1, 2, 4, 5, 6]. Since BFS finds the shortest path in an unweighted graph, and this is the only valid option that reaches node 6, it is the shortest path.\n\nThe edges are <1,2>, <2,4>, <5,4>, <5,6>. In an undirected graph, these are equivalent to (1,2), (2,4), (4,5), (5,6).\nThe shortest path from node 1 to node 6 is found by traversing the connected edges.\nStarting from 1, we must go to 2.\nFrom 2, we can go to 4.\nFrom 4, we can go to 5.\nFrom 5, we can go to 6.\nThis gives the path [1, 2, 4, 5, 6] with a length of 4 edges. This is the only valid path among the options that connects 1 to 6 and uses only the given edges.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{C}$.",
