    "global index": "MMLUPro_computer science_9336",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Two computers communicate with each other by sending data packets across a local area network. The size of these packets is 1,000 bytes. The network has the capacity to carry 1,000 packets per second. The CPU time required to execute the network protocol to send one packet is 10 milliseconds. The maximum rate at which one computer can send data to another is approximately\n\nOptions: \nA. 10,000 bytes/second\nB. 200,000 bytes/second\nC. 750,000 bytes/second\nD. 50,000 bytes/second\nE. 1,000,000 bytes/second\nF. 25,000 bytes/ second\nG. 2,500,000 bytes/second\nH. 100,000 bytes/ second\nI. 5,000,000 bytes/second\nJ. 500,000 bytes/ second\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Here's how to determine the maximum rate:\n\nThe network capacity is 1,000 packets per second. Each packet is 1,000 bytes. Therefore, the network's maximum data transfer rate based on capacity is $1,000 \\text{ packets/second} \\times 1,000 \\text{ bytes/packet} = 1,000,000 \\text{ bytes/second}$.\n\nThe CPU time to send one packet is 10 milliseconds, which is 0.01 seconds. This means the CPU can handle $1 \\text{ packet} / 0.01 \\text{ seconds} = 100 \\text{ packets/second}$.\n\nThe bottleneck is the CPU time. The maximum rate is limited by how many packets the CPU can process. Therefore, the maximum data rate is $100 \\text{ packets/second} \\times 1,000 \\text{ bytes/packet} = 100,000 \\text{ bytes/second}$.\n\nThe correct answer is H.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{H}$.",
