form the vertex sets: $10 \\times 6 = 60$.\n   - In this case, all components have size 2, 1, 1, 1, 1.\n   - Number of trees on 2 vertices is $2^{2-2} = 1$.\n   - Total: $60 \\times 1 \\times 1 \\times 1 \\times 1 = 60$.\n\nSumming these up: $80 + 180 + 60 = 320$.\n\nLet's verify this with a known formula.\nThe number of labeled forests on $n$ vertices with $k$ components is given by $k n^{n-k-1}$. This is for the case where components are not distinguished by their vertex sets.\n\nConsider the problem of counting labeled forests with $k$ components using a general theorem.\nThe number of labeled forests on $n$ vertices with $k$ connected components is $k n^{n-k-1}$. This formula assumes that the components are not distinguished.\n\nHowever, our problem has a strong condition that distinguishes the components based on the presence of vertices 1-5.\n\nLet's look at the options. 320 is an option.\n\nLet's try to re-interpret the problem using Prufer sequences for forests.\n\nConsider an alternative approach using inclusion-exclusion on the number of components.\n\nLet's consider the problem of counting labeled graphs with $n$ vertices and exactly $k$ connected components.\n\nA key result states that the number of labeled forests on $n$ vertices having exactly $k$ components is $k n^{n-k-1}$. If $k=1$, it's $1 \\cdot n^{n-1-1} = n^{n-2}$, the number of labeled trees.\nThis formula counts all possible forests with $k$ components without any constraints on which vertices belong to which component.\n\nOur problem has the constraint that vertices 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 are in different components. This means we are counting forests where 5 specific vertices are in distinct components.\n\nLet $N(n, k)$ be the number of labeled forests on $n$ vertices with $k$ components.\nWe are asked for $N=8$, $k=5$, with the additional condition.\n\nLet's confirm the calculation for the distribution of {6, 7, 8} into 5 distinct components that already contain {1}, {2}, {3}, {4}, {5}.\nComponent 1