320$.\n\nThis detailed breakdown matches option F.\n\nFinal check of the logic:\nWe are forming a forest with 8 vertices and 5 connected components.\nThe constraint is that vertices 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 are in different components.\nThis implies that we have 5 distinct components, and each of {1, 2, 3, 4, 5} belongs to exactly one of these components.\nWe need to distribute the remaining 3 vertices ({6, 7, 8}) among these 5 components.\nFor each way of distributing the vertices, we then form a tree within each component.\nThe number of labeled trees on $k$ vertices is $k^{k-2}$ for $k \\ge 2$, and 1 for $k=1$.\n\nThe calculation seems correct.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{320}$.",
