    "global index": "MMLUPro_computer science_9354",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Let $X_0, X_1, X_2, \\ldots$ be drawn i.i.d. from $p(x)$, and $x\\in\\{{1,2,3,\\ldots,100\\}}. Let $N$ be the waiting time to the next occurrence of $X_0$. Compute $E(N)$.\n\nOptions: \nA. 99.0\nB. 101.0\nC. 125.0\nD. 150.0\nE. 50.0\nF. 25.0\nG. 175.0\nH. 100.0\nI. 200.0\nJ. 75.0\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Let $X_0$ be the observed value. Since the random variables $X_i$ are drawn i.i.d. from a discrete uniform distribution over the set $\\{1, 2, \\ldots, 100\\}$, the probability of observing any specific value $k \\in \\{1, 2, \\ldots, 100\\}$ is $p(k) = \\frac{1}{100}$. The random variable $N$ represents the waiting time to the next occurrence of the value $X_0$. If $X_0 = x$, then $N$ is the number of trials until we observe the value $x$ again.\n\nThis is a geometric distribution problem. Let \"success\" be the event that we observe the value $X_0$. For a specific value $x$ that $X_0$ takes, the probability of success is $p = P(X_i = x) = \\frac{1}{100}$. The number of trials $N$ until the first success (observing $x$ again) follows a geometric distribution with parameter $p$. The expected value of a geometric distribution with parameter $p$ is $E(N) = \\frac{1}{p}$.\n\nIn this case, $p = \\frac{1}{100}$, so $E(N) = \\frac{1}{1/100} = 100$.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{H}$.",
