    "global index": "MMLUPro_computer science_9355",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: There are only three active stations in a slotted Aloha network: A, B and C. Each station generates a frame in a time slot with the corresponding probabilities p_A=0.2, p_B=0.3 and p_C=0.4 respectively. What is the normalized throughput of the system?\n\nOptions: \nA. 0.700\nB. 0.275\nC. 0.250\nD. 0.300\nE. 0.528\nF. 0.452\nG. 0.600\nH. 0.485\nI. 0.350\nJ. 0.500\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The normalized throughput (S) of a slotted Aloha system is given by the formula $S = G * e^{-G}$, where G is the average number of frame arrivals per slot. In this case, G is the sum of the probabilities of each station generating a frame: $G = p_A + p_B + p_C = 0.2 + 0.3 + 0.4 = 0.9$.\nPlugging this value into the throughput formula, we get $S = 0.9 * e^{-0.9}$. Calculating this value gives approximately 0.3659. However, this formula is for the ideal case where new frames can always be generated. For a finite number of stations with given probabilities, the probability of at least one frame being sent is $P(\\text{success}) = p_A(1-p_B)(1-p_C) + (1-p_A)p_B(1-p_C) + (1-p_A)(1-p_B)p_C$. This can be generalized as the probability of exactly one station transmitting, which is the sum of probabilities of each station transmitting while others do not.\n$P(\\text{one transmission}) = p_A(1-p_B)(1-p_C) + (1-p_A)p_B(1-p_C) + (1-p_A)(1-p_B)p_C$\n$P(\\text{one transmission}) = 0.2(1-0.3)(1-0.4) + (1-0.2)0.3(1-0.4) + (1-0.2)(1-0.3)0.4$\n$P(\\text{one transmission}) = 0.2(0.7)(0.6) + (0.8)(0.3)(0.6) + (0.8)(0.7)(0.4)$\n$P(\\text{one transmission}) = 0.084 + 0.144 + 0.224 = 0.452$.\nThe normalized throughput is the probability that exactly one station transmits a frame in a time slot.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{F}$.",
