es during the ADC process.\nFor each sample: 5 instructions, then 12 us ADC.\nLet's assume the CPU can execute these 5 instructions in parallel with the ADC if the ADC is not the bottleneck in its own timing mechanism. But \"triggered by a CPU instruction\" suggests a sequence.\n\nLet's assume the most straightforward interpretation of \"keeping up\": the total time to acquire and process one set of 4 samples must be less than or equal to the time it takes to acquire the next set of 4 samples.\nTotal acquisition time for 4 samples = 4 * 12 us = 48 us.\nTotal processing time for one set = 48 us (ADC) + 30T us (CPU).\n\nSo, 48 + 30T <= 48. This implies T <= 0. This is the fundamental contradiction.\n\nLet's consider a different perspective.\nThe system produces an output every time it processes 4 inputs.\nThe rate of the ADC is the bottleneck on input.\nOne ADC conversion takes 12 us. For 4 inputs, this is 48 us of ADC time dedicated to conversions for one set.\nThe CPU must execute 30 instructions during this process.\n\nLet's assume the total time for one cycle is limited by the total time the CPU is busy or needs to wait for the ADC.\nTotal time = (5 instructions + 12 us) * 4 + 10 instructions * T\nThis is still the same expression.\n\nWhat if the constraint isn't that the total time is <= 48, but rather the total CPU time must be such that it doesn't delay the ADC conversions beyond a certain point?\n\nLet's assume the answer is one of the options and try to find a logical path.\nLet's try Option I: 1.2 microseconds.\nIf T = 1.2 us, total time = 48 + 30 * 1.2 = 48 + 36 = 84 us.\n\nLet's consider the *available* CPU time.\nThe ADC takes 48 microseconds in total for the 4 samples.\nIf the CPU is to keep up, it must execute 30 instructions within the *total time allotted for one processing cycle*.\n\nWhat is the total time allotted for one processing cycle? It's tied to the ADC rate.\nIf the ADC takes 12 us per sample, and we have 4 samples, the *rate* at which new data is available is every 12 us if sampled continuously, or a batch every 48 us.\n\nLet's assume the system must operate on a 48 microsecond cycle.\nIn this 48 microsecond cycle, the ADC performs its 48 microseconds of work.\nThe CPU must perform its 30 instructions within this same 48 microsecond cycle.\n\nSo, the time spent by the CPU on instructions must be such that it fits within the 48 microsecond window.\nHowever, the ADC operation itself takes 48 microseconds. This means there is no time left for CPU instructions if the total cycle is 48 microseconds.\n\nThis is where buffering matters. Buffering allows the CPU to prepare its work while the ADC is busy.\n\nLet's model the processing rate.\nThe system needs to complete 30 instructions and 48 microseconds of ADC per cycle.\nThe rate of the ADC is the fundamental limiting factor on how often a cycle can be completed.\n\nConsider the total processing needed: 48 us of ADC time + 30 instructions.\nThis processing needs to happen within the real-time constraint.\nThe real-time constraint is dictated by the ADC.\n\nLet's assume that the time to convert the output from digital to analog (which is ignored) would also take time.\n\nLet's consider the options again. If T = 1.2 microseconds.\nTotal CPU instruction time = 30 * 1.2 = 36 microseconds.\nTotal ADC time = 48 microseconds.\nCombined \"workload\" = 36 + 48 = 84 microseconds.\n\nThis 84 microseconds of work needs to be completed within a certain period. What is that period?\n\nPerhaps the constraint is that the total *instruction execution time* for processing a set of 4 samples must be less than or equal to the time the ADC is busy? This would mean 30T <= 48. T <= 48/30 = 1.6 microseconds.\nThis fits option F (1.6 microseconds).\n\nLet's test this hypothesis.\nIf T = 1.6 microseconds, then the total CPU processing time is 30 * 1.6 = 48 microseconds.\nThe ADC time is also 48 microseconds.\nThis means that the CPU work and the ADC work could potentially be interleaved and completed within a 48-microsecond timeframe if perfectly overlapped.\n\nLet's re-evaluate:\nTo digitize 4 signals, the ADC takes 4 * 12 = 48 microseconds.\nTo average and output 4 signals, the CPU takes 10 instructions.\nTo trigger each of the 4 signals, the CPU takes 5 instructions. Total for triggers: 4 * 5 = 20 instructions.\nTotal CPU instructions = 10 + 20 = 30 instructions.\nTotal CPU time = 30 * T.\n\nIf the system is to keep up, the work done by the CPU and the ADC must be completed within a cycle dictated by the ADC.\nThe ADC itself takes 48 microseconds.\nIf the CPU time (30T) must not exceed this, then 30T <= 48.\nT <= 48 / 30 = 1.6 microseconds.\n\nThis seems like a plausible interpretation that fits one of the options. The CPU's task (30 instructions) must be completed within the time the ADC is performing its primary operation (48 microseconds of conversions).\n\nLet's confirm this with the wording: \"maximum average instruction execution time that allows the microcomputer to keep up with the input-output data rates\".\nThe input data rate is paced by the ADC's 12 microseconds per conversion. For 4 samples, this is a 48-microsecond process.\nThe CPU must perform its associated tasks within this same timeframe. Buffering helps with interleaving.\n\nIf T = 1.6 microseconds, then 30T = 48 microseconds.\nThis means the CPU is busy for exactly the same amount of time the ADC is busy.\nThis suggests an ideal overlap where the CPU completes its programmed tasks precisely as the ADC finishes its conversions, and the output is generated.\n\nConsider the total time for one full cycle.\nIt's the sum of ADC time and CPU time *if they were sequential*.\nBut with buffering, they are interleaved.\nThe total time is determined by the longest path.\n\nIf the CPU executes 30 instructions, taking 30T time, and the ADC takes 48 microseconds.\nIf 30T <= 48, it means the CPU's entire task can be performed within the time the ADC is doing its work.\nThis would allow