    "global index": "MMLUPro_computer science_9383",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Consider $x(t)$ to be given as, $$ x(t)=10 \\cos (20 \\pi-\\pi / 4)-5 \\cos (50 \\pi t) $$ What is minimum sampling rate (/Hz) such that $y(t)=x(t)$ ?\n\nOptions: \nA. 30\nB. 20\nC. 40\nD. 50\nE. 35\nF. 70\nG. 25\nH. 60\nI. 100\nJ. 80\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The given signal is $x(t)=10 \\cos (20 \\pi t-\\pi / 4)-5 \\cos (50 \\pi t)$.\nThis signal is a sum of two cosine functions.\nThe first cosine function has an angular frequency of $\\omega_1 = 20\\pi$ rad/s, which corresponds to a frequency $f_1 = \\omega_1 / (2\\pi) = (20\\pi) / (2\\pi) = 10$ Hz.\nThe second cosine function has an angular frequency of $\\omega_2 = 50\\pi$ rad/s, which corresponds to a frequency $f_2 = \\omega_2 / (2\\pi) = (50\\pi) / (2\\pi) = 25$ Hz.\nAccording to Nyquist's sampling theorem, the sampling rate must be at least twice the highest frequency component present in the signal to avoid aliasing.\nIn this case, the highest frequency component is $f_{\\text{max}} = \\max(f_1, f_2) = \\max(10, 25) = 25$ Hz.\nTherefore, the minimum sampling rate $f_s$ must be $f_s \\ge 2 f_{\\text{max}} = 2 \\times 25 = 50$ Hz.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{D}$.",
      "success": true,
