  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_computer science_9424",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A sorted list of 120 integers is to be searched to determine whether the value 100 is in the list. Assuming that the most efficient searching algorithm is used, what is the maximum number of elements that must be examined?\n\nOptions: \nA. 100\nB. 30\nC. 120\nD. 10\nE. 8\nF. 7\nG. 15\nH. 12\nI. 20\nJ. 60\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The problem describes searching for a value in a sorted list of 120 integers using the most efficient searching algorithm. For a sorted list, the most efficient searching algorithm is binary search. Binary search repeatedly divides the search interval in half. The maximum number of elements examined in a binary search is logarithmic to the number of elements.\n\nTo find the maximum number of elements examined, we can calculate $\\lceil\\log_2(n)\\rceil$, where n is the number of elements in the list. In this case, n = 120.\n\n$\\log_2(120)$ is between $\\log_2(64) = 6$ and $\\log_2(128) = 7$.\nSince $2^7 = 128$ and $2^6 = 64$, we know that $6 < \\log_2(120) < 7$.\nTherefore, $\\lceil\\log_2(120)\\rceil = 7$.\n\nLet's trace the maximum number of comparisons (examinations) for a list of size 120:\n1. Interval size: 120. Compare middle element. Remaining elements at most 60.\n2. Interval size: 60. Compare middle element. Remaining elements at most 30.\n3. Interval size: 30. Compare middle element. Remaining elements at most 15.\n4. Interval size: 15. Compare middle element. Remaining elements at most 7.\n5. Interval size: 7. Compare middle element. Remaining elements at most 3.\n6. Interval size: 3. Compare middle element. Remaining elements at most 1.\n7. Interval size: 1. Compare middle element. The element is found or not.\n\nIn the worst case, 7 comparisons are needed to narrow down the search to a single element or determine its absence. So, the maximum number of elements examined is 7.\n\nComparing this with the given options:\nA. 100\nB. 30\nC. 120\nD. 10\nE. 8\nF. 7\nG. 15\nH. 12\nI. 20\nJ. 60\n\nThe calculated maximum number of elements examined is 7, which corresponds to option F.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{F}$.",
