    "global index": "MMLUPro_computer science_9427",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Consider the following instance variable and methods in the same class.\n private int[][] matrix;\n /** Precondition: array.length > 0.\n * @return the largest integer in array\n */\n private int max(int[] array)\n {{ /* implementation not shown */ }}\n /** @return num1 if num1 >= num2; otherwise return num2\n */\n public int max(int num1, int num2)\n {{ /* implementation not shown */ }}\n Suppose matrix has a current value of\n 2 1 4 8\n 6 0 3 9\n 5 7 7 6\n 1 2 3 4\n What will be returned by the following method call in the same class?\n max(max(matrix[2]), max(matrix[3]))\n\nOptions: \nA. 8\nB. 0\nC. 1\nD. 2\nE. 9\nF. 4\nG. 5\nH. 7\nI. 10\nJ. 6\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The problem asks us to evaluate a nested method call involving a 2D array and overloaded `max` methods. The call is `max(max(matrix[2]), max(matrix[3]))`.\n\nFirst, let's understand what `matrix[2]` and `matrix[3]` refer to. Given the matrix:\n2 1 4 8\n6 0 3 9\n5 7 7 6\n1 2 3 4\n\n`matrix[2]` refers to the third row (index 2) of the matrix, which is `[5, 7, 7, 6]`.\n`matrix[3]` refers to the fourth row (index 3) of the matrix, which is `[1, 2, 3, 4]`.\n\nNow, let's evaluate the inner `max` calls. We have an overloaded `max` method that takes an integer array and returns the largest integer in the array.\n\nFor `max(matrix[2])`, we call `max` with the array `[5, 7, 7, 6]`. The largest integer in this array is 7.\n\nFor `max(matrix[3])`, we call `max` with the array `[1, 2, 3, 4]`. The largest integer in this array is 4.\n\nNow, the outer `max` call becomes `max(7, 4)`. We have another overloaded `max` method that takes two integers and returns the larger of the two.\n\nCalling `max(7, 4)` will return 7 because 7 is greater than or equal to 4.\n\nTherefore, the method call will return 7.\n\nLet's look at the options:\nA. 8\nB. 0\nC. 1\nD. 2\nE. 9\nF. 4\nG. 5\nH. 7\nI. 10\nJ. 6\n\nThe returned value is 7, which corresponds to option H.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{H}$.",
