  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_computer science_9448",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: If the peak voltage value of a signal is 20 times the peak voltage value of the noise, what is the SNR? What is the $\\mathrm{{SNR}}_{{\\mathrm{{dB}}$ (in 3 sig.fig.)?\n\nOptions: \nA. 15.0\nB. 13.0\nC. 30.0\nD. 34.0\nE. 26.0\nF. 40.0\nG. 18.0\nH. 24.0\nI. 20.0\nJ. 10.0\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The Signal-to-Noise Ratio (SNR) is defined as the ratio of the signal power to the noise power.  Since power is proportional to the square of the voltage, if the peak voltage of the signal is 20 times the peak voltage of the noise (let $V_{s}$ be the signal voltage and $V_{n}$ be the noise voltage, so $V_{s} = 20 V_{n}$), then the SNR in terms of voltage is $V_{s}/V_{n} = 20$.\n\nThe SNR in decibels is given by $\\mathrm{SNR}_{\\mathrm{dB}} = 10 \\log_{10} (\\frac{P_{s}}{P_{n}})$. Since $P \\propto V^2$, we have $\\frac{P_{s}}{P_{n}} = (\\frac{V_{s}}{V_{n}})^2 = (20)^2 = 400$.\nTherefore, $\\mathrm{SNR}_{\\mathrm{dB}} = 10 \\log_{10} (400) \\approx 10 \\times 2.602 = 26.02$.\n\nRounding to 3 significant figures, $\\mathrm{SNR}_{\\mathrm{dB}} = 26.0$.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{E}$.",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 190,
        "output_tokens": 283,
        "total_tokens": 473
      },
      "provider": "openrouter",
      "error": null
    },
    "cost": null,
    "accuracy": null
  },
  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_computer science_9449",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Given 3 Colors whose RGB representations are given as follows: Color 1: (0.5, 0.5, 0.5), Color 2: (0.4, 0.6, 0.5), Color 3: (0.3, 0.7, 0.5), Which Color does not carry chrominance (Color) Information? Answer with 1 or 2 or 3.\n\nOptions: \nA. None of the above\nB. Both 1 and 2\nC. Both 2 and 3\nD. All of the above\nE. 3\nF. 1\nG. Only 1 and 2 carry chrominance information\nH. 2\nI. Only 2 and 3 carry chrominance information\nJ. Both 1 and 3\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The question asks which color does not carry chrominance (color) information. Chrominance represents the color aspect of a signal (hue and saturation), while luminance represents the brightness. In RGB, luminance is generally associated with the average of the R, G, and B components, while chrominance is related to the differences between these components.\n\nLet's analyze each color:\n\nColor 1: (0.5, 0.5, 0.5)\nHere, all three components are equal. This represents a shade of gray. In a grayscale image, there is no color information, only brightness.\n\nColor 2: (0.4, 0.6, 0.5)\nThe components are different, indicating a color.\n\nColor 3: (0.3, 0.7, 0.5)\nThe components are different, indicating a color.\n\nTherefore, Color 1 does not carry chrominance information as it is a shade of gray.\n\nNow let's look at the options:\nA. None of the above\nB. Both 1 and 2\nC. Both 2 and 3\nD. All of the above\nE. 3\nF. 1\nG. Only 1 and 2 carry chrominance information\nH. 2\nI. Only 2 and 3 carry chrominance information\nJ. Both 1 and 3\n\nOption F states \"1\". This corresponds to Color 1, which we identified as not carrying chrominance information.\n\nThe luminace can be approximated by the average of the R, G, and B values.\nColor 1: (0.5 + 0.5 + 0.5) / 3 = 0.5\nColor 2: (0.4 + 0.6 + 0.5) / 3 = 1.5 / 3 = 0.5\nColor 3: (0.3 + 0.7 + 0.5) / 3 = 1.5 / 3 = 0.5\n\nIn all three colors, the luminance is the same. However, chrominance is related to the deviations from the average.\nFor Color 1, the deviations are (0.5-0.5, 0.5-0.5, 0.5-0.5) = (0, 0, 0). This implies no color information.\nFor Color 2 and Color 3, the deviations are not all zero, indicating the presence of color information.\n\nThe most likely correct answer is the option that identifies Color 1 as not carrying chrominance information.\n\nThe correct option is F.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{F}$.",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 239,
        "output_tokens": 589,
        "total_tokens": 828
      },
      "provider": "openrouter",
      "error": null
    },
    "cost": null,
    "accuracy": null
  },
  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_computer science_9450",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In a CSMA/CD network with a data rate of 10 Mbps, the minimum frame size is found to be 512 bits for the correct operation of the collision detection process. What should be the minimum frame size (in bits) if we increase the data rate to 1 Gbps?\n\nOptions: \nA. 25600\nB. 10000\nC. 102400\nD. 32000\nE. 51200\nF. 6400\nG. 204800\nH. 256000\nI. 409600\nJ. 12800\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The minimum frame size in a CSMA/CD network is directly proportional to the data rate and the propagation delay. The formula for the minimum frame size (M) is given by:\n\n$M = 2 \\times$ (maximum propagation delay) $\\times$ (data rate)\n\nIn CSMA/CD, the minimum frame size is set such that a frame transmitted must take at least the duration of the maximum propagation delay to travel across the network and back for collision detection. This ensures that the sender can detect a collision before it finishes transmitting the frame.\n\nLet $R_1$ be the initial data rate and $M_1$ be the initial minimum frame size.\nLet $R_2$ be the new data rate and $M_2$ be the new minimum frame size.\n\nWe are given:\n$R_1 = 10$ Mbps $= 10,000,000$ bits/second\n$M_1 = 512$ bits\n\nWe need to find $M_2$ when $R_2 = 1$ Gbps $= 1,000,000,000$ bits/second.\n\nThe maximum propagation delay (T_prop_max) is a property of the network medium and hardware, and it remains constant regardless of the data rate.\nSo, $M_1 = 2 \\times T_{\\text{prop\\_max}} \\times R_1$\nAnd $M_2 = 2 \\times T_{\\text{prop\\_max}} \\times R_2$\n\nFrom the first equation, we can find the maximum propagation delay:\n$T_{\\text{prop\\_max}} = M_1 / (2 \\times R_1)$\n$T_{\\text{prop\\_max}} = 512 \\text{ bits} / (2 \\times 10,000,000 \\text{ bits/second})$\n$T_{\\text{prop\\_max}} = 512 / 20,000,000$ seconds\n$T_{\\text{prop\\_max}} = 256 \\times 10^{-7}$ seconds\n\nNow, we can calculate the new minimum frame size:\n$M_2 = 2 \\times T_{\\text{prop\\_max}} \\times R_2$\n$M_2 = 2 \\times (256 \\times 10^{-7} \\text{ seconds}) \\times (1,000,000,000 \\text{ bits/second})$\n$M_2 = 2 \\times 256 \\times 10^{-7} \\times 10^9$ bits\n$M_2 = 512 \\times 10^2$ bits\n$M_2 = 51200$ bits\n\nAlternatively, we can use the ratio:\n$M_2 / M_1 = (2 \\times T_{\\text{prop\\_max}} \\times R_2) / (2 \\times T_{\\text{prop\\_max}} \\times R_1)$\n$M_2 / M_1 = R_2 / R_1$\n$M_2 = M_1 \\times (R_2 / R_1)$\n$M_2 = 512 \\text{ bits} \\times (1 \\text{ Gbps} / 10 \\text{ Mbps})$\n$M_2 = 512 \\text{ bits} \\times (1000 \\text{ Mbps} / 10 \\text{ Mbps})$ (since 1 Gbps = 1000 Mbps)\n$M_2 = 512 \\text{ bits} \\times 100$\n$M_2 = 51200$ bits\n\nComparing this result with the given options:\nA. 25600\nB. 10000\nC. 102400\nD. 32000\nE. 51200\nF. 6400\nG. 204800\nH. 256000\nI. 409600\nJ. 12800\n\nThe calculated minimum frame size is 51200 bits, which corresponds to option E.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{E}$.",
