  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_computer science_9465",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Company X shipped 5 computer chips, 1 of which was defective, and Company Y shipped 4 computer chips, 2 of which were defective. One computer chip is to be chosen uniformly at random from the 9 chips shipped by the companies. If the chosen chip is found to be defective, what is the probability that the chip came from Company Y?\n\nOptions: \nA. 3 / 9\nB. 4 / 9\nC. 5 / 9\nD. 5 / 6\nE. 3 / 4\nF. 1 / 3\nG. 1 / 2\nH. 2 / 9\nI. 2 / 3\nJ. 1 / 9\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Let D be the event that the chosen chip is defective, and Y be the event that the chip came from Company Y.\nThe total number of chips is 5 (from X) + 4 (from Y) = 9.\nThe number of defective chips from Company X is 1.\nThe number of defective chips from Company Y is 2.\nThe total number of defective chips is 1 + 2 = 3.\n\nWe are asked to find the probability that the chip came from Company Y given that it is defective, which is P(Y | D).\nUsing Bayes' theorem, P(Y | D) = P(D | Y) * P(Y) / P(D).\n\nP(D | Y) is the probability of choosing a defective chip given it came from Company Y. There are 4 chips from Y and 2 are defective, so P(D | Y) = 2/4 = 1/2.\nP(Y) is the probability of choosing a chip from Company Y. There are 9 chips in total and 4 are from Y, so P(Y) = 4/9.\nP(D) is the probability of choosing a defective chip. There are 3 defective chips out of 9 total, so P(D) = 3/9 = 1/3.\n\nNow, substitute these values into Bayes' theorem:\nP(Y | D) = (1/2) * (4/9) / (1/3)\nP(Y | D) = (4/18) / (1/3)\nP(Y | D) = (2/9) / (1/3)\nP(Y | D) = (2/9) * 3\nP(Y | D) = 6/9 = 2/3.\n\nAlternatively, we can directly use the definition of conditional probability.\nThe event of interest is that the chip is defective AND from Company Y.\nThe number of defective chips from Company Y is 2.\nThe total number of defective chips is 3.\nSo, the probability that a defective chip came from Company Y is the number of defective chips from Y divided by the total number of defective chips.\nP(Y | D) = (Number of defective chips from Y) / (Total number of defective chips) = 2 / 3.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{I}$.",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 215,
        "output_tokens": 519,
        "total_tokens": 734
      },
      "provider": "openrouter",
      "error": null
