    "global index": "MMLUPro_computer science_9279",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Let F: K \\times R \\to MF:K×R→M be a secure PRF. For m \\in Mm∈M define E(k,m) = \\big[ r \\gets R,\\ \\text{{output }} \\big(r,\\ F(k,r) \\oplus m\\big)\\ \\big]E(k,m)=[r←R, output (r, F(k,r)⊕m) ] Is EE symantically secure under CPA?\n\nOptions: \nA. It depends on the size of R\nB. Yes, but only if R is large enough so r never repeats (w.h.p)\nC. Yes, but only if M is small enough so m never repeats (w.h.p)\nD. No, unless F is a weak PRF\nE. No, unless F is a strong PRF\nF. Yes, but only if K is large enough so k never repeats (w.h.p)\nG. It depends on the size of K\nH. No, there is always a CPA attack on this system\nI. It depends on what F is used\nJ. Yes, whenever F is a secure PRF\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The provided encryption scheme $E(k,m) = [r \\gets R, \\text{output } (r, F(k,r) \\oplus m)]$ is essentially a variation of the One-Time Pad (OTP) combined with a Pseudo-Random Function (PRF). If $R$ is chosen from a sufficiently large space (e.g., the same size as $M$ or larger) and $r$ is chosen randomly for each encryption, then for a fixed key $k$, $F(k,r)$ acts like a random mask. This structure strongly resembles an OTP where the \"key\" is generated by the PRF. A secure PRF is designed to behave like a random function, and when combined with a random $r$, it generates a nearly random mask. This property is sufficient for semantic security under CPA, provided the PRF is secure.\n\nThe correct answer is **J**.\n\nExplanation:\nThe scheme $E(k,m) = [r \\gets R, F(k,r) \\oplus m]$ is a form of a one-time pad if $r$ is chosen uniformly and independently from a sufficiently large space for each encryption. The PRF $F(k,r)$ generates a pseudo-random mask. If $F$ is a secure PRF, then $F(k,r)$ will be indistinguishable from a truly random value for an adversary without the key $k$. This makes the encryption semantically secure under CPA, as an adversary cannot distinguish between encryptions of two different messages. The size of $R$ being large enough is implicitly assumed for $r$ to be effectively random for each encryption, and a secure PRF handles the randomness of the output.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{J}$.",
      "success": true,
