    "global index": "MMLUPro_computer science_9470",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Assume that a TLS connection has been established successfully between a client and a server. Establishing the session included checking the server certificate and executing a DiffieHelmann exchange, but the client did not provide a client certificate. Further, assume that the client and server are honest, that the client and server don’t leak their keys, and that the cryptography is good. Which of the following attacks does TLS protect against?\n1. An attacker replacing bytes sent by a client with bytes of the attacker’s own choosing.\n2. An attacker reading the plaintext bytes sent by a client.\n\nOptions: \nA. False, True\nB. False, False\nC. False, Partly True\nD. Partly True, False\nE. True, False\nF. True, Partly False\nG. True, Partly True\nH. Partly False, True\nI. True, True\nJ. Partly True, True\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Let's analyze each attack in the context of a successfully established TLS connection with the given assumptions.\n\n1.  **An attacker replacing bytes sent by a client with bytes of the attacker’s own choosing.** This describes a \"man-in-the-middle\" attack where an attacker intercepts and modifies data in transit. TLS, through its use of message authentication codes (MACs) or authenticated encryption with associated data (AEAD) which is part of the negotiated cipher suite, ensures the integrity of the transmitted data. If an attacker modifies the bytes, the MAC will not match, and the recipient will detect the tampering. Therefore, TLS protects against this. So, the answer to the first part is \"True\".\n\n2.  **An attacker reading the plaintext bytes sent by a client.** The core purpose of TLS is to provide confidentiality. After the handshake, including the Diffie-Hellman exchange, the client and server have established a shared secret key used to encrypt all subsequent application data. An attacker who intercepts the traffic will only see ciphertext, not the original plaintext. Therefore, TLS protects against eavesdropping and reading plaintext. So, the answer to the second part is \"True\".\n\nBased on this analysis, both statements are protected by TLS. This corresponds to \"True, True\".\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{I}$.",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 246,
        "output_tokens": 273,
        "total_tokens": 519
      },
      "provider": "openrouter",
      "error": null
