      "generated_answer": "Mergesort requires efficient splitting and merging operations. Splitting an array or a linked list in half is generally O(1) or O(n) depending on how it's implemented and whether we're talking about the initial split or finding the middle element. Merging two sorted sub-arrays (or sub-lists) is the crucial part for achieving O(n log n) time complexity. Merging two sorted arrays can be done in O(n) time by using auxiliary space. Merging two sorted linked lists can also be done in O(n) time by rearranging pointers. Therefore, all three data structures can support mergesort with O(n log n) time complexity if implemented appropriately, with arrays often being preferred in practice due to cache locality.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{D}$.",
