    "global index": "MMLUPro_computer science_9101",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which of the following problems can be solved by a standard greedy algorithm?\nI. Finding a minimum spanning tree in an undirected graph with positive-integer edge weights\nII. Finding a maximum clique in an undirected graph\nIII. Finding a maximum flow from a source node to a sink node in a directed graph with positive-integer edge capacities\n\nOptions: \nA. None of the above\nB. III only\nC. I, II and III\nD. III, and II, but not always I\nE. I, and II, but not always III\nF. I and II only\nG. II only\nH. II and III only\nI. I only\nJ. I and III only\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Let's analyze each problem:\n\nI. Finding a minimum spanning tree (MST) in an undirected graph with positive-integer edge weights. Algorithms like Kruskal's and Prim's are standard greedy algorithms that correctly solve this problem.\n\nII. Finding a maximum clique in an undirected graph. This is a known NP-hard problem, and no standard greedy algorithm is guaranteed to find the optimal solution for all cases. Greedy approaches for clique finding typically yield approximate solutions.\n\nIII. Finding a maximum flow from a source node to a sink node in a directed graph with positive-integer edge capacities. The Ford-Fulkerson algorithm, which can be implemented greedily by repeatedly finding augmenting paths, can solve this problem. However, to guarantee termination and find the maximum flow efficiently, especially with integer capacities, some variants (like Edmonds-Karp) use a specific greedy strategy (shortest augmenting path).\n\nBased on this analysis, problem I can be solved by a standard greedy algorithm. Problem III can also be solved by a greedy approach (e.g., Ford-Fulkerson with augmenting paths). Problem II cannot be solved optimally by a standard greedy algorithm.\n\nTherefore, the problems that can be solved by a standard greedy algorithm are I and III.\n\nComparing with the options:\nJ. I and III only.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{J}$.",
