ings:\nA = BC\nB = x...x (one or more x's)\nC can be B (so x...x)\nC can be D.\n  D can be y.\n  D can be Ey. E is z. So D can be zy.\n\nSo, the possible strings generated are:\nA = BC\nB can be x, xx, xxx, ...\nC can be B (so x, xx, ...) or D (so y, zy).\n\nCase 1: C is B.\nA = BB\nB = x...x\nA = (x...x)(x...x)  -> any string of two or more x's.\n\nCase 2: C is D.\nA = BD\nB = x...x\nD = y or zy\n\nSubcase 2.1: D = y\nA = By\nA = (x...x)y\nExamples: xy, xxy, xxxy, ...\n\nSubcase 2.2: D = zy\nA = Bzy\nA = (x...x)zy\nExamples: xzy, xxzy, xxxzy, ...\n\nLet's check the options against these possibilities:\n\nA. **xy**: Matches (x...x)y with one x. Possible.\nB. **xxzy**: Matches (x...x)zy with two x's. Possible.\nC. **xz**: Does not match any pattern. \"xz\" requires 'x' followed by 'z'. B produces 'x's. D produces 'y' or 'zy'. C can be B or D.\n   If B produces 'x', C needs to produce 'z'. C can only produce 'x's (if it's B) or 'y'/'zy' (if it's D). So 'z' is not possible from C.\n   Therefore, 'xz' is not possible.\n\nD. **xxxxxy**: Matches (x...x)y with five x's. Possible.\nE. **xyz**: Does not match any pattern. It has 'x' followed by 'yz'.\n   If B is 'x', C needs to produce 'yz'. C can be B (produces 'x's) or D (produces 'y' or 'zy'). Neither is 'yz'.\n   So 'xyz' is not possible.\n\nF. **xyzy**: Does not match any pattern.\n   If B is 'x', C needs to produce 'yzy'. C can be B (produces 'x's) or D (produces 'y' or 'zy'). Neither is 'yzy'.\n   So 'xyzy' is not possible.\n\nNow we have three candidates: C, E, and F. The question asks for *which of the following is not a sentence that is generated*. This implies there is only one such sentence among the options. Let's re-examine the derivation very carefully.\n\nLet's start with the potential strings and see if they can be derived.\n\n**A. xy**\nA -> BC\nB -> x\nC -> D -> y\nSo: A -> BC -> x C -> x D -> x y. **Possible.**\n\n**B. xxzy**\nA -> BC\nB -> x | Bx. B can generate xx.\nC -> D -> Ey. E -> z. So, D can gener