g_2(N)$.\n\nConsider the number of nodes N = $2^{h_{min}+1}-1 + \\text{additional nodes}$.\nThe maximum height is h.\nIf $h/h_{min} \\le c$, this puts a constraint on the structure of the tree.\n\nIt has been shown that for a full binary tree where the ratio of the lengths of any two root-to-leaf paths is bounded by c, the height h satisfies $h \\le c \\log_2(N)$.\n\nThe reasoning is based on the fact that if $h > c \\log_2 N$, then for a strictly binary tree, we must have $h_{min} < \\log_2 N$, and $h/h_{min} > c$, which contradicts the given condition.\n\nLet's verify this. Assume $h > c \\log_2 N$.\nSince $N \\le 2^{h+1}-1$, we have $h \\ge \\log_2(N+1)-1$.\nSince $N \\ge 2h_{min}+1$, we have $h_{min} \\le (N-1)/2$.\n\nIf $h > c \\log_2 N$, then $h/c > \\log_2 N$.\nWe need to show that this implies $h/h_{min} > c$ for some $h_{min}$.\nIf $h > c \\log_2 N$, can we always find a configuration such that $h_{min} < (\\log_2 N)$?\nIf $h_{min}$ is small, that implies the tree is skewed.\n\nConsider a strictly binary tree. The number of nodes at depth d is at most $2^d$.\nThe total number of nodes is $N = \\sum_{d=0}^{h} n_d$, where $n_d \\le 2^d$ and $n_h \\ge 1$.\nAlso, for $0 \\le d < h$, $n_d = 2 n_{d+1}$ or $n_d = n_{d+1} + n'_{d+1}$, where $n'_{d+1}$ is the number of nodes with only one child at depth d+1. But in a strictly binary tree, every non-leaf node has two children.\n\nThe condition implies that the tree cannot be too \"skinny\" or too \"bushy\" in an unbalanced way. This limits the height relative to the number of nodes. Option G, $h \\le c \\log_2(N)$, represents this limitation.\n\nFinal check: If $h \\le c \\log_2(N)$, and for a nearly balanced tree $h \\approx \\log_2(N)$, this is satisfied with $c \\ge 1$.\nIf the tree is somewhat skewed, $h$ can be greater than $\\log_2(N)$ but $h_{min}$ would be smaller such that $h/h_{min} \\le c$.\nThis inequality $h \\le c \\log_2(N)$ captures the essence of bounding the height given the constraint on path length ratios.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{G}$.",
