    "global index": "MMLUPro_computer science_9265",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Let T (n) be defined by T(1) = 7 and T(n + 1) = 3n + T(n) for all integers n ≥ 1. Which of the following represents the order of growth of T(n) as a function of n?\n\nOptions: \nA. Θ(n^3 log n)\nB. Θ(n log n^2)\nC. Θ(sqrt(n))\nD. Θ(n log n)\nE. Θ(log n)\nF. Θ(n^3)\nG. Θ(n)\nH. Θ(n^2 sqrt(n))\nI. Θ(n^2 log n)\nJ. Θ(n^2)\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find the order of growth of T(n), we can expand the recurrence relation:\nT(n) = T(n-1) + 3(n-1)\nT(n-1) = T(n-2) + 3(n-2)\n...\nT(2) = T(1) + 3(1)\n\nSubstituting these back, we get:\nT(n) = T(1) + 3(1) + 3(2) + ... + 3(n-1)\nT(n) = 7 + 3 * (1 + 2 + ... + (n-1))\nThe sum of the first (n-1) integers is given by the formula (n-1)n/2.\nSo, T(n) = 7 + 3 * (n-1)n/2\nT(n) = 7 + (3/2) * (n^2 - n)\nT(n) = (3/2)n^2 - (3/2)n + 7\n\nThis is a quadratic polynomial in n. The order of growth of a polynomial is determined by its highest degree term. In this case, the highest degree term is (3/2)n^2.\nTherefore, the order of growth of T(n) is Θ(n^2).\n\nComparing this with the given options:\nA. Θ(n^3 log n)\nB. Θ(n log n^2) = Θ(2n log n) = Θ(n log n)\nC. Θ(sqrt(n))\nD. Θ(n log n)\nE. Θ(log n)\nF. Θ(n^3)\nG. Θ(n)\nH. Θ(n^2 sqrt(n))\nI. Θ(n^2 log n)\nJ. Θ(n^2)\n\nThe correct order of growth is Θ(n^2), which corresponds to option J.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{J}$.",
