    "global index": "MMLUPro_computer science_9333",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which of the following conditions can be expressed by a Boolean formula in the Boolean variables p_1, p_2, p_3, p_4 and the connectives ∧,∨ (without ¬)?\nI. At least three of p_1, p_2, p_3, p_4 are true.\nII. Exactly three of p_1, p_2, p_3, p_4 are true.\nIII. An even number of p_1, p_2, p_3, p_4 are true.\n\nOptions: \nA. None of the above\nB. All of the above\nC. I and III\nD. Only II and III\nE. III only\nF. Only I and II\nG. I only\nH. II and III\nI. II only\nJ. I and II\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Let's analyze each condition:\n\nCondition I: \"At least three of $p_1, p_2, p_3, p_4$ are true.\" This can be expressed by considering all combinations where three or four variables are true. For example, $(p_1 \\land p_2 \\land p_3) \\lor (p_1 \\land p_2 \\land p_4) \\lor (p_1 \\land p_3 \\land p_4) \\lor (p_2 \\land p_3 \\land p_4) \\lor (p_1 \\land p_2 \\land p_3 \\land p_4)$. This uses only conjunctions and disjunctions.\n\nCondition II: \"Exactly three of $p_1, p_2, p_3, p_4$ are true.\" To express \"exactly three\", we can use the \"at least three\" condition and subtract the \"at least four\" condition, but since we can't use negation, this becomes tricky. However, we can directly express it by summing up all unique combinations of three variables being true: $(p_1 \\land p_2 \\land p_3) \\lor (p_1 \\land p_2 \\land p_4) \\lor (p_1 \\land p_3 \\land p_4) \\lor (p_2 \\land p_3 \\land p_4)$. This uses only conjunctions and disjunctions.\n\nCondition III: \"An even number of $p_1, p_2, p_3, p_4$ are true.\" This means zero or two or four variables are true.\nZero variables true: $\\neg p_1 \\land \\neg p_2 \\land \\neg p_3 \\land \\neg p_4$. This requires negation.\nTwo variables true: $(p_1 \\land p_2) \\lor (p_1 \\land p_3) \\lor (p_1 \\land p_4) \\lor (p_2 \\land p_3) \\lor (p_2 \\land p_4) \\lor (p_3 \\land p_4)$. This is possible.\nFour variables true: $p_1 \\land p_2 \\land p_3 \\land p_4$. This is possible.\n\nHowever, the question states \"without ¬\". This is a crucial constraint. For condition III, we need to express \"zero variables true\", which definitively requires negation without any alternative using only $\\land$ and $\\lor$. The combination of \"exactly zero\" cannot be formed using only $\\land$ and $\\lor$ from the given variables $p_i$ being true. Therefore, Condition III cannot be expressed without negation.\n\nRevisiting condition II: \"Exactly three of $p_1, p_2, p_3, p_4$ are true.\" This can indeed be expressed as the disjunction of all combinations where exactly three are true, as shown above, which only uses $\\land$ and $\\lor$.\n\nTherefore, only conditions I and II can be expressed.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{J}$.",
