  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_computer science_9390",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Consider the following three scenarios, which could be modeled using three data structures— dictionary/map, queue, and stack. Scenario 1: Cars line up in a single lane at a car wash. As each driver reaches the entrance of the car wash, the driver gets out of the car. An automatic pulley moves the car through the car wash as the driver walks to the exit of the car wash to pay and pick up the car. Scenario 2: Contestants auditioning for a reality television talent show are assigned a unique numeric ID upon completing a registration form. Scenario 3: Tennis balls are sold in a cylindrical can that can hold a maximum of 3 balls, where each ball except the bottom one rests on top of the ball below it. The 3 balls are placed in the can one at a time through one opening at the top of the can. The 3 balls are removed from the can one at a time through the same opening. Which of the following shows the data structures that best model the scenarios? Scenario 1 | Scenario 2 | Scenario 3\n\nOptions: \nA. Dictionary/map | Stack | Queue\nB. Queue | Stack | Dictionary/map\nC. Stack | Stack | Queue\nD. Stack | Queue | Dictionary/map\nE. Stack | Dictionary/map | Queue\nF. Queue | Queue | Stack\nG. Dictionary/map | Queue | Stack\nH. Dictionary/map | Dictionary/map | Stack\nI. Queue | Dictionary/map | Stack\nJ. Dictionary/map | Queue | Queue\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Let's analyze each scenario:\n\n*   **Scenario 1:** Cars lining up in a car wash perfectly demonstrates a First-In, First-Out (FIFO) principle. The car that arrives first is the first one to be washed. This is the behavior of a queue.\n\n*   **Scenario 2:** Contestants are assigned unique numeric IDs. This implies a way to store and retrieve information based on a unique identifier. A dictionary or map is ideal for this, where the contestant ID would be the key and their associated information would be the value.\n\n*   **Scenario 3:** Tennis balls in a cylindrical can, where balls are added and removed from the same opening, and the last ball placed in is the first one removed. This is a Last-In, First-Out (LIFO) principle, which is the characteristic behavior of a stack.\n\nTherefore, the best data structures for the scenarios are Queue, Dictionary/map, and Stack, respectively.\n\nLooking at the options, Option I matches this: Queue | Dictionary/map | Stack.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{I}$.",
