    "global index": "MMLUPro_computer science_9365",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which of the following algorithms has running time Θ(n^2) in the worst case but Θ(n log n) on average?\n\nOptions: \nA. Counting Sort\nB. Shell Sort\nC. Insertion Sort\nD. Bubblesort\nE. Mergesort\nF. Selection Sort\nG. Bucket Sort\nH. Radix Sort\nI. Quicksort\nJ. Heapsort\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The question asks for an algorithm that has a worst-case running time of $\\Theta(n^2)$ and an average-case running time of $\\Theta(n \\log n)$. Let's analyze the options:\n\n*   **A. Counting Sort:** $\\Theta(n+k)$, where k is the range of input. This is linear in n if k is proportional to n.\n*   **B. Shell Sort:** Worst-case is not $\\Theta(n^2)$ but depends on the gap sequence. Average case is also hard to define precisely for all gap sequences, but generally better than $\\Theta(n^2)$.\n*   **C. Insertion Sort:** Worst-case: $\\Theta(n^2)$ (already sorted input or reverse-sorted input). Average-case: $\\Theta(n^2)$.\n*   **D. Bubblesort:** Worst-case: $\\Theta(n^2)$ (reverse-sorted input). Average-case: $\\Theta(n^2)$.\n*   **E. Mergesort:** Worst-case: $\\Theta(n \\log n)$. Average-case: $\\Theta(n \\log n)$.\n*   **F. Selection Sort:** Worst-case: $\\Theta(n^2)$. Average-case: $\\Theta(n^2)$.\n*   **G. Bucket Sort:** $\\Theta(n+k)$ on average if input is uniformly distributed. Worst-case can be $\\Theta(n^2)$ if items fall into the same bucket.\n*   **H. Radix Sort:** $\\Theta(nk)$ where k is the number of digits. This is typically linear in n for fixed k.\n*   **I. Quicksort:** Worst-case: $\\Theta(n^2)$ (e.g., always picking the smallest or largest element as pivot). Average-case: $\\Theta(n \\log n)$.\n*   **J. Heapsort:** Worst-case: $\\Theta(n \\log n)$. Average-case: $\\Theta(n \\log n)$.\n\nBased on this analysis, Quicksort (I) is the only algorithm that fits the description of $\\Theta(n^2)$ in the worst case and $\\Theta(n \\log n)$ on average.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{I}$.",
