    "global index": "MMLUPro_computer science_9303",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Two alternatives for interconnecting a set of processors with bidirectional links are (1) the fully interconnected network, in which each processor is directly connected to every other processor, and (2) the ring network, in which each processor is connected to two other processors. The worst-case path length for a network is the maximum, over all pairs of nodes in the network, of the minimum length paths (measured in number of links) between the nodes. For each type of interconnection of n processors, a figure of merit can be formed as the product of the number of links required for the network times the worst-case path length connecting any two processors. The ratio of this figure of merit for the fully interconnected network compared to that of the ring network, for even n > 2, is\n\nOptions: \nA. (n-1)/n\nB. 1/(2n)\nC. 1/(n^2)\nD. n/(n-1)\nE. 1/(n(n-1))\nF. 1/n\nG. 1/(n+1)\nH. n^2\nI. (n+1)/n\nJ. 2n\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Let's analyze the figure of merit for each network.\n\nFor the fully interconnected network with $n$ processors:\nLinks: Each processor is connected to every other processor, so the number of links is $n(n-1)/2$.\nWorst-case path length: The path length between any two directly connected processors is 1. Thus, the worst-case path length is 1.\nFigure of merit (FOM_full) = (Number of links) * (Worst-case path length) = $(n(n-1)/2) * 1 = n(n-1)/2$.\n\nFor the ring network with $n$ processors:\nLinks: Each processor is connected to two others, forming a cycle. The number of links is $n$.\nWorst-case path length: In a ring of $n$ nodes, the maximum shortest path length occurs between two nodes that are diametrically opposite if $n$ is even, or as far as possible if $n$ is odd. For even $n > 2$, the maximum path length is $n/2$.\nFigure of merit (FOM_ring) = (Number of links) * (Worst-case path length) = $n * (n/2) = n^2/2$.\n\nThe ratio of the figure of merit for the fully interconnected network compared to that of the ring network is:\nRatio = FOM_full / FOM_ring = $(n(n-1)/2) / (n^2/2)$\nRatio = $(n(n-1)/2) * (2/n^2)$\nRatio = $n(n-1)/n^2$\nRatio = $(n-1)/n$\n\nComparing this to the given options:\nA. (n-1)/n\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{A}$.",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 307,
