    "global index": "MMLUPro_computer science_9128",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Let G = (V, E) be a finite directed acyclic graph with |E| > 0. Which of the following must be true?\nI. G has a vertex with no incoming edge.\nII. G has a vertex with no outgoing edge.\nIII. G has an isolated vertex, that is, one with neither an incoming edge nor an outgoing edge.\n\nOptions: \nA. I and III only\nB. None of the above\nC. I and II only\nD. II and III only\nE. I, II, and a vertex with one outgoing edge\nF. I, II, and a vertex with one incoming edge\nG. All of the above (I, II, III)\nH. II only\nI. I only\nJ. III only\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Let's analyze each statement for a finite directed acyclic graph (DAG) with at least one edge.\n\nStatement I: G has a vertex with no incoming edge.\nIn a finite DAG, we can start a traversal from any vertex and follow outgoing edges. Since the graph is acyclic, this traversal must terminate. If we consider the set of all vertices, there must be at least one vertex that cannot be reached by following any path, which implies it has no incoming edge. Equivalently, if every vertex had an incoming edge, we could trace backward indefinitely in an infinite graph, but in a finite graph, this would imply a cycle, contradicting the DAG property. Thus, a DAG must have at least one source vertex (a vertex with no incoming edge). This statement is true.\n\nStatement II: G has a vertex with no outgoing edge.\nSimilarly, in a finite DAG, we can consider paths. If every vertex had an outgoing edge, we could start at any vertex and extend a path indefinitely. However, since the graph is finite, this would eventually lead to a repeated vertex, forming a cycle, which is not allowed in a DAG. Therefore, there must be at least one vertex with no outgoing edge (a sink vertex). This statement is true.\n\nStatement III: G has an isolated vertex, that is, one with neither an incoming edge nor an outgoing edge.\nThe problem states that |E| > 0, meaning there is at least one edge in the graph. An isolated vertex has no incoming and no outgoing edges. If there is an edge, say from vertex u to vertex v, then vertex u has an outgoing edge, and vertex v has an incoming edge. It is possible to have a DAG with at least one edge where every vertex has either an incoming or an outgoing edge (or both), without having any isolated vertices. For example, consider a graph with two vertices and one edge from the first to the second. The first vertex has an outgoing edge, and the second vertex has an incoming edge. Neither is isolated. Thus, this statement is not necessarily true.\n\nBased on the analysis, statements I and II must be true.\n\nFrom the given options, the one that states \"I and II only\" is the correct choice.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{C}$.",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 225,
        "output_tokens": 475,
        "total_tokens": 700
      },
      "provider": "openrouter",
      "error": null
    },
    "cost": null,
    "accuracy": null
  },
  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_computer science_9099",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which of the following statements about circuits is (are) true?\nI. Combinational circuits may have feedback; sequential circuits do not.\nII. Combinational circuits have a \"memoryless\" property; sequential circuits do not.\nIII. Both sequential and combinational circuits must be controlled by an external clock.\n\nOptions: \nA. II and III\nB. I and III only\nC. I and II only\nD. I only\nE. None of the above\nF. II only\nG. I, II, and III\nH. All of the above\nI. II and III only\nJ. III only\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Let's analyze each statement:\n\n*   **I. Combinational circuits may have feedback; sequential circuits do not.** This is **false**. Combinational circuits, by definition, do not have feedback paths. Sequential circuits **do** have feedback, as this is what allows them to store state or \"remember\" past inputs.\n\n*   **II. Combinational circuits have a \"memoryless\" property; sequential circuits do not.** This is **true**. The output of a combinational circuit depends only on its current inputs. Sequential circuits, on the other hand, have memory elements (like flip-flops), so their output depends on current inputs *and* their past state.\n\n*   **III. Both sequential and combinational circuits must be controlled by an external clock.** This is **false**. Combinational circuits do not inherently require a clock. Clock signals are primarily used to synchronize operations in sequential circuits, particularly for state changes in memory elements. Not all sequential circuits *must* be controlled by an *external* clock; some might use internal clock generation or be asynchronous.\n\nBased on this analysis, only statement II is true.\n\nReviewing the options:\n*   A. II and III (III is false)\n*   B. I and III only (I and III are false)\n*   C. I and II only (I is false)\n*   D. I only (I is false)\n*   E. None of the above\n*   F. II only (This matches our analysis)\n*   G. I, II, and III (I and III are false)\n*   H. All of the above (I and III are false)\n*   I. II and III only (III is false)\n*   J. III only (III is false)\n\nThe correct option is F.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{F}$.",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 191,
        "output_tokens": 392,
        "total_tokens": 583
      },
      "provider": "openrouter",
      "error": null
