ptions carefully, ensuring standard algorithm behavior.\n\nA: False (Insertion sort better when sorted)\nB: False (Comparisons in selection sort are independent)\nC: False (Data movements in selection sort are generally fixed at n-1 swaps)\nD: True (Comparisons in bubble sort are independent of arrangement in basic form)\nE: False (Data movements in bubble sort depend greatly on arrangement)\nF: False (Comparisons in insertion sort depend on arrangement)\nG: True (Comparisons in selection sort are independent of arrangement)\nH: False (Insertion sort worst case is descending, less efficient)\n\nWe have two true statements: D and G. If we must pick one, let's consider if there's a subtler point. The initial sentence mentions \"data movement\". While D and G are about comparisons, the question asks for *a* true statement. Among the options about data movement (C and E), both appear false.\n\nLet's assume the question setter intended for one of the \"true\" statements to be the answer. In many contexts, the fixed number of comparisons in bubble sort (naive) is a very commonly cited property, as is the fixed number of comparisons in selection sort.\n\nLet's scrutinize \"efficiency\" in terms of data movements.\n- Selection sort: O(n) data movements (swaps).\n- Insertion sort: O(n^2) data movements in the worst case.\n- Bubble sort: O(n^2) data movements in the worst case.\n\nConsider the context of a \"large list of numbers\".\n\nIf the array is initially sorted in ascending order.\n- Selection sort: ~n^2 comparisons, n-1 movements.\n- Insertion sort: ~n comparisons, ~n movements. Insertion sort is more efficient. A is false.\n\nIf the array is initially sorted in descending order.\n- Insertion sort: ~n^2 comparisons, ~n^2 movements.\n- Selection sort: ~n^2 comparisons, ~n-1 movements. Selection sort is more efficient (in terms of movements). H is false.\n\nLet's re-examine the possibility of C being true. If \"data movement\" means any write to an array location, then 