    "global index": "MMLUPro_computer science_9368",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Two processors, M-5 and M-7, implement the same instruction set. Processor M-5 uses a 5-stage pipeline and a clock cycle of 10 nanoseconds. Processor M-7 uses a 7-stage pipeline and a clock cycle of 7.5 nanoseconds. Which of the following is (are) true?\nI. M-7’s pipeline has better maximum throughput than M-5’s pipeline.\nII. The latency of a single instruction is shorter on M-7’s pipeline than on M-5’s pipeline.\nIII. Programs executing on M-7 will always run faster than programs executing on M-5.\n\nOptions: \nA. All three statements are true\nB. None of the statements are true\nC. I only\nD. II only\nE. I and II\nF. I and II only\nG. II and III only\nH. II and III\nI. I and III\nJ. III only\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Let's analyze each statement:\n\nStatement I: Throughput is measured as the number of instructions completed per unit of time.\nFor M-5: Clock cycle = 10 ns. In a 5-stage pipeline, ideally, one instruction finishes every clock cycle. So, throughput is $1 / 10$ ns.\nFor M-7: Clock cycle = 7.5 ns. In a 7-stage pipeline, ideally, one instruction finishes every clock cycle. So, throughput is $1 / 7.5$ ns.\nSince $1 / 7.5 > 1 / 10$, M-7 has better maximum throughput. Thus, statement I is true.\n\nStatement II: Latency is the time it takes for a single instruction to complete from start to finish.\nFor M-5: 5 stages, 10 ns per stage. Latency = 5 stages * 10 ns/stage = 50 ns.\nFor M-7: 7 stages, 7.5 ns per stage. Latency = 7 stages * 7.5 ns/stage = 52.5 ns.\nThe latency of a single instruction is shorter on M-5. Thus, statement II is false.\n\nStatement III: Programs executing on M-7 will always run faster than programs executing on M-5.\nWhile M-7 has better maximum throughput, this doesn't guarantee faster execution for all programs. Factors like pipeline stalls due to data dependencies, control dependencies, branch mispredictions, and the overhead of instruction fetching and other pipeline stages can affect the actual performance. If M-5 has fewer stalls or better branch prediction, it could potentially run some programs faster despite its lower theoretical throughput. Thus, statement III is false.\n\nBased on the analysis:\nStatement I is true.\nStatement II is false.\nStatement III is false.\n\nTherefore, only statement I is true.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{C}$.",
      "success": true,
