    "global index": "MMLUPro_computer science_9200",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Statement 1| The set of all rectangles in the 2D plane (which includes non axisaligned rectangles) can shatter a set of 5 points. Statement 2| The VC-dimension of k-Nearest Neighbour classifier when k = 1 is infinite.\n\nOptions: \nA. True, True\nB. False, True\nC. Depends on the dataset, True\nD. False, Depends on the dataset\nE. Depends on the dataset, Depends on the dataset\nF. True, Depends on the dataset\nG. True, False\nH. False, False\nI. Depends on the dataset, False\nJ. True, Not enough information\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Let's analyze each statement:\n\n**Statement 1:** The set of all rectangles in the 2D plane can shatter a set of 5 points.\nA set of points is shattered by a class of functions if, for every possible labeling of the points, there exists a function in the class that realizes that labeling. The VC-dimension of the set of axis-aligned rectangles in 2D is 4. This means that they can shatter any set of 4 points, but not necessarily 5. However, the question states \"all rectangles,\" which includes non-axis-aligned rectangles. The VC-dimension of the set of all rectangles (including non-axis-aligned) is 5 for 2D. Therefore, this statement is true.\n\n**Statement 2:** The VC-dimension of k-Nearest Neighbour classifier when k = 1 is infinite.\nThe VC-dimension of the 1-Nearest Neighbour classifier in 2D is indeed infinite. This is because for any finite set of points, we can always find a way to label them such that the 1-NN classifier cannot perfectly separate them.\n\nSince both statements are true, the correct option is A.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{A}$.",
