    "global index": "MMLUPro_computer science_9240",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Statement 1| PCA and Spectral Clustering (such as Andrew Ng’s) perform eigendecomposition on two different matrices. However, the size of these two matrices are the same. Statement 2| Since classification is a special case of regression, logistic regression is a special case of linear regression.\n\nOptions: \nA. Neither true nor false, Neither true nor false\nB. False, Neither true nor false\nC. True, False, Neither true nor false\nD. False, True\nE. True, Neither true nor false\nF. Neither true nor false, True\nG. True, True\nH. False, False\nI. True, False\nJ. Neither true nor false, False\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Let's analyze each statement:\n\n**Statement 1:** PCA (Principal Component Analysis) typically performs eigendecomposition on the covariance matrix or the correlation matrix of the data. Spectral Clustering, on the other hand, performs eigendecomposition on the affinity matrix (or Laplacian matrix derived from it) of the data. The size of these matrices are not necessarily the same. The covariance/correlation matrix is $n \\times n$, where $n$ is the number of features. The affinity/Laplacian matrix is $m \\times m$, where $m$ is the number of data points. Therefore, Statement 1 is false.\n\n**Statement 2:** Logistic regression is a classification algorithm, while linear regression is a regression algorithm. While both are discriminative models and can involve linear combinations of features, logistic regression uses a sigmoid function to map outputs to probabilities, making it suitable for classification. Linear regression aims to predict continuous values. Classification and regression are distinct problem types, and logistic regression is not a special case of linear regression when considering their fundamental objective. Therefore, Statement 2 is false.\n\nSince both statements are false, the correct option indicates \"False, False.\"\n\nComparing this with the given options:\nA. Neither true nor false, Neither true nor false\nB. False, Neither true nor false\nC. True, False, Neither true nor false\nD. False, True\nE. True, Neither true nor false\nF. Neither true nor false, True\nG. True, True\nH. False, False\nI. True, False\nJ. Neither true nor false, False\n\nThe correct choice is H.\n\n\\boxed{H}",
