  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_computer science_9342",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Statement 1| Maximizing the likelihood of logistic regression model yields multiple local optimums. Statement 2| No classifier can do better than a naive Bayes classifier if the distribution of the data is known.\n\nOptions: \nA. True, True\nB. Both statements are not known\nC. Both Statements are partially true\nD. Statement 1 is not known and Statement 2 is True\nE. Statement 1 is not known and Statement 2 is False\nF. False, False\nG. Statement 1 is False and Statement 2 is not known\nH. True, False\nI. False, True\nJ. Statement 1 is True and Statement 2 is not known\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Statement 1 is **False**. The log-likelihood function for logistic regression is convex, which means it has a single global optimum and no local optimums.\n\nStatement 2 is **True**. If the data distribution is known, the optimal classifier is the Bayes classifier, which corresponds to the decision rule of naive Bayes under certain independence assumptions that are satisfied if the distribution is known.\n\nTherefore, the correct option is **I. False, True**.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{I}$.",
