    "global index": "MMLUPro_philosophy_9629",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: When was the Old Babylonian version of Gilgamesh modified into the standard version?\n\nOptions: \nA. Toward the end of the first millennium BCE\nB. Towards the middle of the first millennium BCE\nC. Toward the end of the second millennium BCE\nD. Toward the middle of the second millennium BCE\nE. At the start of the second millennium BCE\nF. In the mid-first millennium BCE\nG. At the start of the first millennium BCE\nH. Toward the end of the third millennium BCE\nI. At the start of the third millennium BCE\nJ. In the middle of the third millennium BCE\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The most commonly accepted scholarly dating for the compilation of the Standard Babylonian Epic of Gilgamesh is around the Kassite period into the Neo-Babylonian period, which broadly falls within the second half of the second millennium BCE or early first millennium BCE. Option D, \"Toward the middle of the second millennium BCE,\" and Option G, \"At the start of the first millennium BCE,\" are the closest fits within the provided choices. Considering the development and compilation of the standard version, it is generally believed to have been a process that continued over a long period, with significant revisions and additions happening after much of the earlier material was composed. However, the final \"standard\" form is often associated with later redactions. Among the choices, the middle of the second millennium BCE (Option D) marks the period when many of the individual stories that would be incorporated into the epic were already in circulation. The refinement into a more cohesive narrative likely occurred thereafter.\n\nWhile the exact date is debated, the compilation and standardization of the Epic of Gilgamesh into its most familiar form (the Standard Babylonian version) is generally placed in the period spanning the Kassite rule and the subsequent periods. This places the major formative stages of the Standard Babylonian version much later than the Old Babylonian period. The Old Babylonian version existed much earlier, and the Standard version is a compilation and revision of those earlier tales.\n\nLet's re-evaluate the options with this context.\nA. Toward the end of the first millennium BCE (too late)\nB. Towards the middle of the first millennium BCE (could be part of the process, but the core compilation is earlier)\nC. Toward the end of the second millennium BCE (plausible for later stages of standardization)\nD. Toward the middle of the second millennium BCE (Many individual stories were circulating, but not the standardized epic in its final form. This is the period of the Old Babylonian version's influence and the beginning of its compilation.)\nE. At the start of the second millennium BCE (This is when the Old Babylonian period begins, and the earliest forms of Gilgamesh stories existed, but not the Standard version.)\nF. In the mid-first millennium BCE (too late for the primary compilation)\nG. At the start of the first millennium BCE (Plausible for later stages of standardization)\nH. Toward the end of the third millennium BCE (too early)\nI. At the start of the third millennium BCE (too early)\nJ. In the middle of the third millennium BCE (too early)\n\nConsidering the widely accepted scholarly views, the Standard Babylonian Epic of Gilgamesh, as we know it, is thought to have been compiled by Sîn-lēqi-unninni during the Kassite period, which starts around the mid-2nd millennium BCE and extends into the early 1st millennium BCE. Therefore, the period of its compilation and modification into the standard version falls roughly between the mid-second millennium BCE and early first millennium BCE.\n\nLooking at the options:\nOption D: \"Toward the middle of the second millennium BCE\" aligns with the beginning of the Kassite period and the potential start of extensive compilation and modification.\nOption C: \"Toward the end of the second millennium BCE\" and Option G: \"At the start of the first millennium BCE\" also fall within the plausible timeframe for the completion and widespread circulation of the Standard Babylonian version.\n\nHowever, often the \"modification into the standard version\" implies a more mature compilation. The middle of the second millennium BCE is when many of the constituent stories were already forming and being adapted from earlier traditions. The comprehensive compilation and standardization into the epic we know is more likely in the latter part of the second millennium BCE and early first millennium BCE.\n\nLet's consider the options again, focusing on \"modified into the standard version.\" This suggests a substantial revision and unification of earlier tales.\n\nTypically, scholars place the compilation of the Standard Babylonian Epic of Gilgamesh by Sîn-lēqi-unninni in the 13th or 12th century BCE, which falls towards the end of the second millennium BCE. Some scholars extend this compilation period further into the early first millennium BCE.\n\nBetween options C and G, option C, \"Toward the end of the second millennium BCE,\" is the most specific and widely accepted period for the compilation of the Standard Babylonian version by Sîn-lēqi-unninni.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{C}$.",
