  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_philosophy_9817",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Identify the antecedent of the following conditional proposition: The university raises tuition if, and only if, both the governor approves of it and the board of trustees recommends it.\n\nOptions: \nA. The university raises tuition.\nB. The governor approves of it.\nC. The board of trustees recommends it.\nD. None of the above\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The sentence uses the biconditional \"if, and only if\". In a biconditional statement \"P if and only if Q\", P is the consequent and Q is the antecedent, or vice versa; the terms are interchangeable. However, in standard conditional propositions \"If P, then Q\", P is the antecedent. When \"if\" introduces the subsequent clause, that clause becomes the antecedent.\n\nThe structure of the given proposition is \"A if, and only if, B and C\". Here, 'A' is \"The university raises tuition\", and 'B and C' is \"both the governor approves of it and the board of trustees recommends it.\"\n\nTherefore, the antecedent is \"both the governor approves of it and the board of trustees recommends it.\"\nSince this combined statement is not one of the options, let's re-evaluate the question with the common understanding of \"if P then Q\" where P is the antecedent.\n\nLet P = The university raises tuition.\nLet Q = The governor approves of it.\nLet R = The board of trustees recommends it.\n\nThe statement is P if and only if (Q and R).\nThis can be broken down into two conditional statements:\n1. If P, then (Q and R).\n2. If (Q and R), then P.\n\nIn symbolic logic, if we write P ↔ (Q ∧ R), there isn't a single \"antecedent\" in the same way as in a simple \"if P then Q\" statement. Each side of the biconditional can be seen as an antecedent for the other side.\n\nHowever, if we are forced to pick from the given options, and considering that \"if\" often introduces the antecedent *even when stated after the consequent*, let's analyze the phrasing: \"The university raises tuition if, and only if, both the governor approves of it and the board of trustees recommends it.\"\n\nThe part that comes directly after \"if\" is \"both the governor approves of it and the board of trustees recommends it.\" This entire phrase acts as the condition for \"The university raises tuition.\"\n\nLet's assume the question is looking for the \"cause\" or the \"condition\" that leads to the \"effect\" (the university raising tuition).\n\nIf we rephrase it as \"If (both the governor approves of it and the board of trustees recommends it), then the university raises tuition, and if the university raises tuition, then (both the governor approves of it and the board of trustees recommends it).\"\n\nIn the conditional \"If (X), then (Y)\", X is the antecedent. So here, X is \"both the governor approves of it and the board of trustees recommends it.\" None of the options match this complete phrase.\n\nLet's consider another interpretation. Sometimes, in a biconditional P iff Q, P is considered the \"main\" proposition, and Q is its condition. In this case, \"The university raises tuition\" is the proposition, and \"both the governor approves of it and the board of trustees recommends it\" is a complex condition.\n\nIf the question is implicitly asking for an antecedent within the complex condition, it's poorly formed.\n\nLet's stick to the formal definition in \"P if and only if Q\". \"P is if and only if Q\" is equivalent to \"(P implies Q) and (Q implies P)\".\n\nIn the first implication \"The university raises tuition implies (the governor approves and the board recommends)\", the antecedent is \"The university raises tuition\".\nIn the second implication \"(the governor approves and the board recommends) implies The university raises tuition\", the antecedent is \"(the governor approves and the board recommends)\".\n\nGiven the options, and the common convention where \"if\" introduces the antecedent:\n\"The university raises tuition\" is the consequent for the condition \"both the governor approves of it and the board of trustees recommends it\".\n\"Both the governor approves of it and the board of trustees recommends it\" is the consequent for the condition \"The university raises tuition\".\n\nSince \"if\" precedes \"both the governor approves of it and the board of trustees recommends it\", this is the more direct \"antecedent\" (the con