      "generated_answer": "The question asks to identify the antecedent of the conditional proposition. A conditional proposition is of the form \"If P, then Q\", where P is the antecedent and Q is the consequent. In this proposition, \"When the university raises tuition\" is the condition that triggers the rest of the statement. Therefore, \"The university raises tuition\" is the antecedent.\n\nThe structure of the given proposition is \"When A, then (B or not C)\".\n\"When the university raises tuition\" corresponds to statement A.\n\"then either the governor approves of it or the board of trustees doesn't prevent it\" corresponds to the consequent.\n\nTherefore, the antecedent is \"The university raises tuition\".\n\nLooking at the options:\nA. The board of trustees prevents it. (This is part of the consequent)\nB. The university raises tuition. (This matches our antecedent)\nC. The board of trustees approves it. (This is not mentioned in the proposition)\nD. The governor and the board of trustees don't prevent it. (This is a conjunction not directly present)\nE. The governor approves of it. (This is part of the consequent)\nF. The board of trustees doesn't prevent it. (This is part of the consequent)\nG. The governor and the board of trustees prevent it. (This is not directly present)\nH. The governor disapproves of it. (This is the negation of \"The governor approves of it\")\nI. The university doesn't raise tuition. (This is the negation of the antecedent)\nJ. The university lowers tuition. (This is not mentioned in the proposition)\n\nThe correct antecedent is \"The university raises tuition\".\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{B}$.",
