for the entire time this person was alive).\n\nSo, the structure is [(B ∨ D) • L] ⊃ H.\n\nLet's re-evaluate the options given this structure. None of the options exactly match [(B ∨ D) • L] ⊃ H. This suggests a potential misinterpretation of the variables or the sentence structure among the given options.\n\nLet's consider if L might be the main premise. \"A person is considered to have lived with you for all of the present year if the person was born or died in the present year AND your home was this person's home for the entire time this person was alive.\"\n\nLet's consider the possibility that the statement could be interpreted as: IF the person was born or died in the present year AND your home was this person's home for the entire time this person was alive, THEN the person lived with you for all of the present year. This is [(B ∨ D) • L] ⊃ H.\n\nLet's look at another interpretation of the sentence's structure where the conditions for H might be presented in a different order within the propositional logic.\n\nConsider the condition \"your home was this person's home for the entire time this person was alive\" (L). This condition is only relevant if the person was alive during the present year. However, the sentence implies that \"born or died in the present year\" also defines the timeframe of being alive within this year.\n\nLet's assume for a moment that the phrasing implies: If (born or died in the present year) AND (was alive), THEN lived with you. But the \"was alive\" part is implied by born *or* died in the present year.\n\nLet's re-evaluate the most direct translation:\nIf ((Born in present year) OR (Died in present year)) AND (Home was this person's home for the entire time this person was alive), THEN (Person lived with you for all of the present year).\nThis is: [(B ∨ D) • L] ⊃ H.\n\nNone of the options perfectly match this. This indicates a potential issue with the question or the options. However, if we are forced to choose the \"best\" symbolization, we need to look for the option that most closely captures the logical structure.\n\nLet's reconsider the sentence from the perspective of which statement implies another.\n\nLet's revisit option F: [(B ∨ D) • H] ⊃ L. This means if a person was born or died in the present year AND lived with you for all the present year, THEN their home was your home for the entire time they were alive. This doesn't seem right.\n\nLet's look at option D: (B ∨ D) ⊃ (H • L). This means if a person was born or died in the present year, THEN they lived with you for all the present year AND your home was their home for their entire alive time. This also doesn't seem right because the sentence connects the living with you part being a consequence of the conditions, not a premise with the birth/death condition.\n\nLet's re-read the sentence VERY carefully: \"A person is considered to have lived with you for all of the present year (H) IF (the person was born (B) or died (D) in the present year AND your home was this person's home for the entire time this person was alive (L)).\"\n\nThe \"if\" clause outlines the conditions that LEAD TO H. So H is the consequent.\nConditions are: (B ∨ D) AND L.\n\nSo the structure is: (B ∨ D) • L ⊃ H.\n\nSince this exact structure is not available, let's analyze which option represents a plausible reordering or grouping that might be intended, or if there's a common misunderstanding of logical implication.\n\nLet's re-examine option F: [(B ∨ D) • H] ⊃ L. This implies that if the person was born or died, and lived with you, then they lived with you the whole time. This doesn't quite capture it.\n\nLet's consider if L (lived at home the whole alive time) is more of a prerequisite for the entire condition to be meaningful. But the sentence structure says \"if X and Y, then Z\".\n\nLet's re-evaluate the options based on the core components and their relationships.\nWe have the conditions: (B ∨ D) and L.\nWe have the conclusion: H.\n\nThe structure is clear