So, we are looking for a case where M is True and N is False.\nIn such a case, Premise 1 (M ∨ N) becomes T ∨ F, which is True.\nNow consider Premise 2 (~M · O). If M is True, then ~M is False.\nSo, ~M · O becomes F · O, which is always False, regardless of the value of O.\n\nThis means that it is impossible for both premises to be true when N is false. If N is false, and M ∨ N is true, then M must be true. But if M is true, then ~M is false, making ~M · O false. Therefore, the argument is valid.\n\nLet's recheck the truth table analysis.\nWe need a row where (M ∨ N) is T and (~M · O) is T, and N is F.\nFor N to be False, we look at rows 3, 4, 7, 8.\nRow 3: M=T, N=F, O=T. M ∨ N = T ∨ F = T. ~M · O = F · T = F. Premise 2 is false.\nRow 4: M=T, N=F, O=F. M ∨ N = T ∨ F = T. ~M · O = F · F = F. Premise 2 is false.\nRow 7: M=F, N=F, O=T. M ∨ N = F ∨ F = F. Premise 1 is false.\nRow 8: M=F, N=F, O=F. M ∨ N = F ∨ F = F. Premise 1 is false.\n\nThere is no row where both premises are true and the conclusion is false. Therefore, the argument is valid.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{A}$.",
