    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To determine the validity of the argument using indirect truth tables, we assume the premises are true and the conclusion is false, and see if we can derive a contradiction.\nThe argument is:\nPremise 1: (K · L) ⊃ M\nPremise 2: N ⊃ ~M\nPremise 3: N ∨ K\nConclusion: L ⊃ K\n\nWe assume the conclusion is false. For L ⊃ K to be false, L must be true and K must be false.\nNow we work with the premises being true and the assignments L=T, K=F.\n\nFrom Premise 1: (K · L) ⊃ M becomes (F · T) ⊃ M, which is F ⊃ M. This is always true, so it doesn't give us information about M.\nFrom Premise 3: N ∨ K becomes N ∨ F. For this to be true, N must be true.\nSo far we have: L=T, K=F, N=T.\n\nNow let's look at Premise 2: N ⊃ ~M. Substituting N=T, we get T ⊃ ~M. For this to be true, ~M must be true, which means M must be false.\nSo, we have derived: L=T, K=F, N=T, M=F.\n\nLet's check if these assignments make all the premises true and the conclusion false.\nPremise 1: (K · L) ⊃ M becomes (F · T) ⊃ F, which is F ⊃ F, which is True.\nPremise 2: N ⊃ ~M becomes T ⊃ ~F, which is T ⊃ T, which is True.\nPremise 3: N ∨ K becomes T ∨ F, which is True.\nConclusion: L ⊃ K becomes T ⊃ F, which is False.\n\nSince we found a case where all premises are true and the conclusion is false, the argument is invalid. The counterexample is when L is true, K is false, N is true, and M is false.\n\nLet's examine the given options:\nA. Valid - Incorrect.\nB. Invalid. Counterexample when L, M, and N are true and K is false. In this case, L=T, M=T, N=T, K=F.\nPremise 1: (K · L) ⊃ M = (F · T) ⊃ T = F ⊃ T = T\nPremise 2: N ⊃ ~M = T ⊃ ~T = T ⊃ F = F. This makes Premise 2 false, so this is not a valid counterexample.\n\nC. Invalid. Counterexample when L and M are true and K and N are false. In this case, L=T, M=T, K=F, N=F.\nPremise 1: (K · L) ⊃ M = (F · T) ⊃ T = F ⊃ T = T\nPremise 2: N ⊃ ~M = F ⊃ ~T = F ⊃ F = T\nPremise 3: N ∨ K = F ∨ F = F. This makes Premise 3 false, so this is not a valid counterexample.\n\nD. Invalid. Counterexample when L and N are true and K and M are false. In this case, L=T, N=T, K=F, M=F.\nPremise 1: (K · L) ⊃ M = (F · T) ⊃ F = F ⊃ F = T\nPremise 2: N ⊃ ~M = T ⊃ ~F = T ⊃ T = T\nPremise 3: N ∨ K = T ∨ F = T\nConclusion: L ⊃ K = T ⊃ F = F\nThis case makes all premises true and the conclusion false, so it is a valid counterexample.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{D}$.",
