we must have A = False and B = False.\nWith A = False and B = False, Premise 2 (A ∨ C) ∨ B becomes (False ∨ C) ∨ False, which simplifies to C.\nSo, if A = False and B = False, then Premise 2 is true if and only if C is true.\nTherefore, if A = False and B = False, and we want Premise 2 to be true, we must have C = True.\nIn this case (A=False, B=False, C=True):\nPremise 1: ~False · ~False = True · True = True.\nPremise 2: (False ∨ True) ∨ False = True ∨ False = True.\nConclusion: C = True.\nHere, all premises are true and the conclusion is true. This is not a counterexample.\n\nIt seems there might be a misunderstanding in our manual search or in interpreting the potential counterexamples. Let's systematically evaluate some of the given counterexample options.\n\nLet's consider Option D: Invalid. Counterexample when B is true and A and C are false.\nA = False, B = True, C = False\nPremise 1: ~A · ~B = ~False · ~True = True · False = False.\nSince Premise 1 is false, this is not a counterexample.\n\nLet's consider the scenario where the conclusion is false. So, C is false.\nWe want to find a case where Premise 1 (~A · ~B) and Premise 2 ((A ∨ C) ∨ B) are both true when C is false.\n\nIf C is false, Premise 1 requires A = False and B = False.\nLet's check Premise 2 with A = False, B = False, C = False:\nPremise 2: (False ∨ False) ∨ False = False ∨ False = False.\nSo, if A, B, and C are all false, Premise 1 is true, but Premise 2 is false.\n\nLet's re-examine the premises and conclusion.\nThe argument is:\n1. ~A · ~B\n2. (A ∨ C) ∨ B\nTherefore, C.\n\nWe are looking for a situation where 1 and 2 are true, but C is false.\nIf C is false, then the conclusion is false.\nFor premise 1 (~A · ~B) to be true, both ~A and ~B must be true. This implies A is false and B is false.\nNow substitute A=False, B=False, and C=False into premise 2:\nPremise 2: (False ∨ False) ∨ False = False ∨ False = False.\nIn this case, premise 2 is false. So, A=False, B=False, C=False is not a counterexample because premise 2 is not true.\n\nLet's try to make premise 2 true when C is false.\nPremise 2 is (A ∨ C) ∨ B. If C is false, this becomes (A ∨ False) ∨ B, which simplifies to A ∨ B.\nSo, for premise 2 to be true when C is false, we need A ∨ B to be true.\nNow we also need premise 1 (~A · ~B) to be true. This requires A to be false and B to be false.\nBut if A is false and B is false, then A ∨ B is false, which contradicts the requirement for premise 2 to be true.\n\nThis suggests that it might be impossible for both premises to be true simultaneously. If we cannot find a case where both premises are true, the argument is vacuously valid. However, we are asked to determine if it's valid or invalid and provide a counterexample if invalid.\n\nLet's construct the full truth table to be sure. There are 3 variables (A, B, C), so 2^3 = 8 rows.\n\n| A | B | C | ~A | ~B | ~A · ~B (P1) | A ∨ C | (A ∨ C) ∨ B (P2) | C (Conc) |\n|---|---|---|----|----|--------------|-------|------------------|----------|\n| T | T | T | F  | F  | F            | T     | T                | T        |\n| T | T | F | F  | F  | F            | T     | T                | F        |\n| T | F | T | F  | T  | F            | T     | T                | T        |\n| T | F | F | F  | T  | F            | T     | T                | F        |\n| F | T | T | T  | F  | F            | T     | T                | T        |\n| F | T | F | T  | F  | F            | F     | T                | F        |\n| F | F | T | T  | T  | T            | T     | T                | T        |\n| F | F | F | T  | T  | T            | F     | F                | F        |\n\nWe are looking for a row where P1 is True and P2 is True, and Conc is False.\nLet's examine rows where P1 is True: Rows 7 and 8.\nRow 7: A=F, B=F, C=T. P1=T, P2=T, Conc=T. (Valid case)\nRow 8: A=F, B=F, C=F. P1=T, P2=F, Conc=F. (P2 is false, not a counterexample)\n\nThere are no rows where P1 is true, P2 is true, and C is false. This means the argument is