    "global index": "MMLUPro_psychology_2189",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Detection of a just-noticeable difference (JND) depends on the\n\nOptions: \nA. presence of a \"no stimulus\" control and the sensitivity of the signal-detection equipment\nB. initial determination of the absolute threshold and the variation of the difference threshold\nC. frequency of the existing stimulus and the presence of one or more sensory modalities\nD. intensity of the new stimulus and that of the stimulus already present\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The correct answer is **D**.\n\nA just-noticeable difference (JND), also known as the difference threshold, is the minimum difference between two stimuli that a person can detect 50% of the time. This detection inherently relies on comparing a new stimulus to one that is already present, and the magnitude of this difference is key. The intensity of both the existing stimulus and the potential new stimulus directly influences whether a noticeable difference can be perceived. Options A, B, and C are less directly related to the fundamental definition and determinants of a JND.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{D}$.",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 145,
