  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_psychology_2521",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: While treating a client, you decide that you want to consult with a colleague to discuss a specific aspect of the case that is giving you trouble. According to APA’s Ethics Code and the Canadian Code of Ethics:\n\nOptions: \nA. you should obtain permission from both the client and the consultant before discussing the case.\nB. you must always obtain permission from the client before talking to a consultant.\nC. you must obtain permission from the client only if you will be revealing sensitive information to the consultant.\nD. you only need to obtain permission from the client if the consultant is not part of the same organization.\nE. you must obtain permission from the client only if you will be revealing the client's identity to the consultant.\nF. you can consult with a colleague without client's permission only if it's an emergency situation.\nG. \"you do not have to obtain permission from the client under any circumstances since the consultant is “clearly connected\"\" with the case.\"\nH. you don't have to obtain permission from the client as long as he/she has already signed a general waiver.\nI. you should discuss the matter with the client but the final decision is up to the professional's discretion.\nJ. you don't need to obtain permission if the consultation is about a general aspect of the case and not about the client specifically.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Both the APA Ethics Code and the Canadian Code of Ethics emphasize the importance of client confidentiality. Generally, when discussing a client's case with a colleague for consultation purposes, client permission is required. This is to protect the client's privacy and ensure informed consent regarding the use of their information.\n\nLet's analyze the options:\nA. Requires permission from both client and consultant. While ethical consultants will maintain confidentiality, the primary ethical obligation is to the client's permission. The consultant's permission isn't typically a requirement for the treating therapist to consult.\nB. You must always obtain permission from the client before talking to a consultant. This is the most consistently upheld ethical principle regarding client confidentiality in consultations.\nC. You must obtain permission from the client only if you will be revealing sensitive information. Even seemingly non-sensitive information can become identifiable when linked to other details. Therefore, permission is generally needed for any discussion of a specific case.\nD. You only need to obtain permission from the client if the consultant is not part of the same organization. The ethical principle of confidentiality applies regardless of the organizational structure.\nE. You must obtain permission from the client only if you will be revealing the client's identity. Even without revealing the identity, discussing specific case details can breach confidentiality if the information is sufficiently identifying.\nF. You can consult with a colleague without client's permission only if it's an emergency situation. While emergencies can sometimes override standard procedures, consultation for general difficulty is not typically considered an emergency that bypasses consent for confidentiality.\nG. You do not have to obtain permission from the client under any circumstances since the consultant is “clearly connected” with the case. This statement disregards the client's right to confidentiality and control over their information.\nH. You don't have to obtain permission from the client as long as he/she has already signed a general waiver. General waivers often have limitations, and specific consent for consultation may still be required, especially for protected health information.\nI. You should discuss the matter with the client but the final decision is up to the professional's discretion. While the professional has discretion in treatment, they do not have discretion to override client confidentiality without consent.\nJ. You don't need to obtain permission if the consultation is about a general aspect of the case and not about the client specifically. Even general aspects can reveal details about the client's situation and therefore require consent.\n\nThe most accurate and universally applied ethical principle from the given options is the necessity of obtaining client permission before discussing their case in a consultation.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{B}$.",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 343,
        "output_tokens": 541,
        "total_tokens": 884
      },
      "provider": "openrouter",
      "error": null
    },
    "cost": null,
    "accuracy": null
  },
