    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Exposing individuals to arguments against their views, arguments that are then strongly refuted, may serve to\n\nOptions: \nA. make them more argumentative\nB. make them more susceptible to accepting opposing views\nC. weaken their resistance to later persuasive appeals\nD. increase their attitudinal ambivalence\nE. strengthen their existing views\nF. lead them to develop new, unrelated opinions\nG. render them indifferent to the attitude object\nH. inoculate chem against later persuasive appeals\nI. confuse them about their original beliefs\nJ. cause them to reject all future arguments\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "This question relates to psychological concepts of persuasion and cognitive resistance. The scenario describes exposing individuals to counter-arguments that are then refuted. This process can reinforce existing beliefs and make individuals more resilient to future challenges. Of the options provided, the one that best describes this phenomenon is inoculation.\n\nThe correct answer is **H**.\n\nExplanation: This scenario describes a form of psychological inoculation. By being exposed to and refuting counterarguments, individuals become better prepared and more resistant to similar arguments in the future. Thus, their existing views are strengthened against subsequent persuasive attempts.",
