    "global index": "MMLUPro_psychology_2281",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: You are subpoenaed to appear at a deposition to testify about a former client of yours. During the deposition, the attorney for the opposing party asks you to provide confidential client information. You should:\n\nOptions: \nA. provide the information only if it puts the former client in a favorable light.\nB. provide the information if the client has given verbal permission.\nC. provide the information if you believe it is pertinent to the case.\nD. provide the information if the opposing party's attorney insists.\nE. refuse to provide the information unless the client is present.\nF. disclose the information if it has already been a reasonable time since the client-attorney relationship ended.\nG. provide the information since privilege is waived in this situation.\nH. provide the information if it doesn't harm the client's case.\nI. assert the privilege unless the client has signed a waiver.\nJ. refuse to provide the information without a court order.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The most likely correct answer is **I**.\n\nAttorneys have a duty to protect client confidences even after the attorney-client relationship has ended. The attorney-client privilege is generally not waived by the passage of time or by the attorney's belief that the information is pertinent or harmless. A client's informed consent, usually in writing, is required to waive privilege. Asserting the privilege is the appropriate action until such waiver is obtained or a court orders disclosure.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{I}$.",
