      "generated_answer": "The temporary pain relief from scratching is best explained by the \\textbf{gate-control theory}, which suggests that the act of scratching activates non-painful sensory pathways that close the \"gate\" to pain signals being sent to the brain. This modulation of pain occurs at the spinal cord level, temporarily inhibiting the perception of pain. Once scratching stops, the \"gate\" reopens, and the pain returns.\n\n\\(\\boxed{\\text{J}}\\)",
