    "global index": "MMLUPro_psychology_2047",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which of the following is an example of a double-bind message\n\nOptions: \nA. A brother tells his sister, \"You can use my car if you want to, but remember last time when you had an accident.\"\nB. A coach tells a player, \"You can try that move if you want, but remember it didn't work out last time.\"\nC. A teacher tells a student, “You can do that if you want to, but I would appreciate it if you would not\nD. A doctor tells a patient, \"You can skip the medication if you want, but the pain might return.\"\nE. A teacher tells a student, \"You can skip the homework if you want, but your grades might drop.\"\nF. A father tells his son, “I sure hope you can come to the movies with us tonight,” when his tone and posture thar he does not hope so\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The correct answer is **F**.\n\nA double-bind message is characterized by contradictory messages, where one instruction negates the other, making it impossible to respond appropriately without failing. In option F, the father's verbal message (\"I sure hope you can come\") is contradicted by his non-verbal cues (tone and posture), putting the son in a no-win situation. The other options present clear consequences, not contradictory demands.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{F}$",
      "success": true,
