    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Today Susan took a pill for her allergy that raised her blood pressure, caused her heart to beat faster, and raised her body temperature. Now caught in traffic, she feels angry. Yesterday, when she took the pill, she was with her husband. When her blood pressure rose, her heart speeded up, she got hotter, and she felt amorous. This description exemplifies\n\nOptions: \nA. two-factor theory\nB. Incentive theory\nC. Opponent-process theory\nD. the adaptation-level phenomenon\nE. Cognitive dissonance theory\nF. Arousal theory\nG. Cannon-Bard theory\nH. Cognitive Appraisal theory\nI. James-Lange theory\nJ. Drive-reduction theory\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "openai/gpt-4o",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The scenario described in the question aligns with the \\(\\boxed{A}\\) option, which is the two-factor theory. Susan's emotional experiences (anger in traffic and amorous feelings with her husband) vary despite the same physiological changes because the two-factor theory posits that emotion is based on physiological arousal followed by cognitive interpretation of that arousal. Her emotional interpretation is influenced by the context and her cognitive labeling of the physiological state.",
