    "global index": "MMLUPro_psychology_2530",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: You regularly waive the co-payment for your low-income clients who are covered by insurance and believe this is acceptable since you normally bill your clients on a sliding fee scale. This is:\n\nOptions: \nA. ethical, as long as you are not profiting from the insurance company.\nB. a common and ethical practice.\nC. unethical, as it is considered insurance fraud.\nD. a common practice, but unethical.\nE. ethical, only if the co-payment is waived for all clients, regardless of income.\nF. ethical as long as you don't bill the insurance company for an increased hourly fee in order to collect your full fee.\nG. ethical, only if the clients are made aware of the potential implications to their insurance coverage.\nH. ethical only if the insurance company is aware of the arrangement.\nI. unethical, unless the client agrees to pay the co-payment at a later date.\nJ. ethical as long as the insurance company does not explicitly prohibit such arrangements.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The core of the issue is whether waiving a co-payment, especially when a sliding scale is already in place for other services, constitutes a misrepresentation to the insurance company. Insurance policies typically require co-payments as a form of cost-sharing. Intentionally waiving this, without the insurer's knowledge or explicit permission, can be seen as a scheme to avoid the patient's financial responsibility as outlined by the insurance plan, potentially defrauding the insurer by receiving the full billed amount while the patient pays less than agreed upon.\n\nThe most likely correct answer is C.\n\nExplanation: Insurance co-payments are part of the contract between the patient and the insurance company. Waiving them without the insurer's knowledge can be considered a fraudulent practice because it misrepresents the amount the patient is responsible for paying. This misrepresentation can lead to the provider receiving a higher reimbursement than intended by the insurance plan.\n\n\\boxed{C}",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
