  {
    "global index": "MMLU_formal_logic_121",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Use indirect truth tables to determine whether the following argument is valid. If the argument is invalid, choose an option which presents a counterexample. (There may be other counterexamples as well.)\n(K · L) ⊃ M\nN ⊃ ~M\nN ∨ K / L ⊃ K\n\nOptions: \nA. Valid\nB. Invalid. Counterexample when L, M, and N are true and K is false\nC. Invalid. Counterexample when L and M are true and K and N are false\nD. Invalid. Counterexample when L and N are true and K and M are false\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The solution involves using indirect truth tables to determine the validity of the argument. An indirect truth table assumes the argument is invalid and tries to find a row where all premises are true and the conclusion is false. If such a row exists, the argument is invalid, and the truth values of the atomic propositions in that row constitute a counterexample.\n\nLet's represent the premises and conclusion:\nPremise 1: P1: (K · L) ⊃ M\nPremise 2: P2: N ⊃ ~M\nPremise 3: P3: N ∨ K\nConclusion: C: L ⊃ K\n\nTo use an indirect truth table, we assume the premises are true and the conclusion is false.\nP1 is True, P2 is True, P3 is True, and C is False.\n\nFor C (L ⊃ K) to be False, L must be True and K must be False.\nSo, L = T, K = F.\n\nNow, let's substitute these values into the premises and check for consistency:\n\nP1: (K · L) ⊃ M\n(F · T) ⊃ M\nF ⊃ M\nThis statement is True regardless of the truth value of M.\n\nP3: N ∨ K\nN ∨ F\nThis statement must be True. For N ∨ F to be True, N must be True.\nSo, N = T.\n\nNow let's check P2 with N = T and M's truth value undetermined:\nP2: N ⊃ ~M\nT ⊃ ~M\nFor this to be True, ~M must be True, which means M must be False.\nSo, M = F.\n\nLet's summarize the truth values we found:\nK = F\nL = T\nN = T\nM = F\n\nNow let's check if these assignments make all premises true and the conclusion false.\nP1: (K · L) ⊃ M\n(F · T) ⊃ F\nF ⊃ F\nTrue.\n\nP2: N ⊃ ~M\nT ⊃ ~F\nT ⊃ T\nTrue.\n\nP3: N ∨ K\nT ∨ F\nTrue.\n\nC: L ⊃ K\nT ⊃ F\nFalse.\n\nSince we found a consistent assignment of truth values where all premises are true and the conclusion is false, the argument is invalid. The counterexample is when K is false, L is true, M is false, and N is true.\n\nLet's check the given options:\nA. Valid - Incorrect.\nB. Invalid. Counterexample when L, M, and N are true and K is false.\nIn this case, K=F, L=T, M=T, N=T.\nP1: (F · T) ⊃ T => F ⊃ T => T\nP2: T ⊃ ~T => T ⊃ F => F. This premise is false, so this is not a counterexample.\n\nC. Invalid. Counterexample when L and M are true and K and N are false.\nIn this case, K=F, L=T, M=T, N=F.\nP1: (F · T) ⊃ T => F ⊃ T => T\nP2: F ⊃ ~T => F ⊃ F => T\nP3: F ∨ F => F. This premise is false, so this is not a counterexample.\n\nD. Invalid. Counterexample when L and N are true and K and M are false.\nIn this case, K=F, L=T, M=F, N=T. This matches our derived counterexample.\nP1: (F · T) ⊃ F => F ⊃ F => T\nP2: T ⊃ ~F => T ⊃ T => T\nP3: T ∨ F => T\nC: T ⊃ F => F\nAll premises are true, and the conclusion is false. This is a valid counterexample.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{D}$.",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 192,
        "output_tokens": 885,
        "total_tokens": 1077
      },
      "provider": "openrouter",
      "error": null
    },
    "cost": null,
    "accuracy": null
  },
  {
    "global index": "MMLU_formal_logic_43",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion:  Construct a complete truth table for the following pairs of propositions. Then, using the truth tables, determine whether the statements are logically equivalent or contradictory. If neither, determine whether they are consistent or inconsistent. Justify your answers.\n~O ⊃ P and O ∨ P\n\nOptions: \nA. Logically equivalent\nB. Contradictory\nC. Neither logically equivalent nor contradictory, but consistent\nD. Inconsistent\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Here's the truth table:\n\n| O | P | ~O | ~O ⊃ P | O ∨ P |\n|---|---|----|-------|-------|\n| T | T | F  | T     | T     |\n| T | F | F  | T     | T     |\n| F | T | T  | T     | T     |\n| F | F | T  | F     | F     |\n\nComparing the columns for \"~O ⊃ P\" and \"O ∨ P\", we see that they have the same truth values in every row. This means the propositions are logically equivalent.\n\nA. Logically equivalent\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{A}$.",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 147,
        "output_tokens": 150,
        "total_tokens": 297
      },
      "provider": "openrouter",
      "error": null
    },
    "cost": null,
    "accuracy": null
  },
  {
    "global index": "MMLU_formal_logic_72",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion:  Which of the given formulas of PL is the best symbolization of the following sentence?\nA person is considered to have lived with you for all of the present year if the person was born or died in the present year and your home was this person's home for the entire time this person was alive.\n\nOptions: \nA. [(B ∨ D) • H] ⊃ L\nB. [L ⊃ (B ∨ D)] • H\nC. L ⊃ [(B ∨ D) • H]\nD. (B ∨ D • H) ⊃ L\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The sentence states that *if* a person lived with you at home the entire time they were alive, *then* they are considered to have lived with you for all of the present year. The condition for living with you for the whole year is the person being born or dying in the present year AND your home being their home for their entire life.\n\nLet L represent \"The person lived with you for all of the present year.\"\nLet B represent \"The person was born in the present year.\"\nLet D represent \"The person died in the present year.\"\nLet H represent \"Your home was this person's home for the entire time this person was alive.\"\n\nThe sentence can be broken down: \"A person is considered to have lived with you for all of the present year (L) *if* [the person was born or died in the present year (B ∨ D) *and* your home was this person's home for the entire time this person was alive (H)].\"\n\nThis structure \"L if (condition)\" translates to \"(condition) ⊃ L\".\nThe condition is \"(B ∨ D) • H\".\nTherefore, the correct symbolization is [(B ∨ D) • H] ⊃ L.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{A}$.",
