    "global index": "MMLU_formal_logic_71",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion:  Use indirect truth tables to determine whether each set of propositions is consistent. If the set is consistent, choose an option with a consistent valuation. (There may be other consistent valuations.)\n~N ≡ (O · P)\n~O ⊃ Q\nN · ~Q\n\nOptions: \nA. Inconsistent\nB. Consistent. Consistent valuation when N, O, and P are true and Q is false\nC. Consistent. Consistent valuation when N and O are true and P and Q are false\nD. Consistent. Consistent valuation when N and P are true and O and Q are false\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Let's analyze the given propositions using an indirect truth table approach. We assume the set is consistent and try to find a valuation that makes all propositions true.\n\nThe propositions are:\n1. ~N ≡ (O · P)\n2. ~O ⊃ Q\n3. N · ~Q\n\nAssume the set is satisfiable. This means there exists an assignment of truth values to N, O, P, and Q such that all three propositions are true.\n\nFrom proposition 3, N · ~Q is true. This implies that N is true and ~Q is true.\nIf ~Q is true, then Q must be false.\n\nNow we have N is true and Q is false. Let's use this information in the other propositions.\n\nFrom proposition 1, ~N ≡ (O · P).\nSince N is true, ~N is false.\nSo, False ≡ (O · P).\nFor this equivalence to be true, (O · P) must be false. This means that either O is false, or P is false, or both are false.\n\nFrom proposition 2, ~O ⊃ Q.\nSince Q is false, the consequent is false. For the implication to be true, the antecedent ~O must be false.\nIf ~O is false, then O must be true.\n\nNow we have a contradiction:\nFrom evaluating proposition 1 (given N is true and Q is false), we deduced that (O · P) must be false.\nFrom evaluating proposition 2 (given Q is false), we deduced that O must be true.\n\nIf O is true, then for (O · P) to be false, P must be false.\nLet's check this potential valuation: N=True, O=True, P=False, Q=False.\n\n1. ~N ≡ (O · P)\n   ~True ≡ (True · False)\n   False ≡ False  (This is true)\n\n2. ~O ⊃ Q\n   ~True ⊃ False\n   False ⊃ False (This is true)\n\n3. N · ~Q\n   True · ~False\n   True · True (This is true)\n\nAll three propositions are true with the valuation N=True, O=True, P=False, Q=False. Therefore, the set of propositions is consistent. We need to find the option that reflects this.\n\nLet's re-examine the options based on our findings. We found a consistent valuation where N is true, O is true, P is false, and Q is false. None of the provided options explicitly state this exact valuation. However, the question asks for *a* consistent valuation if the set is consistent. Let's double check our derivation.\n\nWe derived N=True, ~Q=True (so Q=False).\nFrom ~N ≡ (O · P), and ~N is false, so (O · P) is false.\nFrom ~O ⊃ Q, and Q is false, so ~O must be false, meaning O is true.\nIf O is true, and (O · P) is false, then P must be false.\n\nSo, the valuation is N=True, O=True, P=False, Q=False.\n\nLet's re-examine the options carefully. It seems there might be a misunderstanding in how the options are presented or an error in the options themselves matching our derived consistent valuation.\n\nLet's re-read the question: \"If the set is consistent, choose an option with a consistent valuation.\"\n\nLet's check if any provided option *could* lead to consistency by trying to work backwards or if there's a mistake in our reasoning.\n\nLet's assume option B is correct: N=True, O=True, P=True, Q=False.\n1. ~N ≡ (O · P)  => ~True ≡ (True · True) => False ≡ True (False) -> Option B is incorrect.\n\nLet's assume option C is correct: N=True, O=True, P=False, Q=False.\n1. ~N ≡ (O · P) => ~True ≡ (True · False) => False ≡ False (True)\n2. ~O ⊃ Q => ~True ⊃ False => False ⊃ False (True)\n3. N · ~Q => True · ~False => True · True (True)\nThis valuation (N=True, O=True, P=False, Q=False) makes all statements true. Therefore, the set is consistent and option C describes a consistent valuation that matches our findings.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{C}$.",
