    "global index": "MMLU_formal_logic_108",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Use indirect truth tables to determine whether the following argument is valid. If the argument is invalid, choose an option which presents a counterexample. (There may be other counterexamples as well.)\nH ≡ (I · ~J)\nK ≡ ~H\n~(K ⊃ J) / I ≡ J\n\nOptions: \nA. Valid\nB. Invalid. Counterexample when I, H, and K are true and J is false\nC. Invalid. Counterexample when I and H are true and J and K are false\nD. Invalid. Counterexample when K is true and I, H, and J are false\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To determine validity using indirect truth tables, we assume the premises are true and the conclusion is false, and then check for contradictions.\n\nPremises:\n1. H ≡ (I · ~J)\n2. K ≡ ~H\n3. ~(K ⊃ J)\n\nConclusion:\nI ≡ J\n\nWe want to see if we can make premises 1, 2, and 3 true, and the conclusion (I ≡ J) false.\n\nIf I ≡ J is false, then either (I is true and J is false) or (I is false and J is true).\n\nLet's try to make the conclusion false by setting I = T and J = F.\n\nFrom premise 3, ~(K ⊃ J), if J is F, then for this to be true, K must be T.\nSo, K = T.\n\nFrom premise 2, K ≡ ~H, if K is T, then ~H must be T, which means H = F.\n\nNow let's check premise 1, H ≡ (I · ~J).\nWe have H = F, I = T, and J = F.\nSo, ~J = T.\nThe right side (I · ~J) becomes (T · T), which is T.\nPremise 1 becomes F ≡ T, which is false. This assignment is not a counterexample because it makes a premise false.\n\nLet's try the other way to make the conclusion false: I = F and J = T.\n\nFrom premise 3, ~(K ⊃ J), if J is T, then (K ⊃ J) is always true regardless of K's truth value. So, ~(K ⊃ J) would be false. This means we cannot set J to T if we want premise 3 to be true. This implies that the combination I=F and J=T can never make all premises true.\n\nLet's re-examine the conditions for premise 3: ~(K ⊃ J).\nThis is equivalent to ~(~K v J), which is K & ~J.\nFor premise 3 to be true, both K must be true and J must be false.\nSo, K = T and J = F.\n\nNow let's use these values with the other premises.\nFrom premise 2: K ≡ ~H. Since K = T, then ~H must be T, so H = F.\n\nFrom premise 1: H ≡ (I · ~J). We have H = F and J = F. So ~J = T.\nThe right side is (I · T), which simplifies to I.\nSo premise 1 becomes F ≡ I. For premise 1 to be true, I must be F.\n\nSo, a potential assignment to make the premises true is:\nK = T, J = F, H = F, I = F.\n\nNow let's check if this assignment makes the conclusion I ≡ J false.\nI = F and J = F.\nI ≡ J becomes F ≡ F, which is true.\nThis assignment makes the premises true and the conclusion true. This does not help us find a counterexample.\n\nLet's consider the case where the argument is invalid. This means there exists a combination of truth values for I, J, H, and K such that all premises are true, but the conclusion is false.\n\nWe found that for premise 3 ~(K ⊃ J) to be true, we must have K=T and J=F.\n\nFrom premise 2: K ≡ ~H. Since K=T, we have T ≡ ~H, which means ~H is T, so H=F.\n\nFrom premise 1: H ≡ (I · ~J). Since H=F and J=F (~J=T), we have F ≡ (I · T), which simplifies to F ≡ I. For premise 1 to be true, I must be F.\n\nThus, the only way to make all premises true is:\nK = T, J = F, H = F, I = F.\n\nWith these values, let's check the conclusion I ≡ J:\nI = F, J = F. So, F ≡ F, which is True.\nThis scenario makes the premises true and the conclusion true. This means the argument is valid.\n\nLet's re-check the logic of premise 3: ~(K ⊃ J). This is equivalent to K & ~J. So, K must be True and J must be False.\nK = T, J = F.\n\nPremise 2: K ≡ ~H.\nT ≡ ~H means ~H = T, so H = F.\n\nPremise 1: H ≡ (I · ~J).\nF ≡ (I · ~F).\nF ≡ (I · T).\nF ≡ I.\nThis means I must be F for premise 1 to be true.\n\nSo, the only assignment that makes all premises true is:\nI = F, J = F, H = F, K = T.\n\nNow, check the conclusion: I ≡ J.\nI = F, J = F.\nF ≡ F is True.\n\nSince the only way to make the premises true results in the conclusion also being true, the argument is valid.\n\nLet's check the options provided.\nA. Valid\n\nLet's re-evaluate if we made any mistake.\nIf the argument is invalid, we need premises true, conclusion false.\nPremise 3: ~(K ⊃ J) implies K & ~J. So K=T, J=F.\nPremise 2: K ≡ ~H. T ≡ ~H implies H=F.\nPremise 1: H ≡ (I · ~J). F ≡ (I · ~F) => F ≡ (I · T) => F ≡ I. So I=F.\n\nThis leads to I=F, J=F, H=F, K=T.\nCheck conclusion I ≡ J: F ≡ F is True.\nThis implies the argument is valid.\n\nLet's check the provided options again. If the argument is valid, option A is correct.\n\nLet's assume there is a counterexample and see if any of the options fit.\nIf invalid, there is a case where premises are true, and conclusion is false.\nConclusion false means I is T and J is F, OR I is F and J is T.\n\nCase 1: I=T, J=F.\nFrom premise 3 (K & ~J): K & ~F => K & T. So K=T.\nFrom premise 2 (K ≡ ~H): T ≡ ~H => ~H=T => H=F.\nCheck premise 1 (H ≡ (I · ~J)): F ≡ (T · ~F) => F ≡ (T · T) => F ≡ T. This is False. So this case is not a counterexample.\n\nCase 2: I=F, J=T.\nFrom premise 3 (K & ~J): K & ~T => K & F. This is always False. So premise 3 cannot be true if J=T.\nThis means there is no scenario where I=F and J=T, all premises are true.\n\nLet's re-examine the options if the argument is invalid. It seems very likely this argument is valid.\n\nLet's confirm the truth table for equivalence and implication.\nP ≡ Q is true when P and Q have the same truth value.\nP ⊃ Q is false only when P is true and Q is false.\n~P is true when P is false.\n\nLet's assume the argument is invalid and try to find a scenario where premises are True and conclusion is False.\nConclusion I ≡ J is False means (I=T and J=F) or (I=F and J=T).\n\nScenario 1: I=T, J=F\nPremise 3: ~(K ⊃ J) => ~(K ⊃ F). For this to be True, K ⊃ F must be False. This happens when K=T and F=F, which is impossible. So K ⊃ F is always True if J is F? No. K ⊃ F is true unless K is T and F is F. So K ⊃ F is true if K is F, and K ⊃ F is false if K is T.\nSo, for ~(K ⊃ J) to be True when J=F, K ⊃ F must be False. This requires K to be True.\nSo, K=T, J=F.\n\nPremise 2: K ≡ ~H. T ≡ ~H. So ~H=T, H=F.\n\nPremise 1: H ≡ (I · ~J). F ≡ (T · ~F). F ≡ (T · T). F ≡ T. This is False.\nSo this assignment (I=T, J=F, K=T, H=F) makes premise 1 false. This is not a counterexample.\n\nScenario 2: I=F, J=T\nPremise 3: ~(K ⊃ J) => ~(K ⊃ T). K ⊃ T is always True regardless of K. So ~(K ⊃ T) is always False.\nThis means that it's impossible for premise 3 to be true if J is true.\nTherefore, arguments with J=T cannot have all premises true.\n\nThis strongly suggests the argument is valid.\n\nLet me review the options. If the argument is valid, the answer is A.\n\nLet's try to falsify the conclusion: I ≡ J.\nAssume I is True and J is False.\nWe derived K=T, H=F.\nSubstitute into H ≡ (I · ~J): F ≡ (T · ~F) => F ≡ (T · T) => F ≡ T which is false.\nThis means that if I is True and J is False, one of the premises is necessarily false. So I=T, J=F cannot be part of a counterexample.\n\nAssume I is False and J is True.\nPremise 3: ~(K ⊃ J) => ~(K ⊃ T). Since K ⊃ T is always True, ~(K ⊃ T) is always False.\nThis means if J is True, Premise 3 is False.\nSo I=F, J=T cannot be part of a counterexample.\n\nSince there is no way to make the premises true and the conclusion false, the argument is valid.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{A}$.",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 196,
        "output_tokens": 2330,
        "total_tokens": 2526
      },
