    "global index": "MMLU_formal_logic_57",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion:  Use indirect truth tables to determine whether each set of propositions is consistent. If the set is consistent, choose an option with a consistent valuation. (There may be other consistent valuations.)\n(T ⊃ U) ⊃ (W ⊃ U)\nT ⊃ ~(U ⊃ X)\n(T ⊃ ~X) ⊃ W\n\nOptions: \nA. Inconsistent\nB. Consistent. Consistent valuation when U, W, and X are true and T is false\nC. Consistent. Consistent valuation when W and X are true and T and U are false\nD. Consistent. Consistent valuation when T and U are true and W and X are false\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Let's analyze the given propositions using indirect truth tables. The goal is to find a valuation where all propositions are true. If no such valuation exists, the set is inconsistent.\n\nWe are given three propositions:\n1. (T ⊃ U) ⊃ (W ⊃ U)\n2. T ⊃ ~(U ⊃ X)\n3. (T ⊃ ~X) ⊃ W\n\nLet's assume a consistent valuation exists and try to derive a contradiction. We can try to make the antecedents of the conditional statements true and the consequents false.\n\nConsider option B: U, W, and X are true, and T is false.\nLet's evaluate each proposition with T=F, U=T, W=T, X=T.\n\nProposition 1: (F ⊃ T) ⊃ (T ⊃ T)\n(T) ⊃ (T)\nT\n\nProposition 2: F ⊃ ~(T ⊃ T)\nF ⊃ ~(T)\nF ⊃ F\nT\n\nProposition 3: (F ⊃ ~T) ⊃ T\n(F ⊃ F) ⊃ T\n(T) ⊃ T\nT\n\nSince all three propositions are true with this valuation, the set of propositions is consistent.\n\nLet's check the other options to be sure.\n\nConsider option C: W and X are true, and T and U are false.\nLet's evaluate each proposition with T=F, U=F, W=T, X=T.\n\nProposition 1: (F ⊃ F) ⊃ (T ⊃ F)\n(T) ⊃ (F)\nF\nSince proposition 1 is false, this is not a consistent valuation.\n\nConsider option D: T and U are true, and W and X are false.\nLet's evaluate each proposition with T=T, U=T, W=F, X=F.\n\nProposition 1: (T ⊃ T) ⊃ (F ⊃ T)\n(T) ⊃ (T)\nT\n\nProposition 2: T ⊃ ~(T ⊃ F)\nT ⊃ ~(F)\nT ⊃ T\nT\n\nProposition 3: (T ⊃ ~F) ⊃ F\n(T ⊃ T) ⊃ F\n(T) ⊃ F\nF\nSince proposition 3 is false, this is not a consistent valuation.\n\nTherefore, option B provides a consistent valuation.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{B}$.",
