    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Let's analyze the premises and the options to find the immediate consequence.\n\nPremises:\n1. ~E ⊃ ~F\n2. G ⊃ F\n3. H ∨ ~E\n4. H ⊃ I\n5. ~I\n\nWe are looking for a conclusion that can be derived in a single step from the given premises.\n\nLet's examine the options:\n\nA. E ⊃ F: This is the contrapositive of premise 1 (~E ⊃ ~F). The contrapositive of a conditional statement is logically equivalent to the original statement. Therefore, E ⊃ F is an immediate consequence of ~E ⊃ ~F.\n\nB. F ⊃ G: This is the contrapositive of premise 2 (G ⊃ F). The contrapositive of G ⊃ F is ~F ⊃ ~G. This is not directly derivable in one step.\n\nC. H ⊃ ~E: This is not directly derivable.\n\nD. ~H: We can derive ~H using premises 4 and 5. From H ⊃ I and ~I, by modus tollens, we can derive ~H. This is a one-step deduction using two premises (H ⊃ I and ~I).\n\nNow let's re-read the question carefully: \"Which of the following propositions is an immediate (one-step) consequence in PL of the given premises?\"\n\nOption A, E ⊃ F, is equivalent to ~E ⊃ ~F. The logical equivalence allows us to infer it directly.\n\nOption D, ~H, is a consequence of Modus Tollens applied to H ⊃ I and ~I. This is also a one-step deduction.\n\nHowever, the question asks for \"an\" immediate consequence. Both A and D are one-step consequences. Let's check if there's any nuance being missed. Often, \"immediate consequence\" refers to a derivation that uses the fewest number of premises or is a direct application of a rule of inference.\n\nLet's consider if there's a preferred answer. The equivalence for option A comes from a single premise. The deduction for option D uses two premises (H ⊃ I and ~I). In the context of many logic systems, deriving a logically equivalent statement from a single premise is considered an immediate consequence.\n\nLet's assume the question is asking for a consequence derived by applying a rule of inference to one or more premises where the result is one of the options.\n\nOption A is the contrapositive of ~E ⊃ ~F. The contrapositive rule states that P ⊃ Q is equivalent to ~Q ⊃ ~P. So, ~E ⊃ ~F is equivalent to ~(~F) ⊃ ~(~E), which simplifies to F ⊃ E. This is not option A. Let's recheck the contrapositive. The contrapositive of P ⊃ Q is ~Q ⊃ ~P. So, the contrapositive of ~E ⊃ ~F is ~(~F) ⊃ ~(~E), which is F ⊃ E. This is not option A.\n\nAh, there was a mistake in my initial reasoning for option A. Let's reassess.\n\nPremise 1: ~E ⊃ ~F. The contrapositive of this is ~(~F) ⊃ ~(~E), which is F ⊃ E.\n\nLet's go back to option A: E ⊃ F. This is indeed the contrapositive of ~F ⊃ ~E. We don't have ~F ⊃ ~E as a premise.\n\nLet's re-evaluate all options and look for direct applications of rules.\n\nConsider the possibility of a typo or misinterpretation. If the question meant E ⊃ F is equivalent to ~F ⊃ ~E, and one of the premises was ~F ⊃ ~E, then A would be an immediate consequence.\n\nLet's re-examine the given premises and deductive steps:\n\nFrom premises 4 (H ⊃ I) and 5 (~I), by Modus Tollens, we can derive ~H. This is a one-step deduction. So option D is a one-step consequence.\n\nLet's consider other options.\nPremise 3: H ∨ ~E.\nPremise 5: ~I.\nPremise 4: H ⊃ I.\n\nIf we were to look for a longer chain, we could use H ∨ ~E and H ⊃ I.\nIf H is true, then I is true (from H ⊃ I).\nIf H is false, then ~E is true (from H ∨ ~E).\n\nLet's assume option D (~H) is indeed a one-step consequence.\n\nNow let's reconsider option A: E ⊃ F.\nWe have ~E ⊃ ~F.\nThe contrapositive of this is F ⊃ E.\n\nLet's assume the question is well-formed and there's a unique best answer.\nOption D (~H) can be derived using Modus Tollens from H ⊃ I and ~I. This is a standard one-step inference.\n\nLet's consider if any other option can be derived in one step.\nOption B: F ⊃ G. This is the contrapositive of G ⊃ F, which is ~F ⊃ ~G. We have G ⊃ F, so its contrapositive is ~F ⊃ ~G. So, ~F ⊃ ~G is derivable. Option B is F ⊃ G. This is not directly derivable in one step from G ⊃ F.\n\nOption C: H ⊃ ~E. We have H ∨ ~E. This is not a direct implication.\n\nGiven the options, Option D (~H) is the most straightforward one-step consequence derived using Modus Tollens.\n\nFinal check:\nPremise 4: H ⊃ I\nPremise 5: ~I\nRule: Modus Tollens (If P ⊃ Q and ~Q, then ~P)\nApplication: P is H, Q is I. We have H ⊃ I and ~I. Therefore, we can conclude ~H.\nThis is a one-step deduction.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{D}$.",
