      "generated_answer": "Let's construct the truth table for the argument:\n\n| X | Y | Z | ~X | ~X ⊃ Y | Y ⊃ Z | ~Z | (~X ⊃ Y) ∧ (Y ⊃ Z) | ((~X ⊃ Y) ∧ (Y ⊃ Z)) ⊃ ~Z |\n|---|---|---|----|--------|-------|----|--------------------|-----------------------------|\n| T | T | T | F  | T      | T     | F  | T                  | F                           |\n| T | T | F | F  | T      | F     | T  | F                  | T                           |\n| T | F | T | F  | T      | T     | F  | T                  | F                           |\n| T | F | F | F  | T      | T     | T  | T                  | T                           |\n| F | T | T | T  | T      | T     | F  | T                  | F                           |\n| F | T | F | T  | T      | F     | T  | F                  | T                           |\n| F | F | T | T  | F      | T     | F  | F                  | T                           |\n| F | F | F | T  | F      | T     | T  | F                  | T                           |\n\nThe argument is valid if the conjunction of the premises implies the conclusion in every row where all premises are true. We check the rows where (~X ⊃ Y) and (Y ⊃ Z) and ~Z are all true.\n\nIn the given argument, the premises are:\n1. ~X ⊃ Y\n2. Y ⊃ Z\n3. ~Z\n\nAnd the conclusion is:\n~X\n\nWe need to find a row where all premises are true, but the conclusion is false. Looking at the truth table above:\n\nIn row 1: X=T, Y=T, Z=T.\n~X is F.\n~X ⊃ Y is T (F ⊃ T).\nY ⊃ Z is T (T ⊃ T).\n~Z is F.\nThe premises are not all true in this row.\n\nWe are looking for a scenario where ~X ⊃ Y is true, Y ⊃ Z is true, and ~Z is true, but ~X is false.\n\nLet's re-examine the truth table for when all premises are true.\nWe need to find a row where (~X ⊃ Y) is true, (Y ⊃ Z) is true, and ~Z is true.\n\nChecking the rows:\nRow 2: X=T, Y=T, Z=F. ~X = F. ~X ⊃ Y = T. Y ⊃ Z = F. Premise 2 is false.\nRow 4: X=T, Y=F, Z=F. ~X = F. ~X ⊃ Y = T. Y ⊃ Z = T. ~Z = T. All premises are true.\nIn this row, X = T, Y = F, Z = F.\n~X is F (which is the conclusion). In this row, the premises are (T), (T), (T), and the conclusion is (F). This is incorrect. In this row, ~X is F. We need to find a counterexample where ~X is TRUE but the premises are true and conclusion is FALSE.\n\nLet's re-evaluate the condition for invalidity. The argument is invalid if there is AT LEAST ONE row where all premises are true AND the conclusion is FALSE.\n\nLet's focus on finding a situation where the premises are true and the conclusion is false.\nWe want:\n1. ~X ⊃ Y is T\n2. Y ⊃ Z is T\n3. ~Z is T\nAND\n4. ~X is F (meaning X is T)\n\nIf ~X is F, then X is T.\nIf X is T, then ~X ⊃ Y becomes F ⊃ Y, which is always T regardless of Y. So premise 1 is satisfied if X is T.\nIf ~Z is T, then Z is F.\nIf Z is F, then Y ⊃ Z becomes Y ⊃ F. For this to be true, Y must be F.\n\nSo, we have found a scenario:\nX = T\nY = F\nZ = F\n\nLet's check the premises and conclusion with these values:\n~X = F\n~X ⊃ Y = F ⊃ F = T (Premise 1 is true)\nY ⊃ Z = F ⊃ F = T (Premise 2 is true)\n~Z = ~F = T (Premise 3 is true)\n~X = F (Conclusion)\n\nWe have found a row where all premises (~X ⊃ Y, Y ⊃ Z, ~Z) are true, and the conclusion (~X) is false. This means the argument is invalid.\n\nThe conditions for this counterexample are X is true, Y is false, and Z is false.\n\nLet's check the options to see which one matches this counterexample.\n\nOption A. Valid - Incorrect.\nOption B. Invalid. Counterexample when X, Y, and Z are true.\nIf X=T, Y=T, Z=T:\n~X ⊃ Y is T (F ⊃ T)\nY ⊃ Z is T (T ⊃ T)\n~Z is F. Premise 3 is false. This is not a counterexample.\n\nOption C. Invalid. Counterexample when Y and Z are true and Z is false. This option is contradictory as Z cannot be both true and false.\n\nOption D. Invalid. Counterexample when X is true and Y and Z are false.\nThis matches our derived counterexample: X=T, Y=F, Z=F.\nLet's re-verify the truth values from the table for this case:\nRow 4: X=T, Y=F, Z=F\n~X = F\n~X ⊃ Y = F ⊃ 