 = T ⊃ (T ⊃ F) = T ⊃ F = F (False)\n\nSince we found an assignment for which all premises are true and the conclusion is false, the argument is invalid. We need to find an option that reflects this invalidity and provides a counterexample that matches our finding.\n\nLet's re-examine the options to see if any combination of true/false aligns with our identified counterexample: {L=T, M=F, N=T, O=F, P=F, Q=F, R=T}.\n\nOption B: Invalid. Counterexample when L, M, O, Q, and R are true and N and P are false. This is not our counterexample.\nOption C: Invalid. Counterexample when L, N, O, Q, and R are true and M and P are false. This is not our counterexample.\nOption D: Invalid. Counterexample when L, N, and R are true and M, O, P, and Q are false. This is not our counterexample.\n\nThere seems to be an issue with the provided options, as our derived counterexample is not listed. However, if we are forced to choose an option, it implies that one of them should be correct and represent *a* counterexample, not necessarily the *only* one, and perhaps we made an error in the initial assignments. Let's re-evaluate the goal: find a counterexample.\n\nLet's start with the conclusion being false: L=T, and R=T, P=F.\nPremise 3 is R ⊃ ~Q. If R=T, then ~Q must be T, so Q=F.\nPremise 2 is (N ⊃ O) ⊃ (~P ⊃ Q). With P=F, Q=F, this is (N ⊃ O) ⊃ (T ⊃ F), which is (N ⊃ O) ⊃ F. For this to be T, (N ⊃ O) must be F, meaning N=T and O=F.\nSo far: L=T, R=T, P=F, Q=F, N=T, O=F.\nPremise 1 is L ⊃ [(M ∨ ~N) ⊃ O]. With L=T, [(M ∨ ~N) ⊃ O] must be T.\nSubstitute N=T and O=F: [(M ∨ ~T) ⊃ F] = [(M ∨ F) ⊃ F] = [M ⊃ F].\nFor M ⊃ F to be T, M must be F.\n\nOur counterexample is: L=T, M=F, N=T, O=F, P=F, Q=F, R=T.\n\nLet's re-examine the options, assuming there might be subtle variations or that one of them might be the intended correct answer despite not perfectly matching our exact derivation. Often, when constructing counterexamples, we try to make as many variables true as possible.\n\nLet's try to make the argument invalid and check the options again. The problem states to use indirect truth tables. Our method of assuming premises are true and conclusion is false *is* the method of indirect truth tables.\n\nLet's re-read the options very carefully.\nOption D: Invalid. Counterexample when L, N, and R are true and M, O, P, and Q are false.\nLet's test this option's assignment: L=T, N=T, R=T, M=F, O=F, P=F, Q=F.\n\nCheck Premises:\nP1: L ⊃ [(M ∨ ~N) ⊃ O] = T ⊃ [(F ∨ ~T) ⊃ F] = T ⊃ [(F ∨ F) ⊃ F] = T ⊃ [F ⊃ F] = T ⊃ T = T (True)\nP2: (N ⊃ O) ⊃ (~P ⊃ Q) = (T ⊃ F) ⊃ (~F ⊃ F) = F ⊃ (T ⊃ F) = F ⊃ F = T (True)\nP3: R ⊃ ~Q = T ⊃ ~F = T ⊃ T = T (True)\n\nCheck Conclusion:\nL ⊃ (R ⊃ P) = T ⊃ (T ⊃ F) = T ⊃ F = F (False)\n\nThis counterexample from option D works! Thus, the argument is invalid.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{D}$.",
