  {
    "global index": "MMLU_formal_logic_30",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion:  Use indirect truth tables to determine whether each set of propositions is consistent. If the set is consistent, choose an option with a consistent valuation. (There may be other consistent valuations.)\nA ⊃ (~B ∨ C)\nC ⊃ D\nA ∨ (B · ~D)\nA ≡ (B ⊃ D)\n\nOptions: \nA. Inconsistent\nB. Consistent. Consistent valuation when A and D are true and B and C are false\nC. Consistent. Consistent valuation when A, B, and D are true and C is false\nD. Consistent. Consistent valuation when C and D are true and A and B are false\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Let's use indirect truth tables to determine consistency. We will assume the set is inconsistent and try to find a contradiction. An indirect truth table works by assuming the propositions are *all* true and then looking for a contradiction. If we can find a set of truth values for the atomic propositions that makes all the given propositions true, then the set is consistent. If we find a contradiction, meaning no such truth assignment exists, then the set is inconsistent.\n\nWe are given the following propositions:\n1. $A \\supset (\\neg B \\vee C)$\n2. $C \\supset D$\n3. $A \\vee (B \\cdot \\neg D)$\n4. $A \\equiv (B \\supset D)$\n\nLet's assume a consistent valuation exists and try to find truth values for A, B, C, and D that make all four propositions true. We will explore the options first, as they provide potential consistent valuations.\n\n**Option B: Consistent valuation when A is true, D is true, B is false, and C is false.**\nLet A=T, D=T, B=F, C=F.\n\nCheck proposition 1: $A \\supset (\\neg B \\vee C)$ becomes $T \\supset (\\neg F \\vee F)$ which is $T \\supset (T \\vee F)$ which is $T \\supset T$, which is $T$. (True)\nCheck proposition 2: $C \\supset D$ becomes $F \\supset T$, which is $T$. (True)\nCheck proposition 3: $A \\vee (B \\cdot \\neg D)$ becomes $T \\vee (F \\cdot \\neg T)$ which is $T \\vee (F \\cdot F)$ which is $T \\vee F$, which is $T$. (True)\nCheck proposition 4: $A \\equiv (B \\supset D)$ becomes $T \\equiv (F \\supset T)$ which is $T \\equiv T$, which is $T$. (True)\n\nSince all propositions are true with this valuation, the set is consistent.\n\n**Option C: Consistent valuation when A, B, and D are true and C is false.**\nLet A=T, B=T, D=T, C=F.\n\nCheck proposition 1: $A \\supset (\\neg B \\vee C)$ becomes $T \\supset (\\neg T \\vee F)$ which is $T \\supset (F \\vee F)$ which is $T \\supset F$, which is $F$. (False)\nThis valuation makes proposition 1 false, so this option is incorrect.\n\n**Option D: Consistent valuation when C and D are true and A and B are false.**\nLet C=T, D=T, A=F, B=F.\n\nCheck proposition 1: $A \\supset (\\neg B \\vee C)$ becomes $F \\supset (\\neg F \\vee T)$ which is $F \\supset (T \\vee T)$ which is $F \\supset T$, which is $T$. (True)\nCheck proposition 2: $C \\supset D$ becomes $T \\supset T$, which is $T$. (True)\nCheck proposition 3: $A \\vee (B \\cdot \\neg D)$ becomes $F \\vee (F \\cdot \\neg T)$ which is $F \\vee (F \\cdot F)$ which is $F \\vee F$, which is $F$. (False)\nThis valuation makes proposition 3 false, so this option is incorrect.\n\nSince option B yields a consistent valuation, the set of propositions is consistent.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{B}$.",
