    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To determine consistency, we assume the negation of the conjunction of all propositions and try to find a contradiction. Let's analyze the provided options.\n\nOption B suggests E is true and F, G, H, I, J are false.\n1. (E ⊃ F) ⊃ G becomes (T ⊃ F) ⊃ F, which is F ⊃ F, which is T.\n2. (~E ∨ F) ≡ (H ∨ I) becomes (~T ∨ F) ≡ (F ∨ F), which is (F ∨ F) ≡ F, which is F ≡ F, which is T.\n3. I ⊃ (J ⊃ ~H) becomes F ⊃ (F ⊃ ~F), which is F ⊃ (F ⊃ T), which is F ⊃ T, which is T.\n4. ~G ⊃ ~J becomes ~F ⊃ ~F, which is T ⊃ T, which is T.\nSince all propositions can be true simultaneously, this set of propositions is consistent.\n\nOption C suggests E and H are true and F, G, I, J are false.\n1. (E ⊃ F) ⊃ G becomes (T ⊃ F) ⊃ F, which is F ⊃ F, which is T.\n2. (~E ∨ F) ≡ (H ∨ I) becomes (~T ∨ F) ≡ (T ∨ F), which is (F ∨ F) ≡ T, which is F ≡ T, which is F.\nThis creates a contradiction, so this valuation is not consistent.\n\nOption D suggests E and I are true and F, G, H, J are false.\n1. (E ⊃ F) ⊃ G becomes (T ⊃ F) ⊃ F, which is F ⊃ F, which is T.\n2. (~E ∨ F) ≡ (H ∨ I) becomes (~T ∨ F) ≡ (F ∨ T), which is (F ∨ F) ≡ T, which is F ≡ T, which is F.\nThis creates a contradiction, so this valuation is not consistent.\n\nTherefore, Option B represents a consistent valuation.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{B}$.",
