    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The question asks to use indirect truth tables to determine the validity of an argument and provide a counterexample if it's invalid. An indirect truth table works by assuming the premises are true and the conclusion is false to see if a contradiction arises. If no contradiction arises, it means a counterexample exists, proving the argument invalid.\n\nLet's assume the premises are true and the conclusion is false.\nPremise 1: E ⊃ (F ∨ ~G) is True\nPremise 2: F ⊃ (~E ∨ ~G) is True\nConclusion: ~(E ≡ F) is False, which means E ≡ F is True.\n\nIf E ≡ F is True, then E and F have the same truth value.\n\nCase 1: E is True and F is True.\nFrom Premise 1: T ⊃ (T ∨ ~G) which is T ⊃ T, which is True. This is consistent.\nFrom Premise 2: T ⊃ (~T ∨ ~G) which is T ⊃ (F ∨ ~G). For this to be true, (F ∨ ~G) must be True.\nSo, F ∨ ~G is True. Since F is True, this condition is satisfied.\nNow we need to find a value for G such that both premises are true and the conclusion is false.\nIf E and F are True, then E ≡ F is True, so ~(E ≡ F) is False. We need to check if the premises can be true in this scenario.\n\nPremise 1: E ⊃ (F ∨ ~G) becomes T ⊃ (T ∨ ~G) which is always True.\nPremise 2: F ⊃ (~E ∨ ~G) becomes T ⊃ (~T ∨ ~G) which is T ⊃ (F ∨ ~G).\nFor this to be True, (F ∨ ~G) must be True.\nSo, we need F ∨ ~G to be True. Since F is True, F ∨ ~G is always True, regardless of G.\n\nSo, if E = True and F = True, then E ≡ F is True, and ~(E ≡ F) is False.\nBoth premises are also true when E = True and F = True.\nPremise 1: T ⊃ (T ∨ ~G) = T\nPremise 2: T ⊃ (~T ∨ ~G) = T ⊃ (F ∨ ~G). This is True if F ∨ ~G is True. Since F is True, F ∨ ~G is True.\n\nTherefore, when E is True and F is True, the premises are true and the conclusion is false. This is a counterexample. We need to check the options.\n\nOption B says \"Invalid. Counterexample when E, F, and G are true\".\nIf E=T, F=T, G=T:\nPremise 1: T ⊃ (T ∨ ~T) = T ⊃ (T ∨ F) = T ⊃ T = T\nPremise 2: T ⊃ (~T ∨ ~T) = T ⊃ (F ∨ F) = T ⊃ F = F.\nThis makes Premise 2 false, so this is not a counterexample.\n\nOption C says \"Invalid. Counterexample when E and F are true and G is false\".\nIf E=T, F=T, G=F:\nPremise 1: T ⊃ (T ∨ ~F) = T ⊃ (T ∨ T) = T ⊃ T = T\nPremise 2: T ⊃ (~T ∨ ~F) = T ⊃ (F ∨ T) = T ⊃ T = T\nConclusion: ~(E ≡ F) = ~(T ≡ T) = ~T = F.\nHere, both premises are true and the conclusion is false. This is a counterexample.\n\nOption D says \"Invalid. Counterexample when E, F, and G are false\".\nIf E=F, F=F, G=F:\nPremise 1: F ⊃ (F ∨ ~F) = F ⊃ (F ∨ T) = F ⊃ T = T\nPremise 2: F ⊃ (~F ∨ ~F) = F ⊃ (T ∨ T) = F ⊃ T = T\nConclusion: ~(E ≡ F) = ~(F ≡ F) = ~T = F.\nHere, both premises are true and the conclusion is false. This is also a counterexample.\n\nHowever, we found earlier that if E=T and F=T, the premises are true and the conclusion is false, regardless of G. So, if E=T, F=T, and G is either T or F, it forms a counterexample.\n\nLet's re-examine the options provided. Since option C provides a specific counterexample where the argument is invalid, and it is a valid counterexample, this is the most likely correct answer.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{C}$.",
