    "global index": "MMLU_formal_logic_35",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Use indirect truth tables to determine whether the following argument is valid. If the argument is invalid, choose an option which presents a counterexample. (There may be other counterexamples as well.)\nP ⊃ (Q ∨ R)\n(Q · S) ⊃ ~P\n~(~Q ∨ R) / ~P\n\nOptions: \nA. Valid\nB. Invalid. Counterexample when P, Q, R, and S are true\nC. Invalid. Counterexample when P and Q are true and R and S are false\nD. Invalid. Counterexample when P, R, and S are true and Q is false\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To use indirect truth tables for validity, we assume all premises are true and the conclusion is false and look for a contradiction.\nPremise 1: P ⊃ (Q ∨ R) is True\nPremise 2: (Q · S) ⊃ ~P is True\nConclusion: ~P is False. This implies P is True.\n\nFrom ~P being False, we know P is True.\nSince P is True and Premise 1 (P ⊃ (Q ∨ R)) is True, then (Q ∨ R) must be True.\nSince P is True and Premise 2 ((Q · S) ⊃ ~P) is True, the antecedent (Q · S) must be False, because the consequent (~P) is False.\nSo, we have P is True, (Q ∨ R) is True, and (Q · S) is False.\nIf (Q · S) is False, then either Q is False or S is False (or both).\nWe also have (Q ∨ R) is True.\n\nLet's consider the case when P is True.\nIf P is True, then ~P is False.\nPremise 2 is (Q · S) ⊃ ~P. For this to be true when ~P is false, (Q · S) must be false. This means either Q is false or S is false, or both are false.\nPremise 1 is P ⊃ (Q ∨ R). Since P is true, for this to be true, (Q ∨ R) must be true. This means either Q is true or R is true, or both are true.\n\nWe need to find a situation where P is True, (Q ∨ R) is True, and (Q · S) is False, and the conclusion ~P is False.\nLet's try to make the premises true and the conclusion false.\nAssume ~P is False, so P is True.\nSince P is True and P ⊃ (Q ∨ R) is True, then Q ∨ R is True.\nSince P is True and (Q · S) ⊃ ~P is True, and ~P is False, then (Q · S) must be False.\n\nSo we need P = T, (Q ∨ R) = T, and (Q · S) = F.\nLet's examine the options' counterexamples.\nB. P, Q, R, S are true.\nP=T, Q=T, R=T, S=T.\nP ⊃ (Q ∨ R): T ⊃ (T ∨ T) = T ⊃ T = T (Premise 1 TRUE)\n(Q · S) ⊃ ~P: (T · T) ⊃ ~T = T ⊃ F = F (Premise 2 FALSE)\nThis is not a counterexample because a premise is false.\n\n\nC. P and Q are true and R and S are false.\nP=T, Q=T, R=F, S=F.\nP ⊃ (Q ∨ R): T ⊃ (T ∨ F) = T ⊃ T = T (Premise 1 TRUE)\n(Q · S) ⊃ ~P: (T · F) ⊃ ~T = F ⊃ F = T (Premise 2 TRUE)\n~P: ~T = F (Conclusion FALSE)\nHere, all premises are true, and the conclusion is false. This makes the argument invalid.\n\nD. P, R, and S are true and Q is false.\nP=T, Q=F, R=T, S=T.\nP ⊃ (Q ∨ R): T ⊃ (F ∨ T) = T ⊃ T = T (Premise 1 TRUE)\n(Q · S) ⊃ ~P: (F · T) ⊃ ~T = F ⊃ F = T (Premise 2 TRUE)\n~P: ~T = F (Conclusion FALSE)\nHere, all premises are true, and the conclusion is false. This also makes the argument invalid.\n\nLet's re-examine the problem and options. The instructions state \"If the argument is invalid, choose an option which presents a counterexample.\" Both C and D are valid counterexamples based on our analysis. However, we need to choose the *most likely* correct answer. Let's try to deduce a specific assignment of truth values from the premises while making the conclusion false.\n\nWe want P = T, (Q ∨ R) = T, and (Q · S) = F.\nIf P = T, then ~P = F.\nFrom (Q · S) ⊃ ~P being True, and ~P being False, we must have (Q · S) = False.\nThis means that at least one of Q or S must be False.\nFrom P ⊃ (Q ∨ R) being True, and P being True, we must have (Q ∨ R) = True.\nThis means that at least one of Q or R must be True.\n\nSo we have:\nP = T\n(Q ∨ R) = T\n(Q · S) = F\n~P = F\n\nWe need to satisfy these conditions.\nCase 1: Q is true.\nIf Q is true, then from (Q ∨ R) = T, this is satisfied.\nFrom (Q · S) = F, if Q is true, then S must be false.\nSo, P=T, Q=T, S=F. Since Q is true, (Q ∨ R) = T is satisfied regardless of R's value.\nLet's check R=F (this corresponds to option C):\nP=T, Q=T, R=F, S=F\nP ⊃ (Q ∨ R): T ⊃ (T ∨ F) = T\n(Q · S) ⊃ ~P: (T · F) ⊃ ~T = F ⊃ F = T\n~P: ~T = F\nThis is a valid counterexample.\n\nLet's check R=T (not explicitly in options but valid if Q=T, S=F, P=T).\nP=T, Q=T, R=T, S=F\nP ⊃ (Q ∨ R): T ⊃ (T ∨ T) = T\n(Q · S) ⊃ ~P: (T · F) ⊃ ~T = F ⊃ F = T\n~P: ~T = F\nThis is also a valid counterexample.\n\nCase 2: Q is false.\nIf Q is false, then from (Q ∨ R) = T, R must be true.\nFrom (Q · S) = F, if Q is false, this is satisfied regardless of S's value.\nSo, P=T, Q=F, R=T.\nLet's check S=T (this corresponds to option D):\nP=T, Q=F, R=T, S=T\nP ⊃ (Q ∨ R): T ⊃ (F ∨ T) = T\n(Q · S) ⊃ ~P: (F · T) ⊃ ~T = F ⊃ F = T\n~P: ~T = F\nThis is a valid counterexample.\n\nLet's check S=F (not explicitly in options but valid if P=T, Q=F, R=T).\nP=T, Q=F, R=T, S=F\nP ⊃ (Q ∨ R): T ⊃ (F ∨ T) = T\n(Q · S) ⊃ ~P: (F · F) ⊃ ~T = F ⊃ F = T\n~P: ~T = F\nThis is also a valid counterexample.\n\nBoth C and D present valid counterexamples. However, we need to select the \"most likely\" correct answer. Let's review the structure of the problem and options. Often, there's a unique solution intended. Let's re-evaluate the premises and conclusion to see if one option is more directly derived or if there's a simpler path to contradiction.\n\nWe established: P=T, Q ∨ R=T, Q · S=F.\nOption C: P=T, Q=T, R=F, S=F.\nP=T (consistent)\nQ ∨ R = T ∨ F = T (consistent)\nQ · S = T · F = F (consistent)\nConclusion: ~P = ~T = F (consistent with conclusion being false)\nAll premises are true, conclusion is false.\n\nOption D: P=T, Q=F, R=T, S=T.\nP=T (consistent)\nQ ∨ R = F ∨ T = T (consistent)\nQ · S = F · T = F (consistent)\nConclusion: ~P = ~T = F (consistent with conclusion being false)\nAll premises are true, conclusion is false.\n\nSince both C and D are valid counterexamples, let's consider if there's any ambiguity in the given information or if one option might arise more naturally from a step-by-step deduction.\n\nLet's try to directly derive the values.\nAssume conclusion is false: ~P is F, so P is T.\nPremise 1: P ⊃ (Q ∨ R) is T. Since P is T, (Q ∨ R) is T.\nPremise 2: (Q · S) ⊃ ~P is T. Since ~P is F, (Q · S) must be F.\n\nSo, we need P=T, (Q ∨ R)=T, (Q · S)=F.\n\nConsider Option C: P=T, Q=T, R=F, S=F.\nP=T (matches)\nQ ∨ R = T ∨ F = T (matches)\nQ · S = T · F = F (matches)\n\nConsider Option D: P=T, Q=F, R=T, S=T.\nP=T (matches)\nQ ∨ R = F ∨ T = T (matches)\nQ · S = F · T = F (matches)\n\nBoth are equally valid counterexamples. It is possible the question intends for a specific order of evaluating truth values. Without further context or clarification on what \"most likely\" means here, we rely on the direct derivation of truth assignments that satisfy the conditions for a counterexample.\n\nLet's think if there's any implication from the order of the disjunction and conjunction expressions. Generally, when trying to falsify a disjunction (Q v R), we might first try to make one part true, and when falsifying a conjunction (Q . S), we might try to make one part false.\n\nGiven P=T and (Q v R)=T and (Q . S)=F.\nIn option C, Q=T, S=F. This makes Q.S=F. And Q=T makes Q v R = T.\nIn option D, Q=F, S=T. This makes Q.S=F. And R=T makes Q v R = T.\n\nHowever, often the simpler truth assignments are encountered first. If we try to satisfy Q v R = T, we could set Q=T or R=T.\n\nIf we set Q=T:\nThen from Q · S = F, S must be F.\nAnd from Q ∨ R = T, R can be anything.\nThis leads to P=T, Q=T, S=F.\nIf we choose R=F (Option C), it fits.\n\nIf we set Q=F:\nThen from Q ∨ R = T, R must be T.\nAnd from Q · S = F, this is already satisfied as Q=F. S can be anything.\nThis leads to P=T, Q=F, R=T.\nIf we choose S=T (Option D), it fits.\n\nIn many standard truth-table exercises where multiple counterexamples exist, any one of them is accepted. However, since a specific option is requested, there might be a preference. Without more specific instructions on how to choose among valid counterexamples, it's hard to definitively pick one over the other if both are logically sound.\n\nLet's assume the question originates from a source where there is a single correct answer. Rechecking the initial steps:\nP=T\nQ ∨ R = T\nQ · S = F\n\nOption C: P=T, Q=T, R=F, S=F. This assignment satisfies all derived conditions.\nOption D: P=T, Q=F, R=T, S=T. This assignment also satisfies all derived conditions.\n\nIf we consider how the options are constructed, it might be that one represents a more \"direct\" or \"minimal\" contradiction found. However, both are direct.\n\nLet's consider if there's any possibility of error in the premise interpretation or the deduction.\nP ⊃ (Q ∨ R)\n(Q · S) ⊃ ~P\n~(~Q ∨ R) / ~P\n\nHold on, there's a third premise: ~ (~Q ∨ R). Let's redo the analysis with all premises.\nWe assume all premises are true and the conclusion is false.\nConclusion: ~P is False, so P is True.\n\nPremise 1: P ⊃ (Q ∨ R) is True. Since P=T, then Q ∨ R is True.\nPremise 2: (Q · S) ⊃ ~P is True. Since ~P=F, then (Q · S) must be False.\nPremise 3: ~ (~Q ∨ R) is True. This means (~Q ∨ R) is False.\nFor (~Q ∨ R) to be False, both ~Q must be False AND R must be False.\nIf ~Q is False, then Q is True.\nIf R is False, then R is False.\n\nSo, from Premise 3, we directly derive that R=F and Q=T.\nNow let's check if these derived values are consistent with our other deductions.\nWe have P=T, Q=T, R=F.\nFrom Premise 1: Q ∨ R = T ∨ F = T. This is consistent.\nFrom Premise 2: Q · S = T · S. For this to be False, S must be False.\nSo the assignment P=T, Q=T, R=F, S=F makes all premises true and the conclusion false.\n\nLet's check this assignment with the original statements:\nP=T, Q=T, R=F, S=F\n\nPremise 1: P ⊃ (Q ∨ R) = T ⊃ (T ∨ F) = T ⊃ T = T (TRUE)\nPremise 2: (Q · S) ⊃ ~P = (T · F) ⊃ ~T = F ⊃ F = T (TRUE)\nPremise 3: ~ (~Q ∨ R) = ~ (~T ∨ F) = ~ (F ∨ F) = ~F = T (TRUE)\nConclusion: ~P = ~T = F (FALSE)\n\nThis assignment (P=T, Q=T, R=F, S=F) is a valid counterexample where all premises are true and the conclusion is false. This corresponds to Option C.\n\nSince we found a definitive counterexample through direct deduction from all premises, and it matches one of the options, this is the correct answer. The inclusion of the third premise was crucial and significantly simplified the deduction.\n\nFinal check on other options given this new information:\nOption B: P, Q, R, S are true.\nPremise 3: ~ (~Q ∨ R) = ~ (~T ∨ T) = ~ (F ∨ T) = ~T = F. Premise 3 is false. Not a counterexample.\n\nOption D: P, R, and S are true and Q is false.\nP=T, Q=F, R=T, S=T.\nPremise 3: ~ (~Q ∨ R) = ~ (~F ∨ T) = ~ (T ∨ T) = ~T = F. Premise 3 is false. Not a counterexample.\n\nTherefore, only Option C is a valid counterexample.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{C}$.",
