  {
    "global index": "MMLU_formal_logic_32",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion:  Which of the following propositions is an immediate (one-step) consequence in PL of the given premises?\nU ⊃ W\nW ⊃ (X ≡ ~Y)\n(X ≡ ~Y) ⊃ Z\n~Z\n\nOptions: \nA. U ⊃ Z\nB. U ∨ W\nC. ~W\nD. ~(X ≡ ~Y)\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Here's how to determine the correct answer:\n\n1.  **Analyze the premises:**\n    *   `U ⊃ W`\n    *   `W ⊃ (X ≡ ~Y)`\n    *   `(X ≡ ~Y) ⊃ Z`\n    *   `~Z`\n\n2.  **Look for one-step deductions:** We want a conclusion that can be derived directly from the premises using a single rule of inference.\n\n3.  **Evaluate the options:**\n    *   A. `U ⊃ Z`: This requires multiple steps (Hypothetical Syllogism: `U ⊃ W` and `W ⊃ (X ≡ ~Y)` leads to `U ⊃ (X ≡ ~Y)`; `U ⊃ (X ≡ ~Y)` and `(X ≡ ~Y) ⊃ Z` leads to `U ⊃ Z`). This is not an immediate consequence.\n    *   B. `U ∨ W`: There's no direct rule to derive a disjunction as an immediate consequence from these conditional and negated premises.\n    *   C. `~W`: We have `(X ≡ ~Y) ⊃ Z` and `~Z`. By Modus Tollens, we can infer `~(X ≡ ~Y)`. We also have `W ⊃ (X ≡ ~Y)`. Applying Modus Tollens again with `~(X ≡ ~Y)` and `W ⊃ (X ≡ ~Y)`, we can infer `~W`. This takes two steps. Let's re-examine to see if there's a single step. *Correction*: The question asks for **an immediate** consequence in PL of the **given premises**. Let's look closer at the premises and how they relate.\n    *   D. `~(X ≡ ~Y)`: We have `(X ≡ ~Y) ⊃ Z` and `~Z`. This is a direct application of Modus Tollens. If `P ⊃ Q` and `~Q`, then `~P`. Here, `P` is `(X ≡ ~Y)` and `Q` is `Z`. Thus, `~(X ≡ ~Y)` is an immediate consequence.\n\nLet's re-evaluate Option C. To get `~W`, we *must* first use Modus Tollens on `(X ≡ ~Y) ⊃ Z` and `~Z` to get `~(X ≡ ~Y)`, and *then* use Modus Tollens on `W ⊃ (X ≡ ~Y)` and `~(X ≡ ~Y)` to get `~W`. So, `~W` is a *two-step* consequence.\n\nOption D is a direct, one-step application of Modus Tollens.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{D}$.",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 143,
        "output_tokens": 614,
        "total_tokens": 757
      },
      "provider": "openrouter",
      "error": null
    },
    "cost": null,
    "accuracy": null
  },
  {
    "global index": "MMLU_formal_logic_76",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion:  Select the best translation into predicate logic: Ms. Jackson lent me some sugar, but neither Janet nor Latoya did.\n\nOptions: \nA. Lm ∨ ~(Jl ∨ Ll)\nB. Lm • ~(Lj • Ll)\nC. Lm • ~(Lj ∨ Ll)\nD. Lm ∨ ~(Lj • Ll)\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Let Lxy be \"x lent y sugar\".\nLet m be \"me\".\nLet j be \"Janet\".\nLet l be \"Latoya\".\n\nMs. Jackson lent me some sugar can be represented as Lm.\nJanet did not lend me some sugar can be represented as ~Lj.\nLatoya did not lend me some sugar can be represented as ~Ll.\n\n\"neither Janet nor Latoya did\" means Janet did not and Latoya did not. This is ~Lj • ~Ll.\nUsing De Morgan's law, ~Lj • ~Ll is equivalent to ~(Lj ∨ Ll).\n\nThe statement \"Ms. Jackson lent me some sugar, but neither Janet nor Latoya did\" combines the two parts with \"but\", which translates to the logical conjunction \"•\".\n\nSo, the best translation is Lm • ~(Lj ∨ Ll).\n\nComparing this with the given options:\nA. Lm ∨ ~(Jl ∨ Ll) - Incorrect because of the OR connective.\nB. Lm • ~(Lj • Ll) - Incorrect because ~(Lj • Ll) is equivalent to ~Lj ∨ ~Ll, not ~Lj • ~Ll.\nC. Lm • ~(Lj ∨ Ll) - Matches our derived translation.\nD. Lm ∨ ~(Lj • Ll) - Incorrect because of the OR connective and the structure of the second part.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{C}$.",
      "success": true,
