    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion:  Select the best translation into predicate logic:: Some blue herons migrate.\n\nOptions: \nA. (∀x)[(Hx • Bx) • Mx]\nB. (∃x)[(Hx • Bx) ⊃ Mx]\nC. (∀x)[(Hx • Bx) ⊃ Mx]\nD. (∃x)[(Hx • Bx) • Mx]\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The question asks for the best translation of \"Some blue herons migrate\" into predicate logic. This statement asserts the existence of at least one individual that is both a blue heron and migrates.\n\nLet's analyze the options:\n- Option A: (∀x)[(Hx • Bx) • Mx] means \"For all x, x is a heron AND x is blue AND x migrates.\" This universal quantification is incorrect because the statement is about \"some,\" not \"all.\"\n- Option B: (∃x)[(Hx • Bx) ⊃ Mx] means \"There exists an x such that if x is a heron and x is blue, then x migrates.\" The conditional (⊃) here is problematic. It implies that if something is not a blue heron, the statement holds true vacuously. This doesn't accurately capture the existential claim.\n- Option C: (∀x)[(Hx • Bx) ⊃ Mx] means \"For all x, if x is a heron and x is blue, then x migrates.\" Like option A, this is a universal quantification, which is incorrect for a statement about \"some.\"\n- Option D: (∃x)[(Hx • Bx) • Mx] means \"There exists an x such that x is a heron AND x is blue AND x migrates.\" This option correctly uses existential quantification (∃x) to express \"some\" and the conjunction (•) to assert that the individuals do both.\n\nTherefore, option D is the best translation.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{D}$.",
