      "generated_answer": "The sentence is \"If either George enrolls or Harry enrolls, then Ira does not enroll.\"\nLet G represent \"George enrolls\", H represent \"Harry enrolls\", and I represent \"Ira enrolls\".\nThe phrase \"either George enrolls or Harry enrolls\" can be symbolized as (G ∨ H).\nThe phrase \"Ira does not enroll\" can be symbolized as ~I.\nThe sentence has the structure \"If X, then Y\", where X is \"either George enrolls or Harry enrolls\" and Y is \"Ira does not enroll\".\nTherefore, the symbolization is (G ∨ H) ⊃ ~I.\n\nComparing this to the options:\nA. (G ⊃ H) ∨ I - Incorrect structure.\nB. (G ∨ H) ⊃ I - Incorrect negation of I.\nC. (G ⊃ H) ∨ ~I - Incorrect structure and implication.\nD. (G ∨ H) ⊃ ~I - Matches our Derived symbolization.\n\nThe correct answer is D.\n\nThe sentence states a conditional. The antecedent is \"either George enrolls or Harry enrolls\", which is (G ∨ H). The consequent is \"Ira does not enroll\", which is ~I. Therefore, the correct symbolization is (G ∨ H) ⊃ ~I.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{D}$.",
