    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Let's break down the sentence into its propositional components and logical connectives.\n\n\"England's importing beef is a necessary condition for France's subsidizing agriculture\" can be symbolized as $F \\implies E$.\n\"China's promoting human rights is not a sufficient condition for South Africa's supplying diamonds\" means it is NOT the case that (if China's promoting human rights, then South Africa's supplying diamonds). This can be symbolized as $\\neg (C \\implies S)$.\n\nThe sentence states \"Either [first part] or [second part]\". This translates to a disjunction ($\\lor$).\n\nSo the sentence is $(F \\implies E) \\lor \\neg (C \\implies S)$.\n\nNow let's look at the options:\n\nA. $(E \\equiv F) \\lor \\neg (C \\equiv S)$: This uses biconditionals, which doesn't match the \"necessary condition\" and \"not a sufficient condition\" language.\nB. $(E \\equiv F) \\lor (\\neg C \\implies S)$: Again, uses a biconditional, and the second part of the disjunction is a conditional, not a negated conditional.\nC. $(E \\implies F) \\lor \\neg (C \\implies S)$: This matches our derived symbolization exactly if we interpret \"England's importing beef is a necessary condition for France's subsidizing agriculture\" as $E \\implies F$. However, the standard interpretation of \"A is a necessary condition for B\" is $B \\implies A$.\nD. $(F \\implies E) \\lor \\neg (C \\implies S)$: This matches our derived symbolization if we interpret \"England's importing beef is a necessary condition for France's subsidizing agriculture\" as $F \\implies E$.\n\nLet's re-evaluate \"England's importing beef is a necessary condition for France's subsidizing agriculture\". If E is necessary for F, it means that if F happens, then E must have happened. This is correctly symbolized as $F \\implies E$.\n\nThe second part, \"China's promoting human rights is not a sufficient condition for South Africa's supplying diamonds\", means that it is not the case that if China's promoting human rights (C) then South Africa's supplying diamonds (S). This is $\\neg(C \\implies S)$.\n\nCombining these with \"either...or\" ($\\lor$): $(F \\implies E) \\lor \\neg(C \\implies S)$.\n\nComparing to the options, option D is $(F \\implies E) \\lor \\neg (C \\implies S)$.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{D}$.",
