      "generated_answer": "Let E symbolize \"England's importing beef.\"\nLet F symbolize \"France's subsidizing agriculture.\"\nLet C symbolize \"China promotes human rights.\"\nLet S symbolize \"South Africa supplies diamonds.\"\n\nThe sentence can be broken down into two parts connected by \"or\" (disjunction).\n\nPart 1: \"England's importing beef is a sufficient condition for France's subsidizing agriculture.\"\n\"A is a sufficient condition for B\" is symbolized as A ⊃ B.\nSo, this part is E ⊃ F.\n\nPart 2: \"China doesn't promote human rights when and only when South Africa supplies diamonds.\"\n\"China doesn't promote human rights\" is symbolized as ~C.\n\"when and only when\" is the biconditional operator (≡).\nSo, this part is ~C ≡ S.\n\nThe entire sentence is the disjunction of these two parts: (E ⊃ F) ∨ (~C ≡ S).\n\nComparing this with the options:\nA. (E ⊃ F) ∨ (~C ⊃ S) - Incorrect, the second part is a conditional, not a biconditional.\nB. (F ⊃ E) ∨ (S ⊃ ~C) - Incorrect, the direction of the implications and the structure of the second part are wrong.\nC. (E ⊃ F) ∨ ~(S ≡ C) - Incorrect, the negation is applied to the entire biconditional in an incorrect way.\nD. (E ⊃ F) ∨ (~C ≡ S) - Correct.\n\nThe correct answer is D. The first part \"England's importing beef is a sufficient condition for France's subsidizing agriculture\" is symbolized as $E \\supset F$. The second part \"China doesn't promote human rights when and only when South Africa supplies diamonds\" is symbolized as $\\lnot C \\equiv S$. The entire sentence is the disjunction of these two, thus $(E \\supset F) \\lor (\\lnot C \\equiv S)$.\n\n\\boxed{D}",
