    "global index": "MMLU_formal_logic_88",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion:  S Select the best translation into predicate logic. Some kind people help some endangered species. (Ex: x is endangered; Kx: x is kind; Px: x is a person; Sx: x is a species; Hxy: x helps y)\n\nOptions: \nA. (∃x){{(Kx • Px) • (∃y)[(Ey • Sy) • Hxy]}}\nB. (∃x)(∃y){{[(Kx • Sx) • (Ey • Py)] • Hxy}}\nC. (∃x)(∃y){{[(Kx • Sx) • (Ey • Py)] • Hyx}}\nD. (∃x)(∃y){{[(Kx • Px) • (Ey • Sy)] ⊃ Hxy}}\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The question asks for the best translation into predicate logic of the statement \"Some kind people help some endangered species.\" Let's analyze the options:\n\n*   **A. (∃x){{(Kx • Px) • (∃y)[(Ey • Sy) • Hxy]}}**: This translates to \"There exists an x such that x is kind and x is a person, and there exists a y such that y is endangered and y is a species, and x helps y.\" This correctly captures \"Some kind people help some endangered species.\" There is at least one kind person who helps at least one endangered species.\n\n*   **B. (∃x)(∃y){{[(Kx • Sx) • (Ey • Py)] • Hxy}}**: This translates to \"There exists an x and there exists a y such that x is kind and x is a species, and y is endangered and y is a person, and x helps y.\" This is incorrect because 'x' is described as a species and 'y' as a person, which contradicts the original statement.\n\n*   **C. (∃x)(∃y){{[(Kx • Sx) • (Ey • Py)] • Hyx}}**: This translates to \"There exists an x and there exists a y such that x is kind and x is a species, and y is endangered and y is a person, and y helps x.\" This is incorrect for the same reasons as B regarding the properties of x and y, and also the direction of help is reversed.\n\n*   **D. (∃x)(∃y){{[(Kx • Px) • (Ey • Sy)] ⊃ Hxy}}**: This translates to \"For all x and for all y, if x is kind and x is a person and y is endangered and y is a species, then x helps y.\" This uses universal quantifiers and implication, which is not what \"some\" indicates. \"Some\" implies existential quantifiers.\n\nOption A correctly uses existential quantifiers for both \"some kind people\" and \"some endangered species,\" and it correctly assigns the properties of being kind and a person to the helper (x) and the properties of being endangered and a species to the helped (y).\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{A}$.",
