  {
    "global index": "MMLU_formal_logic_84",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion:  Use the following key to translate the given formula of PL to natural, English sentences.\nA: Marina reads a Percy Jackson book.\nB: Izzy plays Minecraft.\nC: Emily stops working.\nD: Russell makes dinner.\nE: Ashleigh stops by.\n(A ∨ ~E) ≡ (D • ~B)\n\nOptions: \nA. Either Marina reading a Percy Jackson book or Ashleigh not stopping by is a necessary condition for both Russell making dinner and Izzy not playing Minecraft.\nB. Either Marina reading a Percy Jackson book or Ashleigh not stopping by is a necessary and sufficient condition for Russell making dinner and Izzy playing Minecraft.\nC. Marina reads a Percy Jackson book unless Ashleigh stops by if, and only if, both Russell makes dinner and Izzy doesn't play Minecraft.\nD. Either Marina reads a Percy Jackson book or Ashleigh does not stop by just in case both Russell makes dinner and Izzy doesn't play Minecraft.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The given formula is (A ∨ ~E) ≡ (D • ~B).\nThe symbol '≡' represents \"if and only if\", which means \"necessary and sufficient condition\".\nThe symbol '∨' represents \"or\".\nThe symbol '~' represents \"not\".\nThe symbol '•' represents \"and\".\n\nTranslating the left side (A ∨ ~E): \"Marina reads a Percy Jackson book or Ashleigh does not stop by\".\nTranslating the right side (D • ~B): \"Russell makes dinner and Izzy does not play Minecraft\".\n\nCombining these with '≡': \"Marina reads a Percy Jackson book or Ashleigh does not stop by if and only if Russell makes dinner and Izzy does not play Minecraft\".\n\nLet's analyze the options:\nA. \"necessary condition for both Russell making dinner and Izzy not playing Minecraft.\" This implies (D • ~B) is sufficient for (A ∨ ~E), but not necessarily vice-versa. The '≡' symbol means it's a biconditional (\"if and only if\").\nB. \"Either Marina reading a Percy Jackson book or Ashleigh not stopping by is a necessary and sufficient condition for Russell making dinner and Izzy playing Minecraft.\" The second part of the condition should be \"Russell making dinner and Izzy not playing Minecraft\", not \"Izzy playing Minecraft\".\nC. \"Marina reads a Percy Jackson book unless Ashleigh stops by if, and only if, both Russell makes dinner and Izzy doesn't play Minecraft.\" \"Unless Ashleigh stops by\" can be interpreted as \"or Ashleigh does not stop by\" (~E). So this is A ∨ ~E. The \"if, and only if\" matches the '≡'. The second part \"both Russell makes dinner and Izzy doesn't play Minecraft\" matches D • ~B.\nD. \"Either Marina reads a Percy Jackson book or Ashleigh does not stop by just in case both Russell makes dinner and Izzy doesn't play Minecraft.\" \"Just in case\" can be interpreted as \"if\", but not necessarily \"if and only if\".\n\nOption C correctly captures the \"if and only if\" relationship and translates the logical connectives accurately.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{C}$.",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 261,
        "output_tokens": 466,
        "total_tokens": 727
      },
      "provider": "openrouter",
      "error": null
    },
    "cost": null,
    "accuracy": null
  },
