    "global index": "MMLU_formal_logic_26",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion:  Which of the given formulas of PL is the best symbolization of the following sentence?\nThe restaurant serves pie or cheesecake or ice cream for dessert.\n\nOptions: \nA. P ∨ (C ∨ I)\nB. (P ∨ C) ∨ I\nC. P ∨ C ∨ I\nD. Options A and B\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The sentence \"The restaurant serves pie or cheesecake or ice cream for dessert\" expresses a disjunction that can be grouped in a few ways. In propositional logic, the order of disjunctions does not matter due to associativity. Therefore, all the options A, B, and C represent the same logical proposition. Since options A and B are equivalent and represent the stated sentence, option D is the most comprehensive correct answer.\n\nThe formula $P \\vee (C \\vee I)$ means that either pie is served, or (cheesecake or ice cream is served).\nThe formula $(P \\vee C) \\vee I$ means that either (pie or cheesecake is served), or ice cream is served.\nThe formula $P \\vee C \\vee I$ is a shorthand for either pie is served, or cheesecake is served, or ice cream is served.\n\nAll three forms are logically equivalent due to the associative property of disjunction. Therefore, options A and B are both valid symbolizations and represent the same logical statement as C. Thus, option D, \"Options A and B,\" is the most accurate answer as it acknowledges the validity of both A and B, which are indeed correct and equivalent.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{D}$.",
      "success": true,
