  {
    "global index": "MMLU_formal_logic_101",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion:  Select the best English interpretation of the given arguments in predicate logic.\nWn ∨ Wm\n(∀x)[Lx ⊃ (Dx ⊃ ~Wx)]\nLn • Dn\t/ ~(∀x)~Wx\n\nOptions: \nA. Either Nancy or Marvin are at work. All lawyers are not at work if they are out to dinner. Nancy is a lawyer and out to dinner. So not everything is not at work.\nB. Either Nancy or Marvin are at work. All lawyers are out to dinner if they are not at work. Nancy is a lawyer and out to dinner. So not everything is not at work.\nC. Either Nancy or Marvin are at work. All lawyers are out to dinner if they are not at work. Nancy is a lawyer and out to dinner. So not everything is at work.\nD. Either Nancy or Marvin are at work. All lawyers are not at work if they are out to dinner. Nancy is a lawyer and out to dinner. So not everything is at work.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Let's break down the predicate logic statements and their English interpretations.\n\n*   **Wn ∨ Wm**: This translates to \"Either Nancy is at work (Wn) or Marvin is at work (Wm).\"\n*   **(∀x)[Lx ⊃ (Dx ⊃ ~Wx)]**: This is a bit more complex. It means \"For all x, if x is a lawyer (Lx), then if x is out to dinner (Dx), then x is not at work (~Wx).\" A more natural phrasing is: \"All lawyers are not at work if they are out to dinner.\" or \"If a lawyer is out to dinner, then they are not at work.\"\n*   **Ln • Dn**: This translates to \"Nancy is a lawyer (Ln) and Nancy is out to dinner (Dn).\"\n*   **/ ~(∀x)~Wx**: This is the conclusion. \"~(∀x)~Wx\" is double negation, equivalent to \"(∃x)Wx\". This means \"There exists an x such that x is at work,\" or more simply, \"Someone is at work.\"\n\nNow let's evaluate the options:\n\n*   **Option A**: \"Either Nancy or Marvin are at work. All lawyers are not at work if they are out to dinner. Nancy is a lawyer and out to dinner. So not everything is not at work.\"\n    *   \"Not everything is not at work\" is a correct translation of \"~(∀x)~Wx\" as it means \"it is not the case that everything is not at work\", which implies at least one thing *is* at work.\n\n*   **Option B**: \"Either Nancy or Marvin are at work. All lawyers are out to dinner if they are not at work. Nancy is a lawyer and out to dinner. So not everything is not at work.\"\n    *   The second premise \"All lawyers are out to dinner if they are not at work\" translates to (∀x)[~Wx ⊃ (Lx ⊃ Dx)], which is different from the given premise. Also, the conclusion is correct.\n\n*   **Option C**: \"Either Nancy or Marvin are at work. All lawyers are out to dinner if they are not at work. Nancy is a lawyer and out to dinner. So not everything is at work.\"\n    *   The second premise is incorrect as in B. The conclusion \"So not everything is at work\" translates to ~(∀x)Wx, meaning there is at least one thing that is *not* at work, which is not the given conclusion.\n\n*   **Option D**: \"Either Nancy or Marvin are at work. All lawyers are not at work if they are out to dinner. Nancy is a lawyer and out to dinner. So not everything is at work.\"\n    *   The second premise \"All lawyers are not at work if they are out to dinner\" is correctly translated. However, the conclusion \"So not everything is at work\" is incorrect, as explained in C.\n\nComparing option A to the predicate logic, it matches all the premises and the conclusion precisely. The conclusion \"~(∀x)~Wx\" is a bit convoluted in English, but \"not everything is not at work\" is a literal and correct interpretation, indicating that there is at least one thing that *is* at work.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{A}$.",
