              |\n| T | F | F  | F | T  | T      | T               |\n| F | T | T  | T | F  | F      | T               |\n| F | F | T  | F | T  | F      | T               |\n\nNow we compare the truth values of \"(G ∨ ~H) ⊃ G\" (T, T, T, F) and \"~G ≡ (~H · G)\" (T, T, T, T).\nThey are not logically equivalent because their truth values differ in the last row.\nThey are not contradictory because there are rows where both are true.\nSince there are rows where both are true, they are consistent.\n\nThe question asks \"determine whether the statements are logically equivalent or contradictory. If neither, determine whether they are consistent or inconsistent.\"\nSince they are neither logically equivalent nor contradictory, we check for consistency.\nIn rows 1, 2, and 3, both statements are true. Therefore, they are consistent.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{C}$.",
