Row 2: F and T\nRow 3: F and F\nRow 4: F and F\nRow 5: F and F\nRow 6: F and T\nRow 7: T and F\nRow 8: F and F\n\nSince there is no row where both propositions are true, the statements are not consistent. They are also not logically equivalent. Therefore, they must be contradictory. This means their conjunction is always false.\n\nLet's be more precise.\nContradictory statements: Their conjunction is always false.\nConsistent statements: There is at least one case where both statements are true.\nLogically equivalent statements: Their truth values are identical in all cases.\n\nFrom the truth table, there is no row where both the column for $\\sim(J \\vee K) \\cdot L$ and the column for $(L \\supset J) \\cdot K$ are 'T'. Therefore, it is impossible for both statements to be true at the same time. This means they are contradictory.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{B}$.",
