    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The inventory turnover rate measures how many times a company has sold and replaced its inventory during a period. The most common formula for this calculation is to divide the Cost of Goods Sold (COGS) by the Average Inventory. Average inventory is typically calculated by summing the beginning and ending inventory for the period and dividing by two.\n\nLet's analyze the options:\nA. Dividing total sales by ending inventory is incorrect.\nB. Dividing total sales by average inventory is incorrect.\nC. Subtracting COGS from average inventory is not how turnover is calculated.\nD. Adding COGS to ending inventory is not a standard calculation for turnover.\nE. Dividing average inventory by COGS calculates the inverse of inventory turnover.\nF. Dividing beginning inventory by COGS is not the standard formula.\nG. Dividing the sum of beginning and ending inventory by COGS is an incorrect formulation.\nH. Dividing the cost of goods sold by the average inventory is the correct formula.\nI. Multiplying COGS by average inventory is not the correct formula.\nJ. Subtracting average inventory from total sales is not how turnover is calculated.\n\nThe correct answer is **H**.",
