    "global index": "MMLUPro_business_52",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The average salary of the Bedford Manufacturing Co. rose 60% during a 10-year period. This rise amounted to $42.50 a week. (a) Find the average weekly salary at the beginning of the 10-year period, and (b) at the end of the 10-year period.\n\nOptions: \nA. $75.83, $118.33\nB. $90.83, $133.33\nC. $80.83, $123.33\nD. $55.83, $98.33\nE. $50.83, $93.33\nF. $70.83, $113.33\nG. $45.83, $88.33\nH. $85.83, $128.33\nI. $60.83, $103.33\nJ. $65.83, $108.33\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Let S_{begin} be the average weekly salary at the beginning of the 10-year period.\nLet S_{end} be the average weekly salary at the end of the 10-year period.\n\nWe are given that the rise in salary amounted to $42.50 a week. This means:\nS_{end} - S_{begin} = $42.50\n\nWe are also given that the salary rose 60% during the 10-year period. This means the rise is 60% of the original salary:\nS_{end} - S_{begin} = 0.60 * S_{begin}\n\nNow we can substitute the first equation into the second:\n$42.50 = 0.60 * S_{begin}\n\nTo find S_{begin}, we divide $42.50 by 0.60:\nS_{begin} = $42.50 / 0.60\nS_{begin} = $70.8333...\n\nSo, the average weekly salary at the beginning of the 10-year period is approximately $70.83.\n\nNow, to find the average weekly salary at the end of the 10-year period, we add the rise to the beginning salary:\nS_{end} = S_{begin} + $42.50\nS_{end} = $70.8333... + $42.50\nS_{end} = $113.3333...\n\nSo, the average weekly salary at the end of the 10-year period is approximately $113.33.\n\nWe need to find the option that matches ($70.83, $113.33).\nLooking at the options:\nF. $70.83, $113.33\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{F}$.",
