      "generated_answer": "To solve this problem, we first need to calculate the area of the floor and the area of each tile. Then, we can determine the number of tiles needed and calculate the total cost.\n\nThe dimensions of the floor are 13'6\" by 18'8\".\nConvert feet to inches:\n13'6\" = (13 * 12) + 6 = 156 + 6 = 162 inches\n18'8\" = (18 * 12) + 8 = 216 + 8 = 224 inches\n\nArea of the floor in square inches = 162 inches * 224 inches = 36288 square inches.\n\nEach tile is 9\" by 9\".\nArea of one tile = 9 inches * 9 inches = 81 square inches.\n\nNumber of tiles needed = Area of the floor / Area of one tile\nNumber of tiles needed = 36288 sq in / 81 sq in = 448 tiles.\n\nThe price of each tile is 9 and 3/4 cents per tile.\nPrice per tile = 9.75 cents.\n\nTotal cost in cents = Number of tiles * Price per tile\nTotal cost in cents = 448 * 9.75 cents = 4368 cents.\n\nTo convert cents to dollars, divide by 100:\nTotal cost in dollars = 4368 cents / 100 = $43.68.\n\nNow let's compare this result with the given options:\nA. $42.50\nB. $49.99\nC. $46.25\nD. $40.75\nE. $47.50\nF. $48.95\nG. $43.88\nH. $51.20\nI. $45.00\nJ. $44.00\n\nOur calculated cost is $43.68, which is closest to option G. $43.88. The small difference might be due to rounding in the options or a slight variation in how the price is interpreted.\n\nLet's recheck the calculations:\n13'6\" = 13.5 feet.\n18'8\" = 18 + 8/12 feet = 18 + 2/3 feet = 56/3 feet.\nArea in square feet = 13.5 * (56/3) = (27/2) * (56/3) = (9 * 56) / 2 = 9 * 28 = 252 square feet.\n\nSince tiles are 9\" x 9\", which is 0.75 ft x 0.75 ft.\nArea of one tile in square feet = 0.75 * 0.75 = 0.5625 square feet.\n\nNumber of tiles needed = Area of the floor / Area of one tile\nNumber of tiles needed = 252 sq ft / 0.5625 sq ft = 448 tiles.\n\nThe price per tile is 9 3/4 cents = 9.75 cents.\nTotal cost in cents = 448 * 9.75 = 4368 cents.\nTotal cost in dollars = 4368 / 100 = $43.68.\n\nThe closest option to $43.68 is $43.88.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{G}$.",
