    "global index": "MMLUPro_business_141",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Assume the Black-Scholes framework. For $t \\ge 0$, let $S(t)$ be the time-$t$ price of a nondividend-paying stock. You are given:\n(i) $S(0)=0.5\n(ii) The stock price process is $\\frac{{dS(t)}}{{S(t)}} = 0.05dt+0.2dZ(t)$ where $Z(t)$ is a standart Brownian motion.\n(iii) $E[S(1)^\\alpha]=1.4$, where $\\alpha$ is a negative constant.\n(iv) The continuously compounded risk-free interest rate is $3%$.\nConsider a contingent claim that pays $S(1)^\\alpha$ at time 1. What is the time-0 price of the contigent claim?\n\nOptions: \nA. 1.450\nB. 1.475\nC. 1.415\nD. 1.250\nE. 1.360\nF. 1.390\nG. 1.325\nH. 1.500\nI. 1.287\nJ. 1.372\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The problem states that the contingent claim pays $S(1)^\\alpha$ at time 1, where $\\alpha$ is a negative constant.\nThe time-0 price of this contingent claim, denoted by $C(0)$, is given by the expected payoff discounted at the risk-free rate.\nTherefore, $C(0) = e^{-rt} E[S(t)^\\alpha]$ where r is the risk-free rate and t is the time of maturity.\nIn this case, $r = 0.03$, $t = 1$, and we are given $E[S(1)^\\alpha] = 1.4$.\nSo, $C(0) = e^{-0.03 \\times 1} \\times 1.4$.\nCalculating this value: $C(0) = e^{-0.03} \\times 1.4 \\approx 0.9704455 \\times 1.4 \\approx 1.3586237$.\nLooking at the options, the closest value to 1.3586 is 1.360.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{E}$.",
      "success": true,
