      "generated_answer": "Here's how to solve this problem:\n\n**Step 1: Calculate the total savings at retirement.**\n\n* **Quarterly savings:** $1000\n* **Savings duration:** 30 years\n* **Number of quarters in 30 years:** $30 \\text{ years} \\times 4 \\text{ quarters/year} = 120 \\text{ quarters}$\n* **Interest rate per quarter:** 6% annual / 4 quarters = 1.5% = 0.015\n\nWe can use the future value of an ordinary annuity formula:\n$FV = P \\times \\frac{((1 + r)^n - 1)}{r}$\n\nWhere:\n* $FV$ = Future Value\n* $P$ = Periodic payment ($1000)\n* $r$ = Interest rate per period (0.015)\n* $n$ = Number of periods (120)\n\n$FV = 1000 \\times \\frac{((1 + 0.015)^{120} - 1)}{0.015}$\n$FV = 1000 \\times \\frac{((1.015)^{120} - 1)}{0.015}$\n$FV = 1000 \\times \\frac{(6.07076 - 1)}{0.015}$\n$FV = 1000 \\times \\frac{5.07076}{0.015}$\n$FV = 1000 \\times 338.0507$\n$FV \\approx 338,050.70$\n\nSo, Aisha will have approximately $338,050.70 at retirement.\n\n**Step 2: Calculate the monthly annuity payout.**\n\n* **Retirement account balance:** $338,050.70\n* **Payout duration:** 25 years\n* **Number of months in 25 years:** $25 \\text{ years} \\times 12 \\text{ months/year} = 300 \\text{ months}$\n* **Interest rate per month:** 4.8% annual / 12 months = 0.4% = 0.004\n\nWe can use the present value of an ordinary annuity formula and solve for the payment ($PMT$):\n$PV = PMT \\times \\frac{(1 - (1 + r)^{-n})}{r}$\n\nWhere:\n* $PV$ = Present Value of the annuity ($338,050.70)\n* $PMT$ = Monthly payment (what we need to find)\n* $r$ = Interest rate per period (0.004)\n* $n$ = Number of periods (300)\n\nRearranging the formula to solve for $PMT$:\n$PMT = PV \\times \\frac{r}{(1 - (1 + r)^{-n})}$\n\n$PMT = 338,050.70 \\times \\frac{0.004}{(1 - (1 + 0.004)^{-300})}$\n$PMT = 338,050.70 \\times \\frac{0.004}{(1 - (1.004)^{-300})}$\n$PMT = 338,050.70 \\times \\frac{0.004}{(1 - 0.30198)}$\n$PMT = 338,050.70 \\times \\frac{0.004}{0.69802}$\n$PMT = 338,050.70 \\times 0.0057306$\n$PMT \\appro