  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_business_17",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: George is seen to place an even-money $100,000 bet on the Bulls to win the NBA Finals. If George has a logarithmic utility-of-wealth function and if his current wealth is $1,000,000, what must he believe is the minimum probability that the Bulls will win?\n\nOptions: \nA. 0.525\nB. 0.800\nC. 0.450\nD. 0.575\nE. 0.750\nF. 0.350\nG. 0.650\nH. 0.300\nI. 0.700\nJ. 0.400\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Let $W$ be the current wealth, which is $W = \\$1,000,000$.\nLet $B$ be the bet amount, which is $B = \\$100,000$.\nThe bet is an \"even-money\" bet, which means if the Bulls win, George wins the bet amount, and if they lose, he loses the bet amount.\n\nIf the Bulls win, George's wealth will be $W + B = \\$1,000,000 + \\$100,000 = \\$1,100,000$.\nIf the Bulls lose, George's wealth will be $W - B = \\$1,000,000 - \\$100,000 = \\$900,000$.\n\nGeorge has a logarithmic utility-of-wealth function, which can be represented as $U(w) = \\ln(w)$, where $w$ is his wealth.\n\nLet $p$ be the probability that the Bulls will win. Then the probability that the Bulls will lose is $1-p$.\n\nGeorge will make the bet if the expected utility of betting is greater than or equal to the utility of not betting.\nThe utility of not betting is $U(W) = \\ln(1,000,000)$.\n\nThe expected utility of betting is given by:\n$E[U(\\text{betting})] = p \\cdot U(W+B) + (1-p) \\cdot U(W-B)$\n$E[U(\\text{betting})] = p \\cdot \\ln(1,100,000) + (1-p) \\cdot \\ln(900,000)$\n\nGeorge will make the bet if $E[U(\\text{betting})] \\ge U(W)$:\n$p \\cdot \\ln(1,100,000) + (1-p) \\cdot \\ln(900,000) \\ge \\ln(1,000,000)$\n\nWe are looking for the minimum probability $p$ for which George makes the bet. This occurs when the expected utility of betting is exactly equal to the utility of not betting:\n$p \\cdot \\ln(1,100,000) + (1-p) \\cdot \\ln(900,000) = \\ln(1,000,000)$\n\nNow, let's solve for $p$:\n$p \\cdot (\\ln(1,100,000) - \\ln(900,000)) = \\ln(1,000,000) - \\ln(900,000)$\n$p \\cdot \\ln\\left(\\frac{1,100,000}{900,000}\\right) = \\ln\\left(\\frac{1,000,000}{900,000}\\right)$\n$p \\cdot \\ln\\left(\\frac{11}{9}\\right) = \\ln\\left(\\frac{10}{9}\\right)$\n$p = \\frac{\\ln\\left(\\frac{10}{9}\\right)}{\\ln\\left(\\frac{11}{9}\\right)}$\n\nNow, let's calculate the values:\n$\\ln\\left(\\frac{10}{9}\\right) \\approx \\ln(1.1111) \\approx 0.10536$\n$\\ln\\left(\\frac{11}{9}\\right) \\approx \\ln(1.2222) \\approx 0.20067$\n\n$p \\approx \\frac{0.10536}{0.20067} \\approx 0.52504$\n\nSo, the minimum probability that the Bulls will win is approximately 0.525.\n\nLet's check the options:\nA. 0.525\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{A}$.",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 221,
        "output_tokens": 910,
        "total_tokens": 1131
      },
      "provider": "openrouter",
      "error": null
    },
    "cost": null,
    "accuracy": null
  },
