    "global index": "MMLUPro_business_389",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: TheMotorollaElectronics Company, in taking inventory, discovers that it has 30 widgets on hand. Company records reveal the following information: Quantity Cost Per Unit Inventory, Dec 2. 6 $2.30 Purchase, Jan 15. 352 $2.50 Purchase, Feb 4. 101 $2.20 Purchase, Feb 14. 645 $2.00 Find the value of the widgets in their inventory under the LIFO method.\n\nOptions: \nA. $78.50\nB. $68.00\nC. $65.50\nD. $75.00\nE. $2.30\nF. $62.40\nG. $13.80\nH. $60\nI. $73.80\nJ. $70.20\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The question asks for the value of 30 widgets on hand using the LIFO (Last-In, First-Out) method. This means the most recently purchased widgets are assumed to be sold first, so the remaining inventory consists of the oldest purchases. We need to sum the cost of the 30 widgets starting from the earliest purchase dates until we reach 30 units.\n\nThe purchases were:\nInventory, Dec 2: 6 units @ $2.30\nPurchase, Jan 15: 352 units @ $2.50\nPurchase, Feb 4: 101 units @ $2.20\nPurchase, Feb 14: 645 units @ $2.00\n\nTo value the 30 widgets using LIFO, we take the most recent purchases first. However, the question asks for the value of the widgets *in their inventory*, which implies the remaining inventory. In LIFO, the cost of goods sold are the most recent purchases, and the remaining inventory are the oldest purchases.\n\nSo, we need to value the *ending inventory* of 30 widgets using LIFO. This means we assume the last 30 widgets purchased are the ones that have been sold, and the oldest widgets are still in inventory.\n\nSince we have 30 widgets on hand, we take the oldest ones:\nFrom Inventory, Dec 2: 6 units @ $2.30 = $13.80\nWe still need 30 - 6 = 24 units.\nFrom Purchase, Jan 15: 24 units @ $2.50 = $60.00\n\nTotal value = $13.80 + $60.00 = $73.80\n\nLet's re-read the question and interpretation. \"Find the value of the widgets in their inventory under the LIFO method.\" This usually refers to the ending inventory.\n\nUnder LIFO, the cost attributed to inventory is based on the *earliest* purchases. So, if there are 30 widgets on hand, we value them using the costs of the first widgets that came into the inventory.\n\nThe purchases in chronological order of oldest to newest are:\n1. Inventory, Dec 2: 6 units @ $2.30\n2. Purchase, Jan 15: 352 units @ $2.50\n3. Purchase, Feb 4: 101 units @ $2.20\n4. Purchase, Feb 14: 645 units @ $2.00\n\nSince we have 30 widgets on hand, and LIFO assumes the most recent items are sold first, the remaining inventory consists of the oldest items.\nWe take the first 30 units from the oldest available purchases.\n\n- From \"Inventory, Dec 2\": 6 units at $2.30 each. Cost = 6 * $2.30 = $13.80.\n- We still need 30 - 6 = 24 units.\n- From \"Purchase, Jan 15\": We take the next 24 units at $2.50 each. Cost = 24 * $2.50 = $60.00.\n\nThe total value of the 30 widgets in inventory under LIFO is the sum of these costs: $13.80 + $60.00 = $73.80.\n\nLet's check the options.\nA. $78.50\nB. $68.00\nC. $65.50\nD. $75.00\nE. $2.30\nF. $62.40\nG. $13.80\nH. $60\nI. $73.80\nJ. $70.20\n\nOur calculated value is $73.80, which matches option I.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{I}$.",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 255,
        "output_tokens": 866,
        "total_tokens": 1121
      },
      "provider": "openrouter",
      "error": null
