  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_business_483",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Mr. Simmons has the choice of buying a 6(1/4), $1,000 bond at 98, or 10 shares of 6% preferred stock, par valued at $100 per share, at 101. Which investment gives the greater effective yield? By how much? Perform all calculations to the nearest (1/10)%.\n\nOptions: \nA. The preferred stock\nB. The bond\nC. Both yield the same\nD. The preferred stock, by 0.7%\nE. The preferred stock, by 1.2%\nF. The bond, by 0.5%\nG. The bond, by 2.0%\nH. Cannot be determined from the information given\nI. The preferred stock, by 0.3%\nJ. The bond, by 1.5%\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To determine the effective yield for each investment, we need to calculate the annual income from each and divide it by the actual cost.\n\nFor the bond:\nThe bond has a par value of $1,000 and a coupon rate of $6\\frac{1}{4}\\%$.\nThe annual interest income from the bond is $6.25\\% \\times \\$1,000 = \\$62.50$.\nThe bond is bought at 98, which means $98\\%$ of its par value.\nThe actual cost of the bond is $0.98 \\times \\$1,000 = \\$980$.\nThe effective yield of the bond is $\\frac{\\$62.50}{\\$980} \\times 100\\% \\approx 6.377\\%$.\n\nFor the preferred stock:\nThere are 10 shares of 6% preferred stock, with a par value of $100 per share.\nThe total par value of the preferred stock is $10 \\text{ shares} \\times \\$100/\\text{share} = \\$1,000$.\nThe annual dividend income from the preferred stock is $6\\% \\times \\$1,000 = \\$60$.\nThe preferred stock is bought at 101, which means $101\\%$ of its par value for each share.\nThe actual cost of one share of preferred stock is $1.01 \\times \\$100 = \\$101$.\nThe total actual cost of 10 shares of preferred stock is $10 \\text{ shares} \\times \\$101/\\text{share} = \\$1010$.\nThe effective yield of the preferred stock is $\\frac{\\$60}{\\$1010} \\times 100\\% \\approx 5.940\\%$.\n\nComparing the yields:\nThe bond has an effective yield of approximately 6.377%.\nThe preferred stock has an effective yield of approximately 5.940%.\n\nThe bond gives the greater effective yield.\nThe difference in yield is $6.377\\% - 5.940\\% = 0.437\\%$.\nRounding to the nearest (1/10)%, the difference is 0.4%.\n\nLet's recheck the calculations and options.\n\nBond Calculation:\nAnnual income = $6.25\\% \\times \\$1000 = \\$62.50$\nCost = $98\\% \\times \\$1000 = \\$980$\nYield = $(\\$62.50 / \\$980) \\times 100\\% = 6.37755... \\% \\approx 6.4\\%$ (to the nearest tenth of a percent)\n\nPreferred Stock Calculation:\nAnnual dividend income = $6\\% \\times (10 \\text{ shares} \\times \\$100/\\text{share}) = 6\\% \\times \\$1000 = \\$60$\nCost per share = $101\\% \\times \\$100 = \\$101$\nTotal cost = $10 \\text{ shares} \\times \\$101/\\text{share} = \\$1010$\nYield = $(\\$60 / \\$1010) \\times 100\\% = 5.94059... \\% \\approx 5.9\\%$ (to the nearest tenth of a percent)\n\nThe bond yields approximately 6.4%, and the preferred stock yields approximately 5.9%.\nThe bond yields more.\nThe difference is $6.4\\% - 5.9\\% = 0.5\\%$.\n\nTherefore, the bond gives the greater effective yield by 0.5%.\n\nLet's examine the options again.\nA. The preferred stock\nB. The bond\nC. Both yield the same\nD. The preferred stock, by 0.7%\nE. The preferred stock, by 1.2%\nF. The bond, by 0.5%\nG. The bond, by 2.0%\nH. Cannot be determined from the information given\nI. The preferred stock, by 0.3%\nJ. The bond, by 1.5%\n\nOur calculation indicates that the bond yields more by 0.5%. This matches option F.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{F}$.",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 246,
        "output_tokens": 975,
        "total_tokens": 1221
      },
