ation. Company B's net income is $272,000, and Company A's is $96,000. A higher net income generally reflects better administrative efficiency and profitability. Acquiring Company B would bring a more financially sound and potentially better-administered entity.\n\nLet's re-evaluate condition (c). For short-term profit improvement, Company B's higher net income of $272,000 offers a greater immediate boost than Company A's $96,000.\n\nConsider the profitability ratios:\nCompany A: Net Income / Sales = $96,000 / $3,000,000 = 3.2%\nCompany B: Net Income / Sales = $272,000 / $5,000,000 = 5.44%\n\nCompany B is more profitable on a percentage basis as well.\n\nLet's reconsider option B carefully:\n(a) X has a strong administration but weak sales. Acquiring Company B with higher sales could strengthen the sales aspect.\n(b) X has a strong sales force but a weak administration. Acquiring Company B with higher net income suggests better administration.\n(c) X is looking for short-term improvement in its profits. Company B has much higher net income.\n\nLet's assume there might be a different interpretation. Let's try analyzing each condition to see which company fits best:\n\n**(a) X has a strong administration but weak sales.**\nIf X has weak sales, it might want to acquire a company that can boost its sales. Company B has significantly higher sales ($5,000,000 vs. $3,000,000). This would complement X's weakness.\n\n**(b) X has a strong sales force but a weak administration.**\nIf X has weak administration, it should acquire a company with strong administration. Company B has a much higher net income ($272,000 vs. $96,000), implying better administrative efficiency and profitability.\n\n**(c) X is looking for short-term improvement in its profits.**\nCompany B has a significantly higher net income ($272,000) compared to Company A ($96,000). Acquiring Company B would provide a greater immediate increase in profits.\n\nFollowing this logic, for all three conditions, Company B