    "global index": "MMLUPro_economics_6187",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A white noise process will have\n\n(i) A zero mean\n\n(ii) A constant variance\n\n(iii) Autocovariances that are constant\n\n(iv) Autocovariances that are zero except at lag zero\n\nOptions: \nA. (ii), (iii), and (iv) only\nB. (i), (ii), (iii), and (iv)\nC. (i) and (ii) only\nD. (i), (ii), and (iii) only\nE. (i) and (iii) only\nF. (i), (iii), and (iv) only\nG. (ii) and (iv) only\nH. (i), (ii) and (iv) only\nI. (ii) and (iii) only\nJ. (iii) and (iv) only\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "A white noise process is a fundamental concept in time series analysis, characterized by the absence of any discernible pattern over time. Let's analyze each statement:\n\n(i) **A zero mean:** While not strictly required, a common definition of white noise assumes a zero mean. This simplifies many theoretical results.\n\n(ii) **A constant variance:** This is a key property of white noise. The spread of the data around the mean remains consistent across time.\n\n(iii) **Autocovariances that are constant:** This is incorrect. Autocovariances measure the linear relationship between observations at different time lags. For white noise, these relationships should diminish over time.\n\n(iv) **Autocovariances that are zero except at lag zero:** This is a defining characteristic of white noise. The autocovariance at lag zero is simply the variance, and for all other lags, the autocovariance should be zero, indicating no linear dependence between observations separated in time.\n\nConsidering these properties, options (i) and (ii) are generally true or commonly assumed for white noise, and (iv) is a core defining property. If we consider the most encompassing definition of white noise, it includes a zero mean, constant variance, and zero autocovariances for all non-zero lags.\n\nTherefore, (i), (ii), and (iv) are the most accurate descriptions of a white noise process.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{H}$.",
