      "generated_answer": "The term \"monopsony\" comes from \"mono\" meaning single and \"opsony\" meaning buyer. Therefore, a monopsonist is characterized by being a single buyer in a market. In the context of labor markets, this means they are the sole employer of a particular type of labor.\n\nThe correct answer is **C**. A monopsonist is defined as a single buyer of labor services. Options A and E are consequences of monopsony power, not the definition. Options B and D relate to employment levels compared to competition. Options F and G compare wages to a competitive market. Options H and J relate to the seller side of the market (monopoly). Option I suggests an outcome similar to perfect competition, which is contrary to monopsony.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{C}$.",
