    "global index": "MMLUPro_economics_5897",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Monetarists believe that V and Q are\n\nOptions: \nA. always increasing.\nB. not critical for understanding how money affects the economy.\nC. not related.\nD. always decreasing.\nE. variable.\nF. unpredictable.\nG. inversely proportional.\nH. stable.\nI. directly proportional.\nJ. constant.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Monetarists emphasize the role of money supply in influencing nominal GDP. Their quantity theory of money, MV = PQ, posits that V (velocity of money) and Q (real output) are often assumed to be relatively stable or predictable in the short to medium run, allowing changes in M (money supply) to directly affect P (price level). Therefore, V and Q are considered variable in the sense that they can change, but under monetarist assumptions, they are not wildly fluctuating or unpredictable. However, compared to the other options, \"variable\" acknowledges that they are not fixed but can change over time.\n\nThe most likely correct answer is **E**.",
