    "global index": "MMLUPro_economics_5791",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Marginal cost (MC) is equal to average variable cost (AVC) and average total cost (ATC) when:\n\nOptions: \nA. MC intersects AVC and ATC at their average points.\nB. AVC and ATC are both greater than MC.\nC. marginal cost (MC) intersects AVC and ATC at their maximum points.\nD. AVC and ATC are both less than MC.\nE. AVC and ATC intersect MC at its average point.\nF. MC is above AVC and ATC.\nG. AVC and ATC intersect MC at its minimum point.\nH. MC intersects AVC and ATC at their minimum points.\nI. MC is below AVC and ATC.\nJ. AVC and ATC intersect MC at its maximum point.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The correct answer is H. When marginal cost (MC) intersects average variable cost (AVC) and average total cost (ATC), it does so at their minimum points. This is because MC represents the cost of producing one additional unit. As long as MC is below AVC or ATC, it pulls them down. Once MC rises above AVC or ATC, it pulls them up. Therefore, the intersection occurs at the lowest point of both AVC and ATC.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{H}$.",
