    "global index": "MMLUPro_economics_6390",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which of the following are alternative names for the independent variable (usually denoted by x) in linear regression analysis?\n\n(i) The regressor\n\n(ii) The regressand\n\n(iii) The causal variable\n\n(iv) The effect variable\n\nOptions: \nA. (i), (ii), (iii), and (iv)\nB. (ii), (iii), and (iv) only\nC. (i), (iii), and (iv) only\nD. (i), (ii), and (iii) only\nE. (ii) and (iv) only\nF. (i) and (iii) only\nG. (ii) and (iii) only\nH. (i) and (iv) only\nI. (i) and (ii) only\nJ. (iii) and (iv) only\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The independent variable in linear regression is the variable that is manipulated or observed to see its effect on another variable. It is often called the \"regressor\" because the model essentially \"regresses\" the dependent variable onto it. While it can be considered a \"causal variable\" in some contexts (implying it influences the other variable), the term \"effect variable\" is incorrect as it refers to the dependent variable. The \"regressand\" is another name for the dependent variable. Therefore, the most accurate alternative names from the given options are (i) The regressor and (iii) The causal variable.\n\nThe correct answer is F.",
