  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_economics_6183",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which one of the following is the most appropriate definition of a 99% confidence interval?\n\nOptions: \nA. 99% of the time in repeated samples, the interval would contain the estimated value of the null hypothesis\nB. 99% of the time in repeated samples, the interval would be different from the estimated value of the parameter\nC. 99% of the time in repeated samples, the null hypothesis will not be rejected when it was false\nD. 1% of the time in repeated samples, the interval would not contain the true value of the parameter\nE. 99% of the time in repeated samples, the interval would contain the true value of the null hypothesis\nF. 99% of the time in repeated samples, the null hypothesis will be accepted when it was false\nG. 99% of the time in repeated samples, the null hypothesis will be rejected\nH. 99% of the time in repeated samples, the interval would contain the estimated value of the parameter\nI. 99% of the time in repeated samples, the interval would contain the true value of the parameter\nJ. 99% of the time in repeated samples, the interval would not contain the true value of the null hypothesis\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The most appropriate definition of a 99% confidence interval is that 99% of the time in repeated samples, the interval would contain the true value of the parameter. This means that if we were to take many samples and construct a confidence interval for each, approximately 99% of those intervals would successfully capture the true, but unknown, population parameter. Options B, C, E, F, G, and J incorrectly refer to the null hypothesis, which is a concept related to hypothesis testing, not the definition of a confidence interval. Options A and H refer to the \"estimated value of the parameter,\" which is not the correct interpretation; a confidence interval estimates the *true* value of the parameter. Option D states that 1% of the time the interval would *not* contain the true value, which is the complement of the correct definition, and while true, it's not the primary way the confidence interval is defined. Therefore, option I is the most accurate definition.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{I}$.",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 324,
        "output_tokens": 212,
        "total_tokens": 536
      },
      "provider": "openrouter",
      "error": null
    },
    "cost": null,
    "accuracy": null
  },
  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_economics_5584",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Crowding out\n\nOptions: \nA. occurs when interest rates fall due to government borrowing.\nB. occurs when consumers and firms spend less offsetting expansionary fiscal policy.\nC. occurs when the government reduces its spending in response to inflation.\nD. is a consequence of contractionary fiscal policy.\nE. is one reason fiscal policy is so effective.\nF. is when expansionary fiscal policy leads to a decrease in interest rates.\nG. happens when the government increases taxes to reduce the budget deficit.\nH. causes the aggregate demand curve to shift to the right.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Crowding out is a phenomenon where increased government borrowing leads to higher interest rates, which in turn discourages private investment and consumption. This effect reduces the overall impact of expansionary fiscal policy.\n\nLet's analyze the options:\nA. occurs when interest rates fall due to government borrowing. This is incorrect; government borrowing tends to *increase* interest rates.\nB. occurs when consumers and firms spend less offsetting expansionary fiscal policy. This accurately describes the effect of crowding out, where higher interest rates from government borrowing lead to reduced private spending, thus offsetting the intended expansionary effect.\nC. occurs when the government reduces its spending in response to inflation. This describes contractionary fiscal policy, not crowding out.\nD. is a consequence of contractionary fiscal policy. Crowding out is typically associated with *expansionary* fiscal policy, which involves increased government spending or tax cuts, leading to increased borrowing.\nE. is one reason fiscal policy is so effective. Crowding out is a reason fiscal policy can be *less* effective, not more.\nF. is when expansionary fiscal policy leads to a decrease in interest rates. This is the opposite of crowding out; expansionary fiscal policy tends to *increase* interest rates.\nG. happens when the government increases taxes to reduce the budget deficit. This describes contractionary fiscal policy, not crowding out.\nH. causes the aggregate demand curve to shift to the right. Crowding out, by reducing private spending, would lead to a weaker rightward shift or even a partial leftward shift in aggregate demand due to the offsetting effect.\n\nTherefore, option B is the most accurate description of crowding out.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{B}$.",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 180,
        "output_tokens": 352,
        "total_tokens": 532
      },
      "provider": "openrouter",
      "error": null
    },
    "cost": null,
    "accuracy": null
  },
  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_economics_6062",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Note that statistical tables are not necessary to answer this question. For a sample of 1000 observations, the Dickey-Fuller test statistic values are\n\nOptions: \nA. Equal to those in the left hand tail of a normal distribution\nB. Less negative than (i.e. smaller in absolute value than) those in the left hand tail of a normal distribution\nC. Obtained from a standard normal distribution\nD. Obtained from an analytical formula for the density of the Dickey-Fuller distribution\nE. More negative than (i.e. bigger in absolute value than) those in the left hand tail of a normal distribution\nF. More negative (i.e. bigger in absolute value) for a 10% size of test than a 5% test.\nG. Less negative than (i.e. smaller in absolute value than) those in the right hand tail of a normal distribution\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The Dickey-Fuller test is used to test for the presence of a unit root in a time series. The distribution of the Dickey-Fuller test statistic under the null hypothesis is *not* the standard normal distribution. Instead, it follows a specific non-standard distribution, often referred to as the Dickey-Fuller distribution. Standard statistical tables for the Dickey-Fuller test provide critical values for this specific distribution. Therefore, options C and D are incorrect.\n\nFor hypothesis testing, the critical values are typically found in the tails of the distribution. The Dickey-Fuller test is generally a left-tailed test, meaning that extreme negative values of the test statistic provide evidence against the null hypothesis. Comparing the Dickey-Fuller distribution to a normal distribution, the Dickey-Fuller critical values are usually more negative (larger in absolute value) than those of a standard normal distribution for typical significance levels. This is because the presence of a unit root leads to a different data generating process under the null hypothesis than the stationary process assumed by the normal distribution.\n\nOption F is incorrect because a larger size of the test (e.g., 10% vs. 5%) generally implies a *less* stringent critical value, meaning it will be less negative (smaller in absolute value) to reject the null hypothesis.\n\nOption G is incorrect because the Dickey-Fuller test is typically a left-tailed test, so comparisons to the right tail of a normal distribution are not directly relevant for interpreting the significance of the test statistic.\n\nConsidering the nature of the Dickey-Fuller distribution and its comparison to the standard normal distribution, the test statistic values, especially in the rejection region (left tail), tend to be more negative than what would be expected from a standard normal distribution. This accounts for the \"weak instrument\" problem inherent in unit root testing.\n\nThe most likely correct answer relies on the understanding that the Dickey-Fuller distribution's critical values are more extreme (more negative) than those of a standard normal distribution for typical significance levels.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{E}$.",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 246,
        "output_tokens": 416,
        "total_tokens": 662
      },
      "provider": "openrouter",
      "error": null
    },
    "cost": null,
    "accuracy": null
  },
  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_economics_6197",
