    "cost": null,
    "accuracy": null
  },
  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_economics_5689",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Why are United States coins called \"token money\"?\n\nOptions: \nA. The coins are produced as commemorative tokens for collectors\nB. The coins are minted by private organizations rather than the government\nC. The coins are only accepted as currency within certain states\nD. The value of the metal in each coin is far less than the coin's monetary value\nE. The coins have historical significance\nF. The coins are used as a form of barter rather than as legal tender\nG. The coins are backed by the government's promise to pay the face value\nH. The coins are replicas of ancient currency used for educational purposes\nI. The coins are made of cheap metal\nJ. The coins are used as tokens in games\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The term \"token money\" in the context of currency refers to coins whose intrinsic value (the value of the metal they are made of) is less than their face value or monetary value. This is a fundamental characteristic of modern coinage, including United States coins. The government mints these coins with a stated monetary value, and this value is accepted by the public because it is legal tender.\n\nTherefore, the most likely correct answer is **D**.\n\nThe intrinsic value of the metal in most coins is significantly less than their face value, making them \"token money\" in that their worth is not derived from the material itself but from their designation as legal tender. This distinction is essential for a functional monetary system. The government guarantees the face value, not the precious metal content, for these coins.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{D}$.",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 210,
        "output_tokens": 170,
        "total_tokens": 380
      },
      "provider": "openrouter",
      "error": null
    },
    "cost": null,
    "accuracy": null
  },
  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_economics_5668",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: If OLS is applied separately to each equation that is part of a simultaneous system, the resulting estimates will be\n\nOptions: \nA. Biased, inconsistent, and inefficient.\nB. Biased but consistent\nC. Unbiased and consistent\nD. Biased but inconsistent for one equation only\nE. Unbiased and efficient\nF. Unbiased but inconsistent\nG. It is impossible to apply OLS to equations that are part of a simultaneous system\nH. Consistent but inefficient\nI. It is possible to apply OLS to equations that are part of a simultaneous system, but the results will be unpredictable\nJ. Biased and inconsistent\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The correct answer is **J. Biased and inconsistent**.\n\nWhen OLS is applied separately to equations in a simultaneous system, the endogenous variables on the right-hand side mean that the regressors are correlated with the error term. This correlation violates a key assumption of OLS, leading to biased and inconsistent estimates. These estimators do not converge to the true parameter values even with an infinite sample size.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{J}$.",
