    "global index": "MMLUPro_economics_5642",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which of the following statements is true?\n\nOptions: \nA. The demand for loanable funds consists of savers.\nB. Real GDP is the sum of the money supply and the velocity of money.\nC. The supply of loanable funds is solely determined by the central bank's policies.\nD. Dollars earned today have more purchasing power than dollars earned a year from today.\nE. The velocity of money is the speed at which the stock market indices change.\nF. The demand for loanable funds is entirely driven by government borrowing.\nG. Purchasing power parity ensures that dollars have the same value across all time periods.\nH. The supply of loanable funds consists of investors.\nI. Inflation rates are inversely proportional to the velocity of money at all times.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The question asks which statement is true. Let's analyze each option:\n\nA. The demand for loanable funds comes from borrowers (firms and governments), not savers. Savers supply loanable funds.\nB. Real GDP is not the sum of the money supply and the velocity of money. The equation of exchange is M*V = P*Y, where M is the money supply, V is the velocity of money, P is the price level, and Y is real output (or real GDP).\nC. The supply of loanable funds is primarily determined by the saving decisions of households and firms, though central bank policies can influence it indirectly. It is not solely determined by these policies.\nD. Dollars earned today have more purchasing power than dollars earned a year from today, assuming inflation exists. Due to inflation, the general price level tends to rise over time, reducing the purchasing power of money in the future.\nE. The velocity of money is the average number of times a unit of money is spent in anEconomy over a period. It is not related to the stock market.\nF. The demand for loanable funds is driven by investment by firms, households (e.g., for mortgages), and government borrowing, but it is not entirely driven by government borrowing.\nG. Purchasing power parity (PPP) suggests that exchange rates should adjust to equalize the prices of identical goods and services in different countries. It does not ensure that dollars have the same value across all time periods; that relates to inflation.\nH. The supply of loanable funds comes from savers, not investors. Investors demand loanable funds.\nI. Inflation rates and the velocity of money can have complex relationships, and they are not always inversely proportional.\n\nTherefore, statement D is the only true statement.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{D}$.",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 222,
        "output_tokens": 381,
        "total_tokens": 603
      },
      "provider": "openrouter",
      "error": null
