    "global index": "MMLUPro_economics_6054",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which of the following is correct concerning logit and probit models?\n\nOptions: \nA. The logit model can result in too many observations falling exactly at the midpoint\nB. They use a different method of transforming the model so that the probabilities lie between zero and one\nC. The logit and probit models are identical in every aspect\nD. For the logit model, the marginal effect of a change in one of the explanatory variables is simply the estimate of the parameter attached to that variable, whereas this is not the case for the probit model\nE. Both logit and probit models fail to account for the possibility of error in the dependent variable.\nF. The logit model can result in too many observations falling at exactly zero or exactly one\nG. The logit model is less flexible in terms of shape and is always symmetric, while the probit model can accommodate asymmetry\nH. For the probit model, the marginal effect of a change in one of the explanatory variables is simply the estimate of the parameter attached to that variable, whereas this is not the case for the logit model\nI. The probit model is based on a cumulative logistic function\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Let's analyze each option:\n\nA. This is incorrect. Logit and probit models, by their nature, are designed to handle probabilities that can range from 0 to 1. Extreme values are more a consequence of data or model specification issues than an inherent flaw in the logit transformation itself.\n\nB. This is correct. Both logit and probit models transform a linear combination of predictors such that the resulting probabilities are constrained between 0 and 1. The logit uses the logistic cumulative distribution function, while the probit uses the standard normal cumulative distribution function.\n\nC. This is incorrect. While they are similar in their purpose and structure, they use different underlying distribution functions, leading to slight differences in their predicted probabilities and marginal effects.\n\nD. This is incorrect. For both logit and probit models, the marginal effect of a change in an explanatory variable is *not* simply the parameter estimate. The marginal effect depends on the probability, which is a non-linear function of the parameters.\n\nE. This is incorrect. Both models are designed for binary dependent variables and implicitly account for uncertainty or error in the underlying latent variable that determines the binary outcome.\n\nF. This is incorrect. Similar to option A, the models are designed to predict probabilities. While extreme probabilities can occur, it's not a specific issue of the logit model causing too many observations to fall exactly at zero or one.\n\nG. This is incorrect. The logit distribution is symmetric, but the probit distribution (based on the normal curve) is also symmetric. Neither model inherently accommodates asymmetry in this way without extension.\n\nH. This is incorrect. As explained in D, the marginal effect for the probit model is also not simply the parameter estimate.\n\nI. This is incorrect. The probit model is based on the *cumulative normal distribution function*, not the logistic function. The logistic function is used in the logit model.\n\nTherefore, the most accurate statement is that both models use different methods of transforming the model so that probabilities lie between zero and one.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{B}$.",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 302,
        "output_tokens": 435,
        "total_tokens": 737
      },
      "provider": "openrouter",
      "error": null
    },
    "cost": null,
    "accuracy": null
  },
