  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_economics_6041",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Lowering reserve requirements would\n\nOptions: \nA. restrict banks from making more loans and buying more investments, thus increasing the money supply.\nB. allow banks to make fewer loans and buy fewer investments, thus increasing the money supply.\nC. restrict banks from making more loans and buying more investments, thus decreasing the money supply.\nD. force banks to hold more reserves and make more loans.\nE. force banks to hold fewer reserves and make fewer loans.\nF. force banks to hold more reserves and decrease their investment capacity.\nG. have no impact on the banks' ability to make loans and buy investments.\nH. allow banks to make more loans and buy more investments thus decreasing the money supply.\nI. allow banks to make more loans and buy more investments thus increasing the money supply.\nJ. allow banks more freedom to merge and acquire other businesses.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The correct answer is I.\n\nLowering reserve requirements means banks are required to hold less of their deposits in reserve and can, therefore, lend out a larger portion. This increased ability to lend leads to more loans and investments being made, which expands the money supply in the economy. This is a common monetary policy tool used to stimulate economic activity.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{I}$",
