    "global index": "MMLUPro_economics_6133",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: TheRicardiantheory of land rents led to a movement in the late nineteenth century called the single-tax movement. What was the goal of this movement and what was its economic rationale?\n\nOptions: \nA. To eliminate all taxes but a tax on land\nB. To tax only corporate profits as a means of funding government\nC. To impose a single tax on wealth and capital gains only\nD. To replace all taxes with a single tax on income\nE. To eliminate all taxes\nF. To introduce a single tax on consumption goods and services\nG. To establish a flat tax rate for all forms of income\nH. To levy a single tax on imports and exports to regulate trade\nI. To reduce taxes on land to encourage agriculture\nJ. To increase all taxes\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The correct answer is **A**.\n\nThe single-tax movement, inspired by Ricardo's theory of land rents, aimed to abolish all other taxes and rely solely on a tax on land values. The economic rationale was that land rent accrues to landowners due to societal progress and not individual effort, and therefore, taxing it would capture this unearned increment without distorting economic activity. This would eliminate taxes on labor and capital, encouraging production and investment.",
      "success": true,
