    "global index": "MMLUPro_economics_5909",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Having a fractional reserve banking system means that\n\nOptions: \nA. the central bank holds a certain fraction of the bank's deposits on reserve at all times\nB. a bank cannot lend out all of its deposits\nC. the bank must hold a certain percentage of its deposits in reserve at all times\nD. the central bank holds a certain fraction of GDP on reserve at all times\nE. the central bank reserves the right to set the interest rate on any loan at any time.\nF. a bank can lend out all of its deposits but must maintain a certain level of reserves\nG. all loans must be backed by an equivalent amount of reserves\nH. no single loan can be larger than 20 percent of the bank’s holdings\nI. a bank can only lend out a fixed percentage of its deposits\nJ. the central bank reserves the right to audit any bank at any time\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The correct answer is **C**.\n\nA fractional reserve banking system means that banks are required to hold only a fraction of their deposits in reserve, and can lend out the rest. This allows for credit creation within the economy. Options A, D, E, G, H, I, and J describe either actions of the central bank or incorrect limitations on lending. Option F is partially correct but incomplete as it implies the bank *can* lend out all deposits, when the core concept is that it *cannot* lend out all deposits due to the reserve requirement.",
