  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_economics_5661",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Suppose that the Durbin Watson test is applied to a regression containing two explanatory variables plus a constant with 50 data points. The test statistic takes a value of 1.53. What is the appropriate conclusion?\n\nOptions: \nA. Residuals appear to be negatively autocorrelated\nB. There is a strong linear relationship between the variables\nC. The regression model is incorrectly specified\nD. The residuals are heteroscedastic\nE. Residuals appear not to be autocorrelated\nF. The residuals are homoscedastic\nG. There is no relationship between the variables\nH. Residuals appear to be positively autocorrelated\nI. Residuals appear to be perfectly autocorrelated\nJ. The test result is inconclusive\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To determine the conclusion of the Durbin-Watson test, we need to compare the test statistic (d = 1.53) to the critical values. For a regression with two explanatory variables plus a constant and 50 data points, we would look up the critical values from a Durbin-Watson table. Typically, the lower critical value ($d_L$) for $\\alpha = 0.05$ and k=2 (2 explanatory variables) and N=50 is around 1.50, and the upper critical value ($d_U$) is around 1.62.\n\nOur test statistic d = 1.53 falls between $d_L$ and $d_U$ (1.50 < 1.53 < 1.62). This is the indeterminate region for the Durbin-Watson test. In this region, the test is inconclusive regarding the presence of positive autocorrelation. We cannot definitively reject the null hypothesis of no autocorrelation, nor can we reject the alternative hypothesis of positive autocorrelation.\n\nTherefore, the appropriate conclusion is that the test result is inconclusive.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{J}$.",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 210,
        "output_tokens": 242,
        "total_tokens": 452
      },
      "provider": "openrouter",
      "error": null
    },
    "cost": null,
    "accuracy": null
  },
  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_economics_5815",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: If a Durbin Watson statistic takes a value close to zero, what will be the value of the first order autocorrelation coefficient?\n\nOptions: \nA. Close to either minus one or plus one\nB. Exactly zero\nC. Close to minus one\nD. Close to zero\nE. Exactly minus one\nF. Slightly greater than plus one\nG. Anywhere between minus one and plus one\nH. Exactly plus one\nI. Slightly less than minus one\nJ. Close to plus one\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The Durbin-Watson statistic ($d$) is a test for autocorrelation in the residuals of a regression analysis. It is calculated as:\n\n$d = \\sum_{t=2}^{T} (\\hat{e}_t - \\hat{e}_{t-1})^2 / \\sum_{t=1}^{T} \\hat{e}_t^2$\n\nThe first-order autocorrelation coefficient ($\\rho_1$) is related to the Durbin-Watson statistic by the approximation:\n\n$d \\approx 2(1 - \\rho_1)$\n\nIf the Durbin-Watson statistic ($d$) takes a value close to zero, then:\n\n$0 \\approx 2(1 - \\rho_1)$\n$0 \\approx 1 - \\rho_1$\n$\\rho_1 \\approx 1$\n\nHowever, this approximation is more accurate for positive autocorrelation. For negative autocorrelation, the relationship is different and the Durbin-Watson statistic tends to be close to 4. A value close to zero indicates strong positive autocorrelation. Therefore, the first-order autocorrelation coefficient will be close to plus one.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{J}$.",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 161,
        "output_tokens": 244,
        "total_tokens": 405
      },
      "provider": "openrouter",
      "error": null
    },
    "cost": null,
    "accuracy": null
  },
