  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_economics_6061",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Suppose that we wished to evaluate the factors that affected the probability that an investor would choose an equity fund rather than a bond fund or a cash investment. Which class of model would be most appropriate?\n\nOptions: \nA. A linear regression model\nB. A Poisson regression model\nC. A probit model\nD. A binary logit model\nE. An exponential model\nF. A multinomial logit\nG. A logit model\nH. A tobit model\nI. An ordered logit model\nJ. A GARCH model\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The question asks to evaluate factors affecting the probability of choosing among multiple, distinct investment options: equity fund, bond fund, or cash. These are categorical choices, and the investor is choosing one from a set of more than two mutually exclusive options.\n\n- Linear regression is for continuous dependent variables.\n- Poisson regression is for count data.\n- Probit and binary logit models are for binary choices (yes/no).\n- Exponential models can be used for various purposes, but not directly for multiple categorical choices.\n- A multinomial logit model is specifically designed for situations where the dependent variable is a choice among three or more unordered, mutually exclusive categories.\n- A logit model (binary logit) is for binary choices.\n- Tobit models are for censored dependent variables.\n- Ordered logit models are for dependent variables that have an inherent order.\n- GARCH models are for time series volatility.\n\nSince the options (equity fund, bond fund, cash investment) are unordered and mutually exclusive categories, a multinomial logit model is the most appropriate choice for modeling the probability of choosing among them.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{F}$.",
