  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_economics_5930",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which of the following are plausible approaches to dealing with residual autocorrelation?\n\ni) Take logarithms of each of the variables\n\nii) Add lagged values of the variables to the regression equation\n\niii) Use dummy variables to remove outlying observations\n\niv) Try a model in first differenced form rather than in levels.\n\nOptions: \nA. (ii) and (iv) only\nB. (iii) and (iv) only\nC. (i), (ii), (iii), and (iv)\nD. (i) and (iii) only\nE. (i) and (iv) only\nF. (i), (ii), and (iii) only\nG. (i) and (ii) only\nH. (i), (ii), and (iv) only\nI. (ii), (iii), and (iv) only\nJ. (i), (iii), and (iv) only\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Residual autocorrelation indicates that the error terms in a regression model are correlated. Let's analyze each option:\n\ni) Taking logarithms: This is typically done to address heteroskedasticity or to linearize a non-linear relationship, not directly to solve autocorrelation.\nii) Adding lagged values of variables: This is a common strategy. If present autocorrelation is due to the influence of past values of the independent variables, including them can help capture this dynamic.\niii) Using dummy variables for outliers: This addresses the impact of specific unusual observations, which might contribute to autocorrelation if they're systematic, but it's not a general solution for autocorrelation itself.\niv) Using first differences: Transforming variables into their first differences is a standard method for dealing with autocorrelation, especially in time series data, as it often detrends the data and can remove serial correlation.\n\nTherefore, (ii) and (iv) are the most plausible approaches.\n\nThe correct answer is $\\boxed{A}$.",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 251,
        "output_tokens": 200,
        "total_tokens": 451
