for the uniform.\n\nLet's consider the context of multiple choice questions where one option is often the \"best\" fit.\n(iii) is a very clear and direct example.\n(i) describes the primary benefit.\n\nIf we assume statement (iv) is intended to mean a transformation that generates a negatively correlated variate, and $1/X$ can sometimes achieve this, then A might be plausible if (i) and (iii) are also correct.\n\nLet's go with the most fundamental and universally accepted aspects.\n(i) is correct about the *purpose* and outcome.\n(iii) is a correct and primary *method*.\n\nIf both (i) and (iii) are correct, and considering the options:\nH. (i) and (iii) only\n\nLet's carefully re-read statement (iv). \"taking one over each of the random draws and repeating the experiment using those values as the draws\". For $X \\sim U(0,1)$, $1/X$ *does not* have the same distribution. Its mean is infinite. So (iv) as written is problematic unless specific caveats are understood (e.g., for specific distributions where $1/X$ might also be a valid variate or transformation). This weakens (iv) considerably as a general statement.\n\nGiven this scrutiny, (ii) is false, and (iv) is questionable as a general statement.\nThis leaves (i) and (iii) as the most solid correct statements.\n\nLet's confirm (i) again. \"Antithetic variates work by reducing the number of replications required to cover the whole probability space\". The reduction in variance directly implies fewer replications for the same precision. The \"cover the probability space\" phrase is awkward but can be interpreted as achieving a reliable estimate across the distribution.\n\nTherefore focusing on (i) and (iii) as the most robustly correct statements within the context of general Monte Carlo methods.\n\nThe correct answer is likely H based on the certainty of statements (i) and (iii), and the ambiguity or incorrectness of (ii) and (iv).\n\nFinal check:\n(i) - Correct: Variance reduction leads to fewer required replications for a given precision.\n(ii) - Incorrect: Not about analytical properties of a similar variable.\n(iii) - Correct: Negation is a primary method.\n(iv) - Questionable: $1/X$ does not generally preserve the distribution and can be problematic.\n\nThus, statement (i) and (iii) are the most accurate.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{H}$.",
