  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_economics_6057",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Suppose that the value of $R^2$ for an estimated regression model is exactly zero. Which of the following are true?\n\ni) All coefficient estimates on the slopes will be zero\n\nii) The fitted line will be horizontal with respect to all of the explanatory variables\n\niii) The regression line has not explained any of the variability of y about its mean value\n\niv) The intercept coefficient estimate must be zero.\n\nOptions: \nA. (i), (ii), (iii), and (iv)\nB. (i) and (ii) only\nC. (i) and (iii) only\nD. (iii) and (iv) only\nE. (ii) and (iii) only\nF. (ii) and (iv) only\nG. (ii), (iii), and (iv) only\nH. (i) and (iv) only\nI. (i), (iii), and (iv) only\nJ. (i), (ii), and (iii) only\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "$R^2$ represents the proportion of the variance in the dependent variable that is predictable from the independent variable(s). An $R^2$ of zero means that the model explains none of the variability of $y$ about its mean value. This occurs when the fitted line is horizontal, indicating that the predicted values of $y$ are equal to the mean of $y$, regardless of the values of the explanatory variables.\n\nLet's analyze each statement:\n\ni) All coefficient estimates on the slopes will be zero.\nThis is true. If the slopes are zero, the predicted value of $y$ ($\\hat{y}$) will be equal to the intercept ($\\hat{\\beta}_0$). If the intercept is the mean of $y$, then $\\hat{y} = \\bar{y}$ for all observations. In this case, the regression has explained none of the variability.\n\nii) The fitted line will be horizontal with respect to all of the explanatory variables.\nThis is true, as explained above. If the slopes are zero, the predicted values of $y$ do not change with changes in the explanatory variables, resulting in a horizontal line.\n\niii) The regression line has not explained any of the variability of y about its mean value.\nThis is the definition of $R^2 = 0$. The total sum of squares (SST) represents the total variability of $y$ about its mean. The explained sum of squares (SSR) is the variability explained by the model. $R^2 = SSR/SST$. If $R^2 = 0$, then $SSR = 0$, meaning no variability is explained.\n\niv) The intercept coefficient estimate must be zero.\nThis is false. For $R^2 = 0$, the predicted values of $y$ must be equal to the mean of $y$. The fitted line is given by $\\hat{y} = \\hat{\\beta}_0 + \\hat{\\beta}_1 x_1 + ... + \\hat{\\beta}_k x_k$. If all slopes are zero, then $\\hat{y} = \\hat{\\beta}_0$. For $\\hat{y}$ to be equal to $\\bar{y}$ for all observations, the intercept $\\hat{\\beta}_0$ must be equal to $\\bar{y}$, not necessarily zero.\n\nTherefore, statements (i), (ii), and (iii) are true.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{J}$.",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 271,
        "output_tokens": 511,
        "total_tokens": 782
      },
      "provider": "openrouter",
      "error": null
    },
    "cost": null,
    "accuracy": null
  },
  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_economics_5619",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Why is it that interest payments on government bonds are not included as income currently earned, particularly, when interest on the bonds of private firms is included in na-tional income as earned income?\n\nOptions: \nA. Interest on government bonds is classified as a loan repayment rather than income\nB. Interest on government bonds is reinvested and not paid out\nC. Interest on government bonds is tax-free\nD. Interest payments are deferred to the maturity of the bonds\nE. Interest paid on such debt does not reflect the generation of any current output or income\nF. Government bonds are typically held by foreign investors, and their interest is not counted in domestic income\nG. The interest on government bonds is counted as part of the budget deficit, not national income\nH. Interest on government bonds is considered a transfer payment within the public sector\nI. Government bonds are not considered as income\nJ. Government bonds are not part of the national income\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The most likely correct answer is **E. Interest paid on such debt does not reflect the generation of any current output or income**.\n\nExplanation: National income accounting measures the value of goods and services produced in an economy. Interest payments on government bonds represent a transfer of existing wealth rather than the creation of new output. Therefore, they are not included as earned income in national income calculations.",
      "success": true,
