    "global index": "MMLUPro_law_1615",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: What is the 'Lotus principle'?\n\nOptions: \nA. The so-called Lotus principle is that customary law is superior to treaty law\nB. The so-called Lotus principle is that States have the right to intervene in the internal affairs of other States if it concerns their national security\nC. The so-called Lotus principle is that States are permitted to apply their own domestic laws in disputes with other States\nD. The so-called Lotus principle is that international law governs the relations between States and not the relations between individuals.\nE. The so-called Lotus principle is that States have unlimited jurisdiction over their own citizens, regardless of where they are located\nF. The so-called Lotus principle is that States are free to ignore international law as long as they don't infringe on the sovereignty of other States\nG. The so-called Lotus principle is that 'restrictions upon the independence of States cannot therefore be presumed', or, as it has been construed, 'whatever is not prohibited is permitted in international law'\nH. The so-called Lotus principle is that States are not prohibited to assert their enforcement jurisdiction on the high seas\nI. The so-called Lotus principle is that customary law derives from the combination of State practice and opinio juris\nJ. The so-called Lotus principle is that States are free to choose the Court that they will submit their disputes\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The Lotus principle, as established by the Permanent Court of International Justice in the *Lotus case*, is a fundamental concept in international law. It asserts the freedom of states to act unless there is a prohibition in international law. This means that if a particular action is not forbidden by international law, states are free to undertake it.\n\nLet's analyze the options:\nA. This is incorrect. The Lotus principle does not establish the superiority of customary law over treaty law.\nB. This is incorrect. The Lotus principle is not about the right to intervene in the internal affairs of other states based on national security.\nC. This is incorrect. The Lotus principle is not about applying domestic laws in disputes with other states.\nD. This is incorrect. While international law primarily governs relations between states, the Lotus principle itself doesn't focus on the exclusion of individuals.\nE. This is incorrect. While states have jurisdiction over their citizens, the Lotus principle doesn't grant \"unlimited\" jurisdiction universally.\nF. This is incorrect. States are not free to ignore international law; the principle is about what is *not prohibited*.\nG. This is the most accurate representation of the Lotus principle. The quote \"restrictions upon the independence of States cannot therefore be presumed\" is central to the principle, and its interpretation that \"whatever is not prohibited is permitted in international law\" is its common understanding.\nH. While the Lotus case did deal with jurisdiction on the high seas, this option is a specific application rather than the overarching principle.\nI. This describes the formation of customary law, not the Lotus principle itself.\nJ. This is incorrect. States have choices regarding dispute resolution, but this is not the \"Lotus principle.\"\n\nThe correct answer is **G**.",
