    "global index": "MMLUPro_law_861",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In which instance would a state, under the enabling clause of the Fourteenth Amendment, be most able to regulate?\n\nOptions: \nA. A federal official from discriminating against a person based on race.\nB. A federal official from discriminating against a person based on gender.\nC. A federal official from discriminating against a person based on nationality.\nD. A private company from discriminating against a person based on nationality.\nE. A private individual from discriminating against a person based on race.\nF. A state official from discriminating against a person based on race.\nG. A private individual from discriminating against a person based on gender.\nH. A private individual from discriminating against a person based on nationality.\nI. A private company from discriminating against a person based on race.\nJ. A state official from discriminating against a person based on nationality.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The enabling clause of the Fourteenth Amendment, also known as Section 5, grants Congress the power to enforce the provisions of the amendment through appropriate legislation. These provisions primarily prohibit states from infringing upon the rights of citizens. Therefore, the most direct and strongest application of this clause involves a state's ability to regulate in instances where *state actors* are involved in discrimination.\n\nOption F, \"A state official from discriminating against a person based on race,\" directly implicates a state actor and the Fourteenth Amendment's core protections against racial discrimination by states. The other options involve federal officials, private entities, or private individuals, which are either not directly addressed by the Fourteenth Amendment's enabling clause or are significantly less central to its enforcement power.\n\nThe correct answer is \\boxed{F}.",
      "success": true,
