    "global index": "MMLUPro_law_1469",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Two brothers owned a parcel of real estate as joint tenants. Both brothers signed a deed as grantors conveying the land to the buyer. The first brother handed the deed to the second brother with instructions to take the deed to their lawyer for approval prior to delivery. Brother two, without his brother's permission, took the deed directly to the buyer and collected the sale price. Is this a good deed as to partner one?\n\nOptions: \nA. Yes, the deed was signed by both partners, which proved their intent to sell to buyer, and it was delivered at the time of signing by the fact of affixing their signatures to the document.\nB. Yes, the deed is valid as the buyer was not aware of the internal agreement between the two brothers.\nC. No, the deed is invalid as partner one did not give explicit permission for partner two to deliver the deed.\nD. Yes, the deed is valid as partner two had the authority to finalize the deal.\nE. No, the deed is invalid because partner two collected the sale price without partner one's consent.\nF. Yes, the deed is valid as both brothers had signed it, signifying their agreement to the sale.\nG. Yes, the transfer is valid from both partners because partner two was partner one's apparent agent for purposes of delivering the deed.\nH. No, the deed was invalid as to both grantors because partner two stepped outside his scope of authority.\nI. No, the deed cannot bind partner one because he did not participate in the deed delivery to the buyer and did not intend to deliver the deed up to the grantee at that time.\nJ. No, the deed is not valid as the lawyer did not approve it before delivery.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The question hinges on the concept of delivery of a deed, which is essential for a valid transfer of real property. Delivery requires the grantor's intent to be bound by the deed and the relinquishment of control. In this scenario, the first brother explicitly instructed that the deed be taken to their lawyer for approval *prior* to delivery. This indicates he did not intend to be bound by the deed at the time it was handed to his brother, and that his brother's actions were contrary to his expressed wishes. Therefore, there was no valid delivery as to the first brother.\n\nI. No, the deed cannot bind partner one because he did not participate in the deed delivery to the buyer and did not intend to deliver the deed up to the grantee at that time.\n\nThe crucial element missing here is the intent of the first brother and his actual delivery of the deed. He intended for the deed to be held by his brother until their lawyer approved it, meaning he did not intend to relinquish control and be bound at the moment his brother took possession. Therefore, the deed's delivery to the buyer was not authorized or intended by the first brother.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{I}$.",
      "success": true,
