    "global index": "MMLUPro_law_1389",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: There may be a substantial time period between contract formation and final completion of performance. Concerning these executory contracts, which of the following is the least correct?\n\nOptions: \nA. A fully executory contract is when both parties have completed their performance.\nB. A partially executed contract means that both parties have begun but not completed performance.\nC. An executory contract is when one party has performed and the other party has not yet begun performance.\nD. An executed contract exists when both parties have fully performed and no obligations remain.\nE. A partially executed contract means that at least one party has begun performance.\nF. A partially executory contract is when one party has begun performance and the other party has completed performance.\nG. A fully executed contract exists when neither party has any remaining obligations.\nH. A partially executed contract means that one party has completed performance while the other party has only promised.\nI. A wholly executory contract is where only promises have been exchanged and there has been no performance by either party.\nJ. An executory contract is when both parties have yet to begin performance.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Let's analyze the options in relation to the definition of an executory contract. An executory contract is one where performance is still due from one or both parties.\n\n*   **A. A fully executory contract is when both parties have completed their performance.** This is incorrect. If both parties have completed performance, it's an executed contract, not executory.\n*   **B. A partially executed contract means that both parties have begun but not completed performance.** This is a reasonable description of a partially executory or partially executed contract.\n*   **C. An executory contract is when one party has performed and the other party has not yet begun performance.** This is a form of executory contract.\n*   **D. An executed contract exists when both parties have fully performed and no obligations remain.** This is the correct definition of an executed contract.\n*   **E. A partially executed contract means that at least one party has begun performance.** This is a correct, though broad, statement.\n*   **F. A partially executory contract is when one party has begun performance and the other party has completed performance.** This is a correct description.\n*   **G. A fully executed contract exists when neither party has any remaining obligations.** This is a correct definition.\n*   **H. A partially executed contract means that one party has completed performance while the other party has only promised.** This is a correct description.\n*   **I. A wholly executory contract is where only promises have been exchanged and there has been no performance by either party.** This is a correct definition.\n*   **J. An executory contract is when both parties have yet to begin performance.** This is a correct definition.\n\nThe question asks for the *least correct* statement concerning executory contracts. Option A directly contradicts the definition of an executory contract by equating it with a completed performance, which defines an executed contract.\n\nThe least correct statement is A. An executory contract implies outstanding obligations for at least one party. Stating that both parties have completed their performance defines an executed contract, a concept distinct from executory. Therefore, option A is fundamentally flawed in its definition of an executory contract.\n\n\\boxed{A}",
