    "global index": "MMLUPro_law_1251",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A state law prohibits any barbershop licensed by the state from displaying posters in support of any current candidate for public office or displaying or distributing any campaign literature in support of such a candidate. No other kinds of posters or literature are subject to this prohibition, nor are any other types of commercial establishments in the state subject to similar prohibitions. Is this law constitutional?\n\nOptions: \nA. Yes, because it leaves political candidates free to communicate their campaign messages to voters by other means.\nB. Yes, because it prevents potential bias towards a candidate in a professional establishment.\nC. Yes, because the prohibition is limited to the display of campaign material and does not restrict other forms of political expression.\nD. No, because it imposes a restriction on the content or subject matter of speech in the absence of any evidence that such a restriction is necessary to serve a compelling state interest.\nE. Yes, because the law only applies to current candidates and not former or future ones.\nF. Yes, because the operation of a licensed barbershop is a privilege and therefore is subject to any reasonable restriction imposed by the state.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The question asks whether a state law is constitutional that prohibits licensed barbershops from displaying political campaign materials. This law targets a specific type of speech (political speech in support of a candidate) in a specific type of establishment (barbershops) and no other businesses. Such a selective restriction on speech content without a compelling justification is likely unconstitutional.\n\nOption A is incorrect because the ability of candidates to communicate through other means does not justify restricting their speech in barbershops. Option B is incorrect; preventing potential bias is not a compelling state interest that justifies restricting political speech. Option C is incorrect; while the prohibition is limited to campaign material, it is still a content-based restriction on speech. Option E is irrelevant to the constitutionality of the law. Option F is incorrect; even licensed businesses cannot be subjected to arbitrary restrictions on fundamental rights like free speech.\n\nOption D correctly identifies that the law imposes a content-based restriction on speech and fails to demonstrate a compelling state interest. Laws that restrict the content of speech are subject to strict scrutiny, requiring the government to show a compelling interest and that the law is narrowly tailored to achieve that interest. This law likely fails this test.\n\nThe correct answer is \\boxed{D}.",
