    "global index": "MMLUPro_law_1232",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Farmer Fred was the owner of Grapeview Farm, the largest farm in the County of Grapeview. Fred was advised by his gift and estate attorney to begin making annual fractionalized gifts of the property to his children. Fred executed a conveyance instrument, stating the gifts as \"one-thirtieth of my ownership interest in Grapeview County, the eastern one tenth of the western corner of the property is hereby given to all my children per capita.\" This description of the land to be conveyed is\n\nOptions: \nA. Sufficient, because it gives a clear description of the fractional gift.\nB. Sufficient, because it mentions the name of the county and the property.\nC. Sufficient, because it includes the name of the property and a portion of it.\nD. Insufficient, because it doesn't state the exact measurements of the land to be given.\nE. Insufficient, because the gifts to the children lacked consideration.\nF. Insufficient, because it doesn't specify the exact location of the eastern one-tenth.\nG. Insufficient, because it is too vague.\nH. Sufficient, because the property can be identified.\nI. Sufficient, because it satisfies the title of seisin.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Let's analyze the description of the land to be conveyed: \"one-thirtieth of my ownership interest in Grapeview County, the eastern one tenth of the western corner of the property is hereby given to all my children per capita.\"\n\nHere's a breakdown of the issues:\n\n*   **\"one-thirtieth of my ownership interest in Grapeview County\"**: This is inherently problematic. A county is a political subdivision, not a piece of property that one can own a fractional interest in to gift. This phrase is nonsensical in a property conveyance.\n*   **\"the eastern one tenth of the western corner of the property\"**:\n    *   \"western corner of the property\": While there might be a western corner, how large is this \"corner\"? A corner is a point or an angle, not typically a definable area without more specific dimensions.\n    *   \"eastern one tenth\": One-tenth of *what*? One-tenth of the area of this undefined \"western corner\"? This makes the description incredibly difficult to pinpoint with certainty. The exact location is not specified, and \"western corner\" is not a standard legal description of an area.\n\nNow let's evaluate the options:\n\n*   A. Sufficient, because it gives a clear description of the fractional gift. - The description is far from clear.\n*   B. Sufficient, because it mentions the name of the county and the property. - Mentioning the county is not relevant for the property description, and while the property name is mentioned, the description of the *gifted portion* is the issue.\n*   C. Sufficient, because it includes the name of the property and a portion of it. - The description of the portion is not sufficient.\n*   D. Insufficient, because it doesn't state the exact measurements of the land to be given. - While exact measurements are often good, the primary problem is not the lack of exact measurements but the vagueness of the *area* being described.\n*   E. Insufficient, because the gifts to the children lacked consideration. - This is a gift, and consideration is not required for a valid gift between family members.\n*   F. Insufficient, because it doesn't specify the exact location of the eastern one-tenth. - This is a significant part of the problem, as the \"western corner\" is not well-defined, and therefore \"the eastern one tenth\" of it is also not defined.\n*   G. Insufficient, because it is too vague. - This encompasses the overall problem. The description lacks the certainty and specificity required for a legal description of real property.\n*   H. Sufficient, because the property can be identified. - The *overall property* might be identifiable by its name, but the *specific portion being conveyed by the gift* is not.\n*   I. Sufficient, because it satisfies the title of seisin. - The title of seisin refers to possession and the right to possess, which is distinct from a sufficient property description.\n\nThe description is fundamentally flawed and too vague to reliably identify the specific portion of land being gifted. The terms used (\"western corner,\" \"eastern one tenth\" of that corner) are not precise enough for a legal description, and the initial phrase about owning an interest in a county is nonsensical. Therefore, the description is insufficient. Option G best captures the essence of why it's insufficient.\n\nThe description of the gifted land is clearly too vague to be legally sufficient. Phrases like \"western corner\" are not precise, and calculating \"one tenth\" of an undefined area makes the description impossible to locate with certainty. Therefore, the conveyance instrument fails to provide a definite description of the property being transferred.\n\n\\boxed{G}",
