      "generated_answer": "The question describes a treaty between the President and a foreign government. In the U.S. system, treaties require ratification by two-thirds of the Senate to be valid. While the President negotiates and enters into treaties, the Senate's role is to provide advice and consent, which includes ratification. Therefore, the treaty-making power, specifically the Senate's ratification, is the most relevant principle.\n\n\\boxed{G}",
