    "global index": "MMLUPro_law_1208",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In a secluded county, where prejudice festers and discrimination flourishes, there is a lovely lake, which the county has developed and maintained for recreational purposes. Although it is not the only lake in the county, it is the largest and most scenic, and it attracts visitors from miles around. One of its biggest assets is the excellent fishing and boating, which is available to the public at large. Three years ago, in order to enhance the recreational aspects of the lake, the county leased a sizable portion of the lake and surrounding parkland to a company owned by the most prominent family in the county. The lease required the company to construct and operate a first-rate yacht house and club, complete with bar, restaurant, and private marina, and to pay the county 10% of its net profits as rent. The company set up bylaws, which were reviewed and approved by the county at the time the lease was negotiated. According to the bylaws, the yacht club, complete with its restaurant and bar, would be open to members only, and the membership committee was empowered to set up strict membership \"standards,\" as well as the cost of membership fees and dues. Upon completion of the facilities, the state granted the company a license to sell alcoholic beverages in its restaurant and bar. The membership committee announced that the membership fee was $5,000 and the monthly dues $75. Furthermore, the membership committee had a policy of approving only membership applications from men, while disapproving and denying all applications from women. There were other similar facilities within the county available to women. A woman resident of the county brings suit against the company, claiming that her membership application was denied only because she is a woman, and that its policy of excluding women as a group denies her equal protection rights. Which of the following is the most accurate statement?\n\nOptions: \nA. The plaintiff will prevail, because the company is in violation of the county's approved bylaws.\nB. The plaintiff will prevail if she can prove the company's policy is based on gender discrimination.\nC. The plaintiff will prevail, because the company's policy is discriminatory and violates her civil rights.\nD. The plaintiff will lose, because she can use other facilities within the county.\nE. The plaintiff will lose, because the company is a private entity and not subject to equal protection laws.\nF. The plaintiff will lose, because the company has the right to set its own membership criteria.\nG. The plaintiff will lose, because classifications based on sex have not yet been held to violate the equal protection clause.\nH. The plaintiff will prevail unless the company can prove some important basis for the exclusion of women.\nI. The plaintiff will lose, because other similar facilities are available to women.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The most accurate statement is **E**.\n\nThe core issue here is whether the \"equal protection rights\" apply. The Equal Protection Clause of the U.S. Constitution (and similar state provisions) generally applies to state action, not to purely private entities. While the county leased land to the company, the company itself is a private entity setting its own membership rules. For the equal protection clause to apply, there would need to be significant state involvement or entanglement with the private discrimination, which is not clearly established by the facts given for a direct equal protection claim against the company's membership policy. The lease agreement and approval of bylaws do not automatically transform the private club into a state actor for all purposes, particularly regarding its internal membership policies.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{E}$",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 623,
        "output_tokens": 159,
        "total_tokens": 782
      },
      "provider": "openrouter",
      "error": null
    },
    "cost": null,
    "accuracy": null
  },
  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_law_1815",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Pursuant to a newly enacted statute, Congress created a nine-member agency empowered to promulgate rules governing air quality standards for the nation. In accordance with the statute, the President was authorized to appoint a majority of six members to the agency, and the other three positions were to be filled by the Senate. The nine members were duly appointed to the agency, and all appointees were subsequently approved in confirmation hearings. Thereafter, the agency issued the following regulations:(a) Requiring each motor vehicle operating in the United States to be equipped with a specified air/fuel control device;(b) Requiring each gas or oil furnace located in the United States to be fitted with a specified device to reduce emissions;(c) Requiring each State to establish and maintain a program under which each vehicle and each furnace shall be tested annually for compliance with federal emissions standards. Which of the following arguments would provide the strongest constitutional grounds against the authority of the statute?\n\nOptions: \nA. The President does not have the constitutional power to appoint members to an administrative agency without the approval of Congress.\nB. An administrative agency does not have the constitutional authority to issue regulations that directly affect the economy without the approval of Congress.\nC. The President does not have the constitutional power to appoint a majority of members to an administrative agency.\nD. An administrative agency does not have the constitutional authority to enforce regulations on private property without due process.\nE. The statute is unconstitutional because it grants too much power to a single administrative agency.\nF. An administrative agency does not have the constitutional authority to require states to supervise federal regulatory guidelines such as those enumerated in Subsection (c).\nG. The statute is unconstitutional because it does not provide for judicial review of agency decisions.\nH. An administrative agency does not have the constitutional authority to promulgate regulations that unduly burden interstate commerce.\nI. Congress does not have the executive authority to appoint members to an administrative agency.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The strongest constitutional argument against the authority of the statute relates to the appointment of agency members and Congress's potential overreach in creating such an agency. Let's analyze the options:\n\nA. The President's appointment power is generally subject to Senate confirmation for certain officers, but this option states Congress can appoint more than the Senate. This is not the primary issue, especially since the Senate *did* fill three positions.\n\nB. Administrative agencies are generally empowered to regulate the economy within their statutory scope. This is a common function of administrative law and not inherently unconstitutional.\n\nC. The President is authorized to appoint a majority of members. The statute dictates this, so the argument is against the statute's validity, not the President's power to appoint *without* it. The question is whether Congress can *create* such an appointment structure.\n\nD. Due process is a fundamental requirement, but agencies can issue regulations that affect private property. The argument would be about whether *these specific* regulations violate due process, not a general prohibition.\n\nE. Granting significant power to an agency is not *per se* unconstitutional, provided that power is properly delegated by Congress and subject to constitutional limitations.\n\nF. This is a strong argument, known as the \"anti-commandeering doctrine,\" which prevents Congress from forcing states to implement or enforce federal regulatory programs. Subsection (c) directly mandates states to establish and maintain programs for testing, which is a clear instance of commandeering.\n\nG. The absence of judicial review can be grounds for unconstitutionality, but it's not necessarily the *strongest* argument compared to structural constitutional issues.\n\nH. While agencies can regulate commerce, regulations that \"unduly burden\" interstate commerce can be unconstitutional. However, the regulations described (vehicle devices, furnace devices, and state testing) seem to be aimed at improving air quality, a legitimate federal interest. The burden would need to be proven as \"undue.\"\n\nI. Congress does not have executive authority to appoint members; that is generally the President's role (with Senate confirmation). This option suggests Congress appointed members, which is not what the facts state.\n\nConsidering the options, the \"anti-commandeering\" principle in option F presents the most direct and established constitutional challenge to subsection (c) of the regulations, as it forces states to implement federal mandates.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{F}$.",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 453,
        "output_tokens": 496,
        "total_tokens": 949
      },
      "provider": "openrouter",
      "error": null
    },
    "cost": null,
    "accuracy": null
  },
  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_law_1462",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: On September 15, a card shop sent the following fax to a printing company: \"Please deliver 100 dozen assorted Christmas cards, delivery by November 1. \"On October 10, the printing company shipped 100 dozen assorted Thanksgiving cards to the card shop, which were received on October 12. The following day, the card shop sent the following fax to the printing company: \"Be advised that your shipment is rejected. Order stipulated Christmas cards, not Thanksgiving cards. \"On October 15, the printing company sent the following fax to the card shop: \"Will ship 100 dozen assorted Christmas cards by November 1. Please ship the Thanksgiving cards back to our warehouse and bill us for the shipping charges. \" This fax was received by the card shop, but the card shop did not respond to it or ship the Thanksgiving cards back to the printing company. On October 25, the printing company attempted to deliver 100 dozen assorted Christmas cards to the card shop, but the latter refused to accept. Did the card shop properly reject the October 25 delivery?\n\nOptions: \nA. No, because the card shop did not explicitly agree to the modified offer from the printing company.\nB. No, because the printing company cured the October 10 defective shipment by its tender of conforming goods on October 25.\nC. Yes, because the printing company's shipping of the Thanksgiving cards on October 10 constituted a present breach of contract.\nD. Yes, because the card shop was not obligated to accept the second shipment of Christmas cards.\nE. No, because under the UCC a contract for the sale of goods can be modified without consideration.\nF. Yes, because the printing company did not deliver the correct order initially as requested by the card shop.\nG. Yes, because the card shop had already rejected the initial shipment of Thanksgiving cards.\nH. Yes, because the printing company's shipping of the Thanksgiving cards on October 10 constituted an anticipatory breach.\nI. No, because the card shop did not return the Thanksgiving cards as requested by the printing company.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The question asks whether the card shop properly rejected the October 25 delivery. The printing company's initial shipment was non-conforming (Thanksgiving cards instead of Christmas cards). This non-conformity was a breach of contract. The printing company's subsequent offer to ship Christmas cards did not create a new contract or obligation until accepted by the card shop. Since the card shop did not respond to the October 15 fax, there was no agreement to the modified offer. Therefore, the card shop was not obligated to accept the October 25 delivery, which would have been a second attempt to fulfill an unaccepted modified offer.\n\nLet's analyze the options:\nA. No, because the card shop did not explicitly agree to the modified offer from the printing company. This is a plausible reason why the card shop might have been justified in rejecting the October 25 delivery. However, the question is framed as 'properly reject', implying a right to reject.\nB. No, because the printing company cured the October 10 defective shipment by its tender of conforming goods on October 25. The printing company did not have an absolute right to cure here because the defect was substantial and had already been rejected. Moreover, no new valid contract for Christmas cards existed that they could cure.\nC. Yes, because the printing company's shipping of the Thanksgiving cards on October 10 constituted a present breach of contract. This is correct. The initial shipment was a breach, giving the card shop the right to reject.\nD. Yes, because the card shop was not obligated to accept the second shipment of Christmas cards. This is also true, but it relies on the premise that the second shipment was not under a binding contract.\nE. No, because under the UCC a contract for the sale of goods can be modified without consideration. While true, this doesn't address whether the rejection was proper, but rather the validity of the modification attempt.\nF. Yes, because the printing company did not deliver the correct order initially as requested by the card shop. This is a correct reason for rejecting the initial shipment, but the question is about the October 25 delivery.\nG. Yes, because the card shop had already rejected the initial shipment of Thanksgiving cards. This is also a correct statement, and the prior rejection solidifies the card shop's position concerning the original contract.\nH. Yes, because the printing company's shipping of the Thanksgiving cards on October 10 constituted an anticipatory breach. This is incorrect; it was a present breach, not anticipatory.\nI. No, because the card shop did not return the Thanksgiving cards as requested by the printing company. The card shop was not obligated to return non-conforming goods until the printing company made suitable arrangements.\n\nThe most direct and overarching reason for the card shop properly rejecting the October 25 delivery is that the initial shipment was a breach, and the subsequent offer to ship Christmas cards was not accepted, thus no new contract was formed for the October 25 delivery. Option C directly addresses the initial breach, which is the root cause of the issue. Option G is also a strong contender as the prior rejection is a key factor. However, let's consider the timeline and the legal implications. The contract was for Christmas cards. The printing company breached by sending Thanksgiving cards. The card shop rejected the Thanksgiving cards. The printing company then proposed a modified contract to ship Christmas cards, but this proposal was not accepted. Therefore, when the printing company attempted to deliver Christmas cards on October 25, there was no existing contract that obligated the card shop to accept them.\n\nConsidering the options provided, let's re-evaluate:\nThe core issue is whether the card shop had a right to reject the October 25 delivery. The printing company's initial act of shipping Thanksgiving cards instead of Christmas cards was a material breach of the contract as formed by the fax exchange. The card shop's rejection of the Thanksgiving cards was proper. The printing company's subsequent fax on October 15 was an offer to modify the original contract. Since the card shop did not respond to this offer, it was not accepted. Therefore, no contract existed for the printing company to ship Christmas cards on October 25, and thus the card shop was entirely within its rights to refuse the delivery.\n\nOption C states: \"Yes, because the printing company's shipping of the Thanksgiving cards on October 10 constituted a present breach of contract.\" This is why the card shop had the right to reject the Thanksgiving cards, and it sets the stage for the subsequent events. Because of this prior breach, the cards shop was under no obligation to accept any further performance under the original contract unless the defect was cured within the contract timeframe and terms, or a new contract was formed and breached.\n\nOption D states: \"Yes, because the card shop was not obligated to accept the second shipment of Christmas cards.\" This is also true and is a direct consequence of the lack of an accepted contract for that shipment. However, C explains *why* the card shop was in a position to refuse a second shipment or any subsequent performance without a new agreement.\n\nOption G states: \"Yes, because the card shop had already rejected the initial shipment of Thanksgiving cards.\" This is a factual statement leading to the card shop's current position of demanding the correct goods or nothing further.\n\nLet's consider the framing of the question: \"Did the card shop properly reject the October 25 delivery?\" The rejection of the October 25 delivery is proper because there was no contract obligating acceptance. The *reason* there was no contract is the unaccepted modification. Why was the modification unaccepted or why wasn't the original contract re-established as intended? Because of the initial breach.\n\nOption C directly identifies the fundamental error by the printing company that gives the card shop leverage. The breach on October 10 is the foundational event that allows the card shop to reject any subsequent non-conforming performance or performance based on an unaccepted modification.\n\nFinal consideration: The question is about the *rejection* of the October 25 delivery. Why was this rejection proper? Because there was no valid contract in place for that delivery. The lack of a valid contract stems from the initial breach and the subsequent unaccepted modification. Option C points to the initial breach, which invalidates the original contract's executory portion and necessitates a new agreement or a valid cure. Since a new agreement was not formed, the October 25 delivery was outside of any contractual obligation for the card shop.\n\nRevisiting the options, C is a strong candidate. Let's analyze D again. \"Yes, because the card shop was not obligated to accept the second shipment of Christmas cards.\" Why was it not obligated? Because no valid contract for the second shipment existed. The reason no valid contract existed is the unaccepted modification, which in turn is a consequence of the initial breach. So C explains the initial problem, and D explains the consequence for the October 25th delivery. However, the refusal on Oct 25th is a refusal to perform under a purported contract. The reason there's no contract is the unaccepted modification. The printing company tried to rectify its mistake, but it was not binding on the card shop without acceptance.\n\nLet's focus on what makes the rejection *proper*. A proper rejection means the card shop had the right to reject. This right stems from the fact that the October 25th delivery was not made pursuant to a binding contract. The printing company's October 15th fax was an offer to modify. The card shop's failure to respond meant no acceptance, and thus no new contract. Therefore, the card shop was not obligated to accept the October 25th delivery. This aligns with option D.\n\nLet's compare C and D more closely. C states *why* the card shop had rights in general (due to breach). D states *why* the specific rejection event (Oct 25th delivery) was proper. The question is about the rejection of the October 25 delivery. Thus, D is more directly answering the question about *that specific rejection*. The card shop was not obligated to accept that shipment because no valid contract existed for it.\n\nFinal re-evaluation: The contract was for Christmas cards. The printing company sent Thanksgiving cards. This was a breach. The card shop rightfully rejected it. The printing company proposed sending Christmas cards. The card shop did not respond, meaning no acceptance of this modification. Therefore, there was no contract obligating the card shop to accept the Christmas cards on Oct 25. Thus, the rejection was proper. Option D captures this perfectly.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{D}$.",
